/* GéoCulture — contenu traduit (en) : 558 lieux, 192 événements. Généré, ne pas éditer à la main. */
GC_APPLY_CONTENT({"R":{"tour_eiffel":["Eiffel Tower — Paris, France","A universal symbol of France, the Eiffel Tower was first derided as an “iron wart” before becoming the most visited paid monument in the world, with 7 million visitors a year.","In the 7th arrondissement, on the left bank of a river, facing the Trocadéro hill","330 m puddled-iron tower raised on the Champ de Mars for the 1889 World's Fair","The world's most visited paid monument, the “Iron Lady”, symbol of Paris","The Eiffel Tower in Paris, France"],"notre_dame_de_paris":["Notre-Dame de Paris — France","A jewel of French Gothic, Notre-Dame came close to being lost in the fire of April 2019. Its reconstruction brought together craftspeople from all over the world, and it reopened its doors in December 2024.","Île de la Cité, Gothic cathedral whose construction began in 1163 under Bishop Maurice de Sully","Gothic cathedral with 69 m towers, whose forecourt holds the point zero of the country's roads","Cathedral in France gutted by fire in 2019 and reopened in December 2024","Notre-Dame Cathedral in Paris, France"],"moulin_rouge":["Moulin Rouge — Montmartre, Paris","Founded in 1889 by Joseph Oller and Charles Zidler, the Moulin Rouge popularised the French cancan and welcomed Toulouse-Lautrec, Mistinguett and Édith Piaf. Its feather-and-sequin revues have made it a symbol of party-loving Paris.","Boulevard de Clichy, Pigalle — cabaret founded in 1889 by Charles Zidler and Joseph Oller","Cabaret at the foot of a famous hill, on the Right Bank of the French capital","Parisian cabaret world-famous for the French cancan, Paris","The Moulin Rouge cabaret in Paris, France"],"colisee":["Colosseum — Rome, Italy","The largest amphitheatre of antiquity could be flooded with water to stage mock naval battles. Its 80 exits allowed 80,000 spectators to be evacuated in under 10 minutes.","Monument in the historic centre, between the Roman Forum and the Palatine Hill","Flavian Amphitheatre built under Vespasian in AD 70, Via Sacra, between the Forum and the Palatine","The largest ancient amphitheatre in the world, able to hold 80,000 spectators, Rome","The Colosseum in Rome, Italy"],"chapelle_sixtine":["Sistine Chapel — Vatican","Michelangelo painted the vault's roughly 500 m² in 4 years (1508–1512), standing on a scaffold with his head tilted back — not lying down, as legend has it. The Creation of Adam, in which the fingers of God and man almost touch, remains one of the most reproduced images in history.","A vault painted in fresco over four years, at the heart of a landlocked city-state near a river","Palace chapel, ceiling painted by Michelangelo between 1508 and 1512, commissioned by Pope Julius II","Chapel of the world's smallest sovereign state, seat of the Catholic papacy","The Sistine Chapel in Vatican City (an enclave within Rome)"],"cathedrale_de_sagrada_familia_nuit":["Sagrada Família — Barcelona, Spain","Gaudí devoted the last 43 years of his life to this basilica. Knocked down by a tram in 1926, he was buried in its crypt. Funded by visitors' admission fees and donations, the project was aiming for 2026, the centenary of his death, to complete the towers, but the decoration and the Glory façade will remain under construction for several more years.","Famous church still under construction since 1882, bristling with spindle-shaped towers","Antoni Gaudí's basilica, a building site since 1882 in a Modernista district — still under construction","Europe's most visited unfinished basilica, in Catalonia, Barcelona","The Sagrada Família in Barcelona, Spain"],"alhambra":["Alhambra — Granada, Spain","This Nasrid palace holds some of the finest Islamic decoration in the world. Its Generalife gardens, fed by a 700-year-old hydraulic system, stay in bloom all year round.","14th-century Nasrid palace and fortress on a hill at the foot of a snow-capped sierra","Hill overlooking the Albaicín quarter, facing the Sierra Nevada, Andalusia","Masterpiece of Moorish architecture, the most visited in Spain, Granada","The Alhambra in Granada, Andalusia, Spain"],"big_ben":["Big Ben — London, United Kingdom","Big Ben is not the tower but the 13.7-tonne bell it houses. Silenced during the restoration of the tower from 2017 to 2022, it resumed its regular chimes in November 2022.","Westminster, 500 m from 10 Downing Street, the political heart of the capital","Elizabeth Tower at the Palace of Westminster, completed in 1859, on the banks of the Thames","Iconic clock of the British Parliament, symbol of London","Big Ben in London, United Kingdom"],"stonehenge":["Stonehenge — Wiltshire, United Kingdom","Stonehenge was built in several phases between 3000 and 1500 BC. Its large sarsen blocks, weighing up to 25 tonnes, came from hills about 25 km away, while the smaller “bluestones” were brought from Wales, more than 200 km away.","Ring of megaliths erected between 3000 and 1500 BC","Prehistoric monument in the county of Wiltshire, on a plain in the south of the country, 13 km from Salisbury Cathedral","Europe's most famous prehistoric monument, a UNESCO World Heritage Site","Stonehenge in Wiltshire, England (United Kingdom)"],"acropole":["Acropolis — Athens, Greece","The Parthenon, completed in 432 BC, has almost no straight lines: its columns, tilted slightly inwards and gently swelling, create an optical illusion that makes it look perfectly straight.","156 m limestone rock towering over the city, site of the Parthenon dedicated to Athena","Fortified sacred hill overlooking the Plaka district, at the heart of Attica","The most famous ancient Greek temple in the world, cradle of Western civilisation, Athens","The Acropolis in Athens, Greece"],"palais_de_l_elysee":["Élysée Palace — Paris, France","Built from 1718 to 1722, the Élysée Palace was the residence of Madame de Pompadour, favourite of Louis XV. The presidential residence since 1848, it hides beneath its gardens the PC Jupiter, the command post from which the president can order the use of the nuclear strike force.","Mansion built from 1718 to 1722 for a count, later acquired by a king for his favourite","Presidential residence since 1848, on the Rue du Faubourg-Saint-Honoré, in the 8th arrondissement of a capital","Residence of the President of the French Republic, Champs-Élysées district, between the Grand Palais and the Madeleine","The Élysée Palace in Paris, France"],"chateau_de_versailles":["Palace of Versailles — France","Versailles swallowed 6% of the kingdom's total budget under Louis XIV. Its 800-hectare gardens took 1,200 people to maintain. The king rose at 8:30 in a ceremony called the “Lever”: 200 courtiers jostled for the honour of holding his shirt or handing him his wig.","Former hunting lodge turned into a palace from 1661 onwards to keep the nobility under the sovereign's eye, Hall of Mirrors","Vast château and royal residence in a commune of the Yvelines, 17 km south-west of a great capital","The world's largest château by area, symbol of absolute monarchy, near Paris","The Palace of Versailles, near Paris (France)"],"rijksmuseum":["Rijksmuseum — Amsterdam, Netherlands","Rembrandt's “Night Watch” measures 3.6 m × 4.3 m. To move it during a restoration in 1975, the Dutch authorities cut a special door into the museum wall.","Brick national museum opened in 1885, home to Rembrandt's The Night Watch","Major art museum beside a park, in the museum quarter of a city criss-crossed by canals","The country's leading museum of art and history, in Amsterdam","The Rijksmuseum in Amsterdam, Netherlands"],"maison_d_anne_frank":["Anne Frank House — Amsterdam","Anne Frank wrote her diary between 1942 and 1944 in a secret annex hidden behind a revolving bookcase. Translated into 70 languages, her account is one of the most widely read books in the world.","Annex where a Jewish teenager and her family hid from 1942 to 1944, a diary that became famous","Canal-side house museum in the Jordaan district, on the west side of a city of brick and water","Museum dedicated to the author of a harrowing wartime diary, Amsterdam","The Anne Frank House in Amsterdam, Netherlands"],"mur_de_berlin":["Berlin Wall (East Side Gallery) — Berlin, Germany","On the night of 12 to 13 August 1961, barbed wire cut Berlin in two; the concrete wall replaced it over the following days. It fell on 9 November 1989, not by official decision but after a poorly prepared announcement at a televised press conference. Its longest surviving stretch, the East Side Gallery (1.3 km), has been covered in murals since 1990.","Long open-air mural on the remains of a concrete barrier put up in 1961, eastern sector","Remnant of a painted wall on the banks of the Spree, former eastern sector of a once-divided capital","The largest surviving section of the wall that divided the city from 1961 to 1989, Berlin","The remains of the Berlin Wall, Germany"],"neuschwanstein":["Neuschwanstein Castle — Bavaria, Germany","Ludwig II of Bavaria slept only 11 nights in his fairy-tale castle before being declared insane and dying in mysterious circumstances. Walt Disney drew on it directly for Sleeping Beauty's castle.","Neo-Romanesque castle commissioned in 1869 by a building-mad king devoted to Wagner, left unfinished at his death in 1886","Hilltop neo-Romanesque castle in the Alpine foothills near Füssen, close to a southern border","Fairy-tale castle that inspired the Walt Disney logo, Bavaria","Neuschwanstein Castle in Bavaria, Germany"],"tour_de_pise":["Leaning Tower of Pisa — Italy","The Tower of Pisa has leaned since its second phase of construction in the 12th century, because the ground on one side was too soft. Work carried out from 1990 to 2001 reduced the tilt by 44 cm, stabilising it for the next 200 years.","Bell tower of a cathedral, begun in 1173, leaning by nearly 4° because of soft ground","White marble bell tower on the Square of Miracles, on the Tuscan plain","World-famous leaning tower, Tuscan emblem, Pisa","The Leaning Tower of Pisa in Tuscany, Italy"],"palais_des_doges":["Doge's Palace — Venice, Italy","The Doge's Palace housed the Bridge of Sighs, across which the condemned passed from the interrogation chamber to the prison, heaving a last sigh as they glimpsed Venice.","Gothic palace of a former maritime republic, seat of a merchant power from the 9th to the 18th century","Palace of marble lace on the edge of a lagoon, in a city built on 118 islets linked by bridges","Palace symbolising the power of the Doges, city of canals, Venice","The Doge's Palace in Venice, Italy"],"cathedrale_saint_basile":["Saint Basil's Cathedral — Moscow, Russia","Legend has it that Ivan the Terrible had his architects blinded once the cathedral was finished, so that they could never build anything so beautiful elsewhere. The story is probably apocryphal.","Church with flame-like domes built from 1555 to 1561 under a feared tsar, on a large cobbled square","Cathedral with twisted onion domes on a central square, between a fortress-citadel and a state department store","Cathedral with multicoloured onion domes, emblem of a vast country, Moscow","Saint Basil's Cathedral on Red Square in Moscow, Russia"],"palais_du_parlement":["Palace of the Parliament — Bucharest, Romania","Bucharest's Parliament is the second-largest administrative building in the world after the Pentagon. Ceaușescu had a fifth of Bucharest's old town razed to build it — including 19 Orthodox churches and 7 synagogues.","Administrative colossus commissioned by a dictator in 1984, still partly empty, the world's second-largest building of its kind","Titanic parliamentary palace on Spirii Hill, in the centre of an Eastern European capital","Administrative building reputed to be the heaviest in the world, seat of parliament, Bucharest","The Palace of the Parliament in Bucharest, Romania"],"grande_muraille_de_chine":["Great Wall of China — Mutianyu","Contrary to legend, the Great Wall is not visible to the naked eye from space. But at 21,196 km it remains one of the longest structures ever built by humans — half the Earth's circumference.","Restored section with 23 watchtowers, built under the Ming dynasty (1368–1644) on older foundations","Long wall snaking along mountain ridges, north-east of a vast capital","The longest human-made structure, 21,196 km in total, Mutianyu section near Beijing","The Great Wall of China (Mutianyu section, near Beijing)"],"cite_interdit":["Forbidden City — Beijing, China","With its 9,999 rooms (one fewer than the heavenly palace of the gods, according to tradition), the Forbidden City was home to 24 Chinese emperors. No ordinary citizen set foot inside for 500 years.","Imperial palace of 980 buildings erected between 1406 and 1420, ringed by a moat and crimson walls","Vast palace with golden roofs in the heart of a capital, just north of a world-famous square, surrounded by a moat","The largest palace in the world, home of the emperors for five centuries, Beijing","The Forbidden City in Beijing, China"],"temple_d_or_harmandir_sahib":["Golden Temple (Harmandir Sahib) — Amritsar, India","The Golden Temple is covered in 750 kg of pure gold leaf. Sikhs cook and serve 100,000 free meals a day to all visitors, regardless of religion, caste or nationality.","Gold-covered shrine in the middle of a sacred pool, founded in 1604 by a spiritual master","Gold-domed temple in the centre of a pool, holy city of Punjab, 30 km from a tense border","Sikhism's holiest site, golden dome reflected in the water, Amritsar","The Golden Temple in Amritsar, India"],"taj_mahal":["Taj Mahal — Agra, India","The Taj Mahal changes colour with the light: pinkish at dawn, dazzling white at midday, golden at sunset, silvery by moonlight. Shah Jahan, aged and imprisoned by his son, could see the mausoleum from his window until his death.","White marble mausoleum built from 1632 to 1653 by an emperor for his late wife","Domed mausoleum with four minarets mirrored in a pool, right bank of the Yamuna, Uttar Pradesh","Mughal mausoleum listed as a wonder of the world, Agra","The Taj Mahal in Agra, India"],"mont_fuji":["Mount Fuji — Japan","Fuji has been considered sacred for centuries — women were not allowed to climb it until 1872. Every summer, nearly 200,000 people make the ascent, many of them at night to watch the sunrise.","Perfect 3,776 m volcanic cone, snow-capped summit dormant since 1707","Sacred volcano in the Chūbu region, visible on clear days from a megalopolis 100 km away","The country's highest mountain, a volcano and national symbol, west of Tokyo","Mount Fuji, Japan"],"angkor_wat":["Angkor Wat — Siem Reap, Cambodia","Angkor Wat is the largest religious building in the world. In the 12th century, the city of Angkor that surrounded it was the largest pre-industrial city in the world, with perhaps a million inhabitants.","12th-century temple-mountain dedicated to a Hindu god, five lotus-bud towers, huge moats","Vast Khmer temple with lotus towers, north-west of the country, near a large inland lake","The largest religious building in the world, a Khmer jewel, Siem Reap","The Angkor Wat complex in Siem Reap, Cambodia"],"marina_bay_sands":["Marina Bay Sands — Singapore","The Marina Bay Sands infinity pool, perched nearly 200 m up, is the world's largest elevated pool at 146 m long. It runs along the SkyPark, a 340 m platform resting on the three towers, whose tip juts 67 m out into thin air.","Hotel opened in 2010, three towers topped by a 340 m ship-like platform with an infinity pool","Ocean-liner hotel resting on three towers, central bay of an island city-state on a strait","Iconic hotel with a sky-high pool overlooking the bay, Singapore","Marina Bay Sands in Singapore"],"burj_khalifa":["Burj Khalifa — Dubai, United Arab Emirates","The Burj Khalifa is so tall that residents of the top floors can see two sunsets on the same day: one from the ground, then a second after taking the lift up in a few minutes.","829.8 m glass spire opened in 2010, the tallest structure ever built by humankind","Tapering skyscraper in the heart of a desert metropolis, near a giant shopping centre and dancing fountains","The world's tallest skyscraper, 163 floors, Dubai","The Burj Khalifa in Dubai, United Arab Emirates"],"petra":["Petra — Jordan","The Siq, the gorge leading to Petra, is in places only 3 metres wide between dizzying rock walls. Visitors who walk through this red fissure at sunrise emerge in front of the Treasury — a moment considered one of the most beautiful in the world.","Temple façade carved in the 1st century BC into a rose-pink sandstone cliff, at the end of a narrow gorge","Nabataean cave city carved into the rock, desert mountains in the south of the country","Rose-red city carved into the rock, wonder of the world, southern Jordan","The ancient city of Petra, Jordan"],"mur_des_lamentations":["Western Wall — Jerusalem","The Western Wall is the only remnant of the retaining wall of the Second Temple, destroyed by the Romans in AD 70. Pilgrims slip small pieces of paper bearing prayers into it — around a million a year.","Remnant of the esplanade wall on the west side of a Second Temple, prayer at the foot of huge Herodian stones","Prayer wall in the Jewish Quarter of an old city holy to all three monotheistic faiths, at the foot of the Temple Mount","Judaism's holiest place of prayer, last remnant of the Temple, Jerusalem","The Western Wall in Jerusalem"],"pyramides_de_gizeh":["Pyramids of Giza — Egypt","The Great Pyramid of Khufu remained the tallest building in the world for nearly 3,800 years. Its roughly 2.3 million limestone blocks are fitted with remarkable precision and bound by a simple gypsum mortar.","Three monumental royal tombs of the Fourth Dynasty, around 2500 BC, aligned on a desert plateau","Three pyramids on a desert plateau at the edge of a river, facing a vast metropolis","The only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still standing, on the outskirts of Cairo","The Pyramids of Giza, Egypt"],"sphinx_de_gizeh":["Great Sphinx of Giza — Egypt","The Sphinx's nose was probably broken off deliberately in the late 14th century. 19th-century photographs still show it buried up to its shoulders in the desert sands that had covered it for centuries.","Limestone statue with a lion's body and a human head, 73 m long, carved around 2500 BC","Recumbent guardian statue east of three great pyramids, on a desert plateau","The largest sphinx in the world, guardian of the pyramids, on the outskirts of Cairo","The Sphinx of Giza in Egypt"],"kilimandjaro":["Mount Kilimanjaro — Tanzania","Kilimanjaro has lost more than 80% of its ice cap since 1912 as a result of global warming. Its summit glaciers could disappear completely by the middle of the century.","Roof of a continent at 5,895 m, Kibo's icy crater towering over the savannah, snow near the equator","High snow-capped volcano in the north-east of the country, close to a border and a large neighbouring city","Africa's highest mountain, a snow-capped volcano just south of the equator, near Arusha","Kilimanjaro in Tanzania"],"victoria_falls":["Victoria Falls — Zimbabwe/Zambia","The permanent rainbow created by the spray of Victoria Falls gives rise to a “lunar rainbow” on full-moon nights — one of the rarest natural phenomena in the world, and one of the hardest to photograph.","Curtain of water 108 m high and 1.7 km wide on a great river, “the smoke that thunders” to the locals","Immense waterfalls straddling the border between two southern African countries, on a majestic river","The world's largest waterfall by sheet of falling water, southern Africa","Victoria Falls, on the border between Zimbabwe and Zambia"],"kigali_genocide_memorial":["Kigali Genocide Memorial — Rwanda","The Kigali memorial honours the 250,000 victims of the 1994 Rwandan genocide. In 100 days, around 800,000 Tutsi and moderate Hutu were killed. Rwanda, which has since undergone a remarkable economic transformation, marks the International Day of Reflection every 7 April.","Memorial opened in 2004 on a hillside, honouring the victims of a 1994 genocide","Genocide memorial on a hill in a Central African capital, in the land of a thousand hills","The main memorial to the Rwandan genocide, in the capital, Kigali","The Kigali Genocide Memorial in Rwanda"],"statue_de_l_unite":["Statue of Unity — India (Sardar Patel)","The Statue of Unity depicts Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, the man who united India's 562 princely states into a single nation after independence. At 182 metres, it is twice the height of the Statue of Liberty. Visitors come from all over the world to go up to the observation deck in its chest.","182 m bronze statue erected in 2018 beside a large dam, the tallest in the world","Colossal statue on a river island in the Narmada, Gujarat state","The world's tallest statue (182 m), near Vadodara","The Statue of Unity in India (Sardar Patel)"],"christ_redempteur":["Christ the Redeemer — Rio, Brazil","Christ the Redeemer is struck by lightning about three times a year. Its 2010 restoration included a lightning conductor built into the crown of thorns. It stands 38 metres tall including its pedestal.","38 m statue of Christ with outstretched arms, erected in 1931 atop a 710 m peak above an urban forest","Monumental statue on the summit of Corcovado, Tijuca National Park, overlooking a bay","Iconic statue overlooking Rio de Janeiro, wonder of the world","Christ the Redeemer in Rio, Brazil"],"machu_picchu":["Machu Picchu — Peru","Machu Picchu was built without the wheel, without iron and without writing. Its dry-stone walls withstand earthquakes because each block is slightly rounded, allowing the stones to “dance” when the ground shakes.","Stone city built around 1450 on a pass at 2,430 m, rediscovered in 1911, lost in the mist of the peaks","Terraced Inca city, Urubamba Valley, high cordillera, near a former imperial capital","Lost Inca city in the clouds, wonder of the world, Cusco region","Machu Picchu in Peru"],"statue_de_la_liberte":["Statue of Liberty — New York, USA","Given by France for the centenary of American independence, the statue was built in Paris and then shipped in 1885 in 350 pieces packed into 214 crates. Its internal structure is the work of Gustave Eiffel, and its green colour comes from the natural oxidation of its copper skin.","93 m copper statue including its pedestal, a gift from a friendly nation in 1886, the work of Bartholdi and Eiffel, on an island","Statue on a small island at the entrance to a large bay on the east coast, between two states","Symbol of liberty given by France, Port of New York","The Statue of Liberty in New York, USA"],"grand_canyon":["Grand Canyon — Arizona, USA","The Grand Canyon contains fossils of sea creatures at an altitude of 2,000 metres — proof that the region once lay at the bottom of a warm sea. As you head down to the bottom, you are literally travelling through geological time.","Dizzying gorge carved by a river over 446 km, up to 1,857 m deep, red strata","Immense canyon carved into the Colorado Plateau, a national park since 1919","Canyon carved by the Colorado, 1,857 m deep, northern Arizona","The Grand Canyon in Arizona, United States"],"chichen_itza":["Chichen Itza — Yucatán, Mexico","When excavated in the 20th century, the great Sacred Cenote of Chichen Itza yielded dozens of skeletons, jade jewellery and gold objects — confirming Maya sacrifices. Thousands of offerings are thought to still lie at the bottom of the well.","30 m stepped pyramid dedicated to a serpent god, Maya city where light traces a serpent at the equinoxes","Maya pyramid at the heart of a site in Yucatán, limestone plain, 120 km from a large colonial city","Maya pyramid, wonder of the world, Yucatán Peninsula, near Mérida","Chichen Itza in Yucatán, Mexico"],"chutes_du_niagara":["Niagara Falls — Canada/USA","On 29 March 1848, an ice jam on Lake Erie dried up Niagara Falls for about thirty hours: locals walked across the dry riverbed. In 1969, the American Falls were deliberately dewatered for several months so that the rock could be studied.","57 m horseshoe falls on a border river, permanent mist and boats that take you up close","Famous waterfalls between two cities of the same name on either side of an international border","Famous waterfalls on the border between Canada and the United States","Niagara Falls (Canada / United States border)"],"opera_de_sydney":["Sydney Opera House — Australia","The Sydney Opera House took 14 years to build (1959–1973) and cost 14 times its original budget. To fund it, the New South Wales government set up a special lottery, the Opera House Lottery.","Performing arts venue with white concrete sails, built from 1959 to 1973 on a point jutting out into a harbour","Opera house with shell-shaped roofs on a peninsula, great natural harbour of an island continent","Performing arts venue with shell-shaped roofs, emblem of Sydney","The Sydney Opera House in Australia"],"uluru":["Uluru — Northern Territory, Australia","Uluru is much bigger than it looks: two-thirds of this monolith lies buried underground. The Anangu finally banned climbing the sacred rock in 2019, after decades of negotiations.","348 m red sandstone monolith rising from an arid plain, a sacred site for the First Peoples","Huge sacred rock in the centre of a continent, red desert, 450 km from an isolated town","Great sacred red rock in the heart of the Australian desert, near Alice Springs","Uluru in the Northern Territory, Australia"],"trou_bleu_de_belize":["Great Blue Hole — Belize","Belize's Great Blue Hole was made famous by Jacques-Yves Cousteau, who ranked it among the world's 10 finest dive sites in 1971. Its sheer walls drop to 125 metres and are home to nurse sharks and reef sharks that sleep on the bottom.","Circular underwater sinkhole 300 m across and 125 m deep, ringed by a coral reef","Marine blue hole in the middle of a coral atoll, Caribbean Sea","Famous marine sinkhole in a coral reef, off the coast of Belize","The Great Blue Hole off the coast of Belize"],"palais_du_parlement_budapest":["Hungarian Parliament Building — Budapest, Hungary","The Budapest Parliament is lit up every night and reflected in the Danube — one of the finest night-time views in Europe. Its construction took nearly twenty years (1885–1904) and involved some 100,000 people and 40 million bricks.","96 m neo-Gothic parliament built from 1885 to 1904 on the banks of a great river, 691 rooms","Vast neo-Gothic parliament on a riverbank, Lipótváros district, Pannonian Plain","Europe's largest neo-Gothic parliament, on the Danube, Budapest","The Parliament Building in Budapest, Hungary"],"chateau_de_prague":["Prague Castle — Czech Republic","The Prague Castle complex, covering nearly 70,000 m², is one of the largest in the world. It contains a cathedral, a royal palace, a convent, gardens and the Golden Lane, which legend fills with Rudolf II's alchemists trying to turn lead into gold.","Vast castle complex founded in the 9th century on a hill above a river, 70,000 m²","The world's largest ancient castle complex, on Hradčany hill overlooking the Vltava","The world's largest castle, presidential seat, Prague","Prague Castle, Czech Republic"],"canal_grande":["Grand Canal — Venice, Italy","Venice's Grand Canal is lined along its 3.8 km by 150 palaces built between the 13th and 18th centuries. These buildings are slowly sinking — Venice subsided by 23 cm in the 20th century — which led to the launch of the MOSE project, a system of mobile flood barriers.","Great reverse-S waterway 3.8 km long, lined with 150 palaces, plied by gondolas","Main canal of a lagoon city, from the main railway station to the great maritime basin","Main canal of the city of gondolas, Venice","The Grand Canal in Venice, Italy"],"thermes_de_caracalla":["Baths of Caracalla — Rome, Italy","The Baths of Caracalla closed for good in 537 when the Ostrogoths cut Rome's aqueducts. Since the 1930s, the Rome Opera has staged open-air performances there — Verdi or Puccini against this imposing ancient backdrop.","Huge baths opened in 216, up to 1,600 bathers at a time, colossal brick ruins","Ruins of the largest baths of antiquity, Aventine district, south of a famous amphitheatre","The largest surviving Roman baths, south of the Colosseum, Rome","The Baths of Caracalla in Rome, Italy"],"musee_du_prado":["Museo del Prado — Madrid, Spain","The Prado holds the world's largest collection of paintings by Velázquez, Goya and Titian. Velázquez's “Las Meninas”, which shows the royal family with the artist painting himself, is considered the first “postmodern” painting in history.","Art museum opened in 1819 on a grand avenue, home to Velázquez's Las Meninas","Great museum of classical art near a park, in the Jerónimos district of a capital","The country's largest museum of classical art, Madrid","The Prado Museum in Madrid, Spain"],"tour_de_belem":["Belém Tower — Lisbon, Portugal","Belém Tower was built in 1516 on the banks of the Tagus to salute the explorers setting off for the Indies. This Manueline gem is adorned with maritime motifs, knotted ropes carved in stone and the armillary sphere, emblem of Portugal's Age of Discovery.","Fortified Manueline tower built from 1516 to 1521, jutting out into the water at the mouth of a river","Manueline fortress-tower on the edge of an estuary, Atlantic seaboard, Discoveries district","Manueline tower symbolising the Age of Discovery, Lisbon","Belém Tower in Lisbon, Portugal"],"atomium":["Atomium — Brussels, Belgium","The Atomium in Brussels represents an iron crystal unit cell magnified 165 billion times. Designed as a temporary attraction for Expo 58, it was kept after the exhibition thanks to its popularity. Its spheres house exhibitions and even a flat for rent.","1958 structure depicting an iron crystal unit cell magnified 165 billion times, nine steel spheres","Metal monument shaped like a giant atom, Heysel plateau, site of a World's Fair","Monument shaped like a giant atom, emblem of Brussels","The Atomium in Brussels, Belgium"],"manneken_pis":["Manneken Pis — Brussels, Belgium","The Manneken Pis statue is only 61 cm tall but has a wardrobe of more than 1,000 costumes donated by nations from all over the world. He is regularly dressed in official attire on national holidays — a tradition dating back to the 18th century.","Small 61 cm bronze fountain cast in 1619, on the corner of two cobbled lanes","Tiny fountain statue of a child, on a street corner in the Îlot Sacré, historic Brabantine heart","Famous little fountain of a cheeky boy, symbol of Brussels","Manneken Pis in Brussels, Belgium"],"chateau_de_edimbourg":["Edinburgh Castle — Edinburgh, United Kingdom","Edinburgh Castle stands on a volcanic rock some 340 million years old. It houses the Honours of Scotland, the oldest crown jewels in the British Isles, including a crown remade in 1540. Since 1861, the “One O'Clock Gun” has fired there at exactly 1 pm every day except Sunday, a tradition begun for sailors in the port of Leith.","Fortress on the plug of an extinct volcano, guarding a coronation stone and royal jewels","Castle at the top of the Royal Mile, old town overlooking gardens below","Medieval fortress overlooking the Scottish capital, Edinburgh","Edinburgh Castle in Scotland (United Kingdom)"],"pont_du_gard":["Pont du Gard — France","The Pont du Gard was part of a 50 km aqueduct that supplied Nîmes with water. Its average gradient, barely 25 cm per kilometre, was so gentle that the water flowed with almost no visible movement — a marvel of Roman engineering.","Three-tiered Roman aqueduct of arches, 49 m high, built without mortar in the 1st century over a river","Roman aqueduct bridge spanning the Gardon gorges, southern garrigue scrubland, Occitania","Tallest Roman aqueduct bridge in the world, near Nîmes","The Pont du Gard in Occitania, France"],"mont_saint_michel":["Mont-Saint-Michel — Normandy, France","The bay of Mont-Saint-Michel has the highest tidal range in continental Europe. In 1879, a causeway built to link the Mount to the mainland accelerated the silting up of the bay. The causeway was dismantled and replaced by a bridge in 2014 to let the waters flow freely.","Granite islet crowned by a Benedictine abbey founded in 966, surrounded by strong tides","Island abbey at the far end of a bay with huge tides, English Channel coast, Normandy","Abbey on a tidal islet, one of the most visited sites, near Avranches","Mont-Saint-Michel in Normandy, France"],"chateau_de_chambord":["Château de Chambord — Loire, France","Chambord has a double-helix staircase attributed to Leonardo da Vinci: two visitors can go up and down at the same time without ever meeting. Francis I slept at Chambord for only 72 nights in 32 years — a château designed above all for the splendour of court life.","Renaissance château begun in 1519, famous double-spiral staircase, 426 rooms, deep in a forest","Vast Renaissance château with a bristling roofline, valley of a great river, 15 km from Blois","Largest château of the Loire, Renaissance masterpiece, near Blois","Château de Chambord in the Loire Valley, France"],"basilique_du_sacre_c_ur":["Sacré-Cœur Basilica — Paris, France","The Basilica of Sacré-Cœur was built with private donations after the defeat of 1871 against Prussia — a national vow of penance. Standing 83 metres tall on the Butte Montmartre, it is the highest point in Paris, visible from almost everywhere in the city.","White Romano-Byzantine basilica consecrated in 1919, stone that whitens in the rain, atop a hill","White basilica on top of a 130 m hill, hilltop arrondissement on the Right Bank","White basilica overlooking Paris from the Butte Montmartre","Sacré-Cœur Basilica in Paris, France"],"palais_de_justice":["Palace of Justice — Brussels, Belgium","The largest building erected in the 19th century, bigger than St Peter's Basilica in Rome: this domed colossus by the architect Poelaert crushed the working-class Marolles district, whose evicted residents turned “architek” into an insult still alive in Brussels today.","Colossal domed law courts from 1883, largest building of the 19th century, atop a working-class district","Immense domed law courts, Marolles district, towering over a lower town","Largest building erected in the 19th century, dome overlooking Brussels","The Palace of Justice in Brussels, Belgium"],"fjord_de_geiranger":["Geirangerfjord — Norway","The seven falls of the “Seven Sisters” in the Geirangerfjord are fed solely by snowmelt and rain — there is no spring or lake upstream. Opposite lies the Suitor waterfall, which seems to be “sprinkling” the seven sisters.","15 km arm of the sea hemmed in by sheer cliffs, Seven Sisters and Bridal Veil waterfalls","Deep fjord with famous waterfalls, jagged north-west coast of a Scandinavian country","One of the most spectacular fjords, Ålesund region","The Geirangerfjord in Norway"],"hallgrimskirkja":["Hallgrímskirkja — Reykjavík, Iceland","The statue in front of Hallgrímskirkja church depicts Leifur Eiríksson, who reached America 500 years before Christopher Columbus. It was given to Iceland by the United States for the millennium of the Icelandic parliament in 1930.","73 m Lutheran church in ribbed concrete evoking basalt organ pipes, 41 years in the making","Church tower shaped like basalt columns, on a hill in the heart of the northernmost capital","Iconic church of basalt columns, Reykjavík","Hallgrímskirkja in Reykjavík, Iceland"],"acropole_de_lindos":["Acropolis of Lindos — Rhodes, Greece","The Acropolis of Lindos, on Rhodes, is a sanctuary of Athena whose temple dates from the 4th century BC. Perched on a 116-metre cliff above the Aegean Sea, it offers one of the most spectacular views in Greece. According to the Lindos Chronicle, Alexander the Great made offerings to the goddess there.","4th-century BC Doric temple dedicated to a goddess, on a 116 m rock above a bay","Ancient acropolis above a white village, Dodecanese headland, Aegean Sea","Doric temple on a headland overlooking a bay, island of Rhodes","The Acropolis of Lindos on Rhodes, Greece"],"chateau_de_hohenzollern":["Hohenzollern Castle — Germany","Hohenzollern Castle has been rebuilt three times on the same spot. The current neo-Gothic version dates from 1867. It houses the Prussian crown and the uniform in which Frederick the Great died — relics of the Prussian kingdom that unified Germany.","Ancestral castle of a dynasty, third rebuilding completed in 1867, perched on a wooded mountain","Neo-Gothic castle atop an 855 m peak, uplands of the Swabian Jura","Ancestral seat of the Prussian Hohenzollern dynasty, near Hechingen","Hohenzollern Castle in Germany"],"bibliotheque_du_congres":["Library of Congress — Washington, D.C., USA","The Library of Congress in Washington holds one of the three complete vellum copies of the Gutenberg Bible, as well as Thomas Jefferson's handwritten draft of the Declaration of Independence. It receives more than 10,000 new items every day.","World's largest library, opened in 1897, 170 million items, facing the seat of parliament","Monumental library on Capitol Hill, facing the seat of the federal Congress","World's largest library, Washington, D.C.","The Library of Congress in Washington, D.C., United States"],"opera_garnier":["Palais Garnier — Paris, France","The Palais Garnier rests on a vast cistern, built by Charles Garnier to contain the groundwater that was flooding the building site. This “underground lake”, where firefighter divers train today, inspired Gaston Leroux's 1910 novel “The Phantom of the Opera”.","Lavish opera house opened in 1875, grand marble staircase and ceiling painted by Chagall","Second Empire opera house at the end of a grand boulevard, department-store district","Lavish opera house, setting of The Phantom of the Opera, Paris","The Palais Garnier in Paris, France"],"temple_sens_ji":["Sensō-ji — Tokyo, Japan","Sensō-ji temple in Asakusa is the oldest and most visited in Tokyo, with 30 million visitors a year. Nakamise-dōri, the shopping street leading to the temple, has sold souvenirs since the Edo period. Today's merchants are often direct descendants of the first 17th-century shopkeepers.","Oldest Buddhist temple in the megalopolis, founded in 628, long lane of shops and a giant lantern","Ancient Buddhist temple at the end of a shopping street, old working-class district of Asakusa","Oldest and most visited Buddhist temple in Tokyo","Sensō-ji Temple in Tokyo, Japan"],"temple_d_or":["Golden Pavilion (Kinkaku-ji) — Kyoto, Japan","Kinkaku-ji, the Golden Pavilion, was built in the late 14th century as a retirement villa for the shogun Ashikaga Yoshimitsu and later converted into a Zen temple. The current pavilion is a 1955 reconstruction: the original was burnt down in 1950 by a young monk, a tragedy that inspired Mishima's novel “The Temple of the Golden Pavilion”.","Pavilion covered in gold leaf, built in 1397 for a shogun, reflected in a mirror pond","Golden pavilion beside a pond, gardens in the north of a former imperial capital","Golden pavilion reflected in the water, emblem of Kyoto","The Golden Pavilion (Kinkaku-ji) in Kyoto, Japan"],"palais_de_potala":["Potala Palace — Lhasa, Tibet","The Potala Palace in Lhasa was built at an altitude of 3,700 metres and has 13 storeys, 1,000 rooms and 10,000 shrines. It housed the thrones and tombs of the Dalai Lamas, whose embalmed bodies are preserved in gold-plated stupas.","13-storey, 1,000-room palace-fortress rising from a red hill at an altitude of 3,700 m","Immense Tibetan palace on a hill, centre of a spiritual capital on a high plateau","Former palace of the Dalai Lama overlooking the city, Lhasa","The Potala Palace in Lhasa, Tibet"],"temple_de_borobudur":["Borobudur — Java, Indonesia","Borobudur temple lay buried under volcanic ash and vegetation for 800 years. It was rediscovered by Sir Thomas Raffles in 1814 and restored in the 1970s as part of the largest restoration project ever undertaken by UNESCO.","9th-century stone mandala, 2,672 bas-reliefs and 504 Buddhas on terraces, beneath a volcano","Immense terraced Buddhist temple, volcanic plain of Java, near a former royal capital","World's largest Buddhist temple, near Yogyakarta","Borobudur Temple on Java, Indonesia"],"hagia_sophia":["Hagia Sophia — Istanbul, Turkey","Hagia Sophia was the largest cathedral in the world for nearly 1,000 years. Its dome, 31 metres in diameter and rising to 55 metres, rests on a ring of 40 windows that create the illusion that it is floating in mid-air.","Building with an immense dome rising to 55.6 m, built from 532 to 537, in turn a church and then a mosque, on a peninsula","Former basilica with a colossal dome turned mosque, historic peninsula between two shores","Byzantine masterpiece with an immense dome, Istanbul","Hagia Sophia (Ayasofya) in Istanbul, Turkey"],"temple_de_preah_vihear":["Preah Vihear Temple — Cambodia","Preah Vihear temple was the subject of a territorial dispute between Cambodia and Thailand for decades. The International Court of Justice awarded it to Cambodia in 1962 and confirmed that sovereignty in 2013; the site has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2008.","Khmer Hindu temple dedicated to a god, built in stages from the 9th to the 12th century atop a 625 m plateau","Khmer temple perched on a cliff, Dângrêk plateau, on a disputed border","Spectacular Khmer temple on the edge of a cliff, northern Cambodia","Preah Vihear Temple in Cambodia"],"palais_de_mysore":["Mysore Palace — India","Mysore Palace is lit up by nearly 100,000 light bulbs every Sunday evening and during festivals. The spectacle draws huge crowds and makes the palace sparkle like a jewel at the heart of the city. The Wadiyar royal family still lives in part of the complex.","Indo-Saracenic palace rebuilt in 1912, lit by tens of thousands of bulbs on festival nights","Grand Indo-Saracenic royal palace in a city centre, Karnataka state, 150 km from a metropolis","Royal palace lit by 100,000 bulbs, Mysore","Mysore Palace in India"],"palais_de_shakhrisabz":["Ak-Saray Palace — Uzbekistan","The Ak-Saray Palace in Shakhrisabz was Tamerlane's summer residence, built in the 14th century. Its two entrance pylons reached 65 metres — the tallest in Central Asia. Shakhrisabz, which means “green city”, is Tamerlane's birthplace.","Ruins of a summer palace with a giant 65 m portal, begun in 1380 for a great conqueror of the steppes","Remains of a summer palace in a Silk Road town, Central Asian province","Ruins of Tamerlane's palace in his home town, Shakhrisabz","Shakhrisabz Palace in Uzbekistan"],"mosquee_sultan_ahmed":["Sultan Ahmed Mosque — Istanbul, Turkey","The Blue Mosque, built from 1609 to 1616, is the only mosque in Istanbul with six minarets — an audacity that scandalised Mecca, which then had only four. The interior is lined with 20,000 blue Iznik tiles, each one painted by hand.","Imperial mosque of 1609–1616 with 6 minarets — a scandal at the time — facing a former basilica","Great mosque with six minarets, historic peninsula, facing a former Byzantine building","Blue Mosque with six minarets, opposite Hagia Sophia, Istanbul","The Sultan Ahmed Mosque in Istanbul, Turkey"],"palais_d_ete":["Summer Palace — Beijing, China","The Summer Palace in Beijing was looted and burned by Anglo-French troops in 1860. Empress Cixi had it entirely rebuilt in 1888 by diverting funds intended to modernise the Chinese navy — a decision that contributed to defeat in the Sino-Japanese War.","Imperial summer residence laid out in 1886, immense hand-dug lake at the foot of a hill","Imperial summer palace and vast artificial lake, north-western outskirts of a great capital","Qing imperial summer residence, gardens and great lake, outskirts of Beijing","The Summer Palace in Beijing, China"],"pont_d_umeda":["Umeda Sky Building — Osaka, Japan","The Umeda Sky Building in Osaka consists of two towers linked at the top by a circular hanging garden 170 metres up. Its escalators cross the void between the two towers diagonally — a spectacular 50-metre crossing in the sky.","173 m twin towers joined at the very top by a suspended garden observatory, completed in 1993","Twin skyscrapers with a suspended observatory, Umeda district of a Kansai merchant city","Twin towers linked by a suspended observatory, Osaka","The Umeda Sky Building in Osaka, Japan"],"temple_de_confucius":["Temple of Confucius — Qufu, China","The Temple of Confucius in Qufu is the largest Confucian complex in the world, with 466 buildings. Built in 478 BC, a year after the philosopher's death, it was enlarged by every Chinese dynasty over 2,500 years. Confucius's direct descendants still live there today.","Vast 466-hall temple dedicated to a philosopher, erected from 478 BC in his home town","Great Confucian temple in the sage's home town, Shandong province","Great temple dedicated to Confucius, his home town, Qufu","The Temple of Confucius in Qufu, China"],"rocher_de_sigiriya":["Sigiriya — Sri Lanka","Sigiriya Rock is home to 500 frescoes of celestial maidens (apsaras), of which only 22 have survived the elements. They are among the only large-scale secular paintings from the 5th century still visible anywhere in the world.","5th-century palace-fortress perched on a 200 m rock, frescoes and water gardens of a parricide king","Rock fortress rising from a plain, central province of an Indian Ocean island","5th-century rock fortress and royal gardens, central Sri Lanka","Sigiriya Rock in Sri Lanka"],"tour_cn":["CN Tower — Toronto, Canada","Toronto's CN Tower, completed in 1976 by the Canadian National Railway company, was meant to solve the reception problems caused by skyscrapers. At 553 metres, it was the world's tallest free-standing structure for more than 30 years. Its restaurant turns 360° in 72 minutes, and on a clear day you can see the mist of Niagara Falls, about 70 km away.","553 m telecommunications tower opened in 1976, revolving restaurant at 351 m, long the tallest","Very tall observation tower on the shore of a great lake, waterfront district of a metropolis","Iconic tall observation tower, symbol of Toronto","The CN Tower in Toronto, Canada"],"mausolee_de_khomeini":["Mausoleum of Ruhollah Khomeini — Tehran, Iran","The mausoleum of Imam Khomeini in Tehran is one of the largest religious complexes in the Islamic world. Its 72-metre golden dome is visible from all over the city. It attracts millions of Shia pilgrims a year and is constantly expanding — its construction is still not officially finished.","Vast golden mausoleum with four 91 m minarets, under construction since 1989, millions of pilgrims a year","Large religious mausoleum, Rey district, southern outskirts of a capital of 9 million people","Great mausoleum of Ayatollah Khomeini, outskirts of Tehran","The Mausoleum of Khomeini in Tehran, Iran"],"mosquee_de_djenne":["Great Mosque of Djenné — Mali","Every year, the Great Mosque of Djenné is replastered by hand during a community festival: thousands of residents coat its walls with fresh banco mud to repair the damage of the rainy season — a centuries-old tradition.","World's largest mud-brick building, rebuilt in 1907, bristling with protruding beams","Great mud-brick mosque on a market square, inland delta of a great African river","World's largest banco (mud-brick) mosque, Djenné","The Mosque of Djenné in Mali"],"cites_de_lalibela":["Rock-Hewn Churches of Lalibela — Ethiopia","The 11 churches of Lalibela were carved out of the living rock in just 24 years in the 12th century. According to legend, angels worked at night while the men rested, allowing the work to progress miraculously fast.","Eleven churches each carved from a single block of red tuff in the 12th century, linked by trenches and tunnels","Monolithic churches hewn into the rock, highlands at 2,500 m, Amhara region","Rock-hewn churches, the “Jerusalem of Africa”, Lalibela","The rock-hewn churches of Lalibela, in Ethiopia"],"ile_de_goree":["Gorée Island — Senegal","Gorée was so prosperous from the slave trade that European merchants fought over it for two centuries. The island changed hands 7 times between the Portuguese, Dutch, English and French.","Small 0.36 km² island, a key site of the slave trade, House of Slaves built in 1776","Island of remembrance of the slave trade in the bay of a West African capital, 3 km offshore","Island symbolising the slave trade, opposite Dakar","Gorée Island in Senegal"],"chutes_d_iguazu":["Iguazu Falls — Argentina/Brazil","The Iguazu Falls are so wide that Eleanor Roosevelt exclaimed “Poor Niagara!” on seeing them for the first time. They form the natural border between Argentina and Brazil, and the two countries disputed sovereignty over them for decades.","275 tiered cascades over 2.7 km, including an 82 m “Devil's Throat”, subtropical jungle on a border","Immense stepped falls on the border of two South American countries, subtropical forest","World's widest waterfalls, Argentina–Brazil border","The Iguazu Falls in Argentina/Brazil"],"desert_du_namib":["Namib Desert — Namibia","The Namib is the oldest desert in the world — arid for at least 55 million years. Its Deadvlei (“dead marsh”) holds 900-year-old acacia skeletons, preserved by the extreme dryness. Its Sossusvlei dunes, among the highest in the world (325 m), owe their red colour to iron oxidation.","Red sand dunes up to 325 m, the world's oldest coastal desert bordering a cold ocean","High red dunes of an Atlantic coastal desert, arid national park","Red dunes among the highest in the world, coastal desert of Namibia","The Namib Desert in Namibia"],"parc_national_kruger":["Kruger National Park — South Africa","Kruger is South Africa's largest national park, covering 20,000 km². It is home to the “Big Five” (lion, elephant, buffalo, leopard, rhinoceros) at some of the highest densities in Africa. Its 800 km electric fence was built in the 1970s — the longest fenced perimeter of any nature reserve in the world.","Reserve founded in 1898, as big as a small country, refuge of the Big Five amid bush and rivers","Vast wildlife reserve in the north-east of a southern African country, bordering two neighbours","The country's largest reserve, famous for the Big Five, north-eastern South Africa","Kruger National Park in South Africa"],"karnak":["Karnak — Luxor, Egypt","The hypostyle hall at Karnak, with its 134 columns, the tallest reaching 21 metres, is so vast that Notre-Dame de Paris could fit inside it. The whole complex was enlarged over nearly 2,000 years by some thirty successive pharaohs.","Giant temple built over 2,000 years, forest of 134 columns in a hypostyle hall, on the bank of a great river","Immense ancient temple on the east bank of a river, upper valley, 500 km from a capital","Largest temple of ancient Egypt, Luxor","Karnak Temple in Luxor, Egypt"],"cap_de_bonne_esperance":["Cape of Good Hope — South Africa","The Cape of Good Hope is not the southernmost point of Africa — that is Cape Agulhas, 150 km to the east. But it is where westerly winds and ocean currents meet, creating particularly dangerous sailing conditions. Bartolomeu Dias rounded it in 1488, opening the route to the Indies.","Windswept rocky headland, rounded in 1488, where two oceans meet","Rocky cape at the tip of a peninsula, national park, south-west of a southern African country","Legendary cape at the tip of the Cape Peninsula, near Cape Town","The Cape of Good Hope in South Africa"],"table_mountain":["Table Mountain — Cape Town, South Africa","Table Mountain is one of the oldest mountains in the world: its sandstones were laid down around 450 million years ago. Its summit plateau, often draped in a “tablecloth” of cloud, is home to more plant species than the whole of Great Britain.","1,086 m flat-topped mountain of sandstone some 450 million years old, with a cable car since 1929","Mountain with a summit plateau overlooking a large port city, south-west of a southern country","Iconic flat-topped mountain overlooking Cape Town","Table Mountain in Cape Town, South Africa"],"mosquee_hassan_ii":["Hassan II Mosque — Casablanca, Morocco","The Hassan II Mosque in Casablanca is built partly over the sea, with a glass floor through which you can see the Atlantic Ocean below. Its 210 m minaret projects a laser beam towards Mecca every night.","Mosque inaugurated in 1993, with a 210 m minaret, built partly over the waves","Immense mosque on the Atlantic shore, seaside district of an economic metropolis","One of the largest mosques in Africa, 210 m minaret, Casablanca","The Hassan II Mosque in Casablanca, Morocco"],"chute_angel":["Angel Falls — Venezuela","Angel Falls is so high that in the dry season much of its water evaporates before reaching the ground. After a 979-metre drop, the water arrives as drizzle or as a stream, depending on the weather.","Unbroken 979 m ribbon of water plunging off a sandstone tableland, spotted from a plane in 1933","World's highest waterfall gushing from a table-top plateau, savannah and tepuis","World's highest waterfall (979 m), Venezuelan jungle","Angel Falls in Venezuela"],"canal_de_panama":["Panama Canal","The Panama Canal cuts 15,000 km off the journey between the Atlantic and Pacific oceans. Its construction cost the lives of 25,000 workers between 1881 and 1914, mainly due to malaria and yellow fever.","80 km waterway with locks, opened in 1914, linking two oceans across an isthmus","Ship canal with locks crossing a narrow isthmus, from the Atlantic to the Pacific","Canal linking the Atlantic and the Pacific, near Panama City","The Panama Canal"],"ile_de_paques":["Easter Island — Chile","Easter Island's famous “heads” are in fact whole statues, whose bodies have been buried over the centuries by sediment on the slopes of the Rano Raraku quarry. The island has nearly 900 moai, carved from volcanic tuff.","Isolated volcanic island 3,700 km from any continent, quarry from which nearly 900 stone giants were cut","Remote Polynesian volcanic island in the South Pacific, with monumental statues","Island of the moai statues, South Pacific, Rapa Nui","Easter Island in Chile"],"cathedrale_de_brasilia":["Cathedral of Brasília — Brazil","The Cathedral of Brasília, designed by Oscar Niemeyer and inaugurated in 1970, is a hyperboloid sculpture of 16 curved concrete columns weighing 90 tonnes each. It is entered through a dark tunnel so that worshippers emerge into the light of the glazed nave — a metaphor for the resurrection.","1970 cathedral with 16 hyperboloid concrete columns forming a crown, sunken nave","Futuristic cathedral on the esplanade of a capital built from scratch, federal district","Niemeyer's futuristic cathedral, capital, Brasília","The Cathedral of Brasília in Brazil"],"parc_yellowstone":["Yellowstone National Park — Wyoming, USA","Yellowstone sits on a supervolcano whose last major eruption was about 640,000 years ago; a new eruption on that scale would blanket much of the United States in ash. The Grand Prismatic Spring, a hot spring 90 metres across, owes its colours to thermophilic bacteria.","World's first national park (1872), active supervolcano, geyser erupting every 90 minutes","Vast volcanic park in the Rockies, Wyoming plateau spilling over into two neighbouring states","National park of geysers and bison, north-western Wyoming","Yellowstone National Park in Wyoming, United States"],"galapagos":["Galápagos Islands — Ecuador","The Galápagos archipelago was the evolutionary laboratory of Charles Darwin, who stayed there in 1835. The isolation of its islands produced species found nowhere else in the world: marine iguanas, Darwin's finches with beaks adapted to each island, 200 kg giant tortoises. 97% of the archipelago is strictly protected national park.","Remote volcanic archipelago where, in 1835, a naturalist gathered key observations for his theory of evolution, giant tortoises and marine iguanas","Volcanic archipelago lost in the Pacific, 1,000 km from a continent, unique endemic wildlife","Darwin's islands with endemic wildlife, Pacific Ocean, off Ecuador","The Galápagos Islands, in Ecuador"],"times_square":["Times Square — New York, USA","Times Square takes its name from the New York Times, which moved its headquarters there in 1904 and held the first New Year's “ball drop” there. With 50 million visitors a year, it is the most expensive advertising spot in the world — a billboard can cost $50,000 a day.","Neon crossroads between two major thoroughfares, named after a daily newspaper, famous ball that drops at New Year","Illuminated square at the heart of the continent's largest metropolis, between two avenues","Crossroads of neon, heart of Manhattan, New York","Times Square in New York, United States"],"cap_canaveral":["Cape Canaveral — Florida, USA","Cape Canaveral has been the cradle of the American space age since 1950. The nearby Launch Complex 39A saw Apollo 11 leave for the Moon in 1969. Leased by SpaceX since 2014, the same historic site now hosts Falcon 9 and Falcon Heavy rockets — a symbolic continuity between two eras of space exploration.","Spaceport from which the first crewed lunar mission left in 1969, as did the shuttles, on an ocean coast","Space centre on a coastal island, Brevard County, east coast of Florida","Space centre from which Apollo 11 lifted off, east coast of Florida","Cape Canaveral in Florida, United States"],"teotihuacan":["Teotihuacan — Mexico","Teotihuacan was one of the largest cities in the world in AD 500, with 200,000 inhabitants. No one knows which civilisation built it — the Aztecs, who discovered it centuries later, named it “the place where men become gods”.","Pre-Columbian city founded around 200 BC, immense Pyramids of the Sun and Moon lining an Avenue of the Dead","Pre-Columbian pyramid city in the highlands, 40 km from a huge metropolis","City of great pyramids, 40 km from Mexico City","The archaeological site of Teotihuacan, Mexico"],"great_barrier_reef":["Great Barrier Reef — Queensland, Australia","The Great Barrier Reef is the largest structure built by living organisms, and it is visible from space. Its coral cover fell by about half between the mid-1980s and 2012, because of cyclones, coral-eating starfish and warming-related bleaching. Researchers are freezing coral fragments to make future restoration possible.","World's largest coral system, 2,300 km and 2,900 reefs, visible from space","Vast coral reef running along a north-eastern coast, Coral Sea, State of Queensland","World's largest coral reef, Queensland coast","The Great Barrier Reef in Queensland, Australia"],"sky_tower":["Sky Tower — Auckland, New Zealand","Auckland's Sky Tower, opened in 1997, is the tallest structure in the Southern Hemisphere at 328 metres. It stands on a single concrete column anchored in Auckland's volcanic rock, designed to withstand a magnitude 8 earthquake. Parachutists jump from the top during special events.","328 m telecommunications tower opened in 1997, free-fall jumps from its platform","Very tall observation tower in a port city, North Island, between two harbours","Tallest tower in the Southern Hemisphere, emblem of Auckland","The Sky Tower in Auckland, New Zealand"],"fiordland":["Milford Sound — Fiordland, New Zealand","Milford Sound, a 16 km fjord carved by glaciers, is dominated by Mitre Peak (1,692 m), which rises almost vertically from the dark waters. With more than 6 metres of rain a year, it is one of the wettest places on the planet: after every downpour, hundreds of short-lived waterfalls tumble down its walls. Rudyard Kipling is said to have called it the “eighth wonder of the world”.","16 km glacier-carved fjord, 1,692 m pyramid-shaped peak plunging into dark water, waterfalls","Glacial fjord in a national park, south-west coast of the South Island","Fjord of mountains and waterfalls, south-west of New Zealand's South Island","Milford Sound, in Fiordland, New Zealand"],"bora_bora":["Bora Bora — French Polynesia","Bora Bora is an ancient extinct volcano slowly sinking into the ocean: its central peak, Mount Otemanu, is encircled by a turquoise lagoon and a ring of motu resting on the barrier reef. In places, the lagoon is so shallow that you can walk in it with the water up to your waist.","38 km² volcanic island ringed by a turquoise lagoon and a barrier reef, jagged central peak","Lagoon island in a South Pacific archipelago, 260 km west of a main island","Paradise island with a turquoise lagoon, Society Islands, near Tahiti","Bora Bora, in French Polynesia"],"palau":["Jellyfish Lake — Eil Malk, Palau","Jellyfish Lake (Ongeim'l Tketau), on the island of Eil Malk, is a marine lake cut off from the ocean, home to millions of golden jellyfish. Their sting is so weak that it is harmless to humans, which means you can swim among them. Every day, they cross the lake following the course of the sun, which feeds the symbiotic algae they depend on.","460 m marine lake teeming with millions of harmless golden jellyfish, amid mushroom-shaped islands","Jellyfish lake in the middle of a coral archipelago, western Pacific Ocean","Lake of millions of harmless jellyfish, Rock Islands of Palau","Palau's Jellyfish Lake, in Micronesia"],"mosquee_al_aqsa":["Al-Aqsa Mosque — Jerusalem","Al-Aqsa Mosque is the third holiest site in Islam, on Jerusalem's Haram al-Sharif esplanade. According to Islamic tradition, it is the destination of the Prophet Muhammad's Night Journey (Isra and Mi'raj) from Mecca. It was built in 705 AD by the Umayyad caliph al-Walid I on a site held sacred since antiquity.","Third holiest site of a major religion, built in 705 on a vast esplanade at the heart of an old city","Great mosque on the Temple Mount esplanade, Muslim Quarter of an ancient holy city","Third holiest site in Islam, Haram al-Sharif, Jerusalem","Al-Aqsa Mosque in Jerusalem"],"palmyre":["Palmyra — Syria","Palmyra's queen, Zenobia, defied Rome in 270 AD by conquering Egypt and Anatolia with her army. Captured by Emperor Aurelian, she was paraded through Rome in chains of solid gold — a paradoxical tribute to her greatness.","Ruins of a caravan oasis with an ancient temple, city of a rebel queen, partly destroyed in 2015","Ancient ruins of an oasis in the middle of the desert, 210 km from a Middle Eastern capital","Ancient city of Queen Zenobia, Syrian Desert","Palmyra, in Syria"],"persepolis":["Persepolis — Iran","Persepolis was the symbol of Achaemenid wealth and power. Its bas-reliefs depict 23 subject nations bearing their tribute: a living map of the largest empire of antiquity, which stretched from Greece to India.","Ceremonial capital of an ancient empire founded around 515 BC, Apadana staircase and Gate of All Nations","Ruins of an ancient Persian capital, Fars plain, 60 km from a major historic city","Ruins of Darius's capital, Shiraz region","Persepolis, in Iran"],"coupole_du_rocher":["Dome of the Rock — Jerusalem","The Dome of the Rock, an Islamic shrine built in 691, covers the rock from which the Prophet Muhammad is said to have ascended to heaven during his Night Journey. Its mosaics and faience tiles were fully restored by Jordan in 1959 and 2016. Its golden dome is covered with 5,000 gold plates.","Gold-domed shrine built in 691 over a sacred rock, at the centre of a vast esplanade","Golden dome on the Temple Mount esplanade, ancient holy city of the three monotheistic faiths","Gold-domed shrine on the Haram al-Sharif esplanade, Jerusalem","The Dome of the Rock in Jerusalem"],"cappadoce":["Cappadocia — Turkey","Cappadocia's fairy chimneys were inhabited for thousands of years. Early Christian communities dug entire underground cities there to escape Roman persecution — some could shelter 20,000 people across 8 levels.","Tuff fairy chimneys sculpted by the erosion of volcanic deposits, dwellings and churches carved out from the 4th century","Landscape of fairy chimneys and cave dwellings, Central Anatolian plateau","Moonlike landscape of fairy chimneys, Central Anatolia, near Nevşehir","Cappadocia, in Turkey"],"chateau_de_kronborg":["Kronborg — Helsingør, Denmark","Kronborg Castle inspired Shakespeare's Hamlet, although the playwright probably never visited Denmark. Legend has it that the Viking hero Holger Danske sleeps in its cellars and will awaken if Denmark is ever in danger.","Renaissance fortress from 1574 guarding a strategic strait, legendary setting of a Shakespeare tragedy","Coastal Renaissance castle guarding a strait, north of a peninsula, facing a neighbouring country","Hamlet's castle guarding the Øresund, Helsingør","Kronborg Castle in Helsingør, Denmark"],"opera_de_copenhague":["Copenhagen Opera House — Denmark","The Copenhagen Opera House, opened in 2005, was donated to the Danish state by the foundation of shipping magnate Mærsk Mc-Kinney Møller, at a cost of about 2.5 billion kroner (some €335 million). Its flat roof cantilevers 32 metres out over the quay, and the ceiling of the main auditorium is covered in gold leaf. It is one of the most expensive opera houses ever built.","Glass opera house opened in 2005, gift from a shipping magnate, set on an island facing a royal palace","Modern opera house on a harbour island, facing the royal residence of a Nordic capital","Modern opera house on the harbour, facing Amalienborg, Copenhagen","The Copenhagen Opera House, Denmark"],"vieux_port_de_stockholm":["Gamla stan — Stockholm, Sweden","Gamla stan, Stockholm's old town, occupies the island of Stadsholmen, where the city was founded in the 13th century. Its narrowest lane, Mårten Trotzigs gränd, is just 90 cm wide; its main square, Stortorget, was the scene of the “Stockholm Bloodbath” in 1520.","Medieval old town founded in 1252 on an island, cobbled square and narrow colourful lanes","Medieval island heart of a Nordic capital, between a lake and the Baltic Sea","Medieval old town of Gamla Stan, Stockholm","Gamla stan, Stockholm's old town, in Sweden"],"palais_royal_de_stockholm":["Stockholm Palace — Sweden","Stockholm Palace, built from 1697 to 1760 to designs by Nicodemus Tessin the Younger, has housed the royal family since 1754. With 1,430 rooms, it is one of the largest royal residences still in use in Europe. Many of its rooms are open to the public, and the changing of the guard draws crowds every day.","1,430-room royal palace built from 1697 to 1760 on an old-town island, still in use","Grand royal palace on a historic island, the monarch's residence, on the Baltic shore","One of the largest royal palaces still in use, Stockholm","Stockholm Palace in Sweden"],"bibliotheque_de_l_universite_d_helsinki":["Helsinki University Library (Kaisa) — Finland","The main library of the University of Helsinki, housed since 2012 in the Kaisa House, is the largest university library in Finland. Its arched glass façade and oval atriums stacked floor upon floor let natural light filter all the way down to the reading rooms.","University library opened in 2012, with stacked oval atriums and an arched glass façade on a shopping street","Large university library with Nordic design, in the centre of a capital on the Baltic","Main library of the University of Helsinki, Kaisa House","Helsinki University Library (Kaisa), in Finland"],"vieux_tallinn":["Tallinn Old Town — Estonia","Tallinn has one of the best-preserved medieval old towns in Northern Europe. Its pharmacy, founded in 1422, is one of the oldest pharmacies still operating in the world.","Hanseatic medieval core on two levels — cathedral hill and lower town — enclosed by ramparts","Medieval old town on a peninsula in a Baltic gulf, ramparts and towers","Best-preserved medieval old town in the Baltics, Tallinn","Tallinn Old Town in Estonia"],"chateau_de_trakai":["Trakai Island Castle — Lithuania","Trakai Island Castle, built on an island in Lake Galvė in the 14th century, is the easternmost island castle in Europe. It was the residence of Grand Duke Vytautas, who extended Lithuania as far as the Black Sea, creating the largest state in medieval Europe.","14th-century Gothic castle built on an island, residence of a grand duke, red brick on blue water","Island castle on a glacial lake, former capital, 28 km from today's capital","Red-brick island castle on a lake, Trakai","Trakai Castle on its lake, Lithuania"],"vieille_ville_de_dubrovnik":["Old City of Dubrovnik — Croatia","Dubrovnik's walls, built and strengthened from the 13th to the 17th century, were never breached by an enemy. The Republic of Ragusa was one of the first to ban the slave trade, as early as 1416, centuries before most European nations.","Former merchant city-state ringed by 1,940 m of walls, marble main street and forts overlooking the Adriatic","Fortified old town at the southern tip of a Dalmatian coast, Adriatic Sea","Pearl of the Adriatic, fortified old town, Dubrovnik","The fortified old town of Dubrovnik, Croatia"],"pont_de_mostar":["Stari Most — Mostar, Bosnia and Herzegovina","Mostar's Stari Most, an Ottoman bridge built in 1566, was deliberately destroyed by Croatian shelling in November 1993. Its reconstruction, completed in 2004, used the same 16th-century techniques and the same local stone — tenelija. The rebuilding of this bridge became a symbol of reconciliation.","Humpbacked Ottoman stone bridge from 1566, destroyed by war in 1993 and then rebuilt identically","Old humpbacked Ottoman bridge spanning an emerald river, historic centre in the Balkans","Old Ottoman bridge, symbol of reconciliation, Mostar","The Mostar Bridge in Bosnia and Herzegovina"],"place_wenceslas":["Wenceslas Square — Prague, Czech Republic","Prague's Wenceslas Square isn't really a square but a 750-metre boulevard. It has been the political centre of Bohemia since the Middle Ages. Jan Palach set himself on fire there in 1969 in protest at the Soviet invasion. In 1989, hundreds of thousands of people demonstrated there during the Velvet Revolution.","Long 750 m boulevard-square, stage for the revolutions of 1848, 1968 and the peaceful fall of 1989","Large boulevard-square in the New Town, between the main station and the national museum, Bohemia","Boulevard-square of the Velvet Revolution, Prague","Wenceslas Square in Prague, Czech Republic"],"chateau_de_wawel":["Wawel Castle — Kraków, Poland","Wawel Castle houses the royal crypt where Poland's kings, heroes and national poets are buried. It also keeps the “dragon's tooth” — actually a tooth from a mammoth or woolly rhinoceros — which has hung from a chain at the entrance since the Middle Ages.","Royal castle inhabited until 1596 and its Gothic cathedral, on a hill overlooking a river","Castle and cathedral on a royal hill, left bank of a river, Lesser Poland","Royal castle and cathedral on Wawel Hill, Kraków","Wawel Castle in Kraków, Poland"],"auschwitz_birkenau":["Auschwitz-Birkenau — Poland","Auschwitz-Birkenau was liberated on 27 January 1945 by the Soviet army. That date has become the International Day of Commemoration in Memory of the Victims of the Holocaust. The UNESCO-listed site receives more than 2 million visitors a year.","Largest extermination camp of a totalitarian regime (1940-1945), where more than a million people perished","Memorial at an extermination camp, Central European town, 60 km from a great royal city","Memorial at the Nazi extermination camp, southern Poland","Auschwitz-Birkenau, in Oświęcim, Poland"],"chateau_de_bran":["Bran Castle — Romania","Bran Castle became linked to Dracula through English tourists and publishers looking for a real-life setting for Bram Stoker's novel (1897). Stoker never visited Romania and drew solely on books. Vlad the Impaler, the real and cruel Wallachian prince, probably stayed there only very briefly.","Fortress from 1382 perched on a rock, tied to the legend of a vampire count by an 1897 novel","Fortified castle on a Carpathian pass, between two historic regions of Eastern Europe","So-called “Dracula's Castle” in the Carpathians, Transylvania","Bran Castle in Romania"],"lavra_de_kiev":["Kyiv Pechersk Lavra — Ukraine","The Kyiv Pechersk Lavra, founded in 1051, is the oldest monastery of Kievan Rus'. Its catacombs hold the naturally mummified bodies of medieval monks — a macabre attraction nine centuries old. It was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1990.","Orthodox monastery founded in 1051, network of catacombs with mummified saints, on a hill above a river","Cave monastery on the right bank of a great Eastern European river","Monastery of the Caves, a great centre of Slavic Orthodoxy, Kyiv","The Kyiv Pechersk Lavra (Monastery of the Caves), in Ukraine"],"cliffs_of_moher":["Cliffs of Moher — Ireland","The Cliffs of Moher appear in Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince (2009). They are home to some 30,000 seabirds of around twenty species, including puffins that dig their burrows into the grassy clifftops.","Sheer 214 m cliffs stretching 8 km, 320-million-year-old rock pounded by the ocean","High sea cliffs on the west coast of a North Atlantic island","Spectacular Atlantic cliffs, west of Ireland","The Cliffs of Moher, Ireland"],"chateau_de_blarney":["Blarney Castle — Ireland","Blarney Castle's Stone of Eloquence, set beneath the parapet about 25 metres up, is kissed by leaning over backwards above the drop. According to legend, whoever kisses it receives the gift of “blarney”, the art of speaking with charm and persuasion. Winston Churchill and Mick Jagger are said to have kissed it.","Medieval tower from 1446 holding at its top a stone whose kiss is said to bestow eloquence","Medieval castle and its legendary stone, village 8 km from a major port city","Castle of the Stone of Eloquence, near Cork","Blarney Castle in Ireland"],"cathedrale_de_cologne":["Cologne Cathedral — Germany","Cologne Cathedral took 632 years to build (1248-1880) — the longest construction of any Gothic cathedral in history. Its 157-metre twin spires made it the tallest building in the world from 1880 to 1884. It houses the supposed relics of the Three Kings, brought from Milan in 1164 — which made it the greatest medieval pilgrimage in northern Europe.","157 m Gothic cathedral, begun in 1248 and finished in 1880, housing the Shrine of the Three Kings","Huge Gothic cathedral on a riverbank, facing a railway bridge, Rhineland","Twin-spired Gothic cathedral, a major attraction, Cologne","Cologne Cathedral in Germany"],"foret_noire":["Black Forest — Baden-Württemberg, Germany","The Black Forest is the land of the cuckoo clock and gave its name to the famous gateau. Its dark spruce forests embody the world of Germanic fairy tales. The Schwarzwaldbahn, a railway line completed in 1873, winds its way through 39 tunnels in a fairy-tale landscape.","160 km Hercynian forested massif, source of a great river, land of the cuckoo and the dark forest","Famous forested massif between a great river and a limestone plateau, in the south-west of a Central European country","Forested massif of cuckoo clocks and gateaux, south-west Germany","The Black Forest in Baden-Württemberg, Germany"],"chateau_de_chillon":["Chillon Castle — Switzerland","Lord Byron carved his name on a pillar in the dungeon of Chillon Castle during his visit in 1816. He went on to write “The Prisoner of Chillon”, a poem inspired by François Bonivard, imprisoned for 4 years in the vaults for opposing the Duke of Savoy.","12th-century castle on a rock in a large lake, former prison immortalised by a Romantic poet","Medieval castle on the shore of an Alpine lake, canton of Vaud, between two lakeside towns","Medieval castle on Lake Geneva, near Montreux","Chillon Castle on the shore of Lake Geneva, Switzerland"],"jet_d_eau":["Jet d'Eau — Geneva, Switzerland","Geneva's Jet d'Eau shoots 500 litres per second up to a height of 140 metres. It began in 1886 as a simple safety valve for the city's hydraulic network, before being moved to the harbour in 1891 to become an attraction. In strong winds, its spray can drench people strolling along the jetty.","140 m plume of water propelled at 200 km/h, 500 litres per second, in service since 1891","Giant fountain shooting up from a lake harbour, waterfront of a city on a large lake","Giant fountain on Lake Geneva, symbol of Geneva","The Jet d'Eau in Geneva, Switzerland"],"lac_baikal":["Lake Baikal — Siberia, Russia","Lake Baikal is so deep (1,642 m) that you could stack five Eiffel Towers in it without them poking above the surface. It is home to the Baikal seal (nerpa), one of the very few freshwater seals in the world, whose ancestors are thought to have come from the Arctic; the route and timing of that migration are still debated.","World's oldest and deepest lake (1,642 m), 20% of the planet's liquid fresh water","Huge, very deep lake in southern Siberia, 66 km from a large regional city","Deepest lake in the world, Siberia, near Irkutsk","Lake Baikal in Siberia, Russia"],"registan":["Registan — Samarkand, Uzbekistan","Samarkand's Registan square is adorned with 600-year-old ceramic tiles whose colours are as vivid as on the day they were made. The Timurid craftsmen's secret for producing this unfading lapis-lazuli blue has never been fully unravelled.","Square with three madrasas of blue majolica (1420-1660), jewel of a legendary trade route","Monumental square with blue madrasas, crossroads city on the Silk Road, Central Asia","Most beautiful square in Central Asia, three golden madrasas, Samarkand","The Registan in Samarkand, Uzbekistan"],"palais_de_topkapi":["Topkapı Palace — Istanbul, Turkey","Topkapı Palace was the centre of the Ottoman Empire for 400 years. Its Harem housed up to 600 women and eunuchs. The palace holds Islam's most sacred relics: the Prophet Muhammad's cloak and sword, as well as a tooth and a hair that, according to tradition, belonged to him.","Palace of the sultans from 1465 to 1856, courtyards of pavilions holding sacred relics and legendary diamonds","Imperial palace on the tip of the historic peninsula, between the Golden Horn and the Bosphorus","Palace of the Ottoman sultans and its treasury, Istanbul","Topkapı Palace in Istanbul, Turkey"],"pamukkale":["Pamukkale — Turkey","Pamukkale — “cotton castle” in Turkish — is a unique natural site: 35°C hot springs laden with calcium carbonate have created white travertine terraces and cascades stretching over 2,700 metres. The ancient city of Hierapolis, built above it, had 100,000 inhabitants at its Roman peak.","Tiered white limestone terraces formed by lime-rich hot springs, turquoise pools","Petrified cascades of white travertine, valley in western Anatolia, ancient ruins nearby","White travertine terraces and thermal pools, western Turkey","Pamukkale in Turkey"],"bagan":["Bagan — Myanmar","Bagan's temples are decorated with frescoes depicting the life of the Buddha. After the 1975 earthquake, controversial restorations using concrete and modern bricks were criticised by archaeologists and long delayed its listing: Bagan was only inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2019.","Plain bristling with more than 2,000 temples and stupas, former capital of a kingdom (849-1297)","Plain of thousands of temples beside a great river, central dry zone of an Asian country","Plain of thousands of Buddhist temples, central Myanmar","Bagan, in Myanmar"],"ha_long_bay":["Ha Long Bay — Quảng Ninh, Vietnam","Ha Long Bay has nearly 2,000 limestone islets and pinnacles, sculpted by erosion from rocks whose geological history spans hundreds of millions of years. According to Vietnamese legend, a dragon came down from the sky lashing its tail, and its blows carved out the valleys and bays. The bay is also home to spectacular caves and floating fishing villages.","Bay dotted with nearly 2,000 karst limestone pinnacles and caves, emerald waters scattered with junks","Bay of nearly 2,000 limestone islets in a South-East Asian gulf, a semi-enclosed sea","Bay of limestone pinnacles and junks, north-east Vietnam","Ha Long Bay, in Vietnam"],"rizieres_en_terrasses_de_banaue":["Banaue Rice Terraces — Philippines","The Banaue Rice Terraces, in the Cordillera of Luzon, were carved into the mountainside by the Ifugao people, without metal tools or written plans. Tradition holds them to be 2,000 years old, although some researchers think they are more recent. They climb to 1,500 metres above sea level, and their walls laid end to end would reportedly stretch some 20,000 km, half the way around the Earth.","Rice terraces carved 2,000 years ago into the side of a cordillera, low walls hugging the mountain","Ancient rice terraces in a mountain range on a large tropical island","Ancient rice terraces, mountains of Luzon, Philippines","The Banaue Rice Terraces, Philippines"],"lac_tonle_sap":["Tonlé Sap — Cambodia","Tonlé Sap is the only lake in the world whose river changes direction twice a year. In the rainy season, the Mekong forces its waters back into the lake, which grows fivefold. In the dry season, the flow reverses and the lake gradually drains.","Largest freshwater lake in the region, swelling from 2,500 to 16,000 km² between dry season and monsoon","Large central lake linked to South-East Asia's longest river, stilt villages","Largest lake in South-East Asia, floating villages, central Cambodia","Tonlé Sap Lake in Cambodia"],"gunung_bromo":["Mount Bromo — Java, Indonesia","Mount Bromo is an active volcano inside a giant caldera where 30,000 Tengger people still live. Every year during the Yadnya Kasada festival, they throw offerings — flowers, rice, chickens, goats — into the smoking crater to thank the gods.","Active 2,329 m volcano smoking in the middle of a caldera, 800 m crater ringed by a sea of sand","Smoking volcano in a caldera with its sea of ash, east of a large tropical island","Smoking volcano in a sea of sand, eastern Java","Mount Bromo in Java, Indonesia"],"mrauk_u":["Mrauk U — Myanmar","Mrauk U was the capital of the Kingdom of Arakan from the 15th to the 18th century, and one of the richest cities in South-East Asia. Its hundreds of brick temples, engulfed by vegetation, are compared to those of Bagan but are far less well known. Long reachable mainly by boat on the Kaladan River, the site has stayed off the mass-tourism trail.","Former capital of a kingdom (1430-1784), brick temples on hills, reached mainly by boat","Forgotten archaeological city on a river, coastal state in the north-west of an Asian country","Forgotten city of hilltop temples, western Myanmar","Mrauk U, in Myanmar"],"mosquee_de_dome_des_umayyades":["Umayyad Mosque — Damascus, Syria","The Great Umayyad Mosque of Damascus is one of the oldest mosques in the world (715 AD). It was built over a Byzantine church, itself built on a Roman temple to Jupiter. According to tradition, the head of John the Baptist is kept there in a shrine.","Great mosque built from 706 to 715 on the site of a former basilica, housing a relic of John the Baptist, with the tomb of a legendary sultan at its gates","Ancient great mosque at the heart of one of the oldest inhabited capitals in the world, Middle East","Umayyad Mosque, jewel of Islamic art, Damascus","The Umayyad Mosque in Damascus, Syria"],"massif_de_l_altai":["Altai Mountains — Mongolia","The Mongolian Altai range, in the far west of the country, is the domain of the Kazakh eagle hunters, who train golden eagles for hunting: a tradition several thousand years old kept alive by the “berkutchi”. Every autumn, an eagle festival brings these hunters together near Ölgii. Its valleys also hold thousands of prehistoric petroglyphs.","Glaciated massif rising to 4,374 m, engraved with 12,000-year-old petroglyphs, nomadic eagle hunters","Glaciated massif in the far west of a steppe country, tripoint of three nations","Glaciated massif of the Kazakh eagle hunters, far west of Mongolia","The Altai Mountains in Mongolia"],"tombouctu":["Timbuktu — Mali","Timbuktu, nicknamed “the city of 333 saints”, was in the 15th century the intellectual and spiritual capital of Africa, with 25,000 students and a university renowned throughout the Islamic world. Its 700,000 medieval manuscripts, preserved in private libraries, form a unique archive of pre-colonial African thought.","Legendary Sahel caravan city, crossroads of gold and salt, mud mosques and ancient manuscripts","Legendary city on the banks of a great river, on the southern edge of the Sahara","Mythical city of manuscripts and caravans, northern Mali","Timbuktu, in Mali"],"desert_du_sahara":["Tassili n'Ajjer — Sahara, Algeria","Tassili n'Ajjer, in Algeria, is a vast rocky plateau holding some 15,000 rock paintings and engravings, made mostly between 10,000 and 2,000 BC. These works depict elephants, hippos and crocodiles, proof that the Sahara was once a lush green savannah. UNESCO lists it as both natural and cultural heritage.","Sandstone plateau at 1,800 m covered with 15,000 rock engravings and paintings, deep in the desert","Plateau of prehistoric rock art, central Sahara, far south of a North African country","Rock-art plateau of Tassili n'Ajjer, Algerian Sahara","Tassili n'Ajjer, in the Algerian Sahara"],"medina_de_fes":["Medina of Fez — Morocco","The medina of Fez el-Bali, founded in 789, is the largest car-free urban area in the world — no car can get through. Its thousands of labyrinthine alleys can leave even locals going round in circles. The Chouara tanneries, at work since the 11th century, produce leather by unchanged methods — vats of natural dyes hollowed out of the rock.","World's largest car-free medina, founded in 789, home to the oldest university still operating (859)","Huge medieval medina between a mid-altitude mountain range and hills, north-central part of a kingdom","Medieval medina and the world's oldest university, Fez","The Medina of Fez, in Morocco"],"chateau_de_goree":["Gorée Castle — Senegal (Fort d'Estrées)","Fort d'Estrées, on Gorée Island, was built by the French between 1852 and 1856 to defend the Dakar roadstead; today it houses the Historical Museum of Senegal. The island, a major site of memory of the slave trade, is known for its House of Slaves and its “Door of No Return” opening onto the Atlantic.","Fort built by the French in the mid-19th century, now a history museum, on a small island steeped in memory","Colonial fort on a memorial island in a West African bay, 3 km from the mainland","Colonial fort turned history museum, Gorée Island, off Dakar","Gorée Castle in Senegal (Fort d'Estrées)"],"serengeti":["Serengeti — Tanzania","The Serengeti's Great Migration is guided solely by collective instinct and the wildebeest's search for fresh grass. No animal “leads” the movement: it is a phenomenon of collective intelligence that every year sweeps some 1.5 million wildebeest, accompanied by hundreds of thousands of zebras and gazelles, around an immense circuit between Tanzania and Kenya.","Immense “endless” plain of 14,763 km², stage for the migration of two million wildebeest and zebras","Vast savannah in the north-west of an East African country, extended by a cross-border park","Great migration of nearly two million herbivores, vast Tanzanian savannah","The Serengeti in Tanzania"],"masai_mara":["Masai Mara — Kenya","The wildebeest crossing of the Mara River is one of the most dramatic natural spectacles in the world. Hundreds of animals plunge into waters infested with Nile crocodiles — some of the Mara's crocodiles weigh over 700 kg and have been waiting for the migrations for decades.","1,510 km² reserve crossed by a river, great animal migration from July to October","Wildlife reserve in the south-west of an East African country, extension of a large neighbouring park","Big Five reserve extending the Serengeti, south-west Kenya","The Masai Mara in Kenya"],"chutes_de_kalandula":["Kalandula Falls — Angola","The Kalandula Falls, in Angola, are among the largest in Africa by volume. Plunging 105 metres across a width of 400 metres, they create a permanent mist that sustains lush tropical vegetation all around.","Falls 105 m high and 400 m wide in the rainy season, among the largest on the continent, lush savannah","Great waterfalls on an inland plateau, savannah of a west-central African country","Among the largest waterfalls in Africa, Malanje Province, Angola","The Kalandula Falls in Angola"],"grand_zimbabwe":["Great Zimbabwe — Zimbabwe","Great Zimbabwe, whose name is thought to evoke “houses of stone” in Shona, is the largest complex of stone ruins in sub-Saharan Africa. Its 11-metre-high walls were built without mortar with astonishing precision. It gave its name to the country at independence in 1980.","Capital of a medieval empire (11th-15th c.), 244 m elliptical enclosure built without mortar","Ruins of a medieval dry-stone royal city, centre of a Southern African country","Largest stone site in sub-Saharan Africa, royal ruins of Zimbabwe","Great Zimbabwe in Zimbabwe"],"vallee_des_rois":["Valley of the Kings — Luxor, Egypt","Tutankhamun is the only pharaoh in the Valley of the Kings whose tomb was found almost intact. Howard Carter opened it in 1922 after 10 years of excavation. The curse said to have struck the excavators is now attributed to moulds or pathogenic spores preserved in the stale, enclosed air.","New Kingdom royal necropolis of 63 rock-cut tombs, including that of a young pharaoh (KV62)","Necropolis valley on the west bank of a river, facing an ancient capital, Upper valley","Necropolis of the pharaohs, Tutankhamun's tomb, Luxor","The Valley of the Kings in Luxor, Egypt"],"yellowknife":["Yellowknife — Northwest Territories, Canada","Yellowknife is the best city in the world for watching the northern lights, with 240 aurora nights a year. Its latitude (62°N) places it directly beneath the “auroral oval” — the zone of peak magnetic activity. The aurora can be seen with the naked eye from nightfall in autumn and winter.","Subarctic territorial capital on the shore of a great lake, one of the best spots for watching the aurora","Subarctic town on the shore of a large frozen lake, 62° north, the Far North","Far North capital of the northern lights, Northwest Territories","Yellowknife, in the Northwest Territories, Canada"],"quebec":["Quebec City — Canada","Quebec City is the only fortified city in North America north of Mexico. Its 17th-century ramparts, 4.6 km long, are still perfectly preserved. The Château Frontenac, the most photographed hotel in the world according to Guinness, has towered over Cap Diamant since 1893.","Château-style hotel opened in 1893 by a railway company, overlooking a river from a fortified headland","Fortified upper town on a headland above a great river, the only walled city in North America","Château Frontenac overlooking the fortified city, Quebec","Quebec City, Canada"],"parc_banff":["Banff National Park — Alberta, Canada","Banff National Park is Canada's oldest, founded in 1885 after hot springs were discovered. Its Lake Louise, fed by meltwater from the Victoria Glacier, is such an intense turquoise that it looks artificial. The Château Lake Louise, built by the Canadian railways in 1890, made it the most romantic spot in Canada.","Its country's first national park (1885), turquoise glacial lake and vast icefield, high mountains","Park of mountains and turquoise lakes in the western Rockies, 130 km from a major city","Lake Louise and the Rockies, Banff National Park, western Canada","Banff National Park, in Alberta, Canada"],"monument_valley":["Monument Valley — Arizona/Utah, USA","Monument Valley is the private property of the Navajo Nation, which strictly controls tourism there. Its red rock formations, known as “buttes”, are so emblematic of the American West that they served as the backdrop for dozens of John Ford westerns.","Red sandstone mesas and buttes rising from a desert, backdrop to many classic westerns","Red sandstone buttes on Indigenous land, on the border of two states, Colorado Plateau","Red-mesa landscape of the American West, Arizona–Utah border","Monument Valley, between Arizona and Utah, USA"],"antelope_canyon":["Antelope Canyon — Arizona, USA","Antelope Canyon is a “slot canyon” carved by millennia of erosion from flash floods. In summer, shafts of light pour in through the opening and create “light beams” in the dust — among the most reproduced photographs in the world.","Slot canyon with wavy sandstone walls streaked with light, carved by floods and wind","Slot canyon on Indigenous land, near a small town in northern Arizona","Slot canyon of orange sandstone waves, near Page, Arizona","Antelope Canyon, near Page, Arizona, USA"],"ligne_nazc":["Nazca Lines — Peru","The Nazca Lines were created by removing the dark stones of the desert to reveal the pale sand beneath. They cover 450 km² and depict 70 animals and plants and 300 geometric figures. Maria Reiche devoted 50 years of her life to studying them, living alone in the desert to protect them.","Giant geoglyphs — hummingbird, monkey, spider — drawn in the desert between 500 BC and AD 500, visible from the air","Vast geoglyphs traced across an arid plain in an Andean coastal desert","Mysterious desert geoglyphs, southern Peru","The Nazca Lines, in Peru"],"galeries_d_art_de_buenos_aires":["Museum of Modern Art of Buenos Aires — San Telmo, Argentina","The Museum of Modern Art of Buenos Aires, opened in 1956 at the initiative of the critic Rafael Squirru and the sculptor Pablo Curatella Manes, moved in 1986 into a former brick warehouse of the Nobleza Piccardo tobacco company, in the old San Telmo district. Enlarged and renovated since, it holds more than 6,000 works of modern and contemporary art, mostly Argentine.","Museum of modern art founded in 1956, housed in a former brick tobacco warehouse with plant-covered balconies","Museum of modern art in an old cobbled district of antique dealers, at the heart of a great South American capital","Museum of modern art in the San Telmo district, Buenos Aires","The Museum of Modern Art of Buenos Aires, Argentina"],"carnaval_de_rio":["Rio Carnival — Sambadrome, Brazil","The Rio Carnival is the biggest in the world, with 2 million people a day in the streets. The Sambódromo, designed by Oscar Niemeyer in 1984, can hold 90,000 spectators. The samba schools work all year round on their floats and costumes — each with an annual budget of several million dollars.","700 m parade strip designed in 1984, stands for 90,000 spectators, annual temple of samba","Carnival stadium-avenue in the heart of a city nicknamed “marvellous”, Brazilian coast","Sambadrome of the world's biggest carnival, Rio de Janeiro","The Sambadrome of the Rio de Janeiro Carnival, Brazil"],"lac_titicaca":["Lake Titicaca — Bolivia/Peru","Lake Titicaca is home to the floating islands of the Uros, built entirely from totora reeds on the water. The inhabitants constantly renew their island by adding fresh layers on top of the decaying ones.","World's highest navigable lake (3,812 m), sacred Island of the Sun and villages on floating reed islands","World's highest large navigable lake, high Andean plateau, straddling a border","World's highest navigable lake, Altiplano, Peru–Bolivia border","Lake Titicaca in Bolivia/Peru"],"salar_d_uyuni":["Salar de Uyuni — Bolivia","The Salar de Uyuni holds a considerable share of the world's lithium reserves, the raw material for electric car batteries. After the rains, the entire salt flat is covered by a perfect mirror of a few centimetres of water.","World's largest salt flat (10,582 km²), a perfect mirror after rain, huge lithium reserves","Immense salt expanse on a high plateau at 3,656 m, lithium reserves","World's largest salt flat, sky reflections, south-western Bolivia","The Salar de Uyuni in Bolivia"],"orinoco":["Angostura Bridge — Ciudad Bolívar, Venezuela","Opened in 1967, the Angostura Bridge was for a long time the only bridge across the Orinoco. This suspension bridge, nearly 1,680 m long with a 712 m main span, links Ciudad Bolívar to the north bank, where the river narrows: it was this “strait” (angostura) that once gave its name to the city, and to the famous bitters.","1967 suspension bridge with a 712 m main span, crossing a great tropical river at its narrowest point","Long suspension bridge across a great tropical river, facing a colonial city in the east of a South American country","First bridge over the Orinoco, at Ciudad Bolívar, eastern Venezuela","The Angostura Bridge at Ciudad Bolívar, Venezuela"],"daintree_rainforest":["Daintree Rainforest — Queensland, Australia","The Daintree Rainforest, some 135 million years old by common estimates, is often described as older than the Amazon. It is home to primitive plants, such as tree ferns and Idiospermum, whose lineages date back to the age of the dinosaurs. It is a living museum of evolution.","World's oldest tropical rainforest (135 million years), cassowaries and platypuses, right down to the sea","Primary tropical rainforest in the far north of a coastal state on a southern continent","World's oldest tropical rainforest, Far North Queensland","The Daintree Rainforest, in Queensland, Australia"],"rotorua":["Rotorua — New Zealand","Rotorua permanently smells of sulphur, which locals jokingly call “the smell of money”. Geothermal heat naturally warms homes, hotels and pools there, and its mud pools bubble at nearly 100 °C.","Geothermal area with 30 m geysers and bubbling mud pools, strong smell of sulphur, living Māori culture","Geothermal city on a lakeshore, North Island, geysers and bubbling mud","Geysers and bubbling mud, Māori capital, New Zealand's North Island","Rotorua, in New Zealand"],"iles_marquises":["Marquesas Islands — French Polynesia","The Marquesas Islands are among the inhabited lands furthest from any continent: about 1,400 km from Tahiti and nearly 5,000 km from the American coast. Paul Gauguin spent his last years there and died on Hiva Oa in 1903; Jacques Brel lies in the same cemetery in Atuona.","Volcanic archipelago of 12 islands, cradle of Polynesian settlement, stone tikis, one island sung by Brel","Very remote volcanic archipelago in the South Pacific, 1,400 km north-east of a main island","Archipelago of the tikis, one of the most remote in the inhabited world, South Pacific","The Marquesas Islands in French Polynesia"],"parc_national_de_fiordland":["Fiordland National Park — New Zealand","Fiordland National Park receives up to 9 metres of rain a year — one of the wettest places on Earth. This water creates hundreds of short-lived waterfalls that cascade down the sheer rock walls during and after every downpour.","1.26 million hectares of wilderness, a 340 m-deep fjord, dolphins and swarms of sandflies","The country's largest national park, fjords and forests, far south-west of the South Island","Largest national park of fjords and wilderness, south-west of the South Island","Fiordland National Park in New Zealand"],"nauru":["Nauru — Central Pacific","In the 1980s, Nauru had the highest per capita income in the world thanks to its phosphate deposits. With its resources exhausted, the island is now one of the most indebted countries and suffers from endemic obesity linked to an imported ultra-processed diet.","World's third-smallest state (21 km²), once rich from phosphate, inland lagoon, isolated island","Tiny isolated coral island in the central Pacific, thousands of kilometres from any continent","World's 3rd-smallest country, phosphate island in the Pacific","Nauru, a small island republic in the Pacific"],"vatnajokull":["Vatnajökull — Iceland","The Vatnajökull glacier hides several active volcanoes beneath its ice. During eruptions, the heat melts huge quantities of ice in a matter of hours, creating jökulhlaups — cataclysmic glacial floods that have shaped the Icelandic landscape.","Europe's largest glacier (7,900 km²) capping a volcano, blue ice caves and an iceberg lagoon","Immense ice cap in the south-east of a subarctic volcanic island","Europe's largest glacier, iceberg lagoon, south-east Iceland","Vatnajökull in Iceland"],"colonne_de_trajan":["Trajan's Column — Rome, Italy","Trajan's Column, inaugurated in AD 113, is decorated with a 190-metre spiral frieze that recounts the two Dacian Wars in 2,662 figures: one of the first “comic strips” in history. It was topped by a statue of Trajan, replaced by one of Saint Peter on the orders of Pope Sixtus V in 1588.","38 m marble column erected in 113, spiral frieze recounting wars, at the heart of an ancient forum","Triumphal column carved in a spiral, in an ancient forum, between two historic hills","Triumphal column with 2,662 carved figures, Rome","Trajan's Column in Rome, Italy"],"fontaine_de_trevi":["Trevi Fountain — Rome, Italy","The Trevi Fountain, completed in 1762 to designs by Nicola Salvi, collects about €1.5 million in coins thrown by tourists every year, donated to Caritas Roma. The tradition of tossing a coin over your shoulder to return to Rome was popularised by the film “Three Coins in the Fountain” (1954), and “La Dolce Vita” (1960) sealed its status as an icon.","Large 26 m Baroque fountain completed in 1762, set against a palace, fed by an ancient aqueduct","Monumental Baroque fountain where three streets meet in a historic centre, coin-tossing tradition","The city's largest Baroque fountain, toss a coin to come back, Rome","The Trevi Fountain in Rome, Italy"],"pantheon":["Pantheon — Rome, Italy","The Pantheon in Rome, built in AD 125 by Hadrian, has a dome 43.3 metres in diameter — which remained the world's largest unreinforced dome for 1,300 years. The 9-metre oculus at its top is the only source of light. On rainy days, water falls straight onto the marble floor and is carried away by Roman underground drainage.","Ancient temple rebuilt under Hadrian (118–125), concrete dome with an oculus, the best preserved of Antiquity","Ancient temple with a dome and oculus, on a round piazza in the historic centre","Best-preserved Roman temple, oculus dome, Rome","The Pantheon in Rome, Italy"],"piazza_san_marco":["St Mark's Square — Venice, Italy","Napoleon is said to have called St Mark's Square “the finest drawing room in Europe”. As the lowest point in Venice, it is the first place to flood at high tide (acqua alta). Its campanile, which collapsed on 14 July 1902, was rebuilt identically and inaugurated on 25 April 1912, St Mark's Day.","The only large square of an island city, 11th-century golden basilica, 99 m campanile, seasonal flooding","Large square on the edge of a lagoon, at the end of a grand canal, city built on water","St Mark's Square, Byzantine basilica and campanile, heart of Venice","Piazza San Marco in Venice, Italy"],"pompei":["Pompeii — Campania, Italy","Pompeii was frozen in time by the ash and pumice of Vesuvius in AD 79: inhabitants were caught in the middle of everyday life, and the casts of their bodies preserve their imprint. About two-thirds of the city has been excavated; the rest is held in reserve for the archaeologists of tomorrow.","Roman town buried under 6 m of ash in AD 79, bodies cast in the ash, an ancient city of 66 hectares","Ancient city frozen by an eruption at the foot of a volcano, on a gulf in the south of a Mediterranean peninsula","Roman city frozen by Vesuvius in AD 79, near Naples","Pompeii, in Campania, Italy"],"dome_de_florence":["Florence Cathedral — Italy","The dome of Santa Maria del Fiore, designed by Filippo Brunelleschi and completed in 1436, is still the largest masonry dome ever built. Brunelleschi raised it without wooden centring, thanks to a double shell and herringbone brickwork, and even invented hoisting machines for the building site.","Cathedral with an immense brick dome completed in 1436 by a master builder, 114 m, the first great dome of the Renaissance","Cathedral with a red dome at the heart of a cradle of the Renaissance, Tuscany","Brunelleschi's dome, symbol of the Renaissance, Florence","Florence Cathedral in Italy"],"galerie_des_offices":["Uffizi Gallery — Florence, Italy","Florence's Uffizi Gallery holds one of the greatest collections of Italian Renaissance paintings: Botticelli's The Birth of Venus and Primavera, and Leonardo da Vinci's Annunciation. Vasari designed the building in 1560 for the Medici. The Vasari Corridor, a raised gallery above the Arno, links the Uffizi to the Palazzo Pitti.","Palace-gallery designed in 1560, home to The Birth of Venus and Primavera by a Florentine master","Major Renaissance art museum on a riverbank, in the Tuscan cradle of the Renaissance","Largest museum of Renaissance art, Florence","The Uffizi Gallery in Florence, Italy"],"parc_national_des_cinque_terre":["Cinque Terre National Park — Italy","The vineyard terraces of the Cinque Terre were built by hand, without machinery, on dizzying slopes. Centuries of work have left thousands of kilometres of dry-stone walls, a network often compared in length to the Great Wall of China.","Five villages clinging to cliffs and linked by footpaths, colourful houses tumbling down to the sea","Five colourful villages on sea cliffs, Ligurian coast, north-west of a peninsula","Five colourful villages on the Ligurian cliffs, near La Spezia","Cinque Terre National Park in Italy"],"parc_de_blenheim":["Blenheim Palace — Woodstock, United Kingdom","Blenheim Palace is the only non-royal palace in England — a gift from the nation to the Duke of Marlborough after his victory at Blenheim in 1704. Winston Churchill was born there in 1874. Its park, laid out by Capability Brown, is regarded as the masterpiece of the English natural landscape style.","Baroque palace built from 1705 to 1722 for a duke and general, birthplace of a great prime minister","Grand Baroque palace and its park, rural county, 13 km north of a university city","Baroque palace, Churchill's birthplace, Oxfordshire","Blenheim Palace, in Oxfordshire, United Kingdom"],"tower_bridge":["Tower Bridge — London, United Kingdom","Tower Bridge can open in 5 minutes to let large ships through — which still happens around 800 times a year. Its two medieval-style towers conceal an 11,000-tonne steel structure. Opened in 1894, it was originally painted chocolate brown and cream — its current blue-grey colour dates from 1977.","1894 bascule bridge with two 65 m neo-Gothic towers, its deck rising for ships","Two-towered bascule bridge over a river, between a medieval tower and the south bank","Twin-towered bascule bridge over the Thames, symbol of London","Tower Bridge in London, United Kingdom"],"cathedrale_de_canterbury":["Canterbury Cathedral — United Kingdom","Canterbury Cathedral is the seat of the Archbishop of Canterbury, spiritual leader of the worldwide Anglican Church. Thomas Becket was murdered there in 1170 on the orders of Henry II. His martyrdom made it one of the most important pilgrimages of medieval Europe — immortalised in Chaucer's Canterbury Tales.","Seat of a Church's primate, site of an archbishop's murder in 1170 and of a famous medieval pilgrimage","Great cathedral of a Kent city, in the south-east of the country, 90 km from the capital","Cradle of the worldwide Anglican Church, martyrdom of Thomas Becket, Canterbury","Canterbury Cathedral, England (United Kingdom)"],"parc_guell":["Park Güell — Barcelona, Spain","Park Güell was designed by Gaudí between 1900 and 1914 as a private garden city for 60 families — only 2 houses were built, one of them Gaudí's. Financed by Eusebi Güell, who wanted an ideal city, the commercial venture was a complete failure but gave rise to one of the most visited parks in the world.","Park laid out from 1900 to 1914 by a genius architect, broken-tile mosaics, an undulating bench and a multicoloured lizard","Mosaic park on a hill north of the city, panoramic sea views, Catalonia","Gaudí's colourful mosaic park, terrace with a serpentine bench, Barcelona","Park Güell in Barcelona, Spain"],"santiago_de_compostela":["Santiago de Compostela — Spain","The cathedral of Santiago de Compostela is the end point of the Way of St James. Its giant censer, the “Botafumeiro”, weighs 80 kg and swings at 68 km/h on the end of a 65-metre rope during high masses. Its original purpose was to freshen air heavy with the smell of thousands of unwashed pilgrims.","Cathedral housing the supposed tomb of an apostle, end point of a great pilgrimage, giant censer","Cathedral at the end of a famous pilgrimage, old town in Galicia, north-west of the Iberian Peninsula","Cathedral at the end of the Way of St James, Galicia","Santiago de Compostela Cathedral, Spain"],"parc_national_de_donana":["Doñana National Park — Spain","Doñana is one of the largest wetlands in Europe and a major refuge of the Iberian lynx, long considered the most endangered cat in the world. Flamingos, egrets and imperial eagles nest there in their thousands. Its shifting dunes advance several metres a year, gradually swallowing the pine forest.","543 km² of marshes, dunes and scrubland at a river mouth, refuge of the Iberian lynx and the imperial eagle","Large wetland reserve at the mouth of a river, Andalusia, Gulf of Cádiz","Largest reserve in Western Europe, Iberian lynx, southern Spain","Doñana National Park in Spain"],"acueducto_de_segovia":["Aqueduct of Segovia — Spain","The Aqueduct of Segovia, built without cement between AD 50 and 100, rests on 166 double arches 9 metres wide — 30,000 granite blocks held in place by friction and gravity. It supplied the city with water from the Sierra de Guadarrama until 1973. Its stones bear the marks of the Roman legions that built it.","1st-century Roman aqueduct, 728 m long, 166 granite arches stacked without mortar, up to 28 m high","Two-tiered Roman aqueduct at the entrance to an old town, Castile","Perfectly preserved Roman aqueduct, emblem of Segovia","The Aqueduct of Segovia in Spain"],"evora":["Évora — Portugal","Évora, an old Roman town in the Alentejo, is home to a perfectly preserved 1st-century Temple of Diana and a Capela dos Ossos (chapel of skulls) lined with the bones of 5,000 monks — its inscription reads: “Nós ossos que aqui estamos pelos vossos esperamos” (We bones that are here await yours).","Open-air museum town, columned Roman temple and a chapel lined with 5,000 skulls","Historic city of Roman and medieval monuments, Alentejo plain, 135 km from the capital","Museum town with a Roman temple and a chapel of bones, Alentejo, Évora","Évora, in Portugal"],"palais_de_sintra":["Quinta da Regaleira — Sintra, Portugal","Quinta da Regaleira was dreamt up in the early 20th century by the millionaire Carvalho Monteiro and the Italian architect Luigi Manini. Its grounds hide grottoes, tunnels and a 27 m “initiation well”, whose spiral staircase with nine landings is said to evoke the nine circles of Dante's Inferno.","Early 20th-century neo-Manueline palace, grounds with grottoes and a 27 m initiation well with a spiral staircase","Romantic estate with an initiation well on a wooded serra, west of an Atlantic capital","Quinta with the initiation well, in the hills of Sintra","Quinta da Regaleira in Sintra, Portugal"],"grand_place":["Grand-Place — Brussels, Belgium","Victor Hugo is said to have called the Grand-Place in Brussels “the most beautiful square in the world”. Its Gothic town hall dates from the 15th century, while its gilded guildhalls were rebuilt after the French bombardment of 1695. In even-numbered years, in mid-August, it is covered with an immense carpet of begonias.","Square ringed by gilded guildhalls and a Gothic town hall of 1402, biennial flower carpet","Large monumental square at the heart of the historic Brabant pentagon","Square of gilded façades judged the most beautiful in the world by Victor Hugo, Brussels","The Grand-Place in Brussels, Belgium"],"cathedrale_notre_dame_d_anvers":["Cathedral of Our Lady — Antwerp, Belgium","The Cathedral of Our Lady in Antwerp is the largest Gothic cathedral in Belgium, with its 123-metre spire. It houses four paintings by Rubens, including The Descent from the Cross — regarded as his absolute masterpieces. Rubens lived and died in Antwerp; his house-museum is a few hundred metres away.","Gothic cathedral with a 123 m spire, built from 1352 to 1521, adorned with four Rubens triptychs","Great Gothic cathedral in the historic centre of a port, Flemish province","Gothic cathedral with its Rubens paintings, Antwerp","The Cathedral of Our Lady in Antwerp, Belgium"],"chateau_de_vianden":["Vianden Castle — Luxembourg","Vianden Castle is one of the largest Romanesque and Gothic castles in Western Europe. Victor Hugo stayed in Vianden several times, notably in 1871 during his exile; his house on the banks of the Our is now a museum. The castle's silhouette above the valley is Luxembourg's tourist emblem.","10th–14th-century medieval castle of counts, later of a great princely house, overlooking a river","Medieval castle above a river, in the north of a small landlocked country in Western Europe","The country's most beautiful castle, Our valley, Vianden","Vianden Castle in Luxembourg"],"moulin_de_kinderdijk":["Kinderdijk windmills — Netherlands","Kinderdijk has the largest group of old windmills in the Netherlands, with 19 18th-century mills. They pumped water from the Alblasserwaard, which lies below sea level, into the rivers and out to sea. This ingenious hydraulic system kept farmland from flooding for centuries.","Nineteen windmills built around 1740 to drain a polder, lined up along the canals","Row of windmills on a polder, flat country, 15 km from a major port","Nineteen windmills on a polder, symbol of the Netherlands, near Rotterdam","The Kinderdijk windmills in the Netherlands"],"keukenhof":["Keukenhof — Lisse, Netherlands","Keukenhof, the world's largest flower garden, with 7 million bulbs planted every autumn across 32 hectares, opens for only 8 weeks a year in spring. Its theme changes every year, and its 900 varieties of tulip create a multicoloured carpet visible from the air. It welcomes 1.5 million visitors in two months.","Thirty-two hectares of flowering bulbs — 7 million tulips — open for just eight weeks in spring","Vast flower park in the heart of the bulb region, low-lying maritime country","World's largest flower park, 7 million bulbs, Lisse area","Keukenhof in Lisse, Netherlands"],"chateau_de_hohensalzburg":["Hohensalzburg Fortress — Austria","Hohensalzburg Fortress, towering over Salzburg since 1077, is one of the best-preserved medieval castles in Europe. It was never taken by an enemy in 900 years. A funicular has linked it to the city since 1892 — one of the oldest still in service in Austria.","Medieval fortress from 1077, one of the best preserved in Central Europe, overlooking a city and a river","Hilltop fortress overlooking the old town, birthplace of a great composer","Fortress overlooking Mozart's city, Salzburg","Hohensalzburg Fortress in Austria"],"palais_de_schonbrunn":["Schönbrunn Palace — Vienna, Austria","Schönbrunn Palace had 1,441 rooms, of which only 40 are open to the public. Mozart played here at the age of 6 before Empress Maria Theresa, who, it is said, took him on her lap after his performance.","Summer residence with 1,441 rooms, formal gardens and a 1775 gloriette, where a child prodigy played at six","Vast Baroque palace with French-style gardens, west of a Danube capital","Baroque palace of the Habsburgs, the “Austrian Versailles”, Vienna","Schönbrunn Palace in Vienna, Austria"],"hofburg":["Hofburg — Vienna, Austria","The Hofburg, residence of the Habsburgs for 600 years, is a labyrinth of 2,600 rooms built from the 13th to the 20th century. It houses the Habsburg crown jewels, the Lipizzaners of the Spanish Riding School and Sisi's apartments. Today the seat of the Austrian presidency, it remains open to visitors.","Imperial palace inhabited since 1279, 18 wings and 2,600 rooms, Spanish riding school and an empress's museum","Immense imperial palace in the historic heart of a Danube capital, beside a grand ring boulevard","Imperial palace of the Habsburgs in the heart of Vienna","The Hofburg in Vienna, Austria"],"mines_de_sel_de_wieliczka":["Wieliczka Salt Mine — Poland","Workers in the Wieliczka salt mines found their way through the 300 km of tunnels by carving chapels, statues and even a reproduction of the Last Supper in the salt. The Chapel of St Kinga, carved entirely out of salt, is 54 m long and 12 m high.","Salt mine worked since the 13th century, 287 km of tunnels, a chapel carved in salt 101 m underground","Thousand-year-old salt mine with an underground cathedral, 14 km from a great royal city","Salt mine with a carved chapel, near Kraków","The Wieliczka Salt Mine, Poland"],"chateau_de_malbork":["Malbork Castle — Poland","Malbork Castle, built of red brick on the banks of the Nogat, is the largest castle in the world by area. The Teutonic Knights raised it with millions of bricks fired on site and made it the seat of their order from 1309 to 1457.","World's largest brick castle (210,000 m²), seat of an order of knights from 1309 to 1457","Immense brick fortress on a riverbank, northern region, flat country in Central Europe","World's largest brick fortress, northern Poland","Malbork Castle in Poland"],"chantier_naval_de_gda_sk":["Gdańsk Shipyard — Poland","The Lenin Shipyard in Gdańsk was the birthplace of Solidarność (Solidarity), the trade union founded by Lech Wałęsa in 1980. This non-violent workers' revolution helped bring about the fall of communism in Eastern Europe. The workers' 21 demands, posted on one of the shipyard gates, are listed on UNESCO's Memory of the World Register.","Shipyard that gave birth to a great free trade union, 1980 strikes led by a future president, solidarity memorial","Historic shipyard on a river, major Baltic port, north of the country","Shipyard where Solidarność was born, Gdańsk","Gdańsk Shipyard in Poland"],"forteresse_de_narva":["Narva Castle — Estonia","Narva Castle faces Russia's Ivangorod Fortress just 150 metres away — two medieval fortresses from different countries eyeing each other across the Narva river, the border between the European Union and Russia. The town of Narva was 98% destroyed during the Second World War and completely rebuilt.","Medieval fortress facing, across a river, a rival castle in a neighbouring country","Fortress on a river border in the east of a Baltic country, facing a rival fortress","Fortress facing its rival on the opposite bank, Estonia–Russia border, Narva","Narva Castle in Estonia"],"colline_de_gediminas":["Gediminas Hill — Vilnius, Lithuania","Gediminas Hill, 48 metres above the old town, is home to the Gothic tower of Grand Duke Gediminas (14th century), a symbol of Lithuania. It was on this hill that Gediminas dreamt of a howling iron wolf — an oracle ordering him to found a city on the spot, which would become Vilnius.","14th-century brick tower, remnant of a castle founded around 1322, at the confluence of two rivers","Medieval tower on a hill overlooking a Baroque old town, capital of a Baltic country","Medieval tower, emblem of the Lithuanian capital, Vilnius","Gediminas Hill in Vilnius, Lithuania"],"chateau_de_sigulda":["Turaida Castle — Sigulda, Latvia","Turaida Castle was founded in 1214 on the orders of Albert, Bishop of Riga, on the site of a wooden fortress of the Livonians. Its tall red-brick tower dominates the wooded Gauja valley, at the heart of a museum reserve that also holds the grave of the “Rose of Turaida”, a young woman from a 17th-century legend.","Red-brick episcopal fortress founded in 1214, tall round tower overlooking a wooded valley, legend of a “rose”","Brick castle above a wooded valley nicknamed the local “Switzerland”, 50 km from a Baltic capital","Red-brick castle in the Gauja valley, near Sigulda","Turaida Castle in Sigulda, Latvia"],"maison_de_la_blackhead":["House of the Blackheads — Tallinn, Estonia","The House of the Blackheads in Tallinn was the seat of the Brotherhood of Blackheads, an association of unmarried Hanseatic merchants. Its Renaissance doorway of 1597 is one of the finest in the Baltic. The brotherhood left Estonia in 1940 with the departure of the Baltic Germans and still exists in Germany; the house, owned by the city, now serves as a concert hall.","Guildhouse of a brotherhood of unmarried merchants, 1597 Renaissance façade adorned with a Moor's head","Renaissance building on a main street of the old town, medieval Baltic capital","Renaissance building in Tallinn's old town","The House of the Blackheads in Tallinn, Estonia"],"chateau_de_predjama":["Predjama Castle — Slovenia","Predjama Castle is the largest cave castle in the world, built into a 123-metre cliff. In the 15th century, the knight Erazem Lueger held out there against a siege for a whole year. He was eventually betrayed by a servant, who signalled to the enemy the moment Erazem was using the latrine.","Renaissance fortress set into the face of a 123 m cliff, lair of a robber knight","Castle hanging in a cliff, in the karst hinterland of a small Alpine country","Largest cave castle in the world, central Slovenia","Predjama Castle, Slovenia"],"lac_de_bled":["Lake Bled — Slovenia","Lake Bled is home to Slovenia's only natural island. Tradition has it that newlyweds row out to the island, that the groom carries his bride up the 99 steps to the church, and that the wishing bell is then rung for a happy marriage.","Emerald glacial lake with an island crowned by a church (1465), medieval castle perched on a nearby cliff","Glacial lake with an island and a castle, at the foot of the Julian Alps, north-west of a small Alpine country","Emerald lake with an island church and a castle, Slovenian Alps","Lake Bled, Slovenia"],"plitvice":["Plitvice Lakes National Park — Croatia","The 16 Plitvice Lakes are linked by 92 waterfalls and are forever changing in size and position. New natural travertine dams form through the action of algae, shifting the waterfalls by a few centimetres each year — the landscape is in constant transformation.","Sixteen terraced lakes linked by 92 waterfalls, turquoise waters coloured by travertine, wooden boardwalks over the water","Cascading turquoise lakes in a national park, 130 km from a Balkan capital","Sixteen cascading lakes and 92 turquoise falls, central Croatia","The Plitvice Lakes, Croatia"],"tour_de_belfort":["Citadel and Lion of Belfort — France","The Citadel of Belfort, remodelled by Vauban in the late 17th century, overlooks the town from its rock. At its foot, the Lion of Belfort, sculpted by Bartholdi — the creator of the Statue of Liberty — is 22 metres long and 11 metres high. Assembled from blocks of pink sandstone and completed in 1880, it celebrates the town's resistance during the siege of 1870–1871.","Vauban citadel guarding a strategic gap, huge stone lion from 1880, heroic resistance of 1870","Citadel with a stone lion overlooking a town, on the threshold between two regions in the east of the country","Vauban citadel and Bartholdi's lion, Belfort","The Citadel and Lion of Belfort, France"],"cirque_de_gavarnie":["Cirque de Gavarnie — Hautes-Pyrénées, France","At 422 metres, the Grande Cascade de Gavarnie is the highest waterfall in metropolitan France. Victor Hugo, passing through in 1843, called the cirque “the Colosseum of nature”.","Rock amphitheatre 14 km around, with a 422 m waterfall and a legendary 100 m breach at the top","Glacial cirque with 1,500 m walls on a mountain border, south-west of the country","Spectacular glacial cirque in the Pyrenees, near Gavarnie","The Cirque de Gavarnie, in the Hautes-Pyrénées, France"],"aiguille_du_midi":["Aiguille du Midi — Chamonix, France","The Aiguille du Midi can be reached from Chamonix by the highest cable car in Europe, rising from 1,035 m to 3,842 m in 20 minutes. Its “Step into the Void”, a glass box suspended at 3,842 metres, lets you literally walk on air above the abyss.","3,842 m summit reached by a two-stage cable car (1955), glass walkway above the void","Alpine peak reached by cable car, in the massif of Western Europe's highest summit, Haute-Savoie","3,842 m peak reached by cable car, facing Mont Blanc, near Chamonix","The Aiguille du Midi, Chamonix, France"],"gorges_du_verdon":["Verdon Gorge — France","The Verdon owes its unreal turquoise waters to suspended limestone particles that refract blue light. The gorge, up to 700 metres deep, was not explored until 1905, by the geographer Édouard-Alfred Martel.","Canyon 25 km long and up to 700 m deep, carved by a turquoise river, with a large lake downstream","Great canyon with turquoise waters and a lake downstream, in the hinterland of Provence","Largest canyon in France, 700 m deep, Provence","The Verdon Gorge, France"],"chateau_de_carcassonne":["Cité de Carcassonne — France","Carcassonne's double ring of walls inspired the board game of the same name, which has sold more than 10 million copies worldwide. In the game, players build cities and roads by laying tiles — an idea that came to its designer after a visit to the medieval city.","Fortified city with 3 km of ramparts and 52 towers, restored in the 19th century, beside a river and a canal","Medieval city girded by double ramparts, Occitanie, beside a river and the Canal du Midi","Fortified medieval city, ramparts and towers, Carcassonne","Carcassonne Castle, France"],"palais_des_papes":["Palais des Papes — Avignon, France","The Palais des Papes in Avignon is the largest medieval Gothic building in the world, with 15,000 m² of floor space. The papacy settled there from 1309 to 1377, fleeing the unrest in Rome. At its height, the court of Avignon was richer and more influential than that of Paris.","Immense Gothic palace built from 1335 to 1364, seat of seven popes in exile from their home city","Largest medieval Gothic palace in the world, on the banks of a great river, Provence","14th-century papal palace, largest Gothic palace, Avignon","The Palais des Papes, Avignon, France"],"chateau_de_fontainebleau":["Palace of Fontainebleau — France","The residence of French sovereigns for nearly eight centuries, from Louis VII to Napoleon III, this vast château in the heart of a forest teeming with game saw the Emperor bid farewell to his guard in 1814. Napoleon called it “the true home of kings, the house of the centuries”.","Royal hunting residence since the 12th century, Renaissance gallery, some 1,500 rooms in the heart of a great forest","Royal château in the heart of a forest rich in game, 55 km south-east of the capital","Renaissance royal château in a forest, Fontainebleau","The Palace of Fontainebleau, France"],"falaises_d_etretat":["Cliffs of Étretat — Normandy, France","The cliffs of Étretat, sculpted by the sea from white chalk, were painted dozens of times by Monet. Maupassant spent part of his childhood there, and Maurice Leblanc made the Needle, some fifty metres high, the hiding place of the treasure of the kings of France in The Hollow Needle, an Arsène Lupin adventure.","Alabaster cliffs pierced by three natural arches, painted by an Impressionist master","Chalk cliffs with natural arches, Pays de Caux, English Channel coast, Normandy","Alabaster cliffs with natural arches, Étretat","The cliffs of Étretat, in Normandy, France"],"calanques_de_marseille":["Calanques of Marseille — France","The calanques of Marseille are carved into white limestone laid down more than 100 million years ago, then invaded by the sea after the last ice age. Their turquoise waters owe their colour to their purity and pale seabed. A national park since 2012, the massif is often closed in summer because of the fire risk, and some calanques can only be visited by reservation.","Twenty kilometres of limestone coastline cut into turquoise coves between two port towns","Turquoise limestone coves in a national park, Mediterranean coast near a major port","Turquoise coves between Marseille and Cassis, calanques of Provence","The Calanques of Marseille, France"],"giverny":["Giverny — Normandy, France","The garden at Giverny was created from scratch by Claude Monet, who lived there from 1883 to 1926. His water-lily pond with its Japanese bridges inspired his “Water Lilies” series of 250 paintings — his masterpiece. Monet had the pond dug, a river diverted and the exact species he wished to paint planted.","House and garden of an Impressionist painter, Japanese bridge over a pond of water lilies painted tirelessly","Home and garden of an Impressionist master, Norman countryside, at the confluence of two rivers","Monet's garden and its water-lily pond, Giverny","Monet's garden at Giverny, Normandy, France"],"palais_royal_de_bangkok":["Grand Palace — Bangkok, Thailand","The Grand Palace in Bangkok was built in 1782, when the new capital was founded. Its roofs of multicoloured glazed tiles and its many golden spires make it one of the most spectacular architectural ensembles in South-East Asia.","Grand royal palace and temple of an emerald Buddha, residence of kings since 1782, on a royal island","Grand palace and temple on a royal island by a river, capital in South-East Asia","Grand Palace and Temple of the Emerald Buddha, Bangkok","The Grand Palace, Bangkok, Thailand"],"temple_de_wat_pho":["Wat Pho — Bangkok, Thailand","Wat Pho is home to a 46 m reclining Buddha covered in gold leaf, the soles of its feet inlaid with mother-of-pearl. Regarded as Thailand's first public university, the temple remains the great school of traditional Thai massage, whose positions are carved into its walls.","Temple founded in the 16th century housing a 46 m gilded reclining Buddha, birthplace of traditional massage","Great temple of the reclining Buddha, on the royal island of a South-East Asian capital, near the grand palace","Temple of the Reclining Buddha, oldest in Bangkok","Wat Pho temple, Bangkok, Thailand"],"temple_de_doi_suthep":["Wat Phra That Doi Suthep — Chiang Mai, Thailand","According to legend, the Doi Suthep temple was founded where a white elephant carrying a relic of the Buddha stopped at the top of the mountain, trumpeted three times and then died. It is reached by a 309-step staircase flanked by two nagas, mythical serpents whose bodies form the handrail.","Temple with a golden stupa founded in 1383 at 1,073 m altitude, reached by 309 steps, sacred relic","Golden temple perched on a mountain above a northern city, national park in South-East Asia","Golden hilltop temple overlooking Chiang Mai, northern Thailand","Doi Suthep temple, Chiang Mai, Thailand"],"parc_national_de_khao_yai":["Khao Yai National Park — Thailand","Khao Yai is Thailand's first national park, founded in 1962. Its tropical forests cover 2,168 km² and are home to wild elephants, gaurs, white-handed gibbons and hornbills. The park is also renowned for its wealth of wild orchids. It has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2005.","The country's first national park (1962), wild elephants, gibbons and great waterfalls in the forest","Large forest national park where four provinces meet, in the centre of the country","National park with elephants and gibbons, central Thailand","Khao Yai National Park, Thailand"],"harrods":["Harrods — London, United Kingdom","Harrods, founded in 1849 by Charles Henry Harrod, is one of the most famous department stores in the world, with some 330 departments. Its Food Hall, decorated with Victorian tiles, is renowned for its luxury products. For a long time the store had an exotic pets department: a baby elephant and a lion cub were sold there in the 1960s.","Department store founded in 1849, seven floors and 330 departments, motto “all things, for all people, everywhere”","Famous luxury department store in the upmarket district of Knightsbridge","Famous luxury department store in Knightsbridge, London","Harrods department store in London, United Kingdom"],"musee_national_du_palais":["National Palace Museum — Taipei, Taiwan","The National Palace Museum in Taipei holds one of the largest collections of Chinese imperial art in the world — nearly 700,000 pieces, most of them taken from Beijing by the Nationalist government in 1948–1949 when it withdrew to Taiwan. The works are shown in rotation: it would take decades to see them all on display.","Nearly 700,000 imperial treasures, including a jade cabbage and a meat-shaped stone, spirited out of a palace in 1948","Museum of imperial treasures in the north of a large island capital in East Asia","Largest collection of Chinese imperial art in the world, Taipei","The National Palace Museum, Taipei, Taiwan"],"taipei_101":["Taipei 101 — Taiwan","Taipei 101 has the largest tuned mass damper in the world — a 660-tonne steel sphere suspended from the 92nd floor. Visible from an observation deck, it sways by 1.5 metres to absorb the vibrations from typhoons and earthquakes.","508 m skyscraper (2004) with a huge 660-tonne anti-earthquake pendulum, long the tallest in the world","Very tall skyscraper in the financial district of an island capital in East Asia","508 m skyscraper, symbol of Taipei","Taipei 101, Taipei, Taiwan"],"gyeongbokgung":["Gyeongbokgung — Seoul, South Korea","Built in 1395 at the start of the Joseon dynasty, Gyeongbokgung Palace was burned down during the Japanese invasions of 1592 and then rebuilt in the 19th century. The royal changing of the guard ceremony, re-enacted in period costume in front of Gwanghwamun Gate, is one of the most photographed scenes in Seoul.","Great royal palace from 1395 with 7,700 rooms, monumental gate, at the foot of a wooded mountain","Main royal palace in the historic heart of an Asian capital, backed by a mountain","Main royal palace, changing of the guard, Seoul","Gyeongbokgung, Seoul, South Korea"],"tour_n_seoul":["N Seoul Tower — South Korea","N Seoul Tower, at 480 metres above sea level on Namsan mountain, offers a 360° view over the Korean capital. It is covered in millions of padlocks left by lovers — a tradition so massive that the railings had to be reinforced to bear the weight.","236 m telecommunications tower perched on an urban mountain, railings covered in love locks","Observation tower on top of a mountain in the centre of a great Asian metropolis","Observation tower and love locks, Namsan mountain, Seoul","N Seoul Tower, South Korea"],"dmz":["Korean Demilitarized Zone — Panmunjom, Korea","The Korean Demilitarized Zone, about 4 km wide, has been almost entirely closed to human activity since the 1953 armistice. Paradoxically, it has become a refuge for wildlife: endangered red-crowned cranes, water deer and Asian black bears live there undisturbed; there is even talk of tigers, though unconfirmed.","1953 armistice line and blue negotiation huts, a heavily guarded border between two states","Militarised border zone on the 38th parallel, 60 km north of an Asian capital","Demilitarised zone between the two Koreas, Panmunjom","The DMZ (Demilitarized Zone) at Panmunjom, Korea"],"chateau_de_matsumoto":["Matsumoto Castle — Japan","Matsumoto Castle is one of the few original 16th-century Japanese castles still standing — most were rebuilt in concrete after the war. Its black walls have earned it the nickname “Crow Castle”. Its internal staircases are so steep that they reach a 61° incline.","Black-walled castle nicknamed “the Crow”, built from 1592 to 1614, original keep intact","Black castle with an original keep, on a plain ringed by mountains, central region of an archipelago","Black “Crow” castle, one of the finest original keeps, Matsumoto","Matsumoto Castle, Japan"],"sanctuaire_fushimi_inari":["Fushimi Inari-taisha — Kyoto, Japan","Fushimi Inari Shrine is said to have around 10,000 vermilion torii gates donated by businesses and individuals seeking the favour of Inari, deity of rice and prosperity. These gateways form tunnels stretching 4 km up the mountainside. The stone foxes, Inari's messengers, hold a key, a sheaf or a jewel in their mouths.","Some 10,000 vermilion gateways forming 4 km of tunnels up to the summit of a sacred rice hill","Shinto shrine with thousands of red gateways, on a hillside south of a former capital","Tunnels of thousands of vermilion gateways, rice shrine, Kyoto","Fushimi Inari Shrine, Kyoto, Japan"],"chateau_d_osaka":["Osaka Castle — Japan","Osaka Castle was built by Toyotomi Hideyoshi in 1583 on the remains of a Buddhist temple. After two fires and rebuildings, the current tower dates from 1931. The cherry trees of its Nishinomaru Garden draw hundreds of thousands of visitors every spring for hanami.","Castle with a five-storey keep built in 1583 by a unifier, ringed by moats, cherry blossom in spring","Castle with a green-and-gold keep in a large park, central district of a merchant metropolis","Iconic castle with a golden keep, Osaka","Osaka Castle, Japan"],"himeji_jo":["Himeji Castle — Japan","Himeji Castle, nicknamed “the White Heron” for its immaculate colour, is the best preserved of Japan's medieval castles. Its labyrinthine layout, designed to confuse invaders, includes dead-end corridors, loopholes and deliberately uneven stones to unsettle the enemy.","Six-storey white castle nicknamed “the Heron”, built from 1601 to 1609, 83 original buildings","Immaculate white castle, the best preserved in the country, in a city of the Kansai region","White Heron castle, the best preserved in Japan, Himeji","Himeji Castle, Japan"],"lac_de_kawaguchi":["Lake Kawaguchi — Japan","Lake Kawaguchi is one of the Fuji Five Lakes, formed by the volcano's lava flows. On clear days, the reflection of Mount Fuji in its waters creates one of Japan's most iconic images. Its shores, lined with cherry trees in spring and red maples in autumn, draw photographers from all over the world.","Lake offering the finest reflection of a sacred volcano, shores of cherry trees in spring and red maples in autumn","Lake reflecting a famous volcano, one of the five lakes at its foot, Yamanashi Prefecture","Lake reflecting Mount Fuji, and a pagoda, Yamanashi region","Lake Kawaguchi, Japan"],"shanghai":["Shanghai — China (The Bund)","Shanghai's Bund is a 1.5 km boulevard lined with 52 Art Deco and neoclassical buildings put up in the 1930s by the foreign concessions. Facing it, across the Huangpu River, rise the ultramodern skyscrapers of Pudong — a striking dialogue between two Shanghais.","Riverfront lined with 1.5 km of neoclassical and Art Deco buildings from the 1920s, facing a futuristic tower","Colonial promenade along a river, facing a district of ultramodern skyscrapers","Colonial promenade facing the Pudong skyscrapers, China's largest city, Shanghai","The Bund, Shanghai, China"],"tour_de_perle_d_orient":["Oriental Pearl Tower — Shanghai, China","The Oriental Pearl Tower (468 m, 1994) was the tallest structure in China until 2007. Its pink spheres, stacked at around 90 m, 263 m and 350 m, evoke pearls falling onto a jade plate. Its observation decks look out over one of the densest skylines in the world.","468 m television tower (1994) with pink spheres threaded onto three columns, on a riverbank","TV tower with pink spheres, financial district on a futuristic riverbank, the country's largest city","Iconic TV tower of Pudong, Shanghai","The Oriental Pearl Tower in Shanghai, China"],"guilin":["Guilin — Guangxi, China","Guilin's karst peaks were formed by limestone dissolving over 300 million years. They have inspired generations of Chinese painters and appear on the 20-yuan note. The cormorant fishermen on the Li River, who teach their birds to catch fish without swallowing them, keep alive a 1,000-year-old tradition.","Limestone peaks rising from a river, cormorant fishermen, a landscape printed on a banknote","Landscape of karst peaks along a river, autonomous region in southern China","Karst peaks on the Li River, printed on the 20-yuan note, Guilin area","Guilin, in Guangxi, China"],"mausole_de_l_armee_de_terre_cuite":["Mausoleum of the Terracotta Army — Xi'an, China","The Terracotta Army of Xi'an numbers 8,000 soldiers, 130 chariots and 670 horses, buried to protect Emperor Qin Shi Huang in the afterlife. Every soldier has a unique face. The emperor's central tomb has not yet been opened — China is waiting until it has the technology to preserve it without damage.","Eight thousand life-size terracotta soldiers and horses guarding an imperial tomb, unearthed in 1974","Army of terracotta statues guarding an ancient mausoleum, central province, near a former capital","Terracotta Army of the first emperor, Xi'an","The Mausoleum of the Terracotta Army in Xi'an, China"],"kowloon":["Kowloon Walled City — Hong Kong","The former Kowloon Walled City, demolished in 1994, was the most densely populated place in human history, with 35,000 inhabitants on 0.03 km². Its 300 interlocking buildings formed an almost anarchic 14-storey labyrinth without natural light. A park was built on its site.","Anarchic enclave of 300 interlocking buildings on 2.6 hectares, the highest population density ever known, demolished in 1994","Former overcrowded, lawless urban enclave in a British colony, near a former airport, now a park","Former ultra-dense walled city replaced by a park, Kowloon, Hong Kong","Kowloon Walled City, Hong Kong"],"colline_de_batu":["Batu Caves — Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia","The Batu Caves are a Hindu shrine hollowed out of a 400-million-year-old limestone massif. A staircase of 272 steps leads to the main cave, adorned with a 42.7-metre golden statue of Murugan. Every year, during the Thaipusam festival, hundreds of thousands of pilgrims climb the steps carrying kavadis pierced into their skin.","Limestone caves forming a Tamil shrine, huge 43 m golden statue at the foot of 272 multicoloured steps","Hindu temple in a limestone cave, a hill in the northern suburbs of a capital on the Malay Peninsula","Sacred caves and giant statue of Murugan, near Kuala Lumpur","Batu Hill, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia"],"tours_petronas":["Petronas Towers — Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia","The Petronas Towers were the tallest in the world from 1998 to 2004. Designed by César Pelli, each has an 8-pointed star floor plan formed by two interlocking squares, a motif of Islamic art. The skybridge on the 41st and 42nd floors is not rigidly fixed to the towers: it rests on sliding bearings that allow it to follow their sway.","452 m twin towers (1998) linked halfway up by a skybridge, façade with Islamic geometric patterns","Twin steel skyscrapers in the business district of a tropical metropolis","Petronas Twin Towers, long the tallest in the world, Kuala Lumpur","The Petronas Towers, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia"],"mekong":["Mekong — Laos (Luang Prabang)","Luang Prabang, a former royal capital listed by UNESCO, is famous for its many monasteries. Every dawn, hundreds of saffron-robed monks walk the streets in silence to receive the offerings of the faithful — the “tak bat”. This age-old ritual takes place every morning at around 5.30 am, whatever the season.","Temple town at the confluence of two rivers, 33 monasteries and a morning alms procession for the monks","Former Buddhist royal capital on a great river, north of a landlocked country in South-East Asia","Former royal capital with 33 temples, confluence of two rivers, northern Laos","The Mekong, Laos (Luang Prabang)"],"katmandou":["Kathmandu — Nepal (Boudhanath)","The Boudhanath stupa is the largest in South Asia and the spiritual heart of the Tibetan community in exile in Nepal. Its 13 golden rings symbolise the stages towards Buddhist enlightenment. Surrounded by 50 Tibetan monasteries, it remains one of the most active pilgrimage sites of Vajrayana Buddhism.","Great 36 m Tibetan Buddhist stupa with painted eyes, one of the largest on the subcontinent, a pilgrimage site","Immense stupa with the eyes of the Buddha, 11 km from the centre of a Himalayan capital","Largest stupa in South Asia, Tibetan hub, capital of Nepal","The Boudhanath stupa in Kathmandu, Nepal"],"everest_base_camp":["Everest Base Camp — Nepal","Thousands of tonnes of rubbish have been left on the slopes of Everest by expeditions since 1953. Nepal now requires every climber to bring down at least 8 kg of waste — on top of their own equipment.","Base camp at 5,364 m at the foot of the world's highest peak, chaotic glacier, 12-day trek from an airstrip","Trekking camp at the foot of the world's highest peak, high Himalayan range","Base camp of the world's highest mountain, Nepalese Himalaya","Everest Base Camp, Nepal"],"jaipur":["Jaipur — India (Hawa Mahal)","The Hawa Mahal, the “Palace of the Winds”, was built in 1799 with 953 small windows screened with jalis (stone latticework), allowing the women of the court to watch street life without being seen. Its very shallow five-storey pink sandstone façade evokes a beehive or Krishna's crown.","Screen façade with 953 honeycomb windows over five storeys, built in 1799 to watch the street unseen","Pink palace-façade in the old town of a city nicknamed “the Pink City”, northern Rajasthan","Palace of 953 windows, Pink City of Rajasthan, Jaipur","The Hawa Mahal in Jaipur, India"],"kerala":["Athirappilly Falls — Kerala, India","The Athirappilly Falls, on the Chalakudy River, drop about 25 metres across a width of some hundred metres, deep in the forest of the Western Ghats. Nicknamed “the Niagara of India”, they are the largest waterfalls in Kerala. They have served as a backdrop for many films, including the Baahubali saga.","Wide curtain of water about 25 m high deep in the tropical forest, nicknamed its country's “Niagara”, backdrop for an epic saga","Great waterfalls of a river in the forest of the Western Ghats, in the south-west of a South Asian country","Largest waterfalls in Kerala, Western Ghats forest, near Thrissur","The Athirappilly Falls in Kerala, India"],"hampi":["Hampi — Karnataka, India","Hampi's central market, a colonnaded street 800 metres long, was once lined with shops selling silks, spices and precious stones. High-ranking women wore jewels so heavy that they had to be supported by servants in order to walk.","Ruins of an imperial capital (1336–1646), 500 monuments scattered among a chaos of boulders, ancient temple","Vast temple site amid boulders, on a riverbank, plateau of southern India","Ruins of the capital of the Vijayanagara Empire, boulder landscape, Hampi","Hampi, in Karnataka, India"],"ayutthaya":["Ayutthaya — Thailand","Ayutthaya was destroyed by the Burmese in 1767 after a 14-month siege. The city, home to a million people, was burnt to the ground in a week. The Buddha head entwined in the roots of a banyan fig tree is the most photographed symbol of Thailand.","Ruined former capital of a kingdom (1350–1767), Buddha head gripped by the roots of a tree","Ruins of a royal capital on an island where three rivers meet, centre of a South-East Asian country","Buddhist ruins of the former Siamese capital, central Thailand","Ayutthaya, former capital of Siam, Thailand"],"carthage":["Carthage — Tunisia","Carthage was Rome's rival for two centuries. After its final destruction in 146 BC, Rome is said to have sown salt on its ruins to make them barren — this story is probably a 19th-century myth, but the symbol endures.","Ruins of a Phoenician city founded around 814 BC, great rival of Rome razed in 146, seaside baths","Ancient archaeological site on a Mediterranean gulf, northern suburbs of a North African capital","Ruins of the Punic city that rivalled Rome, on the Gulf of Tunis","Carthage, near Tunis, Tunisia"],"amphitheatre_d_el_djem":["Amphitheatre of El Jem — Tunisia","The amphitheatre of El Jem is the third-largest Roman amphitheatre in the world, after the Colosseum and the one at Capua. Built in the 3rd century AD in Roman Africa, it could hold 35,000 spectators. It served as a fortress in the 7th century during the Berber resistance to the Arab conquest.","35,000-seat Roman amphitheatre built around 238, third largest in the ancient world, the best preserved on its continent","Roman colosseum standing alone in the middle of a plain, centre of the country, 195 km from the capital","Roman amphitheatre, the most impressive in Africa, El Jem","The Amphitheatre of El Jem, Tunisia"],"djemaa_el_fna":["Jemaa el-Fnaa — Marrakesh, Morocco","Marrakesh's Jemaa el-Fnaa square is listed by UNESCO as Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity — a first for a public space. Every evening it turns into a gigantic open-air restaurant with dozens of food stalls.","Great square with a funereal name, a living show of storytellers, snake charmers and henna tattooists","Lively main square of an imperial medina, between a great Almohad minaret and the souks","Great square of storytellers and traders, Marrakesh","Jemaa el-Fnaa square in Marrakesh, Morocco"],"kasbah_d_ait_benhaddou":["Aït Benhaddou — Morocco","Aït Benhaddou has served as a set for more than 20 films and series, including Gladiator, Game of Thrones and Lawrence of Arabia. Only five families still live within the walls of the ksar, the rest of the population having moved to the modern village.","Fortified village of red rammed earth, a caravan stop, set for many blockbuster films and series","Red-earth ksar on a south-facing slope, valley at the gateway to the desert, Ouarzazate region","Rammed-earth kasbah and film set, Aït Benhaddou, southern Morocco","The ksar of Aït Benhaddou in Morocco"],"riviere_okavango":["Okavango River — Botswana","The Okavango Delta is a unique geographical anomaly: it is one of the only rivers in the world that does not flow into the sea but dies in the Kalahari Desert. Every year its floods create an ephemeral 15,000 km² oasis that attracts extraordinary wildlife before gradually evaporating.","River that vanishes into the sand in a 15,000 km² inland delta, a refuge for elephants and hippos","Inland delta with no outlet to the sea, north-west of a landlocked country in southern Africa","Oasis delta in the Kalahari Desert, northern Botswana","The Okavango River, Botswana"],"chutes_de_tisisat":["Blue Nile Falls — Ethiopia","The name of the Tis Issat Falls, on the Blue Nile, means “smoke of fire” in Amharic. They lie some thirty kilometres downstream of Lake Tana, the outlet of the Blue Nile, which supplies most of the Nile's waters. In the rainy season, the curtain of water can reach 400 metres in width, but an upstream hydroelectric dam now greatly reduces their flow.","Falls of the Blue Nile downstream of a great lake, up to 400 m wide, flow reduced by a modern dam","Powerful falls of the Blue Nile, 30 km from a lakeside town, highlands of East Africa","Blue Nile Falls, near Bahir Dar, Ethiopia","The Tis Issat Falls, Ethiopia"],"zanzibar":["Zanzibar — Tanzania","Zanzibar is the birthplace of Freddie Mercury, the singer of Queen, born in Stone Town in 1946. The old town, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, blends Swahili, Arab, Indian and European influences in a maze of alleys with carved doors. Mercury's family left the island in 1964, after the revolution, to settle in England.","Old Swahili town with Arab alleyways and carved doors, former slave market, spice island","Old Swahili city on a large island in the western Indian Ocean, a spice archipelago","UNESCO-listed old Swahili city, spice island, Zanzibar","Stone Town, on the island of Zanzibar, Tanzania"],"chutes_de_murchison":["Murchison Falls — Uganda","Murchison Falls are the most powerful in the world in terms of flow per unit area. The Victoria Nile is forced through a cleft just 7 metres wide — generating extraordinary hydraulic pressure that hurls the water dozens of metres.","The most powerful falls in the world, a great river forced into a gorge 7 m wide","Falls on a very long river in a national park, north of an East African country","The most powerful Nile falls in the world, northern Uganda","Murchison Falls, Uganda"],"chutes_de_ruacana":["Ruacana Falls — Namibia/Angola","The Ruacana Falls on the Kunene River, on the Angola–Namibia border, are among the widest in Africa at 700 metres across. In the rainy season their flow is so powerful that people in neighbouring villages can hear it several kilometres away. A hydroelectric power station was built there in the 1970s.","Falls 120 m high and 700 m wide on a border river, between canyon and arid savannah","Falls on a border river, arid north-western region, between two southern African countries","Kunene falls on the Namibia–Angola border, Kaokoland","Ruacana Falls, Namibia/Angola"],"washington_d_c":["Washington, D.C. — USA (Capitol)","The United States Capitol, where Congress has sat since 1800, is crowned by a cast-iron dome weighing nearly 4,000 tonnes, completed in 1866. Construction carried on during the Civil War — Abraham Lincoln insisted on it, seeing the completion of the dome as a symbol of the Union's survival. The Rotunda houses eight huge historical paintings.","Seat of the federal parliament, 88 m white cast-iron dome completed in 1866, on a hill","Domed seat of Congress, on a hill at the east end of a great esplanade, federal capital","Seat of the US Congress, hill overlooking Washington, D.C.","The Capitol in Washington, D.C., United States"],"golden_gate":["Golden Gate Bridge — San Francisco, USA","The Golden Gate's engineers devised the first modern safety equipment for working at height: hard hats, safety goggles, sun cream. A safety net beneath the bridge saved 19 lives — the survivors formed the “Halfway to Hell Club”.","1937 suspension bridge with a 1,280 m span, 227 m orange towers often shrouded in fog","Large orange suspension bridge at the mouth of a bay, Northern California coast","Orange suspension bridge, emblem of San Francisco","The Golden Gate Bridge in San Francisco, USA"],"alcatraz":["Alcatraz — San Francisco, USA","Al Capone, one of Alcatraz's most famous inmates, played in a jazz band there called “The Rock Islanders”. The island was chosen because its currents and cold waters made any attempt to escape by swimming all but suicidal.","Federal island prison (1934–1963) from which no one officially escaped, cell of a famous gangster","Fortified island turned prison in the middle of a large Californian bay","Al Capone's federal island prison, San Francisco Bay","Alcatraz Island, in San Francisco Bay, USA"],"parc_national_de_zion":["Zion National Park — Utah, USA","Zion Canyon was carved by the Virgin River into Navajo sandstone, ancient petrified dunes some 190 million years old. Its “Narrows”, a gorge several hundred metres deep and in places only a few metres wide, are explored by wading through the water. The Angels Landing hike, with its chains bolted into the cliff, has required a permit drawn by lottery since 2022.","Red sandstone canyon with 600 m walls, the dizzying ridge of Angels Landing, the deep-cut river of the Narrows","Park of red sandstone canyons, Colorado Plateau, 260 km north-east of Las Vegas","Red sandstone canyons and Angels Landing, national park in southern Utah","Zion National Park in Utah, USA"],"las_vegas_strip":["Las Vegas Strip — Nevada, USA","The Las Vegas Strip lines up some of the largest hotel-casinos in the world along nearly 7 km. The Bellagio fountains, with more than 1,200 jets, some of which shoot water 140 metres high, put on a free water ballet. Despite its thousands of swimming pools, the city recycles almost all the water used indoors.","A 6.7 km avenue lined with giant casino hotels and neon, born of a first resort in 1941","Avenue of giant casinos in the middle of the Mojave Desert, southern Nevada","Avenue of casinos and themed hotels, Mojave Desert, Las Vegas","The Las Vegas Strip, in Nevada, USA"],"everglades":["Everglades — Florida, USA","The Everglades are not a swamp but an immense, flat river flowing in slow motion — barely 0.3 km/h — from Lake Okeechobee to the Gulf of Mexico. This 160 km “River of Grass” is the only place in the world where alligators and crocodiles naturally coexist.","Largest subtropical park on the continent, the only place where alligators and crocodiles live side by side, marshes and mangroves","Vast park of subtropical marshes at the southern tip of Florida","Subtropical park where alligators and crocodiles coexist, southern Florida","The Everglades, in Florida, USA"],"tenochtitlan":["Tenochtitlan — Mexico City, Mexico","Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital founded in 1325 on an island in the middle of Lake Texcoco, was the largest city in pre-Columbian America. It was destroyed by Cortés in 1521 and the lake was drained. Mexico City was built directly on top — today's Zócalo sits exactly on the old Aztec central square.","Remains of a great Aztec temple with a twin shrine, excavated since 1978 in the heart of a megacity","Ruins of the great Aztec temple in the historic centre, beneath a highland megacity","Great Aztec Temple in the heart of the old capital, Mexico City","Tenochtitlan, beneath present-day Mexico City, Mexico"],"oaxaca":["Monte Albán — Oaxaca, Mexico","Monte Albán was the capital of the Zapotecs for more than a thousand years. Its builders levelled the top of a mountain, some 400 metres above the Oaxaca Valley, to lay out a vast plaza lined with pyramids, palaces and a ball court. It is home to the “Danzantes”, among the oldest reliefs in Mesoamerica, and to Tomb 7, famous for its treasure of Mixtec goldwork.","Zapotec capital on a levelled mountaintop, occupied from the 5th century BC to the 15th century, platforms and tombs","Zapotec site on a mountain plateau overlooking a valley, 9 km from a colonial city, south of the country","Hilltop Zapotec city overlooking a valley, near Oaxaca","Monte Albán, near Oaxaca, Mexico"],"copper_canyon":["Copper Canyon — Chihuahua, Mexico","Copper Canyon is crossed by the Chepe train, considered one of the most beautiful railway journeys in the world. It passes through 86 tunnels and over 37 bridges as it winds through a canyon deeper than the Grand Canyon — a 16-hour journey between the mountains and the sea.","A set of six canyons deeper than the Grand Canyon (1,870 m), a people of long-distance runners, a legendary train","Network of canyons deeper than the Grand Canyon, western mountain range, north of the country","Canyons deeper than the Grand Canyon, Sierra Madre, northern Mexico","Copper Canyon (Barranca del Cobre), Chihuahua State, Mexico"],"cartagena":["Cartagena — Colombia","The walls of Cartagena de Indias were built after the pirate Francis Drake sacked the city in 1586. Ironically, these Spanish fortifications are now among the best preserved in the Americas and are UNESCO-listed.","Colonial city founded in 1533, 11 km of ramparts and a massive fort from 1657, former Caribbean slave port","Fortified colonial old town on the Caribbean Sea, north coast of South America","Fortified colonial city of Cartagena de Indias, Caribbean coast of Colombia","Cartagena de Indias, Colombia"],"los_roques":["Los Roques — Venezuela","Los Roques is an archipelago of 350 coral islands in the Caribbean Sea, entirely a national park since 1972. Its shallow, unreally blue lagoons are among the clearest in the Caribbean. The white sand beaches cannot be reached by car — the whole archipelago is explored on foot or by boat.","Archipelago of 350 coral islets in a national park, turquoise waters at 30°, a kitesurfing paradise","Coral archipelago with turquoise waters, Caribbean Sea, 168 km off a mainland coast","Archipelago of 350 turquoise coral islets, Caribbean north of Caracas","The Los Roques archipelago, Venezuela"],"choquequirao":["Choquequirao — Peru","Choquequirao, “cradle of gold” in Quechua, is often described as the sacred sister of Machu Picchu. Reachable only after a two-day trek, it receives just a handful of visitors a day, compared with several thousand at Machu Picchu, even though only part of the site has been cleared. A cable car project could turn it into Peru's next mass-tourism destination.","Inca city at 3,085 m, terraces decorated with white stone llamas, reachable only after a two-day trek","Inca city perched above an Andean canyon, reachable only on foot, Cuzco region","Inca city in the clouds, the forgotten sister of Machu Picchu, Peruvian Andes","Choquequirao, Peru"],"sucre":["Sucre — Bolivia","Sucre, the constitutional capital of Bolivia, is nicknamed “the white city” for its immaculate colonial façades. It was in its Casa de la Libertad that the country's act of independence was signed on 6 August 1825; the new nation took the name of Simón Bolívar.","“White city” at 2,810 m, constitutional capital, house where independence was signed in 1825","White colonial city in an Andean valley, constitutional capital of a landlocked country","White city, constitutional capital of Bolivia, Sucre","Sucre, constitutional capital of Bolivia"],"trinidad":["Trinidad — Cuba","Trinidad, Cuba, is a colonial town frozen in time, where cars are a rare sight in the cobbled streets of the centre. Its ochre and pastel houses and its son cubano musicians make it one of the most authentic places in the Caribbean. It was listed by UNESCO in 1988 together with the neighbouring sugar valley, the Valle de los Ingenios.","Colonial town frozen in time, founded in 1514, cobbled square and colourful houses, former sugar capital, valley of the mills","Listed colonial town frozen in time, central province of a Caribbean island","Colonial gem frozen in time, former sugar capital, Trinidad","The colonial town of Trinidad, Cuba"],"la_havane":["Havana — Cuba","Havana has the largest collection of baroque colonial architecture in the Americas. Its old American cars from the 1950s, kept going because of the embargo, are still on the road — maintained by Cuban mechanics who make the missing parts themselves. The Malecón, the seafront promenade, is the city's open-air living room.","Colonial old town and an 8 km sea wall, a 1929 capitol, a famous writer's bar, 1950s cars","Colonial capital on a large bay of a Caribbean island, facing the strait towards the mainland","Colonial capital, baroque old town and seafront, Havana","Havana, capital of Cuba"],"parc_de_kakadu":["Kakadu National Park — Northern Territory, Australia","Kakadu National Park holds Aboriginal rock paintings 20,000 years old that form a living almanac. Today's artists still add paintings to the rock walls, keeping up a tradition unbroken since prehistoric times.","Immense park of nearly 20,000 km², age-old rock paintings, saltwater crocodiles and great waterfalls","Vast park of wetlands and rock art, northern tropics, 250 km from a coastal city","Park of rock paintings and crocodiles, northern Australia","Kakadu National Park, in the Northern Territory, Australia"],"lake_hillier":["Lake Hillier — Western Australia","Lake Hillier in Western Australia is naturally bubblegum pink — a colour that doesn't fade even when you scoop the water into a glass. Its hue comes from a combination of halophilic bacteria, Dunaliella salina algae and salt. The lake is ringed by eucalyptus trees and white sand.","Intensely pink lake coloured by an alga and bacteria, fringed by forest, on a small coastal island","Bright pink lake on an island in a coastal archipelago, southern coast of a southern continent","Iconic pink lake, island off south-western Australia","Lake Hillier, in Western Australia"],"aoraki_mount_cook":["Aoraki / Mount Cook — New Zealand","Aoraki Mount Cook, at 3,724 metres, is New Zealand's highest peak. Sir Edmund Hillary, the first man to climb Everest in 1953, trained there. Its national park lies within a “Dark Sky Reserve” — one of the 20 finest stargazing areas in the world.","The country's highest peak (3,724 m), a long glacier at its foot, training ground of an Everest conqueror","The country's highest peak and its glacier, central alpine range of the South Island","Highest mountain in New Zealand, Southern Alps, South Island","Aoraki Mount Cook, New Zealand"],"hobbiton":["Hobbiton — Waikato, New Zealand","Hobbiton was built in 1999 on a family farm near Matamata, in the Waikato region, for the filming of The Lord of the Rings. Peter Jackson had it rebuilt in durable materials for The Hobbit (2012), turning it into a permanent attraction. Its 44 hobbit holes with round doors are built at different scales to play with the characters' sizes.","Permanent set of the hobbits' village from a fantasy trilogy, 44 round houses on a rolling farm","Film set of a fantasy village on a rolling farm, North Island, 170 km from a major city","Shire set rebuilt in permanent materials, Matamata farm, North Island","Hobbiton village in Waikato, New Zealand"],"vallee_de_franz_josef":["Franz Josef Glacier — New Zealand","The Franz Josef Glacier is among the fastest-moving in the world, advancing up to several metres a day in its steepest sections. Its tongue of ice descends to a few hundred metres above sea level, into the heart of a temperate rainforest: a very rare encounter. Helicopters drop visitors onto its crevasse-riddled surface.","Two rare temperate glaciers descending all the way to the rainforest, ice tongues 12 and 13 km long","Glaciers plunging towards temperate rainforest, west coast of a large southern island, national park","Franz Josef and Fox glaciers, west coast of the South Island","The Franz Josef Valley in New Zealand"],"tonga":["Tonga — South Pacific","Tonga is the last Polynesian kingdom still in existence and the only Pacific nation never to have been formally colonised. From July to October, humpback whales come to breed there, and it is one of the few countries where you can swim with them, accompanied by licensed guides.","The only Polynesian monarchy never colonised, 170 islands, swimming with humpback whales in season","Main island of a Polynesian kingdom in the South Pacific, 2,000 km from a large continent","Last Polynesian kingdom, swimming with whales, South Pacific","The Kingdom of Tonga, South Pacific"],"ile_de_moorea":["Moorea — French Polynesia","Moorea, 17 km from Tahiti, is the remnant of an ancient volcano whose collapse left two deep bays, Cook's Bay and Opunohu Bay, which carve its silhouette into a heart shape. Cook's Bay is named after the navigator, even though he actually anchored in neighbouring Opunohu Bay in 1777. Its jagged peaks and turquoise lagoon make it one of the most spectacular islands in the Pacific.","Basalt sister island of a main island, jagged volcanic peaks and two deep bays, sleeping sharks","Volcanic island with jagged peaks, 17 km from a main island, Society Islands","Tahiti's sister island with volcanic peaks, French Polynesia","Moorea Island in French Polynesia"],"atoll_de_bikini":["Bikini Atoll — Marshall Islands","Bikini Atoll was the site of 23 American nuclear tests between 1946 and 1958, including the “Castle Bravo” H-bomb, about 1,000 times more powerful than the Hiroshima bomb. Its inhabitants, relocated in 1946, attempted to return in the 1970s before being evacuated again in 1978 because of radioactivity. The ships sunk during the tests, including the aircraft carrier USS Saratoga, make it a legendary diving site.","Atoll of 23 nuclear tests (1946–1958), wrecks of ships sunk in the lagoon, still uninhabitable","Abandoned nuclear test atoll, western archipelago of Micronesia, North Pacific","Atoll of the American nuclear tests, radioactive wrecks, Marshall Islands","Bikini Atoll, Marshall Islands (Pacific)"],"iles_fidji":["Fiji — South Pacific","Fiji has 333 islands, about 110 of them inhabited. Its kava culture — a drink made from the root of a pepper plant — is central to social life, and visitors are often welcomed with a kava ceremony. Mineral water drawn from an aquifer on Viti Levu is exported all over the world.","Archipelago of 333 islands, ceremonial dances, a ritual pepper-plant drink, diving with sharks","Tropical archipelago of eastern Melanesia, South Pacific, nearly 3,000 km from a large continent","Archipelago of 333 Melanesian islands, kava ceremonies, South Pacific","The Fiji Islands, South Pacific"],"cathedrale_notre_dame_de_chartres":["Chartres Cathedral — France","Chartres Cathedral is one of the few to have kept its original medieval stained glass. During the Second World War, the people of Chartres took down all 176 windows and buried them in crates in cellars to protect them from the bombing.","Gothic masterpiece built mostly from 1194 to 1220, 176 medieval stained-glass windows including a great rose window, grain-growing plain","Gothic cathedral with famous stained glass in the heart of a town in the Beauce","Gothic cathedral with medieval stained glass, Chartres","Chartres Cathedral (Notre-Dame), France"],"abbaye_du_mont_cassin":["Monte Cassino — Italy","Founded around 529 by Saint Benedict, who wrote his Rule there, Monte Cassino Abbey is the cradle of the Benedictine order. In February 1944, Allied bombers reduced it to ruins, wrongly believing it was occupied by the Germans; after the war it was rebuilt exactly as it had been.","Benedictine abbey founded in 529, destroyed and rebuilt four times, prize of a bloody battle in 1944","Abbey on a mountaintop, southern province of a Mediterranean peninsula, Lazio","Benedictine mother abbey, scene of a ferocious battle in 1944, Monte Cassino","Monte Cassino Abbey, Italy"],"arene_de_verone":["Verona Arena — Italy","The Verona Arena, a 1st-century Roman amphitheatre, is one of the best preserved in the world: although the earthquake of 1117 brought down almost all of its outer ring, of which only a four-arch section survives, its inner tiers of seating remained intact. Since 1913 it has hosted a famous open-air opera festival every summer, where the audience traditionally lights candles at nightfall.","1st-century Roman amphitheatre with 30,000 seats, one of the best preserved, open-air opera in summer","Roman arena in the heart of a city of art, central square, Veneto region","Roman amphitheatre hosting open-air opera, Verona","The Verona Arena, Italy"],"piazza_del_campo":["Piazza del Campo — Siena, Italy","Siena's shell-shaped Piazza del Campo slopes gently down towards the Palazzo Pubblico, which drains away rainwater; its paving is divided into nine sections in tribute to the Government of the Nine. The Palio, a horse race of about 90 seconds around the square, is run there on 2 July and 16 August. Ten of the 17 contrade (districts) take part in each race, against a backdrop of centuries-old rivalries.","Sloping shell-shaped square, 14th-century town hall and 102 m tower, twice-yearly horse race","Large shell-shaped medieval square in the heart of a Tuscan city, famous horse race","Italy's most beautiful medieval square, the Palio, Siena","Piazza del Campo in Siena, Italy"],"cathedrale_de_seville":["Seville Cathedral — Spain","Seville Cathedral is the largest Gothic church in the world. It was built on the Almohad Great Mosque, keeping its minaret, which was turned into a bell tower — the Giralda. Christopher Columbus lies there in a tomb carried by four heralds representing the kingdoms of Castile, León, Aragon and Navarre.","Largest Gothic cathedral in the world, built on a mosque, minaret turned bell tower, tomb of an explorer","Huge Gothic cathedral and its former minaret, old town in Andalusia, on a river","Largest Gothic cathedral in the world and its Giralda, Seville","Seville Cathedral, Spain"],"palais_royal_de_madrid":["Royal Palace of Madrid — Spain","The Royal Palace of Madrid, completed in 1764, is the largest royal palace in Western Europe, with 3,418 rooms. It houses the world's only complete quartet of decorated Stradivarius instruments — two violins, a viola and a cello by the legendary luthier, still played in concerts.","Royal palace with 3,418 rooms built from 1738 to 1764, official but unoccupied residence, facing an opera house","Huge royal palace on a central square of a capital, facing the opera house and gardens","Largest royal palace in Western Europe, Madrid","The Royal Palace of Madrid, Spain"],"tour_des_belem":["Livraria Lello — Porto, Portugal","Porto's Livraria Lello, founded in 1906, is one of the most beautiful bookshops in the world. Its red wooden spiral staircase and colourful skylight are said to have inspired J.K. Rowling during her stay in Porto in the 1990s. Today it sells more entry tickets than books.","Neo-Gothic bookshop from 1906 with a red spiral staircase, reputed to have inspired a wizarding saga","Historic bookshop among the most beautiful in the world, Clérigos district, lower Douro valley","Lello bookshop with its legendary staircase, Porto","Livraria Lello in Porto, Portugal"],"couvent_du_christ":["Convent of Christ — Tomar, Portugal","The Convent of Christ in Tomar was founded by the Knights Templar in the 12th century. When the Order of the Temple was dissolved in 1312, Portugal created the Order of Christ to take over its property. Its famous Manueline window, covered in maritime symbols, ropes and armillary spheres, is the emblem of Manueline art.","12th-century Templar fortress-convent, famous carved Manueline window, seat of an order of knights","Templar fortress-convent on a hill, central region of the country, Santarém district","Templar fortress and Manueline convent, Tomar","The Convent of Christ in Tomar, Portugal"],"grottes_d_altamira":["Cave of Altamira — Spain","The Altamira paintings, made about 14,500 years ago, are so realistic that Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola was accused of fraud after their discovery in 1879. They were only recognised as genuine in 1902, fourteen years after his death. Picasso, after seeing them, is said to have remarked: “After Altamira, all is decadence.”","Cave with 35 painted Palaeolithic bison (c. 14,500 BC), discovered in 1879, the “Sistine Chapel of prehistory”","Decorated prehistoric cave near a medieval village, green coast in the north of the country","Cave of prehistoric bison paintings, Cantabria, northern Spain","The Cave of Altamira, Spain"],"alcazar_de_seville":["Alcázar of Seville — Spain","The Alcázar of Seville is the oldest royal palace still in use in the Western world. Peter I had it built in the Mudéjar style in 1364 with craftsmen from Granada and Toledo. It served as the set for Dorne in the Game of Thrones series. The Spanish royal family still stays there.","Fortified palace founded in 913 and remodelled in the Mudéjar style in 1364, oldest royal palace still in use","Mudéjar palace with patios and gardens, old quarter of an Andalusian city","Mudéjar palace, Europe's oldest royal palace in use, Seville","The Alcázar of Seville, Spain"],"grottes_de_lascaux":["Lascaux Cave — France","The Lascaux paintings were discovered by four teenagers in 1940 while looking for their dog. After being opened to the public, the caves were closed in 1963 because visitors' breath was causing algae and fungi to grow on the paintings.","Decorated Palaeolithic cave (17,000 years old), 1,900 animal figures, including a Hall of the Bulls","Cave of prehistoric rock paintings, Périgord valley, south-west of the country","Prehistoric cave with rock paintings, Dordogne","The Lascaux Caves, France"],"carcassonne":["Carcassonne — France","Carcassonne's double ring of walls inspired the board game of the same name, which has sold more than 10 million copies worldwide. In the game, players build cities and roads by laying tiles — an idea that came to its designer after a visit to the medieval city.","Fortified city with 3 km of ramparts and 52 towers, former Cathar stronghold, restored in the 19th century","Medieval city with a double ring of walls on a hill above a river, Occitanie, 90 km from a major city","UNESCO-listed fortified medieval city, Carcassonne","The Cité de Carcassonne, France"],"musee_d_orsay":["Musée d'Orsay — Paris, France","Turning the Gare d'Orsay into a museum saved a building destined for demolition. In the early 1970s, a demolition permit was even granted to build a large hotel in its place. The project was abandoned, the station was listed as a historic monument in 1973, and it was then converted into a museum that opened in 1986.","Former 1900 railway station turned museum in 1986, home to the world's largest Impressionist collection","Museum of Impressionism in a former railway station, left bank of a great river","Museum of Impressionism in a converted station, Paris","The Musée d'Orsay in Paris, France"],"centre_pompidou":["Centre Pompidou — Paris, France","The Centre Pompidou turned its building “inside out”: all the services (stairs, lifts, ducts) are on the outside, colour-coded (blue = air, green = water, yellow = electricity, red = circulation). Criticised when it opened, the result is now an architectural icon.","1977 modern art museum with inside-out architecture: colourful pipes, ducts and stairs on the façade","Modern art museum with exposed multicoloured structures, Marais district","Modern art museum with colourful pipes, Beaubourg, Paris","The Centre Pompidou in Paris, France"],"musee_du_louvre":["Louvre — Paris, France","With 73,000 m² of exhibition space, it would take 9 full weeks at 8 hours a day to look at every work in the Louvre for 30 seconds. The Mona Lisa, which often disappoints visitors with its small size (77 cm × 53 cm), nonetheless draws 6 million people a year.","Largest museum in the world in a former royal fortress, courtyard with a glass pyramid (1989)","Huge museum in a former royal palace, wing along a great river, courtyard with a glass pyramid","Largest museum in the world, glass pyramid, Paris","The Louvre Museum in Paris, France"],"arc_de_triomphe":["Arc de Triomphe — Paris, France","The Arc de Triomphe was commissioned by Napoleon in 1806, after the victory at Austerlitz, but the Emperor never saw it finished. Beneath its vault the Unknown Soldier has lain since 1921, and his flame has been rekindled every evening at 6.30 pm, without interruption, since 1923.","50 m monumental arch commissioned in 1806, completed in 1836, housing the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier","Great triumphal arch in the middle of a star-shaped square, at the top of a famous avenue","Arc de Triomphe de l'Étoile, Tomb of the Unknown Soldier, Paris","The Arc de Triomphe in Paris, France"],"british_museum":["British Museum — London, United Kingdom","The British Museum was founded in 1753 with the collection of Sir Hans Sloane — 71,000 objects bequeathed to the nation in return for £20,000. Its Great Court, topped by a glass roof of 3,312 unique panels designed by Norman Foster, is the largest covered square in Europe.","Museum founded in 1753, 8 million objects including a trilingual inscribed stone and ancient marbles, glass-roofed courtyard","Great universal museum in the Bloomsbury district, inner courtyard under a glass roof","First national museum in the world, Rosetta Stone, London","The British Museum in London, United Kingdom"],"palais_de_buckingham":["Buckingham Palace — London, United Kingdom","Buckingham Palace has 775 rooms, including 188 staff bedrooms. By ancient tradition, the Crown owns unmarked mute swans on open waters, as well as sturgeon, whales and dolphins caught near the coast. The Changing of the Guard, in red tunics and bearskins, draws thousands of visitors.","Official residence of the sovereign since 1837, 775 rooms, Changing of the Guard and a balcony for appearances","Royal palace at the end of a long avenue, facing a memorial, capital of an island monarchy","Official residence of the King, Changing of the Guard, London","Buckingham Palace in London, United Kingdom"],"tour_de_londres":["Tower of London — United Kingdom","The Tower of London was founded by William the Conqueror in 1066 and has served in turn as a fortress, royal palace, prison and treasury. Its Ravens — six resident ravens — are looked after by a Ravenmaster: legend has it that if the ravens leave, the Tower and the Crown will fall.","Fortress-palace founded in 1066, guards in red uniforms and the Crown Jewels, legendary ravens","Medieval castle on a riverbank, east of an island capital","Medieval fortress with the Crown Jewels, London","The Tower of London, United Kingdom"],"palais_de_hampton_court":["Hampton Court Palace — United Kingdom","The Hampton Court maze, planted around 1690 for William III, is the oldest surviving hedge maze in the United Kingdom. Its route of about 800 metres between yew hedges can take a good half-hour. It postdates by far Henry VIII, who had made this Tudor palace one of his favourite residences.","Tudor palace of a king with six wives (1515), baroque gardens and a 1690 maze on a riverbank","Tudor palace and its maze, residential suburb on a riverbank, south-eastern county","Henry VIII's Tudor palace, maze and gardens, near London","Hampton Court Palace, England (United Kingdom)"],"cathedrale_de_durham":["Durham Cathedral — United Kingdom","Durham Cathedral, with its Norman columns carved with zigzags, is among the first great rib-vaulted buildings in Europe. The writer Bill Bryson called it “the best cathedral on planet Earth”. Its position on a promontory encircled by the Wear and its austere bulk make it one of the most dramatic sights in England.","Norman Romanesque masterpiece begun in 1093 on a promontory encircled by a river, relics of saints","Great Romanesque cathedral on a river peninsula, north-east of the country","UNESCO-listed Norman Romanesque cathedral, Durham","Durham Cathedral, England (United Kingdom)"],"kelvingrove":["Kelvingrove — Glasgow, Scotland","Glasgow's Kelvingrove museum, opened in 1901, displays a Spitfire suspended above its galleries as well as Dalí's famous Christ of Saint John of the Cross. According to a stubborn legend, the building was built “back to front” and the architect threw himself from a tower in despair. In reality, the main entrance was always intended to face the park.","Baroque palace-museum from 1901 mixing fine art and curiosities, a Dalí and a suspended fighter plane","Great museum of art and curiosities in the West End, on a river, major Lowlands city","Glasgow's iconic museum of art and curiosities","Kelvingrove museum in Glasgow, Scotland"],"peninsule_de_dingle":["Dingle Peninsula — Ireland","The Dingle Peninsula has more than 2,000 archaeological monuments, one of the highest densities in Europe. Prehistoric tombs and standing stones sit alongside ring forts and early Christian sites such as the dry-stone Gallarus Oratory: you walk through a landscape that has been inhabited for about 6,000 years.","Scenic coastal road, 7th-century stone oratory and prehistoric forts facing the Atlantic","Wild peninsula at the far west of an Atlantic island, Celtic monuments, County Kerry","Wild Atlantic peninsula with Celtic monuments, Kerry, west of Ireland","The Dingle Peninsula, Ireland"],"speicherstadt":["Speicherstadt — Hamburg, Germany","The warehouses of Hamburg's Speicherstadt were built on oak piles driven into the waters of the Elbe. For a century this red-brick district stored the spices, carpets and cocoa of Germany's trading empire. Today it is home to museums and creative agencies.","World's largest complex of warehouses on piles (1883–1927), red brick spanning canals","District of brick warehouses on canals, on a great river, major northern port","UNESCO-listed district of neo-Gothic brick warehouses on canals, Hamburg","The Speicherstadt in Hamburg, Germany"],"chateau_d_heidelberg":["Heidelberg Castle — Germany","Heidelberg Castle houses one of the largest wine barrels in the world, with a capacity of 221,726 litres. Built in 1751 for the Prince-Elector Charles Theodore, it is said to have been filled only a handful of times: its capacity was more symbolic than practical.","Renaissance ruins on a hill, burned by an army in 1689, famous for a giant wine barrel","Ruined castle overlooking a university town in a valley, south-west of the country","Ruins of the Renaissance castle overlooking Heidelberg and the Neckar","Heidelberg Castle, Germany"],"porte_de_brandebourg":["Brandenburg Gate — Berlin, Germany","For 28 years the Brandenburg Gate was the symbol of Berlin's division: it stood on the line of the Wall, in no man's land, inaccessible from either side. On the night of 9 November 1989, thousands of Berliners spontaneously gathered in front of it to celebrate the fall of the Wall; the gate itself officially reopened on 22 December 1989.","Neoclassical gate from 1791 crowned by a quadriga of Victory, which became the symbol of a reunification","Monumental gate at the end of a grand avenue, in the central district of a Central European capital","Neoclassical gate, symbol of reunification, Berlin","The Brandenburg Gate in Berlin, Germany"],"philharmonie_de_berlin":["Berliner Philharmonie — Germany","The Berliner Philharmonie, designed by Hans Scharoun and opened in 1963, was one of the first major concert halls to place the orchestra in the centre, surrounded by the audience seated in “vineyard” terraces — an architectural revolution. Its acoustics are regularly ranked among the best in the world.","Avant-garde tent-shaped concert hall from 1963, with the orchestra placed in the midst of the audience","Modern concert hall in a cultural quarter, in the centre of a Central European capital","Concert hall with the audience all around the orchestra, Berlin","The Berlin Philharmonie, Germany"],"berne":["Bern — Switzerland (Zytglogge)","The Zytglogge, Bern's medieval clock tower, is tied to a famous anecdote: it was while living just a few steps away, on Kramgasse, that Albert Einstein developed his theory of special relativity in 1905. According to an oft-told story, he wondered what he would see if his tram were moving away from the clock at the speed of light.","Medieval clock tower from 1218 with astronomical automata, on a street where a famous physicist once lived","Clock tower with automata in the old town, inside a river loop, in a federal capital","Medieval clock tower with automata, Bern","The Zytglogge in Bern, capital of Switzerland"],"musee_olympique":["Olympic Museum — Lausanne, Switzerland","Lausanne has been the Olympic capital of the world since 1915 and is home to the Olympic Museum, opened in 1993. It is there that the IOC decides who will host the Games, and it keeps the archives of every Games since Athens 1896. Lausanne is also home to the Court of Arbitration for Sport, which settles all sporting disputes worldwide.","Headquarters of a global sporting institution, sports museum on the shore of a large lake, opened in 1993","Museum of the Olympic movement on the shore of a large lake, canton of Vaud","Headquarters of the International Olympic Committee, on the shore of Lake Geneva, Lausanne","The Olympic Museum in Lausanne, Switzerland"],"abbaye_de_westminster":["Westminster Abbey — London","Westminster Abbey has been the coronation church of English, then British, monarchs since William the Conqueror in 1066. Seventeen monarchs are buried there. The Stone of Destiny, on which the kings of Scotland were crowned, sat beneath the Coronation Chair from 1296 to 1996; returned to Scotland, it came back to Westminster for the coronation of Charles III in 2023.","Abbey church founded in 960, site of coronations since 1066, resting place of great scientists","Gothic coronation abbey next to the parliament of an island capital","Coronation abbey and national pantheon, London","Westminster Abbey in London, United Kingdom"],"chateau_de_windsor":["Windsor Castle — United Kingdom","Windsor Castle is extended by the Long Walk, a straight avenue some 4.2 km long, planted with trees under Charles II in the 1680s. It forms the main axis of Windsor Great Park and runs all the way to the equestrian statue of George III on Snow Hill.","Oldest inhabited royal castle in the world, founded around 1070, with a great round tower, a royal residence","Large inhabited royal castle by a river, in a county west of an island capital","Oldest inhabited royal castle in the world, Windsor","Windsor Castle, England (United Kingdom)"],"chateau_de_bled":["Bled Castle — Slovenia","Bled Castle is first mentioned in 1011 in a document by Emperor Henry II, who gave it to the Bishopric of Brixen. Perched 130 metres above the lake, it overlooks one of the most photographed views in Europe: the lake, the island and the surrounding mountains.","11th-century medieval castle on a 130 m cliff above a lake whose island is crowned by a church","Castle perched on a cliff above a glacial lake with a baroque island, Julian Alps","Clifftop castle overlooking Lake Bled and its island, Slovenia","Bled Castle in Slovenia"],"kotor":["Kotor — Montenegro","Kotor's walls climb 260 metres up the cliffside to St John's Fortress — the walk up takes an hour. The Venetian walled city, at the head of a fjord-like bay, is so well preserved that residents still live in the same 15th-century houses.","Venetian old town whose walls climb 260 m up a mountain, at the head of a deep, steep-sided bay","Medieval walled city at the head of a deep Adriatic bay","Walled old town at the head of a spectacular bay, Kotor","The old town of Kotor in Montenegro"],"plovdiv":["Plovdiv — Bulgaria (old town)","Plovdiv is the oldest continuously inhabited city in Europe, with 8,000 years of history. Its hills hold Thracian, Roman and Byzantine remains. Its 2nd-century Roman amphitheatre, rediscovered in 1972 after a landslide, now hosts open-air concerts and operas with exceptional acoustics.","Among the oldest continuously inhabited cities in Europe, refounded by a Macedonian king around 342 BC, Roman amphitheatre on hills","Old town on hills, Roman amphitheatre, Balkan city on a river","Oldest inhabited city in Europe, Roman amphitheatre, Plovdiv","The old town of Plovdiv, Bulgaria"],"rila_monastery":["Rila Monastery — Bulgaria","Rila Monastery was founded in the 10th century by Saint John of Rila, who lived as a hermit in a nearby cave. Rebuilt in the 19th century after a fire, it is covered in colourful frescoes over some 1,200 m², painted in the 1840s by masters of the Samokov school, including Zahari Zograf.","Monastery founded in the 10th century, arcaded courtyard painted with vivid frescoes, fortified tower, deep in the mountains","Large Orthodox monastery in a mountain gorge, 120 km from a Balkan capital","Largest monastery in Bulgaria, Rila mountains","Rila Monastery in Bulgaria"],"bia_owie_a":["Białowieża — Poland/Belarus","Białowieża Forest is the last primeval lowland forest in Europe — never logged industrially in its protected core. It is home to 500-year-old trees, giant fungi and insects that have vanished almost everywhere else. The European bison (Bison bonasus), extinct in the wild by the 1920s and reintroduced here in 1952, now numbers several hundred animals.","Last primeval lowland forest in Europe, final refuge of the European bison, straddling a border","Virgin lowland forest and wild bison, on the border of two Central European countries","Last primeval forest in Europe, wild bison, eastern Poland","Białowieża Forest, between Poland and Belarus"],"red_fort":["Red Fort — Delhi, India","Delhi's Red Fort, built of red sandstone from 1638 to 1648 for the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan, owes its name to its immense ramparts. In the wake of independence, Jawaharlal Nehru raised the Indian flag there in August 1947 — a gesture every prime minister has repeated each 15 August since, from the Lahori Gate.","Mughal fortress of red sandstone (1638–1648), where the national flag is raised every Independence Day, on a river plain","Large Mughal red sandstone fortress on the bank of a river in the Ganges plain","Mughal Red Fort, symbol of independence, Delhi","The Red Fort in Delhi, India"],"qutb_minar":["Qutb Minar — Delhi, India","The Qutb Minar, a 72.5 m minaret begun in 1193, towers over a complex that also contains an iron pillar some 1,600 years old that does not rust. This phosphorus-rich wrought iron has acquired a thin protective layer of phosphates and iron oxides, a phenomenon that long puzzled metallurgists.","72.5 m red sandstone minaret begun in 1193, next to an ancient iron pillar that does not rust","Tall minaret and ruins of a former sultanate, Ganges plain, south of a large capital","Tallest brick minaret in the world, Qutb Minar, Delhi","The Qutb Minar in Delhi, India"],"ranakpur":["Ranakpur — Rajasthan, India","The Jain temple at Ranakpur, begun in 1437, has 1,444 white marble pillars, each carved differently. It was funded by the Jain merchant Dharna Shah, built to plans by the architect Deepaka, and the site takes its name from King Rana Kumbha, who donated the land.","Jain temple from 1437 with 1,444 marble pillars, no two alike, hidden away in wooded hills","Marble Jain temple in a wooded range, in a state in north-west India, 90 km from a fortress city","Jain temple with 1,444 marble pillars, Rajasthan","The Jain temple of Ranakpur in Rajasthan, India"],"jaisalmer":["Jaisalmer — Rajasthan, India","Jaisalmer Fort has been continuously inhabited for 900 years. Around 3,000 people still live there today — hoteliers, shopkeepers, families. It is one of the only medieval fortresses in the world that is still inhabited.","Yellow sandstone fort (1156) that looks like a sandcastle, one of the few fortresses still inhabited","Golden fortress city in the heart of a desert, far west of Rajasthan","Golden City and its inhabited fort, Thar Desert, Jaisalmer","Jaisalmer Fort in Rajasthan, India"],"varanasi":["Varanasi — Uttar Pradesh, India","The Ganges at Varanasi is so sacred that Hindus believe dying there brings liberation from the cycle of rebirth. Thousands of people come to Varanasi to die every year, entrusted to the “Dom” — the untouchables who tend the cremations at Manikarnika Ghat, which have been burning 24 hours a day for centuries.","Among the oldest inhabited cities in the world, 87 sacred flights of steps leading down to a river, funeral pyres","Holy city with ghats on the bank of a great river, eastern Ganges plain","Hindu holy city on the Ganges, cremations and ghats, Varanasi","Varanasi (Benares), in Uttar Pradesh, India"],"meenakshi_temple":["Meenakshi Temple — Madurai, India","The Meenakshi Temple in Madurai has some 33,000 sculptures on its 14 gateway towers (gopurams), all painted in vivid colours. The towers are restored and repainted roughly every 12 years, ahead of a grand consecration ceremony called Kumbhabhishekam that lasts several days.","Temple with 14 gateway towers covered in 33,000 multicoloured sculptures, dedicated to a goddess","Large Dravidian temple with sculpted towers, in the heart of a southern Indian state","Dravidian temple with multicoloured towers, Madurai","The Meenakshi Temple in Madurai, India"],"ellora_caves":["Ellora Caves — Maharashtra, India","The Ellora Caves contain the Kailasa temple, carved from top to bottom out of a single block of basalt — a feat without equal. Freeing it required removing some 200,000 tonnes of rock, a project launched in the 8th century under the Rashtrakuta dynasty.","34 temples and monasteries carved into basalt from the 6th to the 10th century, including a giant monolithic shrine","Rock-cut cave temples, Deccan Plateau, 30 km from a large city in Maharashtra","Buddhist, Hindu and Jain cave temples, Maharashtra","The Ellora Caves in Maharashtra, India"],"shibuya_crossing":["Shibuya Crossing — Tokyo, Japan","Hundreds of thousands of pedestrians pass through Shibuya Crossing every day. The “scramble crossing” principle, where all pedestrians cross at once in every direction, is not a Japanese invention: it was popularised in North America in the 1940s. Shibuya adopted it in 1973 and took it to its extreme.","Pedestrian crossing where 3,000 people cross at every change of the lights, statue of a faithful dog at the nearby station","Busiest pedestrian crossing in the world, shopping district of a megacity, right outside a station","Busiest pedestrian crossing in the world, Shibuya, Tokyo","Shibuya Crossing in Tokyo, Japan"],"nara":["Nara — Japan (Great Buddha)","Nara's Tōdai-ji houses one of the largest bronze Buddhas in the world — about 15 metres tall and 500 tonnes. At the base of one of the building's wooden pillars is a hole the size of the Buddha's nostril: tradition holds that anyone who manages to squeeze through is assured of enlightenment. Hundreds of visitors try every day.","Temple from 745 housing a 15 m bronze Buddha in the world's largest wooden building, with deer roaming free","Great temple with a bronze Buddha in a deer park, the country's first imperial capital","Great bronze Buddha in the world's largest wooden building, Nara","Tōdai-ji and its Great Buddha in Nara, Japan"],"itsukushima":["Itsukushima — Hiroshima, Japan","Itsukushima Shrine, on the island of Miyajima, is built on stilts over the sea. Its great vermilion torii, about 16 m tall, is not driven into the seabed: it stands up by its own weight alone. At low tide, you can walk right up to its base.","Great orange Shinto gateway standing in the sea, off a sacred island where no one was allowed to be born or to die","Floating Shinto gateway of a sacred island, inland sea in the south-west of the archipelago","Great floating Shinto gateway, Miyajima island, near Hiroshima","Itsukushima Shrine on the island of Miyajima, near Hiroshima, Japan"],"hiroshima_peace_memorial":["Hiroshima Peace Memorial — Japan","The Hiroshima atomic bomb failed to destroy the Genbaku Dome only because it exploded almost directly above it. The vertical blast spared a building that a horizontal shock wave would have flattened. It is now a universal symbol of peace.","The only building left standing directly beneath an atomic explosion on 6 August 1945, its gutted dome preserved","Atomic bomb memorial ruin on a riverbank, a large city in the west of the archipelago","Atomic Bomb Dome, peace memorial, Hiroshima","The Hiroshima Peace Memorial in Japan"],"demilitarized_zone":["Korean Demilitarized Zone — Korea","Paradoxically, the Korean Demilitarized Zone is one of the best-preserved areas in Asia — 70 years with almost no human activity. Endangered red-crowned cranes stop over there and many rare species find refuge. At Panmunjom, around the blue huts of the Joint Security Area, soldiers from North and South face each other just a few metres apart.","Buffer strip 4 km wide and 248 km long since 1953, blue huts in a border village","Militarised zone on the 38th parallel, border between two East Asian states","Demilitarised zone between the two Koreas, Panmunjom","The Demilitarised Zone (DMZ) between the two Koreas"],"jeju_island":["Jeju Island — South Korea","Jeju Island is famous for its haenyeo, women who free-dive without air tanks for abalone, sea urchins and octopus, down to around ten metres. Many are still at it past 70 or 80, and their culture has been inscribed on UNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage list since 2016.","Volcanic island with a 1,950 m volcano and a 7 km lava tube, free-diving women, a triple world designation","Volcanic island with lava tubes, in the sea south-west of an East Asian peninsula","Volcanic island with lava tubes and women divers, southern Korea","Jeju Island, South Korea"],"taroko_gorge":["Taroko Gorge — Taiwan","Taroko Gorge has been carved into marble by the Liwu River over 2 million years. In places, the veined marble walls let through only 2 to 3 metres of light. The road through it was built between 1956 and 1960 by 10,000 soldiers — 212 of them lost their lives.","19 km marble gorge carved by a river, with a cliffside road and winding tunnels","Marble gorge in a national park, central range of a mountainous East Asian island","Spectacular marble gorge, Central Mountain Range of Taiwan","Taroko Gorge in Taiwan"],"halong_bay":["Ha Long Bay — Vietnam (Cat Ba)","Cat Ba is the largest island in the archipelago that closes off Ha Long Bay to the south. Its primary forest is home to the Cat Ba langur, one of the world's most endangered primates, reduced to a few dozen individuals. Its prehistoric caves were inhabited more than 7,000 years ago.","Largest island in a bay of limestone pinnacles, sea caves, mangroves and a floating fishing village","Large island in an archipelago in the Gulf of Tonkin, northern coast of a Southeast Asian country","Main island of Ha Long Bay, caves and mangroves, northern Vietnam","Cat Ba Island, in Ha Long Bay, Vietnam"],"hoi_an":["Hoi An — Vietnam","Hoi An was the most important port in Southeast Asia in the 16th and 17th centuries, before its river silted up. Its historic centre is so well preserved that it seems frozen in time — apart from the colourful lanterns that light up every evening.","Former trading port with Chinese houses and a covered bridge from 1593, alleys lit by silk lanterns","Old lantern-lit trading port on the coast of a central province of a long Southeast Asian country","Old lantern-lit trading port, UNESCO-listed, Hoi An","The Old Town of Hội An, Vietnam"],"wat_phra_kaew":["Wat Phra Kaew — Bangkok, Thailand","The Emerald Buddha of Wat Phra Kaew is only 66 cm tall — a modest size for the most sacred statue in Thailand. Carved from a single piece of jade (not emerald), it is dressed in three different costumes, which the king changes personally each season.","Temple of a 66 cm jade Buddha within the grounds of a grand palace, its robes changed by the king three times a year","Royal temple with a jade Buddha inside a palace, on a royal island by a river, Southeast Asian capital","Temple of the Emerald Buddha in the Grand Palace, Bangkok","Wat Phra Kaew in Bangkok, Thailand"],"prambanan":["Prambanan — Java, Indonesia","The legend of Prambanan tells of a suitor who had to build 1,000 temples in a single night to win a princess's hand. As dawn approached and he had built only 999, the princess lit fires to fake the sunrise and so stopped him.","Three tall 9th-century Hindu temples dedicated to a divine trinity, 47 m towers covered in reliefs","Group of 9th-century Hindu temples, volcanic plain of a large tropical island","9th-century Hindu temples, plain of Java","Prambanan temple on Java, Indonesia"],"kairouan":["Kairouan — Tunisia","Kairouan is the fourth holiest city in Islam and the holiest in North Africa. Its Great Mosque, founded in 670, contains the oldest mihrab and the oldest minbar still in place anywhere in the Islamic world.","Great mosque founded in 670, oldest minaret still standing, fourth holy city of a major religion","Holy city with an ancient great mosque, centre of the country, 160 km from the capital","Fourth holy city of Islam, 7th-century great mosque, Kairouan","Kairouan and its Great Mosque, Tunisia"],"pyramide_de_meroe":["Pyramids of Meroë — Sudan","Sudan's Meroë pyramids outnumber those of Egypt — there are more than 200 — but they are much narrower and far less well known. In the 19th century an Italian adventurer blew the tops off several of them, believing he would find treasure inside.","More than 200 narrow, steep royal pyramids of an ancient kingdom, lined up in a desert","Necropolis of narrow pyramids of an ancient kingdom, Nubian Desert, 200 km from a capital","Nubian pyramids of the Kingdom of Kush, northern Sudan","The Pyramids of Meroë in Sudan"],"tsingy_de_bemaraha":["Tsingy de Bemaraha — Madagascar","Madagascar's Tsingy are forests of limestone blades so sharp they slice through the soles of your shoes. Their Malagasy name means “where one cannot walk barefoot”.","Forest of limestone pinnacles as sharp as blades, a labyrinth of karst sheltering endemic lemurs","Forest of sharp limestone rocks in a national park, west of a large Indian Ocean island","Forest of sharp limestone rocks, western Madagascar","Tsingy de Bemaraha in Madagascar"],"chutes_kaieteur":["Kaieteur Falls — Guyana","Guyana's Kaieteur Falls are surrounded by forest so pristine and inaccessible that no European set eyes on them until 1870. The only way to get there is still by small plane, landing on a grass strip in the middle of the jungle — there is no road.","Single drop of 226 m — five times Niagara — on a river pouring off a table-top plateau","Very high and powerful waterfall deep in the jungle of a small South American country","226 m waterfall, among the most powerful in the world, Guyanese jungle","Kaieteur Falls in Guyana"],"lac_rose":["Lake Retba — Senegal","Lake Retba, in Senegal, owes its pink colour to a microalga (Dunaliella salina) that produces a red pigment to protect itself from the salt. With a salinity about 10 times that of the ocean, salt harvesters float effortlessly, as in the Dead Sea.","Lake of an intense pink caused by a microalga, ten times saltier than the sea, former finish of a legendary rally","Very salty pink lake on a peninsula, 35 km from a large West African capital","Very salty natural pink lake, near Dakar","The Pink Lake (Lac Rose) in Senegal"],"parc_des_volcans":["Volcanoes National Park — Rwanda","Volcanoes National Park protects the Rwandan part of the Virunga Mountains, home to more than half of the world's roughly 1,000 mountain gorillas. A permit costs 1,500 dollars per person for one hour with a gorilla family. Dian Fossey, who studied the gorillas there for 18 years, was found murdered at her Karisoke camp in 1985.","Chain of volcanoes sheltering mountain gorillas, studied by a primatologist from 1967 to 1985","Volcanic park with mountain gorillas, at the tripoint of three Central African countries","Mountain gorillas of the Virunga, northern Rwanda","Volcanoes National Park in Rwanda"],"erg_chebbi":["Erg Chebbi — Morocco","Erg Chebbi, with dunes reaching 150 metres near Merzouga, is one of only two true sand seas in Morocco. The highest dune changes colour with the light: orange in the morning, bright red at midday, golden at sunset. Nomad bivouacs let visitors sleep under the Saharan stars.","150 m ridge of sand dunes, one of the country's two great ergs, Berber bivouacs under the stars","Large sand dunes on the edge of the Sahara, south-east of the country, near a caravan oasis","150 m sand dunes near Merzouga, Moroccan Sahara","Erg Chebbi in Morocco"],"djemila":["Djémila — Algeria","Djémila (formerly Cuicul) is considered the finest example of a Roman mountain town in North Africa. Its 2nd-century temples, forums and theatres lay intact beneath the rubble for 1,500 years. The writer Albert Camus found inspiration there for his essay “The Wind at Djémila”.","Roman city founded in 96, with a forum, temples, an arch and a theatre among the best preserved on a continent","Roman ruins on a plateau at 900 m, in a massif in the north-east of the country, North Africa","One of the best-preserved Roman cities in Africa, UNESCO-listed, Algeria","The Roman site of Djémila in Algeria"],"leptis_magna":["Leptis Magna — Libya","Leptis Magna is one of the best-preserved Roman cities in the world — protected for centuries by the sand. The birthplace of Emperor Septimius Severus, it was adorned with colossal monuments to his glory, including a four-sided triumphal arch among the most spectacular in the Roman world.","Roman city, birthplace of an emperor, with a triumphal arch, a theatre and a forum of 52 columns, by the sea","Vast coastal Roman ruins, Mediterranean coast, 130 km east of the capital","Roman city, birthplace of Septimius Severus, Libyan coast","The Roman site of Leptis Magna in Libya"],"aksum":["Aksum — Ethiopia","The obelisks of Aksum are monolithic funerary stelae up to 33 metres tall. One of them, carried off to Rome on Mussolini's orders in 1937, was only repatriated in 2005, after decades of negotiations, and re-erected on its site in 2008.","Field of ancient stelae of a great empire (1st–7th century), and a church said by tradition to house a sacred ark","Ancient obelisks and stelae of a former empire, northern region of an East African country","Former capital with obelisks and Christian relics, Aksum, Ethiopia","The stelae field of Aksum, Ethiopia"],"rift_valley":["Dallol — Danakil Depression, Ethiopia","The Dallol hydrothermal field, at the heart of the Danakil Depression, lies below sea level in the Afar Rift. Hot, acidic springs deposit salt, sulphur and iron oxides there in yellow, green and ochre formations that change appearance within a few weeks. The nearby village holds the record for the highest average annual temperature recorded in an inhabited place.","Acidic hot springs with yellow and green formations of salt and sulphur, below sea level, world record for average heat","Multicoloured hydrothermal field in a desert depression below sea level, Horn of Africa","Acid springs and coloured salt of the Danakil Depression, Afar Region","Dallol, in the Danakil Depression, Ethiopia"],"stone_town":["Stone Town — Zanzibar, Tanzania","Stone Town, the old town of Zanzibar, is the birthplace of Freddie Mercury, Queen's lead singer, born Farrokh Bulsara in 1946. Sent to boarding school in India at 8, he left the island for good with his family in 1964, during the Zanzibar Revolution. The house where he was born has become a place of pilgrimage for fans from all over the world.","Old Swahili stone town, former slave market, birthplace of a rock singer, carved doors","Swahili city of stone on an Indian Ocean island, off the East African coast","UNESCO-listed old Swahili city, island of Zanzibar","Stone Town, the old town of Zanzibar, Tanzania"],"sossusvlei":["Sossusvlei — Namibia","The Sossusvlei dunes are among the highest in the world, at 325 metres. Their intense red-orange colour comes from the oxidation of the iron in the sand. The Deadvlei pan, once a lake, is dotted with camelthorn trees that died 900 years ago — preserved by the extreme dryness.","Star dunes of red-ochre sand up to 325 m, a pan of age-old dead trees, the oldest desert in the world","Tall orange dunes and dead trees in a coastal desert, arid national park in the south-west","Red dunes and dead trees of Deadvlei, Namibian desert","Sossusvlei in Namibia"],"chobe":["Chobe National Park — Botswana","Chobe National Park has one of the highest concentrations of elephants in Africa, estimated at around 50,000. In the dry season, elephants come down to the Chobe River by the hundreds to drink — creating elephant traffic jams on the banks, to photographers' delight.","Park with one of Africa's highest concentrations of elephants, along a river teeming with game","Large wildlife park in the north of a landlocked country, at the quadripoint of four Southern African countries","Huge herds of elephants on the banks of the Chobe River, northern Botswana","Chobe National Park in Botswana"],"empire_state_building":["Empire State Building — New York, USA","The Empire State Building went up at a record pace of about 4.5 storeys a week. On 28 July 1945, a B-25 bomber lost in fog crashed into the 79th floor, killing 14 people — yet most floors reopened the very next Monday.","102-storey, 443 m Art Deco skyscraper built in 410 days, for a long time the tallest in the world","Art Deco skyscraper in a central district, on a numbered avenue of a great metropolis","Iconic Art Deco skyscraper, Midtown Manhattan, New York","The Empire State Building in New York, USA"],"monument_aux_soldats_inconnus":["Tomb of the Unknown Soldier — Arlington, USA","The Tomb of the Unknown Soldier, in Arlington National Cemetery, has been guarded 24 hours a day since 1937, whatever the weather. The sentinel takes 21 steps, pauses for 21 seconds, then sets off again: an echo of the 21-gun salute, the highest of military honours.","1932 white marble sarcophagus watched over day and night since 1937 by a sentinel who takes 21 steps","Permanently guarded marble tomb in a vast military cemetery, on a hill facing a federal capital","Tomb of the Unknown Soldier in Arlington National Cemetery, near Washington","The Tomb of the Unknown Soldier in Arlington (Virginia), USA"],"kennedy_space_center":["Kennedy Space Center — Florida, USA","Kennedy Space Center was the cradle of the Apollo programme. Its Vehicle Assembly Building (VAB), 160 metres high, is so vast that clouds sometimes form inside it in humid weather. Since 2014, SpaceX has leased Launch Complex 39A, from which the Apollo missions lifted off.","Huge 160 m assembly building and launch pad from which a lunar mission and the shuttles departed","Space centre on a coastal island, east coast of Florida, 80 km from a large city","NASA space centre, Apollo launchers and shuttles, east coast of Florida","The Kennedy Space Center in Florida, USA"],"mount_rushmore":["Mount Rushmore — South Dakota, USA","Mount Rushmore was carved between 1927 and 1941 by Gutzon Borglum and some 400 workers, using dynamite and chisels. Behind the heads, Borglum began digging a “Hall of Records” that was never finished; in 1998, a vault was sealed there containing panels telling the story of the monument and the country. The original plan was to carve the presidents down to the waist.","Faces of four presidents, 18 m high, carved into a granite cliff between 1927 and 1941","Monumental sculpture of presidents in a granite massif, 25 km from a town in the West","Four presidents carved into the rock, Black Hills, South Dakota","Mount Rushmore in South Dakota, USA"],"new_orleans_french_quarter":["French Quarter — New Orleans, USA","New Orleans is built on a marshy delta, partly below sea level. After Hurricane Katrina in 2005, 80% of the city was flooded. The fact that its residents rebuilt and that the music came back even stronger has become a symbol of American resilience.","French colonial quarter founded in 1718, wrought-iron balconies and jazz clubs along a lively street","Lively old colonial quarter on the bank of a great river, a city in the Deep South, Louisiana","Vieux Carré, cradle of jazz, New Orleans","The French Quarter (Vieux Carré) of New Orleans, USA"],"mesa_verde":["Mesa Verde — Colorado, USA","The cliff dwellings of Mesa Verde were abruptly abandoned around 1300 — archaeologists still don't know why. Prolonged drought, conflict or a spiritual migration? The Ancestral Puebloan civilisation vanished without leaving any written explanation.","Largest cliff dwelling in North America, 150 rooms and 23 ritual chambers beneath a cliff, 13th century","Ancestral cliff dwellings, south-west of a mountainous Western state","13th-century Puebloan cliff dwellings, south-western Colorado","Mesa Verde National Park in Colorado, USA"],"graceland":["Graceland — Memphis, Tennessee, USA","Graceland was bought by Elvis Presley in 1957 for 102,500 dollars, when he was 22. He lived there until his death in 1977. The house has remained virtually untouched ever since — its “Jungle Room”, with an indoor fountain and faux fur, is a 1970s time capsule. 650,000 people visit it every year.","Residence bought in 1957 by a rock idol, exotic living room and the singer's grave in the garden","Mansion-museum of a rock legend, in the suburbs of a southern city, western Tennessee","Elvis Presley's mansion, rock pilgrimage site, Memphis","Graceland, Elvis Presley's mansion in Memphis (Tennessee), USA"],"banff":["Banff — Alberta (Moraine Lake), Canada","Moraine Lake owes its intense turquoise colour to the “rock flour” produced by the surrounding glaciers as they grind rock into minute particles. These suspended particles diffract blue light. The lake appeared on the Canadian 20-dollar note from 1969 to 1979.","Turquoise lake fed by glacial flour, ringed by ten peaks, once printed on a banknote","Turquoise glacial lake ringed by summits, Rockies, national park in the west of the country","Turquoise glacial lake and Valley of the Ten Peaks, Canadian Rockies","Moraine Lake, Banff National Park (Alberta), Canada"],"whistler":["Whistler — British Columbia, Canada","Whistler Blackcomb is the largest ski area in North America, with more than 3,300 hectares of skiable terrain. Its Peak 2 Peak Gondola, which links the two summits 436 metres above the valley, holds the record for the longest unsupported span for this type of lift: 3.03 km.","Largest ski area in North America, record-breaking gondola linking two summits, host of the 2010 Olympics","Major ski resort in the Coast Mountains, 125 km from a big Pacific city","Largest ski area in North America, British Columbia","The Whistler ski resort in British Columbia, Canada"],"parc_torres_del_paine":["Torres del Paine National Park — Chile","The name “Paine” (pronounced “PIE-neh”) is thought to come from a Tehuelche word meaning “blue”, a reference to the colour of the glaciers and lakes. The massif's famous granite towers loom over glaciers flowing from the Southern Patagonian Ice Field, whose waters reach the Pacific through the Chilean fjords.","2,500 m granite towers and a glacier, herds of guanacos and pumas, at the end of the continent","Massif of granite towers and a glacier in a national park, far-southern Patagonia","Granite towers and glacier, Torres del Paine, Chilean Patagonia","Torres del Paine National Park, Chile"],"atacama":["Atacama Desert — Chile","The Atacama Desert is so dry that some parts of it have never received measurable rainfall since weather instruments have existed. Yet in certain years, after exceptional rain, a “desert bloom” occurs, carpeting the desert with flowers within 24 hours.","Driest desert in the world, geysers at 4,300 m, a moon-like valley and vast lithium-rich salt mirrors","Extremely arid desert wedged between a high mountain range and an ocean, north of the country","Driest desert in the world, geysers and salt flats, northern Chile","The Atacama, Chile"],"galeria_kaufmann":["Galerías Pacífico — Buenos Aires, Argentina","Galerías Pacífico occupies an 1889 building modelled on the great Parisian department stores, on the pedestrianised Calle Florida. Its central dome is decorated with frescoes painted in 1946 by five leading Argentine muralists, including Antonio Berni and Lino Enea Spilimbergo; today the building houses a shopping centre.","1889 shopping arcade inspired by the great Parisian department stores, its dome covered in frescoes painted in 1946 by five muralists","Shopping arcade with a painted dome on a pedestrianised shopping street, in the heart of a Río de la Plata capital","Arcade with a painted dome on Calle Florida, Buenos Aires","Galerías Pacífico in Buenos Aires, Argentina"],"iles_galapagos":["Galápagos Islands — Ecuador (Fernandina)","Fernandina, in the Galápagos, is the youngest and most pristine island in the archipelago — no introduced mammal species has ever established a population there. Its volcano, La Cumbre, is one of the most active in the archipelago and erupts every few years. In 2019, a Fernandina giant tortoise was found alive — its species had been thought extinct for more than a century.","Youngest and most pristine volcanic island of a remote archipelago, marine iguanas and an active volcano","Island in a remote volcanic Pacific archipelago, 1,000 km from a continent, unique wildlife","Darwin’s islands with their endemic wildlife, Galápagos, Ecuador","The Galápagos Islands, Ecuador (Fernandina)"],"tikal":["Tikal — Guatemala","The Maya temples of Tikal served as the backdrop for the planet Yavin 4 in Star Wars in 1977. George Lucas had the Rebel Alliance scenes filmed in the Guatemalan forest, which has drawn millions of fans ever since.","Classic Maya city whose six pyramids pierce the canopy, the tallest 47 m high, deep in the jungle","Maya city with temples rising out of the rainforest, northern lowlands of a Central American country","Maya city with temples towering above the jungle, northern Guatemala","The Maya site of Tikal, Guatemala"],"monteverde":["Monteverde — Costa Rica","The Monteverde community, in Costa Rica, was founded by American Quakers who refused military service and emigrated in 1951. By protecting the forests above their land, these pacifists inadvertently laid the foundations for one of the most visited nature reserves in Central America.","Cloud forest reserve with 90 orchid species and the quetzal bird, hanging bridges in the canopy","Cloud forest reserve in a Central American mountain range, 167 km from the capital","Cloud forest reserve with orchids and quetzals, Costa Rica","Monteverde Reserve, Costa Rica"],"canal_de_corinthe":["Corinth Canal — Greece","The Corinth Canal, 6.3 km long and only 21 metres wide, was dug between 1882 and 1893 through the limestone of the isthmus. The idea dated back to Antiquity — Nero himself struck the first blows of the pickaxe in AD 67 before giving up. Its 90-metre walls give boats the feeling of sailing through a chasm.","6.3 km water trench dug between 1882 and 1893, 90 m rock walls, the old dream of a Roman emperor","Narrow canal slicing through an isthmus between a peninsula and the mainland, south-eastern Europe","Canal crossing the Isthmus of Corinth, Greece","The Corinth Canal, Greece"],"santorin":["Santorini — Greece","Santorini is the remnant of a giant volcano whose eruption around 1600 BC was one of the most powerful in human history. It triggered a tsunami that devastated the Minoan civilisation on Crete. Some historians believe this catastrophe gave rise to Plato’s myth of Atlantis.","12 km volcanic caldera, white villages with blue domes on the crater rim, famous sunsets","Volcanic caldera island with white houses, Aegean archipelago","Caldera with white houses and blue domes, Santorini, Cyclades","The island of Santorini, Greece"],"mykonos":["Mykonos — Greece","Mykonos, an island in the Cyclades, was a pirates’ lair for centuries before becoming a destination for the international jet set in the 1960s. Its famous windmills, built by the Venetians in the 16th century, ground wheat from the neighbouring islands to supply the merchant fleets.","White island with maze-like lanes and five windmills, the most glamorous port in the archipelago","White island with windmills, central Aegean archipelago","White Cycladic island with windmills, Mykonos","The island of Mykonos, Greece"],"delphes":["Delphi — Greece","The Oracle of Delphi was so influential that Greek cities and foreign kings came to consult it before any major decision. The Pythia delivered her prophecies in a trance; in 2001, researchers suggested that faults beneath the Temple of Apollo may have released gases such as ethylene, capable of inducing that state.","Sanctuary of an ancient oracle, the “navel of the world”, a temple on the slopes of a sacred mountain","Site of the most consulted oracle of Antiquity, on a mountainside, centre of a peninsula in south-eastern Europe","Site of the ancient Oracle, navel of the world, slopes of Mount Parnassus, Greece","The site of Delphi, Greece"],"epidaure":["Epidaurus — Greece","The theatre of Epidaurus is famous for its prodigious acoustics: from the back rows, you can supposedly hear a coin drop in the centre of the orchestra. According to a 2007 study, the rows of limestone seats filter out the low frequencies of background noise and carry the actors' voices right up to the top.","14,000-seat ancient theatre from the 4th century BC with perfect acoustics, still in use today","Large ancient theatre of a healing sanctuary, southern peninsula of a Mediterranean country","Greek theatre with astonishing acoustics, 14,000 seats, Epidaurus","The theatre of Epidaurus, Greece"],"olympie":["Olympia — Greece","The ancient Olympic Games were held at Olympia every four years for nearly 1,200 years, from 776 BC to the end of the 4th century. The Olympic flame for each modern Games is lit there by sunlight concentrated in a parabolic mirror. The site contains the ruins of the Temple of Zeus and the workshop of Phidias, where his gold and ivory statue, now lost, was made.","Ancient sanctuary and birthplace of the Games, a temple that once housed a gold-and-ivory statue that was a wonder of the world","Ancient sanctuary at the confluence of two rivers, western peninsula of southern Europe","Birthplace of the Olympic Games, sanctuary of Zeus, Olympia","The sanctuary of Olympia, Greece"],"mont_etna":["Mount Etna — Sicily, Italy","Etna is the most active volcano in Europe, erupting almost every year. Its lava flows fertilise the Sicilian soil, which explains why locals don’t abandon it despite the risk — the tomatoes grown on its slopes are among the best in the world.","Highest active volcano on a continent (3,357 m), over 250 recorded eruptions, the forge of a blacksmith god","Large active volcano towering over the east coast of a big Mediterranean island","Highest active volcano in Europe, Etna, Sicily","Mount Etna, in Sicily, Italy"],"tropea":["Tropea — Calabria, Italy","Tropea is perched on a cliff of volcanic tuff above a white sand beach and a turquoise sea of exceptional clarity. Ranked among the most beautiful towns in Italy, it is famous for its cipolla rossa PGI — a sweet red onion so renowned that it is made into jams, beers and ice creams.","Medieval town on a tuff cliff, a chapel on an isolated rock, a famous red onion, turquoise sea","Clifftop village overlooking a beach, Tyrrhenian coast at the far south of a peninsula","Medieval clifftop village overlooking a beach, Calabrian coast","Tropea, in Calabria, Italy"],"valle_d_itria":["Valle d'Itria — Apulia, Italy","The Valle d'Itria stretches between Alberobello, Locorotondo, Cisternino and Martina Franca — white villages perched on hills covered with olive trees, vineyards and scattered trulli. This limestone plateau mainly produces white wines, such as Locorotondo and Martina Franca, both DOC appellations.","Karst valley dotted with conical dry-stone houses, vineyards and white hilltop villages","Limestone plateau with white hilltop villages and conical houses, in the heel of a Mediterranean peninsula","Valley of the trulli between Alberobello, Locorotondo and Martina Franca, Apulia","The Valle d'Itria in Apulia, Italy"],"civita_di_bagnoregio":["Civita di Bagnoregio — Lazio, Italy","Civita di Bagnoregio is nicknamed “the dying town”: the edges of its tuff outcrop crumble away regularly, and its dozen or so permanent residents are connected to the world only by a single footbridge. Founded by the Etruscans 2,500 years ago, the town is inexorably shrinking with each landslide.","Dying Etruscan town on a tuff pinnacle, a dozen residents, linked to the world by a single footbridge","Dying village on an isolated tuff pinnacle, clay ravines in the north of a central Italian region","Dying Etruscan town on a pinnacle, a single access bridge, Lazio","Civita di Bagnoregio, in Lazio, Italy"],"portofino":["Portofino — Liguria, Italy","Portofino, a village of 500 inhabitants, is one of the most expensive destinations in Europe. Its trompe-l’œil houses — façades painted with imaginary windows, balconies and decorations — are a centuries-old Ligurian tradition. A weekend rental in one of these houses can cost as much as a car.","Fishing village with trompe-l’œil painted façades around a glamorous square, a small sheltered bay","Picturesque little harbour on a Riviera headland, Ligurian Sea, north-west of a peninsula","Chic fishing village with colourful houses, Gulf of Tigullio, Portofino","Portofino, in Liguria, Italy"],"abou_simbel":["Abu Simbel — Egypt","Between 1964 and 1968, an operation unique in history cut the Abu Simbel temples into blocks and reassembled them 65 metres higher and some 200 metres further back, to save them from the rising waters of Lake Nasser. Around fifty nations took part in this rescue, coordinated by UNESCO.","Two rock-cut temples of a great pharaoh, with a façade of 20 m colossi, moved in the 1960s to escape the waters of a dam","Rock-cut temples on the shore of a large artificial lake, far south of the country, near a border","Temples of Ramesses II moved for the dam, far south of Egypt","The Abu Simbel temples, Egypt"],"wadi_rum":["Wadi Rum — Jordan","The Bedouin of Wadi Rum have inhabited this red sandstone desert for thousands of years. Nabataean inscriptions carved into the rock walls trace the incense and spice trade routes. Lawrence of Arabia camped here during his Arab campaign of 1917–1918.","Desert of red sandstone and granite with natural arches and Bedouin camps, the setting of epic films","Desert of red sandstone mountains, far south of the country, close to a Red Sea port","Moon-like red sandstone desert and Bedouin camps, southern Jordan","Wadi Rum, Jordan"],"masada":["Masada — Israel","The fall of Masada in AD 73 brought the phrase “Masada shall not fall again” into modern Israeli culture. The phrase symbolises the determination never to surrender — Israeli army recruits, notably from the armoured corps, have come here to take their oath.","Fortress of a builder king on a 400 m mesa, Roman siege of AD 73 and the mass suicide of its defenders","Ancient fortress perched on a mesa beside a hypersaline lake, southern desert","Herodian fortress on a mesa, national symbol, shore of the Dead Sea","The fortress of Masada, Israel"],"mer_morte":["Dead Sea — Israel/Jordan","The Dead Sea, 430 metres below sea level, is the lowest point on the Earth’s surface. Its 34% salinity (compared with 3.5% for the oceans) makes swimming impossible — bathers float effortlessly. It is shrinking by 1 metre a year because of the diversion of the Jordan River’s waters.","Hypersaline lake at the lowest point on dry land (-430 m), ten times saltier than the ocean","Hypersaline lake at the bottom of a rift valley, on the border between two Middle Eastern countries","Lake at the lowest point on Earth, Israel–Jordan border","The Dead Sea, Israel/Jordan"],"pont_charles":["Charles Bridge — Prague, Czech Republic","Prague’s Charles Bridge was built according to medieval numerology: the foundation stone was laid on 9 July 1357 at 5:31 a.m., forming the palindromic sequence 1357 9 7 5 31. This “magic” date was meant to guarantee the bridge’s eternal strength.","516 m stone bridge (1357–1402) adorned with 30 Baroque statues, towers at each end, over a river","Medieval bridge with Baroque statues spanning a river between two historic districts, Bohemia","Medieval bridge with 30 Baroque statues over the Vltava, Prague","Charles Bridge, Prague, Czech Republic"],"horloge_astronomique":["Prague Astronomical Clock — Prague, Czech Republic","Prague’s Orloj is one of the oldest astronomical clocks still in operation (1410). According to legend, the town council had its creator, Master Hanuš, blinded so that he could never build such a perfect clock anywhere else.","Medieval astronomical clock from 1410 on a tower, a mechanical procession of the twelve apostles every hour","Astronomical clock on the town hall tower, main square of a Bohemian capital","1410 astronomical clock with its procession of apostles, Prague","The Astronomical Clock, Prague, Czech Republic"],"chateau_de_bojnice":["Bojnice Castle — Slovakia","Bojnice Castle is the most visited castle in Slovakia. It was extensively remodelled in the Romantic Neo-Gothic style in the 19th century by Count János Pálffy, who wanted to create a fairy-tale setting. Its basement contains a natural prehistoric cave built into the complex.","Fairy-tale Neo-Gothic castle remodelled in 1889, moat, round tower and catacombs, on a hill","Romantic fairy-tale castle on a hill in a Central European country, 20 km from a mining town","Most visited romantic castle in the country, Bojnice, Slovakia","Bojnice Castle, Slovakia"],"chateau_de_hrad_spissky":["Spiš Castle (Spišský hrad) — Slovakia","Spiš Castle is the largest medieval fortress in Central Europe, covering 41,000 m². Built in the 12th century on a limestone hill, it housed Hungarian kings and counts palatine, and withstood the Mongol invasion of 1241. A fire partly destroyed it in 1780.","Largest medieval castle ruin in Central Europe (more than 41,000 m²), founded in the 12th century, on a limestone hill","Vast ruined fortress overlooking a plain, east of a Central European country, near the Tatras","Largest fortress in Central Europe, Spiš Castle, Slovakia","Spiš Castle (Spišský hrad), Slovakia"],"hallstatt":["Hallstatt — Austria","Hallstatt’s cemetery is so small that bones exhumed after 10–12 years are decorated with patterns and placed in a skull chapel — the Beinhaus. This unique tradition keeps alive the memory of the dead in a town where the dead outnumber the living.","Lakeside village of houses huddled at the water’s edge, 7,000 years of salt mining, reflections on an Alpine lake","Picture-postcard village at the foot of mountains, on the shore of a lake in an Alpine salt region","UNESCO-listed lakeside salt village, Salzkammergut, Austria","The village of Hallstatt, Austria"],"opera_de_vienne":["Vienna State Opera — Austria","The Vienna State Opera gives some 300 performances a season under the repertory system: a different work almost every night, with very few rehearsals for revivals. Its famous Opera Ball, held every winter, is the most sought-after society event in Austria, broadcast live on national television.","Neo-Renaissance opera house of 1869 with some 300 performances a year, grand gala ball in winter, on a ring boulevard","Grand Neo-Renaissance opera house on the ring boulevard of a Danube capital","Grand Neo-Renaissance opera house with a famous ball, Vienna","Vienna State Opera, Austria"],"grossglockner":["Grossglockner — Austria","The Grossglockner (3,798 m) is the highest peak in Austria and the highest point of the Eastern Alps. Its scenic toll road, one of the most beautiful in Europe, climbs to 2,504 metres through 36 hairpin bends. The Pasterze Glacier, visible from the road, has retreated 3.5 km since 1850 — the fastest glacial retreat in Austria.","Highest peak in the country (3,798 m), a 7 km glacier, a winding Alpine toll road opened in 1935","Highest peak in the country and its glacier, Central Alps, national park of an Alpine country","Highest peak in Austria, glacier and scenic Alpine road","Grossglockner, Austria"],"lac_bled":["Lake Bled — Slovenia (island)","Bled Island is the only natural island in Slovenia. Its bell, cast in 1534, is one of Europe’s “magic” bells: according to tradition, grooms who climb its 99 steps carrying their bride and ring the bell three times will have their wish granted.","Small island with a Baroque church in the middle of a lake, wishing-bell tradition, the country’s only natural island","Island with a Baroque church in the middle of a glacial lake, Julian Alps of a small Alpine country","Island and Baroque church on Lake Bled, Slovenia","The island on Lake Bled, Slovenia"],"grottes_de_postojna":["Postojna Cave — Slovenia","Postojna Cave is home to the olm (Proteus anguinus), a blind amphibian that can live up to 100 years and go 12 years without eating. This translucent, pigmentless creature is nicknamed “the cave dragon”.","24 km of underground passages toured by little train since 1872, refuge of a blind cave amphibian","Largest show cave in Europe, karst of a small Alpine country, 50 km from the capital","Karst passages visited by train, home of the blind olm, Slovenia","Postojna Cave, Slovenia"],"vallee_de_la_so_a":["Soča Valley — Slovenia","The Soča (Isonzo in Italian) is a river with uniquely emerald blue-green waters, rising in the Slovenian Julian Alps. Its valley was the scene of the Battles of the Isonzo (1915–1917) and is today a natural paradise renowned for its gorges and white-water sports.","River with emerald waters, scene of terrible mountain battles in 1915–1917, rising in the Alps","Emerald river with gorges, Julian Alps, on the border between two European countries","Emerald river of the Julian Alps, Soča, Slovenia","The Soča Valley, Slovenia"],"marche_central":["Central Market Hall — Budapest, Hungary","Budapest’s Great Market Hall opened in 1897 and covers 10,000 m². Its first-floor galleries have been selling Hungarian embroidery and paprika for a century. Its roof of glazed Zsolnay tiles is decorated with patterns inspired by Magyar folk art — a hallmark of Budapest Art Nouveau architecture.","Largest covered market hall in the country (1897), roof of colourful glazed tiles, paprika and foie gras","Large covered market hall on a riverbank, capital at the crossroads of Central Europe","Largest covered market hall in the country, colourful tiled roofs, Budapest","Central Market Hall, Budapest, Hungary"],"thermes_szechenyi":["Széchenyi Thermal Bath — Budapest, Hungary","The Széchenyi Baths, opened in 1913, are fed by two natural thermal springs gushing at 74°C and 77°C. With 18 pools, they are the largest thermal baths in Europe. Locals play chess in the outdoor pools — a tradition photographed all over the world.","Largest thermal baths in Europe (1913), springs at 74°C, chess players in steaming pools in the depths of winter","Large Neo-Baroque spa complex in a city park of a Danube capital","Largest thermal baths in Europe, chess in hot water, Budapest","Széchenyi Thermal Bath, Budapest, Hungary"],"chateau_d_if":["Château d'If — Marseille, France","The Château d'If, a fortress built from 1527 onwards on an island off Marseille, served as a state prison: thousands of Protestants were locked up there, as was the young Mirabeau. Alexandre Dumas made it the prison of The Count of Monte Cristo — the novel was such a success that tourists came looking for “Dantès's cell”.","Island fortress from 1524 turned state prison, setting of a famous novel of escape and revenge","Fort-prison on an island off a major Mediterranean port in the south of the country","Island fortress-prison of The Count of Monte Cristo, off Marseille","Château d'If, Marseille, France"],"etangs_de_camargue":["Camargue — France","The Camargue is home to the largest colony of greater flamingos in Europe, with 10,000 breeding pairs. This Rhône delta also hosts France’s only wild horses and bulls. The gardians — Camargue cowboys — carry on a centuries-old herding tradition.","Vast 930 km² delta with pink flamingos, white horses and bulls herded by horsemen","Large marshy river delta between an ancient town and the Mediterranean, Provence","Delta of pink flamingos and white horses, Camargue, Provence","The Camargue lagoons, France"],"hateau_des_baux_de_provence":["Château des Baux-de-Provence — France","Les Baux-de-Provence was one of the most powerful lordships in Provence in the Middle Ages. The village and its ruined castle, partly carved out of the limestone of the Alpilles, overlook a plain of olive groves and vineyards. Its Carrières de Lumières project monumental digital shows onto walls 14 metres high.","Ruined medieval citadel perched on a limestone ridge, stronghold of warrior lords, which gave its name to an ore","Ruined hilltop village on the ridge of a Provençal massif, 20 km from an ancient town","Hilltop village of Les Baux in the Alpilles, Provence","Château des Baux-de-Provence, France"],"gorges_de_l_ardeche":["Gorges de l'Ardèche — France","The Pont d'Arc, a natural arch with a 59-metre span over the Ardèche, is one of the largest natural arches in Europe. The Chauvet Cave, discovered nearby in 1994, contains some of the oldest known cave paintings (around 36,000 years old), listed by UNESCO in 2014.","Natural arch with a 59 m span over a river, 30 km of limestone gorges, decorated prehistoric cave nearby","Large natural arch and limestone gorges explored by canoe, south of a south-eastern region","Largest natural arch in Europe and gorges by canoe, Ardèche","The Gorges de l'Ardèche, France"],"ile_de_re":["Île de Ré — France","The citadel of Saint-Martin-de-Ré, fortified by Vauban and UNESCO-listed, served as the depot from which convicts left for the penal colony in French Guiana until 1938. The island, linked to the mainland by a bridge nearly 3 km long since 1988, lives off salt marshes, vineyards and tourism.","Low-lying island of salt marshes and vineyards, a listed military citadel, linked to the mainland by a long bridge","White island linked by a bridge, salt marshes, Bay of Biscay, Atlantic coast","White island with a citadel and salt marshes, off La Rochelle","Île de Ré, France"],"phare_de_cordouan":["Cordouan Lighthouse — France","The Cordouan Lighthouse, nicknamed “the King of Lighthouses”, is the oldest manned lighthouse in France still in service. Built from 1584 by Louis de Foix, it has a royal chapel, an apartment for the king and quarters for the keepers. UNESCO-listed in 2021.","68 m “King of lighthouses” built from 1584 to 1611 on a rocky plateau, with a royal chapel inside, surrounded by the tides","Oldest manned lighthouse in the country, in the middle of a vast estuary on the Atlantic coast","Oldest manned lighthouse in France, Gironde estuary","Cordouan Lighthouse, France"],"puy_de_dome":["Puy de Dôme — Auvergne, France","In 1648 Pascal had mercury taken to the summit of the Puy de Dôme to prove that atmospheric pressure decreases with altitude — a founding experiment of modern physics. He didn’t make the climb himself, but asked his brother-in-law to do it.","1,465 m dormant volcano, summit of a chain of puys, where atmospheric pressure was proven in 1648","Highest volcano in a volcanic chain, 15 km from a large Auvergne city","Highest volcano in the Chaîne des Puys, Auvergne","The Puy de Dôme, in Auvergne, France"],"gouffre_de_padirac":["Gouffre de Padirac — France","The Gouffre de Padirac, discovered in 1889 by Édouard Martel, hides an underground river that visitors travel up by boat. Medieval legend says the chasm was opened by the Devil himself, stamping his foot on the ground when Saint Martin refused to sell him his soul.","Natural shaft 33 m across plunging 103 m deep, underground river and a vast chamber, explored from 1889","Chasm with an underground river toured by boat, limestone plateau of a south-western region","103 m chasm with an underground river by boat, Lot","The Gouffre de Padirac, France"],"abbaye_de_fontevraud":["Fontevraud Abbey — France","Fontevraud Abbey is the largest medieval abbey in France. It holds the effigies of Richard the Lionheart, Eleanor of Aquitaine and Henry II Plantagenet. Turned into a Napoleonic prison from 1804 to 1963, it still has cells in its nave — a striking mix of the sacred and the penal.","Largest monastic complex in its country, founded in 1101, necropolis of a dynasty of king-counts","Vast medieval abbey and royal necropolis, valley of a great river, 15 km from a historic town","Largest medieval abbey in France, tombs of the Plantagenets, Loire Valley","Fontevraud Abbey, France"],"cap_blanc_nez":["Cap Blanc-Nez — France","Cap Blanc-Nez, a 134-metre chalk cliff facing the English coast, visible in clear weather, was one of the starting points for the first swims across the Channel. It bears the Dover Patrol monument, which commemorates the French and British sailors lost at sea during the First World War.","134 m white chalk cliff facing the English coast, monument to sailors of the Great War","High chalk cliff facing England, Opal coastline in the north of the country","White cliff 34 km from England, Opal Coast","Cap Blanc-Nez, France"],"dunes_du_pilat":["Dune of Pilat — Arcachon, France","The Dune of Pilat is the highest in Europe and keeps advancing 1 to 5 metres a year towards the pine forest. It has already swallowed a road, a campsite and a house since the early 20th century.","Highest sand dune in Europe (107 m, 3 km long), slowly advancing on the forest, facing the ocean","Huge sand dune between ocean and pine forest, south-western Atlantic coast","Biggest dune in Europe, Arcachon Bay, France","The Dune of Pilat near Arcachon, France"],"abbaye_du_thoronet":["Le Thoronet Abbey — Provence, France","Le Thoronet Abbey, one of the three “Cistercian sisters” of Provence, is famous for its austere Romanesque architecture and exceptional acoustics: its long reverberation carries a cappella singing without any amplification. Musicians and vocal ensembles still come here to record.","Cistercian abbey from 1160 with stark Romanesque architecture and perfect acoustics (1.8 s reverberation)","Austere Romanesque abbey at the bottom of a wooded dell, Provençal hinterland, 30 km from a town in the Var","Cistercian abbey with perfect acoustics, Provençal Romanesque, Var","Le Thoronet Abbey in Provence, France"],"alberobello":["Alberobello — Apulia, Italy","The trulli of Alberobello are conical limestone buildings without mortar, inherited from a prehistoric technique. In the 17th century, their removable roofs allowed residents to quickly demolish their homes to avoid royal taxes — a house without a roof was not considered inhabited.","Over a thousand conical limestone houses built without mortar, roofs once dismantled to escape taxes","Town of white conical houses in a valley in the heel of a Mediterranean peninsula","Town of conical trulli houses, Alberobello, Apulia","Alberobello and its trulli, in Apulia, Italy"],"parc_des_abruzzes":["Abruzzo National Park — Italy","Abruzzo National Park was created in 1923 to save the Marsican brown bear from extinction. Around sixty remain today, almost all in the park and its surroundings. The park is also home to Apennine wolves and the Abruzzo chamois, an endemic subspecies saved in the nick of time.","One of the country’s oldest national parks (1923), last refuge of an endemic brown bear and wolf","Mountain national park in a central range of a Mediterranean peninsula","National park with Apennine bears and wolves, Abruzzo","Abruzzo National Park, Italy"],"syracuse":["Syracuse — Sicily, Italy","Syracuse was one of the largest cities of the ancient world in the 5th century BC, rivalling Athens and Carthage. Its rock-cut Greek theatre still hosts performances of Greek tragedies every spring. Archimedes was born here around 287 BC and defended the city against the Romans with his war machines.","Greek colony founded in 734 BC on a small island, a 15,000-seat ancient theatre and a legendary echo cave","Ancient Greek city on the Ionian coast of a large Mediterranean island","Ancient Greek city and its theatre, Ortygia island, Sicily","Syracuse and its Greek theatre, Sicily, Italy"],"palerme":["Palermo Cathedral — Palermo, Italy","Palermo Cathedral, consecrated in 1185 under Archbishop Gualtiero Offamilio, layers Norman, Gothic, Catalan and Baroque styles, topped by a neoclassical dome added in the late 18th century. It holds the royal tombs of Roger II and Emperor Frederick II, as well as the chapel of Saint Rosalia, the city's patron saint.","Cathedral of 1185 blending Norman arches, Gothic towers and a neoclassical dome, housing the tomb of a German emperor","Cathedral of layered styles, from Norman to Baroque, in the capital of a large Mediterranean island","Norman cathedral with royal tombs, Palermo, Sicily","Palermo Cathedral, in Sicily, Italy"],"scala_dei_turchi":["Scala dei Turchi — Sicily, Italy","The Scala dei Turchi owes its name to the Saracen pirates who are said to have used its white marl steps as a shelter during their raids. Marl, a soft limestone, is naturally carved into perfect steps by the action of the waves and the wind.","Dazzling white marl cliff sculpted into natural steps by sea and wind, plunging into the blue","Stepped white cliff on the south coast of a large Mediterranean island","Stepped white cliff sculpted by the sea, southern Sicily","The Scala dei Turchi, in Sicily, Italy"],"vallee_des_temples":["Valley of the Temples — Agrigento, Italy","The 2,500-year-old Greek temples of Agrigento are better preserved than most temples in Greece itself. The Temple of Concordia owes its survival to its conversion into a Christian church in the 6th century, which saved it from being dismantled.","A row of seven Doric temples from the 5th century BC, better preserved than many temples in Greece","Ancient Greek temples on a hill on the south coast of a large Mediterranean island","Seven remarkably well-preserved Greek Doric temples, Agrigento","The Valley of the Temples in Agrigento, Italy"],"palmeraie_de_skoura":["Skoura palm grove — Morocco","The Skoura palm grove is home to ksour — rammed-earth castles — some of which have been inhabited since the 17th century. These mud-brick fortresses, built without architects, withstand earthquakes better than many modern buildings.","Oasis of 45,000 date palms dotted with fortified 17th-century ksour, on a road of kasbahs","Oasis of palm trees and earthen fortresses, pre-Saharan valley, Ouarzazate region","Oasis of 45,000 palms and kasbahs, Moroccan pre-Sahara","The Skoura palm grove, in Morocco"],"gondar":["Gondar — Ethiopia (Fasilides)","Gondar, nicknamed the “Camelot of Africa”, is home to the Fasil Ghebbi — a 17th-century royal enclosure containing 5 castles. The influence of Indian, Arab and Portuguese architects created a unique architectural style. Fasilides' Bath is filled once a year for the Timkat festival (Epiphany) — thousands of worshippers plunge into it.","Royal enclosure ringed by a 900 m wall, with 17th-century castles and the sacred pool of an Epiphany festival","Fortified royal city of castles, former imperial capital of an East African country","Royal enclosure with five 17th-century castles, Gondar, Ethiopia","Gondar and the royal enclosure of Fasil Ghebbi, in Ethiopia"],"parc_de_l_etosha":["Etosha National Park — Namibia","The Etosha salt pan was once a vast inland lake. Dried up thousands of years ago, its white salt bed is now visible from space. In the dry season, elephants, lions, rhinos and zebras converge on the only waterholes — offering unique safari scenes.","Park around a huge 4,800 km² salt pan, waterholes floodlit at night, black rhinos and lions","Wildlife park around a great salt plain, north of a country in southern Africa","Park with a great salt plain and night safaris, Etosha, Namibia","Etosha National Park in Namibia"],"lamu":["Lamu — Kenya","Lamu Old Town is the only medieval Swahili town still inhabited and almost intact. It has no cars or motorbikes — donkeys and boats are the only means of transport, as they have been for centuries.","Oldest Swahili town still inhabited, car-free, roamed by donkeys and dhows, carved doors","Car-free old Swahili town on an island, East African coast, Indian Ocean","Oldest inhabited Swahili town, donkeys and dhows, Lamu, Kenya","Lamu Old Town, in Kenya"],"chutes_de_tugela":["Tugela Falls — South Africa","The Tugela Falls plunge in 5 successive drops from the top of the Drakensberg. In winter the water partially freezes, creating spectacular ice falls visible only from the heights.","Second-highest waterfall in the world (948 m), plunging off a plateau in five successive drops","Very high waterfall in a mountain range, east of a country in southern Africa","World's 2nd-highest waterfall, Drakensberg, South Africa","Tugela Falls in South Africa"],"lac_malawi":["Lake Malawi — Malawi","Lake Malawi is home to more freshwater fish species than any other lake — nearly 1,000 species of endemic cichlids. Its water is so clear you can see down to 20 metres. The lake covers 20% of Malawi's surface area and is the country's main source of animal protein.","Third-largest lake on the continent (29,600 km²), 1,000 species of colourful endemic fish, crystal-clear waters","Great Rift lake with endemic fish, shared between three East African countries","Africa's 3rd-largest lake, 1,000 endemic fish species, Lake Malawi","Lake Malawi, between Malawi, Mozambique and Tanzania"],"boulders_beach":["Boulders Beach — South Africa","The penguin colony at Boulders Beach was founded by just two pairs in 1982. Today it numbers more than 3,000 birds. These African penguins (Spheniscus demersus) are classified as “endangered” — their global population has fallen by 70% since 1970.","Beach of huge granite boulders colonised since 1982 by a colony of African penguins","Penguin beach in a village on the Cape Peninsula, south-west of a country in southern Africa","African penguin beach, Cape Peninsula, South Africa","Boulders Beach, near Cape Town, South Africa"],"ephese":["Ephesus — Turkey (Library of Celsus)","The Library of Celsus in Ephesus, built in AD 135, held 12,000 parchment scrolls. Its façade creates an optical illusion: larger central columns make it look taller than it really is. The tomb of the consul Gaius Julius Celsus lies directly beneath the building he funded.","Two-storey façade of a Roman library from 135, niches holding statues of the virtues, ancient city","Roman library of a great ancient city, Aegean coast of a country straddling two continents","Library of Celsus, jewel of Ephesus, Turkish Aegean coast","The Library of Celsus at Ephesus, in Turkey"],"aspendos":["Aspendos — Turkey","The theatre of Aspendos, built in the 2nd century AD, can hold 15,000 spectators and is so well preserved that it still hosts the Aspendos International Opera Festival every summer. Its 21-metre-high stage wall is the only one from that era still intact in the Mediterranean world.","2nd-century Roman theatre with 15,000 seats, the world's only 21 m stage wall still intact","Remarkably well-preserved Roman theatre, Mediterranean coast of a country straddling two continents","Roman theatre with an intact stage, 15,000 seats, Antalya province","The theatre of Aspendos, in Turkey"],"yosemite":["Yosemite National Park — California, USA","El Capitan, Yosemite's 914-metre vertical granite wall, was climbed for the first time in a free solo — with no rope or protection — by Alex Honnold on 3 June 2017. The ascent was described as “the greatest athletic feat of all time”.","Glacial valley with 900 m granite walls, one pillar climbed free solo, waterfalls and sequoias","Park of sheer granite walls and waterfalls, in the heart of the Sierra Nevada range","Park with 900 m granite walls, El Capitan, California","Yosemite National Park, in California, USA"],"bryce_canyon":["Bryce Canyon National Park — Utah, USA","The “hoodoos” of Bryce Canyon form over 3 million years through alternating freeze-thaw cycles that split the rock. The park loses about 30 cm of its formations every 50 to 65 years — a decline imperceptible on a human scale, but constant.","Natural amphitheatre filled with thousands of pink and orange hoodoos, at 2,700 m altitude","Forest of colourful hoodoos on a high plateau, 90 km from a park of red canyons","Forest of pink hoodoos, Bryce Canyon, Utah","Bryce Canyon, in Utah, USA"],"death_valley":["Death Valley — California, USA","Death Valley holds the world record for the highest recorded temperature (56.7°C in 1913). Paradoxically, it bursts into spectacular wildflowers during rare “superblooms”, when dormant seeds germinate after exceptional rainfall.","Lowest and hottest point on the continent (-85 m, record 56.7°C), stones that slide on their own across a dry lake","Desert basin at the lowest and hottest point on the continent, between two western mountain ranges","Death Valley, lowest and hottest point in the USA, California","Death Valley, in California, USA"],"arches_national_park":["Arches National Park — Utah, USA","Arches National Park contains more than 2,000 natural red sandstone arches over an area of 300 km². Delicate Arch, 16 metres high, is the emblem of Utah printed on the state's licence plates. In 2008 Wall Arch, one of the largest, suddenly collapsed during the night.","Park of 2,000 red sandstone arches, including a slender, world-famous 16 m arch","Park of red sandstone arches on the Colorado Plateau, 8 km from a small hiking town","Park of 2,000 natural arches, Delicate Arch, Utah","Arches National Park, in Utah, USA"],"seattle_space_needle":["Space Needle — Seattle, USA","Seattle's Space Needle was built in about 400 days for the 1962 World's Fair. Its former revolving restaurant, which made a full turn in 47 minutes, gave way in 2018 to “The Loupe”, the world's first rotating glass floor. It was designed to withstand winds of more than 300 km/h and a magnitude 9.1 earthquake.","184 m saucer-shaped observation tower, built for a 1962 World's Fair, revolving restaurant","Flying-saucer-shaped tower in an urban park, major port city of the Pacific Northwest","Saucer tower from the '62 World's Fair, Space Needle, Seattle","The Space Needle in Seattle, USA"],"chicago_cloud_gate":["Cloud Gate — Chicago, USA","Anish Kapoor's Cloud Gate, nicknamed “The Bean”, is made of 168 stainless steel plates welded together with no visible seams. It reflects the Chicago skyline, distorting it. Its 110 tonnes rest on just 6 anchor points — its weight is offset by a structure buried beneath the park.","110-tonne bean-shaped sculpture of polished steel, a distorting mirror of sky and skyscrapers, 2006","Steel mirror sculpture in an urban park, Loop district of a big Great Lakes city","Giant reflective bean sculpture, Millennium Park, Chicago","Cloud Gate (“The Bean”), Millennium Park, Chicago, USA"],"niagara_on_the_lake":["Niagara-on-the-Lake — Canada","Niagara-on-the-Lake was the first capital of Upper Canada in 1792. Burned by the Americans in 1813, it was rebuilt in a uniform Georgian style. Its perfectly preserved Victorian architecture has earned it the title of “prettiest town in Canada”.","Victorian town on the shore of a great lake, fort from 1796, vineyards on an escarpment, theatre festival","Small wine town at the mouth of a famous river, between two great lakes","Victorian town and vineyards near Niagara Falls, Ontario","Niagara-on-the-Lake in Ontario, Canada"],"capilano_suspension_bridge":["Capilano Suspension Bridge — Vancouver, Canada","The Capilano Suspension Bridge sways 70 metres above the Capilano River. The original bridge, built in 1889 with hemp ropes and cedar planks, was replaced by a steel-cable bridge in 1903, then completely rebuilt in 1956.","136 m suspension bridge spanning a canyon 70 m up, built in 1889 in a rainforest","Suspension bridge over a wooded canyon, north shore of a large Pacific coastal city","Capilano Suspension Bridge in the rainforest, Vancouver","Capilano Suspension Bridge, Vancouver, Canada"],"gaspesie":["Gaspé Peninsula — Quebec, Canada","Percé Rock, on the Gaspé Peninsula, is among the largest natural sea arches in the world. Nearby Bonaventure Island hosts one of the largest accessible northern gannet colonies in the world, with more than 100,000 birds.","88 m pierced rock offshore, nearby island home to 100,000 gannets, high coastal mountains","Peninsula with a pierced rock and a bird colony, gulf of a great river, French-speaking North America","Pierced rock and gannet colony, Gulf of St. Lawrence, Quebec","The Gaspé Peninsula, in Quebec, Canada"],"churchill":["Churchill — Manitoba, Canada (polar bears)","Churchill's polar bears gather while waiting for Hudson Bay to freeze so they can go hunting for seals. It is the only town in the world where you can watch wild polar bears from special vehicles in autumn, before they head out onto the ice.","Polar bear capital of the world: hundreds wait for the sea ice in autumn, skies lit by the northern lights","Small Arctic town on the shore of a large bay, reachable only by train or plane","The only town to see wild polar bears, Hudson Bay, Manitoba","Churchill, in Manitoba, Canada (polar bears)"],"baie_de_fundy":["Bay of Fundy — New Brunswick, Canada","The Bay of Fundy's tides, the highest in the world, move around 160 billion tonnes of water with each cycle. At low tide, you can walk at the foot of the Hopewell Rocks, sculpted into giant flowerpots; a few hours later, the sea has risen more than 10 metres and only their wooded tops still show.","Highest tides in the world (16 m), flowerpot-shaped rocks, billions of tonnes of water twice a day","Bay with record tides between two Maritime provinces, on the Atlantic seaboard of a large North American country","World's highest tides and flowerpot rocks, New Brunswick","The Bay of Fundy, in New Brunswick, Canada"],"colca_canyon":["Colca Canyon — Peru","Peru's Colca Canyon ranks among the deepest in the world — about twice the depth of the Grand Canyon. The Andean condor, one of the largest flying birds with a wingspan of more than 3 m, soars there every morning above the Cruz del Cóndor viewpoint.","Canyon more than 3,000 m deep, one of the deepest in the world, where condors soar above pre-Inca terraces","Very deep Andean canyon where condors fly, 150 km from a large city in the south of the country","Colca Canyon and its soaring condors, one of the deepest in the world, Peru","Colca Canyon, in Peru"],"chan_chan":["Chan Chan — Trujillo, Peru","Chan Chan, capital of the Chimú kingdom, was conquered by the Incas in 1470 — 50 years before the Spanish arrived. The Incas so admired the sophistication of Chimú metalwork that they deported hundreds of goldsmiths to Cusco to work at their court.","Largest pre-Columbian adobe city in the world (20 km²), capital of a coastal kingdom (850–1470)","Huge ruined mud-brick city, Andean coastal desert in the north of the country","Largest pre-Columbian adobe city, Chimú capital, near Trujillo","Chan Chan, near Trujillo, Peru"],"carnaval_de_barranquilla":["Barranquilla Carnival — Colombia","The Barranquilla Carnival is the second-largest carnival in the world after Rio's. Inscribed by UNESCO as Intangible Cultural Heritage in 2003, it fuses African, Indigenous and Spanish traditions. Its Batalla de Flores (battle of flowers), the Great Parade and the Garabato (dance of death and life) last 4 days before Shrove Tuesday.","The world's second carnival, four days of cumbia and a battle of flowers, recognised oral heritage","Great carnival of a city on the Caribbean coast of a South American country","World's 2nd carnival after Rio, cumbia and mapalé, Caribbean coast of Colombia","The Barranquilla Carnival in Colombia"],"roraima":["Mount Roraima — Venezuela (tepui)","Mount Roraima (2,810 m) is a tepui — a table mountain of Precambrian sandstone 1.8 billion years old. Its 31 km² summit, permanently in the clouds, is home to unique plant species, 35% of which are endemic. Arthur Conan Doyle drew on it for “The Lost World” (1912) — a plateau where dinosaurs and primitive creatures survive.","Flat-topped sandstone table mountain 1.8 billion years old, summit in the clouds, at the tripoint of three countries","Table-top plateau (tepui) in an Amazonian park, at the tripoint of three South American countries","Table mountain in the clouds, Venezuela–Brazil–Guyana tripoint","Mount Roraima, Venezuela (tepui)"],"lencois_maranhenses":["Lençóis Maranhenses — Brazil","The dunes of Lençóis Maranhenses are constantly shifting in the wind. Despite their desert-like appearance, the rainwater lagoons that form there, at their peak from June to September, are home to fish such as the trahira, which can survive buried in damp mud during the dry season.","155,000 ha of white sand dunes dotted with temporary turquoise lagoons from June to September","Field of white dunes with blue lakes, tropical Atlantic coast in the north-east of the subcontinent","White dunes with temporary turquoise lakes, north-eastern Brazil","The Lençóis Maranhenses, in Brazil"],"ouro_preto":["Ouro Preto — Minas Gerais, Brazil","Ouro Preto (“black gold”, after the iron oxide that tarnished its nuggets) was the epicentre of the 18th-century gold rush that made Brazil the world's leading producer. The town drew in so much wealth that Portugal was able to fund the rebuilding of Lisbon after the 1755 earthquake.","Baroque town born of gold around 1700, thirteen churches including a masterpiece by a disabled sculptor nicknamed “the little cripple”","Baroque gold town on a serra, 95 km from a large mining city in the south-east of the subcontinent","Baroque gold town with 13 churches, Ouro Preto, Minas Gerais","Ouro Preto, in Minas Gerais, Brazil"],"salvador_de_bahia":["Salvador, Bahia — Brazil","Salvador, Bahia, Brazil's first capital (1549–1763), was one of the main slave-trade ports in the Americas: more than a million enslaved Africans landed there. Today its culture — music, food, capoeira, candomblé — remains deeply shaped by its African heritage.","Cobbled colonial quarter, church with 800 kg of gold, birthplace of capoeira and candomblé, former colonial capital","Old colonial town on a large bay, north-east coast of the subcontinent","Former colonial capital, the Pelourinho district and capoeira, Bahia","Salvador, Bahia, in Brazil"],"mendoza":["Mendoza — Argentina (vineyards)","Mendoza's vineyards have been irrigated for centuries by a network of ancient Inca canals, still in use today. The altitude of 800 to 1,500 metres protects the vines from fungal diseases and produces some of the purest wines in the world.","Wine region of high-altitude Malbec, irrigated by canals inherited from the Incas, in the shadow of the Andes","Large high-altitude wine region in the Andean foothills, west of a South American country","Malbec vineyards at the foot of the Andes, Mendoza, Argentina","The vineyards of Mendoza, in Argentina"],"rapa_nui":["Rano Raraku — Easter Island, Chile","Rano Raraku is the volcanic crater where nearly 95% of Easter Island's moai were carved. On its slopes, some 400 statues remain planted in the ground: these “heads” are in fact complete statues, their bodies buried under centuries of sediment.","Volcanic quarry where almost all the giant statues of a remote island were carved, heads emerging from the slope","Crater-quarry dotted with half-buried monumental statues, a very remote volcanic island in the Pacific","Quarry of the moai with giant buried heads, Easter Island","Rano Raraku, on Easter Island (Rapa Nui), Chile"],"waitomo":["Waitomo — New Zealand (caves)","Waitomo's glow-worms are not worms but the larvae of the fungus gnat Arachnocampa luminosa. They produce their blue light to lure other insects into their sticky silk threads. A single larva can produce up to 70 hanging threads to trap its prey.","Karst caves whose ceilings are lit by thousands of bioluminescent glow-worms","Glow-worm caves in a region of the North Island, 200 km from a large city","Caves with ceilings of glowing larvae, North Island of New Zealand","The Waitomo Caves, in New Zealand"],"abel_tasman":["Abel Tasman National Park — New Zealand","Abel Tasman National Park is the smallest in New Zealand, but one of the most visited. Its Abel Tasman Coast Track, one of the country's “Great Walks”, runs for 60 km past golden sand beaches and turquoise coves. Fur seals and little blue penguins frequent its rocks, facing Tasman Bay.","Smallest but busiest of the country's parks, golden granite coast, turquoise beaches, seals","Coastal park of golden granite and turquoise beaches, north of the South Island","Park of golden granite beaches, the country's most visited, South Island of New Zealand","Abel Tasman National Park, in New Zealand"],"iles_cook":["Rarotonga — Cook Islands","Rarotonga, the main island of the Cook Islands, is an ancient volcano encircled by a lagoon and a barrier reef. Every year, the Te Maeva Nui festival celebrates with Polynesian dance and song the anniversary of the country's self-government, gained in 1965 in free association with New Zealand.","Volcanic island with a lagoon and barrier reef, 652 m summit, in free association with a large Oceanian neighbour","Main island of a Polynesian archipelago in the South Pacific, associated with New Zealand","Main island of the Cook Islands, lagoon and reef, South Pacific","The Cook Islands (Rarotonga), South Pacific"],"vanuatu":["Mount Yasur — Tanna, Vanuatu","Mount Yasur, in Vanuatu, is one of the few active volcanoes where you can approach the crater rim to watch its regular explosions. For the people of Tanna island, attached to kastom (traditional custom), the volcano is a sacred, ancestral place.","361 m volcano said to be the most accessible in the world, spitting out sprays of lava every few minutes","Highly active volcano on an island, volcanic archipelago of Melanesia, South Pacific","The world's most accessible active volcano, Tanna Island, Vanuatu","Mount Yasur on Tanna Island, Vanuatu"],"kakadu":["Kakadu National Park — Northern Territory, Australia","Kakadu National Park is home to Aboriginal rock paintings up to 20,000 years old that form a living almanac. Present-day artists continue to add paintings to the rock walls, keeping alive a tradition unbroken since prehistoric times.","Largest park on the continent (nearly 20,000 km²), 20,000-year-old rock art, saltwater crocodiles","Vast park of wetlands and rock art, northern tropics, 150 km from a coastal city","Australia's largest park, Aboriginal rock paintings and saltwater crocodiles","Kakadu National Park, in the Northern Territory, Australia"],"blue_mountains":["Blue Mountains — New South Wales, Australia","The Blue Mountains owe their bluish tint to the haze of eucalyptus oil that constantly hangs in the air. The 5 million eucalyptus trees release a volatile oil that scatters blue light — an optical phenomenon unique to this region.","Sandstone plateau in a blue eucalyptus haze, three twin pinnacles, the world's steepest railway","Plateau of wooded cliffs in a blue haze, 90 km west of a large coastal metropolis","Plateau of cliffs and the Three Sisters, blue eucalyptus haze, New South Wales","The Blue Mountains, in New South Wales, Australia"],"nambung":["Nambung — Western Australia (Pinnacles)","The Pinnacles of Western Australia are limestone formations created from fossilised seashells. 6,000 years ago this place lay at the bottom of the ocean. Today thousands of pillars rise from the sand, some as tall as a person.","Desert studded with thousands of limestone columns up to 3.5 m tall, formed from fossilised shells","Field of limestone columns in a coastal park, 250 km north of a large western city","Desert of limestone columns, the Pinnacles, Western Australia","The Pinnacles of Nambung National Park, Western Australia"],"similan_islands":["Similan Islands — Thailand","The waters of the Similans, ranked by Cousteau among the 10 best dives in the world, are so clear that visibility can reach 40 metres. Whale sharks, regularly sighted there, are the largest fish in the world and feed only on plankton.","Eleven granite islands with 30 m visibility, corals, whale sharks and turtles, marine park since 1982","Diving archipelago in a tropical sea, 85 km off a west coast of Southeast Asia","World-class diving marine park, Andaman Sea, Thailand","The Similan Islands, in Thailand"],"phong_nha":["Phong Nha — Vietnam","Son Doong cave is so large that it has its own weather: clouds, morning mist and internal rainfall. Endemic animal species live there, adapted to perpetual darkness. Visitors can only go with a certified guide — places are limited to 1,000 a year.","400-million-year-old karst hiding the world's largest cave, passages 150–200 m high","Tropical karst massif with giant caves, centre of a long Southeast Asian country","World's largest cave, UNESCO-listed tropical karst, central Vietnam","Phong Nha-Kẻ Bàng National Park, in Vietnam"],"bali":["Bali — Indonesia (Tanah Lot)","Bali is one of the few Hindu islands in Indonesia's sea of Islamic islands. Its unique Balinese religion blends Hinduism, Buddhism and ancestral animist beliefs. Every village has at least 3 temples — one for the gods, one for souls and one for evil spirits.","16th-century Hindu temple perched on a basalt rock, reachable at low tide, guarded by sacred snakes","Temple on a sea rock, west coast of a volcanic island of terraced rice fields, Indian Ocean","Hindu temple on a sea rock, iconic sunset, Bali","Tanah Lot temple, in Bali, Indonesia"],"ubud":["Ubud — Bali, Indonesia","Ubud is the artistic heart of Bali: painters, sculptors and dancers keep the island's traditions alive here. Its terraced rice fields are watered by subak, a thousand-year-old cooperative system run by water temples and inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, and its sacred forest is home to hundreds of macaques.","Terraced rice fields watered by an ancestral irrigation system, Hindu temples and a sacred monkey forest, lush plateau","Cultural town in the centre of a volcanic Indonesian island, plateau between volcanoes and ocean","Cultural capital of Bali with terraced rice fields, Indonesia","Ubud, in Bali, Indonesia"],"phuket":["Phuket — Thailand","The tsunami of 26 December 2004 hit Phuket hard, killing several hundred people on the island and more than 5,000 across southern Thailand. Its swift reconstruction revived tourism: Phuket now welcomes some ten million visitors a year.","45 m white marble statue begun in 2004 on a hilltop, on a large granite island","Large tropical granite island in a strait linking two seas, west coast of a Southeast Asian country","Tropical island with a Big Buddha and Sino-Portuguese architecture, Phuket, Thailand","The island of Phuket, in Thailand"],"kazbegi":["Kazbegi — Georgia","The Gergeti Trinity Church, at 2,170 m above Kazbegi (Stepantsminda), served in wartime as a refuge for Georgia's most precious relics, including the cross of Saint Nino. You can walk up in just over an hour, or go by 4x4.","14th-century Trinity church on a spur at 2,170 m, facing a snow-capped volcano over 5,000 m high","Medieval church perched in a Caucasus gorge, facing a high snowy peak","Orthodox church perched opposite a 5,047 m volcano, Georgian Caucasus","Gergeti Church in Kazbegi, Georgia"],"uplistsikhe":["Uplistsikhe — Georgia","Uplistsikhe, “fortress of the Lord” in Georgian, is a 3,000-year-old rock-cut town carved into the sandstone above the Kura (Mtkvari). It is one of the oldest towns in the Caucasus; a stronghold again in the 8th–10th centuries, it was all but abandoned after the Mongol raids. Among its features is a large columned hall cut into the rock.","3,000-year-old rock-cut city of pink sandstone, some 700 carved chambers, a basilica, beside a great river","Sandstone cave town overlooking a river, central Caucasus, near an old town","Ancient pre-Christian rock-cut capital of the Caucasus, Georgia","The Uplistsikhe rock-cut site, Georgia"],"gobustan":["Gobustan — Azerbaijan","Gobustan National Park holds more than 6,000 petroglyphs spanning up to 40,000 years of human history. The Norwegian Thor Heyerdahl, fascinated by carvings of boats that reminded him of Viking ships, championed the highly controversial theory that the ancestors of the Scandinavians came from this region. The nearby mud volcanoes, among the most numerous in the world, bubble away constantly.","Plateau of 40,000-year-old petroglyphs and active mud volcanoes, on the shore of an enclosed sea","Semi-arid plateau of rock carvings and mud volcanoes, 65 km from an oil capital","Prehistoric rock carvings and mud volcanoes, Azerbaijan","Gobustan, Azerbaijan"],"bakou":["Baku — Azerbaijan (Old City)","Baku's old city, Icheri Sheher, was founded in the 5th century. Its Maiden Tower (12th century), visible from the Caspian Sea, never served as a defensive fortress — its exact purpose remains a mystery. Baku means “wind-pounded city” in Persian — fierce winds sweep through the city constantly.","12th-century medieval walls, a 29 m cylindrical tower of shell limestone and a dynastic palace","Fortified old town on a peninsula of a great enclosed sea, semi-arid, latitude ~40°N","Fortified old town with a cylindrical tower, Baku, Azerbaijan","The Old City of Baku (Icheri Sheher), in Azerbaijan"],"tbilissi":["Tbilisi — Georgia","Tbilisi means “place of warm waters” in Georgian. The city was founded in the 5th century by a king who, according to legend, saw a wounded pheasant heal instantly on entering a hot spring. These springs still flow today in the sulphur baths of old Tbilisi.","Old town with carved wooden balconies and sulphur baths, overlooked by a 4th-century fortress, beside a deeply cut river","Caucasian capital with sulphur baths and ornate balconies, in a valley between two mountain ranges","Capital with sulphur baths and the Narikala fortress, on the Kura, Georgia","Tbilisi, capital of Georgia"],"vardzia":["Vardzia — Georgia","The cave monastery of Vardzia, carved into the volcanic cliff in the 12th century by Queen Tamar, comprises 600 rooms on 13 levels. An earthquake in 1283 brought down the mountain's face, exposing the cells to the open air. The frescoes depicting Queen Tamar are among the most precious works of Georgian art.","12th-century monastic city of 600 rooms on 13 levels carved into a tuff cliff, founded by a queen","Vast cave complex on a cliff above a deep-cut river, south of the country, ~1,300 m","Monastic city carved into the rock in the 12th century, emblem of medieval Georgia","Vardzia cave monastery, Georgia"],"khor_virap":["Khor Virap — Armenia","Mount Ararat, visible from Khor Virap, is regarded as the cradle of Armenian civilisation and the country's national symbol — yet it has lain in Turkey since 1921. Armenians can see it from their border but cannot reach it.","Monastery on a hillock above the pit-prison of an evangelising saint, facing a snow-capped stratovolcano","Isolated monastery on a fertile plain, near a border, facing a great snow-capped volcano","Iconic monastery at the foot of a great volcano, an Armenian national symbol","Khor Virap Monastery, in Armenia"],"garni":["Garni — Armenia","The Temple of Garni is the only Graeco-Roman colonnaded building still standing in Armenia and the whole Caucasus. Built in the 1st century AD, probably under King Tiridates I, it survived the country's Christianisation (301), perhaps converted into a royal summer residence. Toppled by the earthquake of 1679, it was rebuilt in the 1970s.","1st-century Graeco-Roman temple with 24 Ionic columns, on a plateau overlooking a gorge lined with basalt columns","Ancient temple on a volcanic plateau above a gorge, southern Caucasus","The only Graeco-Roman temple in the Caucasus, basalt gorge, Armenia","The Temple of Garni, in Armenia"],"chefchaouen":["Chefchaouen — Morocco","Morocco's blue city owes its colour to the Sephardic Jewish community expelled from Spain in 1492. They painted the walls blue, a colour symbolising the sky and the divine in Jewish tradition. The practice was taken up by the whole town.","Medina founded in 1471, its houses limewashed in indigo blue, alleys climbing towards a square, a mosque","Blue town tucked into a mountain range in the north of the country, where two wadis meet","Blue town with indigo alleys in the Rif Mountains, northern Morocco","Chefchaouen, in Morocco"],"ait_benhaddou":["Aït Benhaddou — Morocco","Aït Benhaddou has served as a set for more than 20 films and series, including Gladiator, Game of Thrones and Lawrence of Arabia. Only five families still live within the walls of the ksar, the rest of the population having moved to the modern village.","11th-century fortified rammed-earth village with truncated-cone towers, backdrop to many Hollywood epics","Red-earth ksar on a wadi, northern slope of a great mountain range, semi-arid zone, ~1,000 m","UNESCO-listed rammed-earth ksar, film set, pre-Saharan Morocco","The ksar of Aït Benhaddou, in Morocco"],"vallee_du_dades":["Dades Valley — Morocco","The Dades Gorges, carved by the river into the High Atlas, are famous for their red sandstone cliffs and their “monkey fingers” formations. Just downstream, the Kelaat M'Gouna area, nicknamed the “Valley of Roses”, celebrates the Damask rose harvest every May, the petals being distilled into rose water. The winding road through the gorges is one of the most spectacular in Morocco.","Gorges cut into red Jurassic sandstone, “monkey fingers” rock formations, earthen kasbahs","Deep valley with red sandstone walls, road of the kasbahs, southern slope of a great mountain range, ~1,500 m","Red sandstone gorges on the road of the kasbahs, Moroccan pre-Sahara","The Dades Valley, Morocco"],"ile_de_djerba":["Djerba — Tunisia","Djerba is traditionally identified with Homer's island of the Lotus-Eaters, where Odysseus and his companions ate the lotus flower that made them forget everything. It is home to the El Ghriba synagogue, one of the most important Jewish pilgrimage sites in the world, founded in the 6th century BC according to tradition.","Island of traditional pottery and one of the oldest synagogues in the world, white-domed, shallow lagoon","Island linked to the mainland by a causeway, shallow gulf, sandy coast, latitude ~34°N","Berber Mediterranean island with an ancient synagogue, Djerba, Tunisia","The island of Djerba, Tunisia"],"tataouine":["Tataouine — Tunisia (Ksour)","The ksour (fortified communal granaries) of Tataouine Governorate, such as Ksar Ouled Soltane and Ksar Hadada, served as sets for Star Wars. George Lucas was won over by the ghorfas — vaulted cells stacked several storeys high — and borrowed the town's name for the planet Tatooine, although the town itself never appears on screen.","Fortified communal granaries with vaulted cells on several storeys (14th–18th c.), Berber architecture","Stone ksour on an arid plateau, between hamada and dunes, eastern edge of a great erg, ~500 m","Fortified Berber granaries, Star Wars set, southern Tunisia","The ksour of Tataouine, in Tunisia"],"douz":["Douz — Tunisia (Sahara Festival)","Douz, the “gateway to the Sahara”, hosts the International Festival of the Sahara every December — the world's largest gathering of nomadic culture. Camel races, equestrian fantasias, sloughi greyhound contests and Gnawa music make the event a deep dive into the nomadic culture of the Maghreb.","Oasis town on the edge of a great erg, December Bedouin festival, camel treks and a palm grove of 400,000 date palms","Oasis, the “gateway to the desert”, between salt lakes and a sea of sand, semi-arid southern governorate","Oasis town, gateway to the Sahara, with a Bedouin festival, Douz, Tunisia","Douz, in Tunisia (Sahara Festival)"],"tipaza":["Tipaza — Algeria","Tipaza, on Algeria's Mediterranean coast, was a Punic trading post that became a Roman colony, whose ruins run down to the sea. Albert Camus, born in Algeria, used it as the setting for his essays “Nuptials at Tipasa” and “Return to Tipasa”, celebrating the light and the ruins by the water; a stele honours him on the site.","Romanised Punic city by the sea, three-aisled basilica and amphitheatre, on a rocky promontory","Coastal ancient ruins on a Mediterranean promontory, 70 km west of a large capital","UNESCO-listed Punic and Roman ancient site, Algerian coast","The ruins of Tipaza, in Algeria"],"volubilis":["Volubilis — Morocco","The 1,800-year-old mosaics of Volubilis are still in place in the open-air ruins — a rarity. After the Roman administration withdrew around 285, the city remained inhabited for centuries and in 788 welcomed Idris I, founder of the first Moroccan dynasty. Damaged by the earthquake of 1755, it was excavated in the 20th century.","Roman city with a triumphal arch from 217, mosaics preserved in situ in the baths, olive groves all around","Roman ruins on a farming plain between two mountain ranges, 30 km from an imperial city, ~400 m","Roman ruins with preserved mosaics, Volubilis, Morocco","The ruins of Volubilis, in Morocco"],"essaouira":["Essaouira — Morocco","Jimi Hendrix stayed in Essaouira in 1969, and the town has nurtured his legend ever since. It is often said that he found the inspiration for “Castles Made of Sand” there, but the song had already come out in 1967 — proof that the myth has outgrown the reality.","City with 18th-century sandstone ramparts and Vauban-style bastions, Atlantic port lashed by the trade winds","Windswept fortified city on the Atlantic coast, listed medina, Gnawa music festival","Fortified city of the winds on the Atlantic, Essaouira, Morocco","Essaouira, in Morocco"],"harar":["Harar — Ethiopia","Harar is the fourth holiest city of Islam and the capital of coffee — Ethiopia is the birthplace of Arabica coffee. Its 82 mosques within an old town of less than 1 km² make it one of the densest Islamic cities. Every evening, hyena feeders lure wild hyenas in as night falls — a century-old tradition.","Fourth holiest city of Islam, 82 mosques inside a 16th-century wall, tradition of feeding hyenas","Old fortified Islamic city on an eastern plateau, 525 km east of the capital","Medieval Islamic city of tame hyenas, Harar, Ethiopia","Harar, in Ethiopia"],"gorilles_de_bwindi":["Bwindi gorillas — Uganda","A permit to track the Bwindi gorillas costs around 800 dollars per person. Part of this sum goes to the neighbouring communities, which has made gorilla protection a major economic resource and helped reduce poaching.","Afromontane forest sheltering around half of the world's mountain gorillas, between 1,160 and 2,607 m","Dense forest of mountain gorillas, Rift massif, bordering a great equatorial lake","Mountain gorilla sanctuary, Bwindi Forest, Uganda","The gorillas of Bwindi, Uganda"],"zanzibar_nungwi":["Zanzibar — Tanzania (Nungwi beach)","Zanzibar is the birthplace of Freddie Mercury, Queen's lead singer. The house where he was born, in Stone Town, draws fans from all over the world. His family left the island in 1964, during the Zanzibar Revolution; he was 17 and never went back.","Northern tip of a large island, white coral sand, fishing dhows and a green turtle rehabilitation centre","White-sand beach at the northern end of an Indian Ocean island, traditional sailing boats","Northern beach of turtles and dhows, Zanzibar island","Nungwi beach, Zanzibar, Tanzania"],"crater_ngorongoro":["Ngorongoro Crater — Tanzania","The Ngorongoro Crater is the largest intact caldera in the world, formed 2 to 3 million years ago when a volcano collapsed. Its 260 km² are home to 25,000 large mammals, including rare black rhinos, in a closed ecosystem nicknamed “Africa's Noah's Ark”.","Intact 600 m-deep caldera teeming with wildlife, next to a vast plain, land of a herding people","World's largest intact caldera, with black rhinos, East African Rift plateau","Volcanic crater teeming with wildlife, Ngorongoro, Tanzania","Ngorongoro Crater, Tanzania"],"socotra":["Socotra — Yemen","Socotra is an isolated archipelago in the Indian Ocean where a third of the plants exist nowhere else, such as the umbrella-shaped dragon's blood tree. Its 6 million years of isolation have earned it the nickname “the Galápagos of the Indian Ocean”.","Isolated archipelago 380 km offshore, umbrella-shaped dragon trees with red sap, extreme plant endemism","Island with unique umbrella-shaped flora, Indian Ocean, off a peninsula of Arabia","Island of umbrella-shaped dragon's blood trees, Socotra, Yemen","Socotra island, Yemen"],"laguna_colorada":["Laguna Colorada — Bolivia","Laguna Colorada is a shallow salt lake on the Bolivian Altiplano, tinted blood red by algae and plankton. At 4,300 metres above sea level, its scarlet waters dotted with islets of white borax host thousands of Andean flamingos.","High-altitude red lake (4,300 m) coloured by algae and sediments, fringed with white borax and flamingos","High-altitude red lake with flamingos, Andean Altiplano in the south of a landlocked country","High-altitude red lake with flamingos, Bolivian Andes","Laguna Colorada, Bolivia"],"zhangjiajie":["Zhangjiajie — China","Zhangjiajie park is famous for its thousands of vertical quartz-sandstone pillars, some of them more than 200 metres high. These misty formations inspired the floating mountains in the film Avatar; its glass bridge was, when it opened in 2016, the highest and longest in the world.","Forest of vertical quartz-sandstone pinnacles drowned in mist, which inspired the floating mountains of a cult film","Forest of rock pillars in the mist and a glass bridge, mountainous province of a large East Asian country","Pillar mountains that inspired Avatar, Zhangjiajie, China","Zhangjiajie park, China"],"perito_moreno":["Perito Moreno Glacier — Argentina","The Perito Moreno Glacier is one of the few glaciers in the world still in equilibrium, advancing 2 metres a day. Its 5-kilometre-wide, 60-metre-high front produces spectacular collapses of ice blocks into Lake Argentino.","Glacier with a 5 km front and 60 m walls that calves in great slabs into a lake, Patagonian ice field","Large active glacier plunging into a lake, southern Patagonia of a South American country","Patagonian glacier that collapses into a lake, Perito Moreno, Argentina","Perito Moreno Glacier, Argentina"],"shirakawa_go":["Shirakawa-go — Japan","Shirakawa-gō is a mountain village famous for its gasshō-zukuri farmhouses, whose steeply pitched thatched roofs are designed to withstand heavy snowfall. Some of these century-old houses were home to as many as 30 people and a silkworm farm.","Village of large houses with very steep thatched roofs like “hands in prayer”, snowy mountain valley","Village of thatched houses in a very snowy valley in the centre of the archipelago","Traditional village of thatched houses, Shirakawa-go, Japan","Shirakawa-go village, Japan"],"hegra":["Hegra — Saudi Arabia","Hegra (Madā'in Sālih) is the largest Nabataean city after Petra, with over 100 monumental tombs carved into sandstone outcrops. The first Saudi site to be inscribed by UNESCO, it was a caravan crossroads on the incense route.","Nabataean tombs carved into sandstone on an incense route, the country's first listed site","Nabataean necropolis with tombs carved into sandstone, desert of north-western Arabia","Ancient city of carved tombs, sister of Petra, Hegra, Saudi Arabia","The site of Hegra, Saudi Arabia"],"kol_suu":["Köl-Suu — Kyrgyzstan","Lake Köl-Suu, tucked away at 3,500 metres in the remote Tian Shan mountains near the Chinese border, stretches between sheer cliffs. Formed by a landslide, its still emerald waters create striking mirror reflections.","Natural dam lake at 3,500 m hemmed in by cliffs, high mountain range, near an eastern border","Isolated emerald lake between cliffs in a high Central Asian mountain range","Isolated emerald lake in the mountains of Kyrgyzstan","Lake Köl-Suu, Kyrgyzstan"],"bandiagara":["Bandiagara — Mali","The Bandiagara Escarpment is a 150-kilometre sandstone cliff where the Dogon people built villages clinging to the rock. Their earthen granaries and the old Tellem dwellings, nestled in crevices, overlook the plain.","Long sandstone escarpment with clinging villages, dwellings of an ancient people and ceremonial masks","Inhabited cliff with earthen granaries of an animist people, Sahelian country of West Africa","Cliff inhabited by the Dogon people, Bandiagara, Mali","The Bandiagara Escarpment, Mali"],"kelimutu":["Kelimutu — Indonesia","The Kelimutu volcano, on the island of Flores, has three crater lakes that change colour independently — from turquoise to red or black — depending on the chemical reactions of minerals. The local Lio people see them as the resting place of the souls of the dead.","Volcano with three crater lakes of different colours, caused by minerals, on an island in the east of the archipelago","Volcano with three coloured lakes on an island in the east of a Southeast Asian archipelago","Volcano with coloured crater lakes, Kelimutu, Indonesia","Kelimutu volcano, Indonesia"],"raja_ampat":["Raja Ampat — Indonesia","The Raja Ampat archipelago, in West Papua, has the greatest marine biodiversity on the planet, with over 1,500 species of fish and 75% of known corals. Its jungle-clad karst islets rise from a turquoise lagoon.","Karst archipelago at the heart of the Coral Triangle, over 1,500 fish species, jungle-covered islets","Archipelago with record marine biodiversity, limestone islets off the western tip of New Guinea","Diving paradise of jungle islets, Raja Ampat, Indonesia","The Raja Ampat archipelago, Indonesia"],"cano_cristales":["Caño Cristales — Colombia","Caño Cristales, nicknamed “the river of five colours”, owes its vivid hues to an aquatic plant, Macarenia clavigera, which turns bright red from August to November. Its rocky bed mixes red, yellow, green, blue and black.","River whose bed is reddened by an aquatic plant, decking it in five colours in season, on a sandstone massif","Multicoloured river on an isolated massif, in the centre of a South American country","River of five colours, Caño Cristales, Colombia","Caño Cristales, Colombia"],"fernando_de_noronha":["Fernando de Noronha — Brazil","The volcanic archipelago of Fernando de Noronha, 350 kilometres off the Brazilian coast, is a marine sanctuary with crystal-clear waters. Its beaches, such as Baía do Sancho, regularly voted among the most beautiful in the world, are home to dolphins and turtles.","Atlantic volcanic archipelago with unspoilt beaches, spinner dolphin sanctuary, crystal-clear waters","Unspoilt archipelago with clear waters, off the north-east coast of the subcontinent","Paradise island off Brazil, Fernando de Noronha","The Fernando de Noronha archipelago, Brazil"],"avenue_des_baobabs":["Avenue of the Baobabs — Madagascar","The Avenue of the Baobabs, near Morondava, is lined with 800-year-old, 30-metre-tall Adansonia grandidieri baobabs. Remnants of a vanished tropical forest, these trees with massive trunks and narrow crowns make a striking sight at sunset.","Avenue of giant, thousand-year-old baobabs of an endemic species, silhouettes outlined against the sunset","Avenue of giant baobabs near a town on the west coast of a large Indian Ocean island","Road lined with giant baobabs, near Morondava, Madagascar","The Avenue of the Baobabs, Madagascar"],"chutes_kalandula":["Kalandula Falls — Angola","The Kalandula Falls, among the largest in Africa, pour the Lucala River over a 105-metre drop and a 400-metre-wide horseshoe. Long inaccessible during the Angolan Civil War, they still see few visitors.","Horseshoe falls 400 m wide, among the largest on the continent, on a plateau river","Vast horseshoe falls in an inland province of a west-central African country","Little-known great horseshoe falls, Kalandula, Angola","Kalandula Falls, Angola"],"nyiragongo":["Mount Nyiragongo — DR Congo","Nyiragongo holds the largest permanent lava lake in the world, bubbling at the bottom of its crater. Its eruptions, among the fastest known — the lava reaches 100 km/h — regularly threaten the nearby city of Goma.","Volcano with a bubbling permanent lava lake, devastating eruptions in 2002 and 2021, near a large city","Volcano with a lava lake in a Rift massif, east of a large Central African country","Volcano with a permanent lava lake, Nyiragongo, DR Congo","Mount Nyiragongo, DR Congo"],"lac_natron":["Lake Natron — Tanzania","Lake Natron, an intense red owing to micro-organisms, is one of the most hostile environments on the planet: its highly alkaline water can reach 60 °C. Yet it is the main breeding site of East Africa's lesser flamingos.","Blood-red alkaline lake laden with natron, sole breeding ground of lesser flamingos, beneath a sacred volcano","Highly alkaline red lake in the Rift Valley, north of an East African country","Red lake where flamingos nest, Lake Natron, Tanzania","Lake Natron, Tanzania"],"fish_river_canyon":["Fish River Canyon — Namibia","Fish River Canyon is the second-largest canyon in the world, 160 kilometres long and 550 metres deep. Carved over 500 million years by the Fish River and tectonic collapses, it winds through a desert landscape in southern Namibia.","160 km canyon carved by a river, the second largest in the world, five-day hiking trail","Huge desert canyon, the second largest in the world, south of a southern African country","Great desert canyon, Fish River Canyon, Namibia","Fish River Canyon, Namibia"],"bwindi":["Bwindi Forest — Uganda","Bwindi Impenetrable Forest is home to nearly half of the world's surviving mountain gorillas. This dense, ancient jungle in western Uganda, 25,000 years old, is one of the few places where you can get close to these endangered primates.","Dense 25,000-year-old jungle, refuge of mountain gorillas, Albertine Rift massif","Rainforest with mountain gorillas, south-west of an East African country","Forest of mountain gorillas, Bwindi, Uganda","Bwindi Forest, Uganda"],"simien":["Simien Mountains — Ethiopia","The Simien massif, nicknamed the “chessboard of the gods”, offers landscapes of jagged peaks and 1,500-metre cliffs. It is home to unique endemic species such as the red-chested gelada baboon and the Ethiopian wolf.","Jagged highlands rising to the country's highest point, gelada baboons and endemic ibex","Steep mountains with endemic wildlife, northern highlands of an East African country","Mountain massif of geladas, Simien, Ethiopia","The Simien Mountains, Ethiopia"],"kazbek":["Mount Kazbek — Georgia","Mount Kazbek, a dormant 5,047-metre volcano, towers over the Georgian Caucasus. At its foot, Gergeti Trinity Church, perched at 2,170 metres, offers one of the country's most iconic views. Legend has it that the Georgian Prometheus, Amirani, was chained there.","5,047 m snow-capped volcano towering over an isolated church, linked to a local legend of a chained titan","High snow-capped volcano overlooking a perched church, Caucasus range","High snow-capped mountain in the Caucasus, Kazbek, Georgia","Mount Kazbek, Georgia"],"lac_sevan":["Lake Sevan — Armenia","Lake Sevan, at 1,900 metres above sea level, is one of the largest high-altitude freshwater lakes in the world. The jewel of Armenia, its peninsula is home to Sevanavank Monastery, founded in the 9th century by monks seeking solitude.","Largest lake in the Caucasus at 1,900 m, 9th-century monastery on a peninsula, deep blue waters","Large mountain lake with an ancient monastery, highlands of the southern Caucasus","High-altitude lake with a monastery, Lake Sevan, Armenia","Lake Sevan, Armenia"],"darvaza":["Darvaza — Turkmenistan","The Darvaza crater, nicknamed the “Gate to Hell”, is a burning gas pit 70 metres across. Lit in 1971 by Soviet geologists to burn off the methane, it has blazed without interruption for more than fifty years in the Karakum Desert.","70 m gas crater ablaze without interruption since 1971, an inferno in the middle of a sandy desert","Permanently burning gas crater in the heart of a great Central Asian desert","Flaming pit, the “Gate to Hell”, Darvaza, Turkmenistan","The Darvaza crater, Turkmenistan"],"khiva":["Khiva — Uzbekistan","Khiva is a Silk Road museum city whose fortified old town, Itchan Kala, is entirely preserved. Its madrasas, its truncated Kalta Minor minaret clad in turquoise tiles and its earthen ramparts evoke the old khanates of Central Asia.","Intact walled town in a Silk Road oasis, squat turquoise minaret, former merchant khanate","Fortified oasis city with a turquoise minaret, Central Asian desert","Ancient fortified Silk Road city, Khiva, Uzbekistan","The city of Khiva, Uzbekistan"],"boukhara":["Bukhara — Uzbekistan","Bukhara, a holy city of Central Asian Islam, preserves more than 140 medieval monuments. Its 47-metre Kalon Minaret, spared by Genghis Khan, its fired-brick Samanid Mausoleum and its covered bazaars bear witness to 2,000 years of history.","Holy merchant city with a minaret spared by the Mongols, Samanid mausoleum, shady pool","Medieval city with a great ancient minaret, Silk Road oasis, Central Asia","Historic Silk Road city, Bukhara, Uzbekistan","The city of Bukhara, Uzbekistan"],"tombouctou":["Timbuktu — Mali","Timbuktu, “the mysterious”, was a major intellectual and commercial centre of the Sahel in the 15th and 16th centuries. Its mud-brick mosques such as Djinguereber and its hundreds of thousands of ancient manuscripts bear witness to its influence on the trans-Saharan route.","Sahelian caravan city with earthen mosques, a treasure trove of manuscripts, former crossroads of gold and salt","Saharan town with mud-brick mosques and ancient manuscripts, edge of the West African desert","Legendary desert city of manuscripts, Timbuktu, Mali","The city of Timbuktu, Mali"],"ghadames":["Ghadames — Libya","Ghadames, “the pearl of the desert”, is a Saharan oasis whose old town of whitewashed earthen houses forms a covered labyrinth. Designed for the heat, the architecture separates the men's covered passages at ground level from the women's rooftop terraces above.","Berber oasis with multi-storey earthen houses linked by covered passages, at a desert tripoint","Covered earthen old town in a Saharan oasis, south-west of a North African country","Oasis of labyrinthine earthen houses, Ghadames, Libya","The oasis of Ghadames, Libya"],"siwa":["Siwa Oasis — Egypt","The Siwa Oasis, isolated in Egypt's Western Desert, is home to the Oracle of Amun, which Alexander the Great came to consult in 331 BC. Its springs, palm groves and the earthen fortress of Shali make it a Berber world apart, long cut off from the rest of Egypt.","Isolated oasis with an ancient oracle consulted by a conqueror, salt fortress, near a great depression","Remote oasis with an ancient oracle, desert in the west of a North African country","Oasis with the oracle visited by Alexander the Great, Siwa, Egypt","The Siwa Oasis, Egypt"],"sabratha":["Sabratha — Libya","Sabratha was a Phoenician trading post that became a flourishing Roman city of Tripolitania. Its ancient theatre with three tiers of columns, among the best preserved in North Africa, overlooked a port exporting ivory and wild animals from the Sahara.","Phoenician trading post turned Roman city, ancient three-tiered theatre, on a desert coast","Roman city with a great seaside theatre, coastline of a North African country","Coastal Roman ruins with a great theatre, Sabratha, Libya","The site of Sabratha, Libya"],"plaine_des_jarres":["Plain of Jars — Laos","The Plain of Jars is scattered with thousands of 2,500-year-old megalithic stone jars, whose funerary use remains a mystery. Some weigh several tonnes. The area was one of the most heavily bombed in the world during the Vietnam War.","Plain dotted with Iron Age megalithic jars, still riddled with unexploded bombs","Field of enigmatic giant stone jars, plateau of a landlocked Southeast Asian country","Plain covered with ancient stone jars, Laos","The Plain of Jars, Laos"],"ninh_binh":["Ninh Binh — Vietnam","Ninh Binh, nicknamed “Ha Long Bay on land”, spreads its karst pinnacles rising out of flooded rice paddies and winding rivers. Visitors glide by rowing boat between caves and limestone peaks, in a landscape that has inspired many films.","Limestone pinnacles shooting up from rice paddies and navigable rivers, nicknamed “Ha Long Bay on land”","Karst pinnacles amid rice paddies and rivers, delta of a long Southeast Asian country","Limestone mountains and navigable rice paddies, Ninh Binh, Vietnam","The Ninh Binh region, Vietnam"],"sapa":["Sa Pa — Vietnam","Sapa, perched in the mountains of north-western Vietnam, is famous for its rice terraces carved into the mountainsides by the Hmong and Dao minorities. Mount Fansipan, the “roof of Indochina”, towers over these often mist-filled valleys.","Mountain rice terraces at the foot of the roof of Indochina, ethnic minority villages","Rice terraces and ethnic minorities in a northern massif of a long Southeast Asian country","Stepped rice paddies in the mountains, Sapa, Vietnam","The Sapa region, Vietnam"],"inle":["Inle Lake — Myanmar","Inle Lake is famous for its Intha fishermen, who row standing up with one leg wrapped around the oar. Its stilt villages, floating gardens and pagodas rise from a high-altitude lake ringed by mountains, in the heart of Shan State.","Lake of fishermen rowing standing on one leg, floating gardens and stilt villages, Shan Plateau","Lake of acrobatic fishermen and stilt houses, eastern plateau of a Southeast Asian country","Lake of stilt villages and leg-rowing fishermen, Inle, Myanmar","Inle Lake, Myanmar"],"komodo":["Komodo — Indonesia","Komodo Island is home to the Komodo dragon, the largest lizard in the world, which can reach 3 metres and 70 kilos. This national park of arid hills and coral reefs, including a pink-sand beach, protects some 3,000 of these predators.","Island home to the world's largest lizard, a giant monitor, pink-sand beach and reefs","Island of giant dragons and a pink beach, small islands of a Southeast Asian archipelago","Island of the world's largest lizards, Komodo, Indonesia","Komodo Island, Indonesia"],"tana_toraja":["Tana Toraja — Indonesia","Toraja land, in the mountains of Sulawesi, is famous for its tongkonan houses with roofs shaped like a ship's prow and its elaborate funeral rites. The dead are buried in cliffs guarded by wooden effigies, the tau-tau.","Region of boat-shaped houses with upswept roofs and tombs hewn into cliffs, effigies of the dead","Region of boat-houses and cliff tombs on a large Southeast Asian island","Culture with unique funeral rites, Tana Toraja, Sulawesi","Tana Toraja, Indonesia"],"chocolate_hills":["Chocolate Hills — Philippines","Bohol's Chocolate Hills number more than 1,200 almost identical conical hills spread over 50 km². Covered in grass that turns brown in the dry season — hence their name — these limestone mounds intrigue geologists and fuel local legends.","More than 1,200 identical conical hills that turn brown in the dry season, on a tropical island","Field of identical brown conical hills, island of a Southeast Asian archipelago","Field of brown rounded hills, Chocolate Hills, Philippines","The Chocolate Hills, Philippines"],"el_nido":["El Nido — Philippines","El Nido, on the northern tip of Palawan, is a marine sanctuary of karst cliffs rising out of turquoise lagoons. Its islets, hidden beaches and coral reefs, reached by bangka, make it one of Asia's most sought-after beach destinations.","Archipelago of turquoise lagoons ringed by limestone cliffs, explored by outrigger canoe","Turquoise lagoons between limestone cliffs on a large western island of a Southeast Asian archipelago","Paradise islands of cliffs and lagoons, El Nido, Philippines","El Nido in the Philippines"],"kata_tjuta":["Kata Tjuta — Australia","Kata Tjuta, or the Olgas, is a group of 36 domes of red conglomerate rising to 546 metres above the plain, higher than nearby Uluru. A sacred site of the Anangu people, its gorges shelter desert wildlife in Australia's Red Centre.","Group of 36 red conglomerate domes sacred to an Aboriginal people, in the heart of a desert","Red rock domes in a central desert, near a famous monolith, Northern Territory","Sacred red rock domes near Uluru, Australian desert","Kata Tjuta in Australia"],"karijini":["Karijini National Park — Australia","Karijini National Park, in the Pilbara, is cut by narrow gorges with red iron walls 2.5 billion years old. Waterfalls, turquoise pools and layered rock form one of Australia's oldest and most spectacular geological landscapes.","Narrow banded-iron gorges 2.5 billion years old, sunken pools, red earth","Deep red gorges with pools in a park in the north-west of a southern continent","Ancient red rock canyons, Karijini, Western Australia","Karijini National Park in Australia"],"cradle_mountain":["Cradle Mountain — Australia","Cradle Mountain, in Tasmania, raises its jagged dolerite ridges above Dove Lake, a glacial relic. This landscape of moorland, temperate rainforest and wombats marks the start of the Overland Track, one of Australia's great hikes.","Jagged dolerite mountain above a glacial lake, temperate rainforest and wombats","Jagged mountain above a glacial lake, large island south of a southern continent","Wild mountain with glacial scenery, Cradle Mountain, Tasmania","Cradle Mountain in Tasmania"],"khajuraho":["Khajuraho Group of Monuments — India","The temples of Khajuraho, built by the Chandela dynasty between 950 and 1050, are famous for their exquisitely fine sculptures, some of which depict erotic scenes. Of the original 85 temples, around twenty survive, adorned with thousands of figures.","Sandstone temples of a medieval dynasty, famous for their carvings of lovemaking, central plain","Temples covered in erotic sculptures, central plain of a South Asian country","Temples with erotic sculptures, Khajuraho, India","The temples of Khajuraho in India"],"danakil":["Danakil Desert — Ethiopia","The Danakil Depression is one of the hottest and lowest places on the planet, 125 metres below sea level. Its multicoloured acid lakes, the sulphur chimneys of Dallol and its lava lakes make it an otherworldly landscape in the Afar Triangle.","Volcanic depression among the hottest in the world (-125 m), yellow acid lakes and a permanent lava lake","Volcanic desert with colourful acid lakes, depression in the north-east of an East African country","Volcanic desert among the hottest in the world, Danakil, Ethiopia","The Danakil Desert in Ethiopia"],"wineglass_bay":["Wineglass Bay — Tasmania, Australia","Wineglass Bay, in Freycinet National Park in Tasmania, curves in a perfect arc of white sand edging turquoise water, framed by the pink granite peaks of the Hazards. Its wine-glass shape and remoteness make it one of Australia's most beautiful beaches.","Perfect crescent bay of white sand at the foot of pink granite peaks, on a wild coast","Arc-shaped beach of white sand and pink granite in a park, large island south of a southern continent","Perfect crescent beach, Wineglass Bay, Tasmania","Wineglass Bay in Tasmania"],"tongariro":["Tongariro National Park — New Zealand","Tongariro National Park, the country's first and a sacred Māori site, brings together three active volcanoes, including Mount Ngauruhoe, which played Mordor in The Lord of the Rings. Its crossing passes emerald lakes and steaming lava fields.","Row of three volcanoes with emerald lakes, a cone that stood in for Mordor on screen, sacred Māori site","Active volcanoes and emerald lakes, central plateau of a large volcanic island in the South Pacific","Volcanoes with colourful lakes, Tongariro Crossing, New Zealand","Tongariro National Park in New Zealand"],"kjeragbolten":["Kjeragbolten — Norway","Kjeragbolten is a 5 m³ boulder wedged in a crevice above a 984-metre drop, on the edge of the Lysefjord. Hikers venture out onto this suspended rock, with no protection whatsoever above the void, for a dizzying photo.","Boulder wedged in a crevice above a drop of nearly 1,000 m, on a fjord plateau","Rock wedged above a precipice overlooking a fjord, south-west of a Scandinavian country","Stone block suspended above the void, Kjeragbolten, Norway","Kjeragbolten in Norway"],"bardenas":["Bardenas Reales — Spain","The Bardenas Reales are a semi-arid desert in Navarre where erosion has sculpted clay hills, plateaus and hoodoos such as Castildetierra. This lunar landscape has served as a backdrop for many films and series.","Clay badlands with eroded buttes and a lone hoodoo, biosphere reserve, film locations","Desert of eroded clay hills, northern region of an Iberian peninsula","Desert landscape of eroded buttes, Bardenas, Spain","The Bardenas Reales in Spain"],"savica":["Savica Falls — Slovenia","The Savica waterfall, in the Slovenian Julian Alps, gushes from a cliff in the shape of the letter A after an underground course. Feeding Lake Bohinj, it inspired the national poet France Prešeren and remains a romantic symbol of the country.","A-shaped waterfall gushing from a karst spring, feeding an alpine lake dear to a national poet","Alpine waterfall feeding a lake, Julian Alps of a small Alpine country","Alpine waterfall feeding a lake, Slovenia","Savica Falls in Slovenia"],"wolfe_creek":["Wolfe Creek Crater — Australia","Wolfe Creek Crater, one of the best-preserved meteorite impact craters in the world, is 880 metres across. Formed around 120,000 years ago in the desert outback of Western Australia, it is known to the Djaru Aboriginal people as Kandimalal.","880 m meteorite impact crater named by an Aboriginal people, around 120,000 years old","Circular impact crater in a desert of a southern continent","Large meteorite crater, Wolfe Creek, Australia","Wolfe Creek Crater in Australia"],"tabernas":["Tabernas Desert — Spain","The Tabernas Desert, in Andalusia, is the only desert in continental Europe. Its arid landscapes of ravines and plateaus hosted the filming of dozens of spaghetti westerns, including Sergio Leone's Dollars Trilogy, on sets that can still be seen today.","The continent's only true desert, badlands that served as a backdrop for spaghetti westerns","Desert of arid hills used as a western set, south of an Iberian peninsula","Only desert in continental Europe, Tabernas, Spain","The Tabernas Desert in Spain"],"gobi":["Gobi Desert — Mongolia","The Gobi Desert, covering 1.3 million km² between Mongolia and China, is famous for its singing dunes, its red Flaming Cliffs and its beds of dinosaur eggs discovered in the 1920s. It is one of the coldest deserts in the world.","Great cold desert with singing dunes and red cliffs yielding fossil dinosaur eggs","Great cold desert with dinosaur fossils, south of a vast Asian steppe country","Great Central Asian desert, Gobi, Mongolia","The Gobi Desert in Mongolia"],"general_sherman":["General Sherman (tree) — Sequoia, California, USA","General Sherman is the largest tree in the world by volume: 1,487 m³ of wood, 83 m tall, 11 m in diameter at the base. Around 2,200 years old, this giant sequoia still adds the equivalent of an 18-metre tree's volume every year.","Giant sequoia, the world's largest tree by volume (1,487 m³), named after a general, high-altitude forest","The world's largest tree by volume, in a Sierra Nevada park, in the west of a large North American country","Biggest tree in the world, national park in California","The General Sherman tree, Sequoia National Park, USA"],"haiku_stairs":["Haiku Stairs — Oahu, Hawaii, USA","The Haiku Stairs, nicknamed the “Stairway to Heaven”, have 3,922 steps climbing a vertiginous ridge of the Ko'olau Range. Built in 1942 to reach a US Navy radio antenna, they have been officially closed since 1987 — which has never stopped hikers.","Stairway of nearly 4,000 steps from 1942 on a ridge, leading to a former radio station, off-limits","Forbidden “Stairway to Heaven” on a ridge, island in a tropical Pacific archipelago","Vertiginous stairway of nearly 4,000 steps, Oahu, Hawaii","The Haiku Stairs (Stairway to Heaven), Hawaii"],"glass_beach":["Glass Beach — Fort Bragg, California, USA","Glass Beach was born from a coastal dump used until 1967: decades of waves polished the glass waste into millions of translucent, multicoloured pebbles. The place has become so famous that visitors leave with the glass... and the beach is dwindling.","Former dump whose glass the ocean has polished until it carpets three coves, closed in 1967","Beach of polished glass pebbles from an old dump, north coast of a Pacific seaboard state in North America","Beach covered in polished glass, northern California coast","Glass Beach, Fort Bragg, California"],"chaussee_geants":["Giant's Causeway — Northern Ireland","The Giant's Causeway lines up some 40,000 hexagonal basalt columns born of an eruption 60 million years ago. Legend has it that the giant Finn McCool built it to cross the sea and fight his Scottish rival.","40,000 hexagonal basalt columns born of an ancient eruption, linked to a giant's legend","UNESCO-listed hexagonal basalt columns, north coast of a British island","Hexagonal volcanic columns, Giant's Causeway, Northern Ireland","The Giant's Causeway, Northern Ireland"],"trolltunga":["Trolltunga — Norway","Trolltunga, “the troll's tongue”, is a rocky spur jutting out 700 m above Lake Ringedalsvatnet. Reaching it takes a 28 km round-trip hike — and often over an hour of queuing for THE photo at the tip of the rock.","Gneiss spur at 1,180 m overhanging a lake 700 m below, like a tongue stuck out over the void","“Troll's tongue” jutting out over a reservoir, fjords of a Scandinavian country","Jutting rock with dizzying photos, Trolltunga, Norway","Trolltunga, Norway"],"horseshoe_bend":["Horseshoe Bend — Arizona, USA","At Horseshoe Bend, the Colorado River traces an almost perfect horseshoe at the bottom of a 300 m gorge carved into Navajo sandstone. The viewpoint, a few kilometres from Lake Powell, draws 2 million visitors a year.","Entrenched meander of a great river in sandstone, seen from the top of a 300 m cliff, downstream of a dam","Horseshoe bend of a river seen from a cliff, desert of south-western North America, near a reservoir","Most photographed horseshoe meander, Arizona","Horseshoe Bend, Arizona"],"mendenhall":["Mendenhall Ice Caves — Alaska, USA","Beneath the Mendenhall Glacier, meltwater sculpts caves of an unreal blue where water trickles under translucent vaults. Ephemeral by nature, they collapse and re-form as the glacier retreats — 800 m lost since 2000.","Blue cavities beneath a rapidly retreating 22 km glacier, flowing from a large coastal icefield","Blue ice caves beneath a glacier, near the capital of a vast state in the far north-west of North America","Blue ice caves beneath a glacier, Alaska","The Mendenhall Glacier caves, Juneau, Alaska"],"zhangye_danxia":["Zhangye Danxia — Gansu, China","Zhangye's “rainbow mountains” unfold ochre, red and yellow strata folded by tectonics: 24 million years of sedimentary deposits tilted up like an upturned mille-feuille, on the edge of the Gobi Desert.","Folded sandstone landforms striped with vivid colours, corridor of an ancient Silk Road","Striped “rainbow mountains”, north-western province of a vast East Asian country","Multicoloured mountains, Zhangye Danxia, China","Zhangye Danxia, the rainbow mountains, China"],"tunnel_amour":["Tunnel of Love — Klevan, Ukraine","The Tunnel of Love is an industrial railway line swallowed by vegetation: over 4 km, the trees trimmed by passing trains form a perfect green vault. Local legend promises couples who walk through it that their wish will come true.","Factory railway whose trains have trimmed a vault of trees, forming a green tunnel","Green tunnel over a railway line, west of an Eastern European country","Railway under an arch of trees, Tunnel of Love, Ukraine","The Tunnel of Love, Klevan, Ukraine"],"jacobs_well":["Jacob's Well — Texas, USA","Jacob's Well is the mouth of an artesian spring that plunges 40 m into a flooded network of 1,800 m of limestone passages. A swimming hole beloved by Texans on the surface, it is also one of the most dangerous cave-diving sites in the United States.","Karst spring of an aquifer with a flooded network mapped over 1,800 m, notorious as deadly for divers","Natural diving well reputed to be dangerous, in the hilly backcountry of a large state in the south of North America","Clear, deep spring-fed sinkhole, Jacob's Well, Texas","Jacob's Well, Texas"],"dunnottar":["Dunnottar Castle — Scotland","Perched on a cliff-ringed headland, Dunnottar once guarded the Honours of Scotland, hidden there to escape Cromwell in 1652. William Wallace burned an English garrison there in 1297.","Ruined fortress on a sea headland, former hiding place of the national crown jewels, besieged in the 17th century","Ruined castle on a cliff, east coast of a nation in the north of a British island","Spectacular fortress on a sea rock, Dunnottar, Scotland","Dunnottar Castle, Scotland"],"ik_kil":["Ik Kil — Yucatán, Mexico","The Ik Kil cenote is a sacred Maya well 60 m across whose water surface lies 26 m below ground, draped with vines that hang down towards the water. The Maya made offerings there to the rain god Chaac.","26 m open-air natural well with hanging vines, near a great Maya city","Sacred Maya natural well with clear waters, on a peninsula of a North American country","Famous circular cenote, Ik Kil, Yucatán","The Ik Kil cenote, Yucatán, Mexico"],"source_argent":["Anse Source d'Argent — La Digue, Seychelles","Anse Source d'Argent, fringed with pink granite boulders sculpted by erosion, is reputed to be the most photographed beach in the world. It is reached by crossing the old L'Union plantation by bike or ox cart.","Beach of pink granite boulders sculpted by erosion, reached through an old vanilla plantation","Pink granite beach among the most photographed in the world, Indian Ocean archipelago","Paradise beach with granite rocks, La Digue, Seychelles","Anse Source d'Argent, Seychelles"],"chittorgarh":["Chittor Fort — Rajasthan, India","Chittorgarh, India's largest fort with 13 km of ramparts, was the scene of three jauhars: rather than surrender, thousands of Rajput women immolated themselves when defeat became inevitable, in 1303, 1535 and 1568.","Largest Rajput fortress (280 ha), scene of three sieges and mass suicides, Tower of Victory","Gigantic fort of a former kingdom, north-western state of a vast South Asian country","India's largest UNESCO-listed fort, Chittorgarh, Rajasthan","Chittorgarh Fort, India"],"hitachi":["Hitachi Seaside Park — Japan","In spring, 4.5 million blue nemophila flowers cover Miharashi Hill in Hitachi Seaside Park, melting the landscape into the sky. In autumn, the same slopes turn scarlet with kochia.","Hill covered in 4.5 million blue flowers in spring, bushes glowing red in autumn","Park with hills blue with flowers, Pacific coast north-east of a megalopolis","Flower park with blue hills, Hitachi, Japan","Hitachi Seaside Park, Japan"],"atlanterhavsveien":["Atlantic Ocean Road — Norway","The Atlanterhavsveien hops from islet to islet for 8.3 km, peaking at the Storseisundet Bridge, whose curve seems to plunge into the sea. In storms, the waves literally sweep across the roadway — which, paradoxically, makes it peak season for photos.","8.3 km road hopping from islet to islet, dizzying curved bridge built through a dozen storms","Coastal road hopping from island to island over the sea, coast of a Scandinavian country","Famous road-bridge over the sea, Atlantic, Norway","The Atlantic Ocean Road, Norway"],"athabasca":["Athabasca Falls — Alberta, Canada","Athabasca Falls drop only 23 m, but their glacier-fed flow from Jasper National Park has sawn a quartzite gorge with perfectly polished potholes. The turquoise water owes its colour to suspended “glacial flour”.","Powerful cataract of a river in quartzite, milky water laden with glacial flour, mountain park","Powerful falls of a glacial river, in the Rockies of a vast North American country","Falls in a great Rockies park, Athabasca, Alberta","Athabasca Falls, Canada"],"fingal":["Fingal's Cave — Staffa, Scotland","Fingal's Cave runs 70 m into the uninhabited island of Staffa, between basalt columns twinned with those of the Giant's Causeway. The resonance of the waves is so musical that it inspired an overture by Mendelssohn in 1829.","70 m basalt sea cave on a deserted island, so musical it inspired a famous overture","Sea cave with basalt columns, small islands in the west of a British nation","Musical sea cave, Fingal, Scotland","Fingal's Cave, Scotland"],"douze_apotres":["The Twelve Apostles — Great Ocean Road, Australia","The Twelve Apostles are 45 m limestone stacks cut off from the cliffs by the Southern Ocean. There were never twelve — eight remain, and erosion topples one per decade while sculpting new ones.","Limestone stacks cut off from the cliff by erosion (2 cm/year), along a legendary coastal road","Sea rock stacks in a coastal park, south of a southern continent","Limestone sea pillars, Twelve Apostles, Great Ocean Road","The Twelve Apostles, Great Ocean Road, Australia"],"shifen":["Shifen Waterfall — Taiwan","Nicknamed “the little Niagara of Taiwan”, Shifen Waterfall spreads a 40 m-wide curtain in the Pingxi valley. The nearby village is famous for its paper lanterns released from the railway line that runs down the main street.","Horseshoe-shaped river waterfall nicknamed “little Niagara”, valley famous for its sky lanterns","Wide horseshoe waterfall near the capital, mountainous East Asian island","Widest waterfall on the island, Shifen, Taiwan","Shifen Waterfall, Taiwan"],"cathedral_cove":["Cathedral Cove — New Zealand","Cathedral Cove owes its name to the monumental limestone arch that links its two beaches like a nave. Accessible only on foot or by kayak, the cove served as a set for The Chronicles of Narnia: Prince Caspian.","Monumental limestone arch in a cove, reached by a coastal path, set for a fantasy film","Cove with a giant natural arch, on a peninsula of a large South Pacific island","Beach with a natural arch, Cathedral Cove, New Zealand","Cathedral Cove, New Zealand"],"capillas_marmol":["Marble Caves — General Carrera Lake, Chile","The Capillas de Mármol are caves of pure marble lapped by the turquoise waters of South America's second-largest lake. 6,000 years of waves have polished blue-veined vaults reachable only by boat or kayak.","Marble caves polished by 6,000 years of waves on a large turquoise lake, visited by rowing boat","Turquoise “marble chapels” on a large lake, Patagonia of a South American country","Marble caves on an Andean lake, Chilean Patagonia","The Marble Cathedral, Chile"],"gaiola":["Gaiola Bay — Naples, Italy","The two islets of Gaiola, linked by a narrow bridge, are said to be cursed: the list of their owners ruined, murdered or drowned has fed the legend. Yet the bay protects an underwater park where sunken Roman remains lie.","Two “cursed” islets linked by a narrow bridge, underwater park with submerged Roman remains","Island said to be cursed, linked by a small bridge, off the coast of a large port city in the south of a Mediterranean peninsula","Legendary islets off Naples, Gaiola Bay","Gaiola Island, Naples, Italy"],"arashiyama":["Arashiyama Bamboo Grove — Kyoto, Japan","In the Arashiyama bamboo grove, 20 m stalks filter a green light and, in the wind, produce a muffled creaking listed among the “100 Soundscapes of Japan” to be preserved.","Path winding between giant bamboos whose rustling is listed as a soundscape, west of the former capital","Forest of giant bamboo near a listed temple, west of a former imperial capital","Famous bamboo path, Arashiyama, Kyoto","The Arashiyama Bamboo Grove, Kyoto"],"son_doong":["Hang Sơn Đoòng — Vietnam","Son Doong is the largest cave passage in the world: up to 200 m high and 150 m wide, with its own jungle, river and clouds. Discovered in 1990 by a farmer, it was only explored in 2009.","Largest known cave passage in the world, collapsed dolines sheltering an underground jungle","Giant cave with an inner jungle, karst massif in the centre of a long Southeast Asian country","Largest cave in the world, Hang Son Doong, Vietnam","Hang Son Doong, Vietnam"],"aogashima":["Aogashima — Japan","Aogashima is an inhabited volcano in the middle of the Philippine Sea: a crater within a crater, home to 160 people. The island can only be reached by a daily helicopter or by a boat that manages to dock only one time in two.","Island volcano with a double caldera home to 160 people, hundreds of km from a capital, erupted in 1785","Inhabited volcanic island with a nested crater, remote East Asian archipelago","Spectacular volcanic island with a double crater, Aogashima, Japan","Aogashima Island, Japan"],"whitehaven":["Whitehaven Beach — Whitsundays, Australia","Whitehaven stretches for 7 km of 98% silica sand, so pure that it never heats up and squeaks underfoot. At its northern end, the tide swirls sand and turquoise into shifting patterns at Hill Inlet.","Seven km of 98% silica sand that never heats up, turquoise swirls of an estuary, barrier islands","Beach with the purest sand in the world, islands at the heart of a great southern coral reef","Iconic white beach, Whitehaven, Whitsundays, Australia","Whitehaven Beach, Australia"],"positano":["Positano — Amalfi Coast, Italy","Positano cascades in pastel houses clinging to the cliffs of the Amalfi Coast. A fishing village ruined in the 19th century — half its population emigrated to America — it was revived by artists and Steinbeck in the 1950s.","Vertical village of pastel houses tumbling down the cliff to a beach, celebrated by an American novelist","Pastel village clinging to the cliffs of a spectacular coast in the south of a Mediterranean peninsula","Much-photographed terraced coastal village, Positano, Amalfi Coast","Positano, Italy"],"to_sua":["To Sua Ocean Trench — Samoa","To Sua, “the giant hole”, is a 30 m-deep natural pool connected to the ocean by a lava tube. You climb down into it by a long wooden ladder set in the middle of the gardens of Lotofaga village.","Volcanic sinkhole linked to the ocean by a lava tube, emerald pool reached by a ladder","Emerald natural pool reached by ladder, island in a Polynesian archipelago","Famous ocean swimming hole, To Sua, Samoa","The To Sua Ocean Trench in Samoa"],"hvitserkur":["Hvítserkur — Iceland","Hvítserkur is a 15 m basalt dyke standing in the sea, resembling a drinking dragon — or, according to legend, a troll turned to stone by the sun. Its “legs” have been reinforced with concrete to delay the waves' inevitable victory.","Dragon-shaped basalt rock drinking from the sea, bird colony, base reinforced with concrete","Animal-shaped sea stack on a north-western peninsula of a subarctic volcanic island","Animal-shaped sea rock, Hvítserkur, Iceland","Hvítserkur, Iceland"],"huashan":["Mount Hua — Shaanxi, China","Huashan, one of the five sacred Taoist mountains, is famous for its “plank walk in the sky”: a few beams bolted to a vertical rock face at 2,100 m, which you edge along in a harness facing the void.","Five-peaked sacred Taoist mountain, plank walkway bolted to a vertical cliff","Mountain with a plank path said to be the most dangerous in the world, in the centre of a vast East Asian country","Mountain with a dizzying trail, Huashan, China","Mount Huashan, China"],"blue_lagoon":["Blue Lagoon — Iceland","The Blue Lagoon was born by accident in 1976: geothermal water discharged by the nearby Svartsengi power station formed a milky lake in the lava. Rich in silica and at 38 °C all year round, it has become Iceland's most visited site.","Milky 38°C lagoon born of a geothermal power plant's runoff, in the middle of a lava field","Milky geothermal bath in a lava field, peninsula of a subarctic volcanic island","Famous milky blue lagoon, Iceland","The Blue Lagoon, Iceland"],"moeraki":["Moeraki Boulders — New Zealand","The Moeraki Boulders are almost perfect calcite spheres, up to 2 m in diameter, scattered across Koekohe Beach. These 60-million-year-old concretions emerge one by one from the clay cliff, which releases them as it erodes.","Giant, perfectly spherical stone balls stranded on a beach, linked to a legendary canoe","Giant stone spheres on a beach, east coast of a large South Pacific island","Mysterious rock spheres, Moeraki, New Zealand","The Moeraki Boulders, New Zealand"],"the_wave":["The Wave — Arizona, USA","The Wave is a sandstone wave frozen since the Jurassic: 190-million-year-old dunes petrified into orange ripples. Access is limited to 64 people a day, chosen by lottery — fewer than 5% of applicants.","Finely laminated orange sandstone wave, access limited to a few dozen visitors a day chosen by lottery","Orange sandstone wave with ultra-restricted access, desert on the border of two western states","Rippled sandstone formation, The Wave, American desert","The Wave, Arizona"],"wave_rock":["Wave Rock — Western Australia","Wave Rock is a granite wave 15 m high and 110 m long, poised to break over the bush for 2.7 billion years. Its vertical stripes come from algae that colour the run-off according to the season.","Hollowed-out flank of an ancient granite rock, forming a frozen wave 15 m high, in the middle of the bush","Striped granite wave in the bush, 300 km from a large city in the west of a southern continent","Rock shaped like a giant wave, Wave Rock, Australia","Wave Rock, Australia"],"maya_bay":["Maya Bay — Phi Phi Islands, Thailand","Maya Bay, an amphitheatre of limestone cliffs closing around a perfect beach, was made world-famous by the film The Beach (2000). A victim of its own success — 5,000 visitors a day — it closed for 3 years to let corals and sharks return.","Cliff-enclosed bay made famous by a cult film, closed for several years to let the corals recover","Beach from a cult film ringed by cliffs, island in an Andaman Sea archipelago","Paradise bay made famous on screen, Maya Bay, Phi Phi","Maya Bay, Phi Phi Islands, Thailand"],"meteores":["Meteora — Greece","At Meteora, 14th-century monasteries crown 300 m conglomerate pinnacles. Monks and supplies were once hauled up in nets on a rope — replaced, so it was said, “only when it broke”.","Six working monasteries perched on conglomerate pinnacles, once reached by a net hauled up by hand","Monasteries perched on rock pillars, on a central plain of a peninsula in south-eastern Europe","Monasteries atop dizzying pinnacles, Meteora, Greece","Meteora, Greece"],"benagil":["Benagil Cave — Algarve, Portugal","Benagil Cave is a sea cathedral whose vault opens to the sky through a perfect oculus, lighting an inner beach that cannot be reached on foot. You get in by swimming, kayaking or taking a boat from the neighbouring beach.","Sea dome pierced by a skylight straight overhead, inner beach unreachable on foot, golden limestone","Sea cave with a natural oculus and a hidden beach, on the south coast of an Iberian country","Dome-shaped cave with an inner beach, Benagil, Algarve","Benagil Cave, Portugal"],"navagio":["Navagio — Zakynthos, Greece","Navagio Beach takes its name from the Panagiotis, a smugglers' coaster that ran aground in 1980 and has been rusting ever since at the foot of 200 m cliffs. “Shipwreck Cove” can only be reached by sea.","Beach ringed by white cliffs, sheltering the rusting wreck of a smuggling ship that ran aground in 1980","Beach with a rusting wreck between white cliffs, on an island in the Ionian Sea","Famous shipwreck beach, Navagio, Zakynthos","Navagio Beach, Zakynthos, Greece"],"mulafossur":["Múlafossur — Faroe Islands, Denmark","At Gásadalur, the Múlafossur waterfall leaps straight off the cliff into the Atlantic, with the village of 18 inhabitants at its back. Until the tunnel opened in 2004, the postman crossed a 400 m pass on foot three times a week to deliver the mail.","Coastal waterfall dropping straight into the ocean in front of a hamlet cut off until a tunnel arrived in 2004","Waterfall plunging into the ocean in front of a village, in a North Atlantic archipelago","Iconic coastal waterfall, Múlafossur, Faroe Islands","Múlafossur, Faroe Islands"],"peles":["Peleș Castle — Romania","Peleș, the summer residence of Romania's kings, completed in 1914, was the first castle in Europe to be fully electrified — with its own power station. Its 160 rooms combine neo-Renaissance panelling, stained glass and a sliding ceiling that opens onto the stars.","Royal mountain residence, Europe's first electrified castle, with a retractable ceiling in the great hall","Neo-Renaissance royal castle tucked into a mountain range in Eastern Europe","Sumptuous fairy-tale castle, Peleș, Romania","Peleș Castle, Romania"],"la_valette":["Valletta — Malta","Valletta was built from scratch by the Knights of St John after the Great Ottoman Siege of 1565, to plans by an assistant of Michelangelo. The smallest capital in the EU, it packs 320 monuments into less than 1 km².","City founded in 1566 by an order of knights, its co-cathedral home to a Caravaggio masterpiece","Fortified island capital, the smallest capital in the European Union, in the Mediterranean","Historic fortified capital, Valletta, Malta","Valletta, Malta"],"ararat":["Mount Ararat — Turkey","Ararat, a 5,137 m volcano overlooking the Turkish–Armenian border, is the biblical mountain where Noah's Ark is said to have come to rest. A national symbol of Armenia, it nevertheless lies in Turkish territory, and climbing it still requires a permit issued by the authorities.","Snow-capped 5,137 m volcano where a biblical ark is said to have run aground, national symbol of a neighbouring people","The country's highest peak, a biblical volcano near three borders, in the east of a transcontinental country","Legendary volcano of Noah's Ark, Mount Ararat, Turkey","Mount Ararat, Turkey"],"havasu":["Havasu Falls — Arizona, USA","Havasu Falls plunges 30 m into turquoise pools at the bottom of the Grand Canyon, on the reservation of the Havasupai — “the people of the blue-green waters”. It can only be reached after a 16 km hike, sometimes booked a year in advance.","Carbonate-saturated blue-green waterfall on a Native American reservation in a great canyon, near a village with no road","Turquoise waterfalls on a Native American reservation at the bottom of a great canyon, south-western North America","Famous blue-green waterfalls, Havasu Falls, Arizona","Havasu Falls, Arizona"],"cerro_torre":["Cerro Torre — Patagonia, Argentina","Cerro Torre, a 3,128 m granite spire in the Fitz Roy massif, is reputed to be one of the hardest peaks in the world. Its first ascent, claimed in 1959 by Cesare Maestri, remains one of the greatest controversies in mountaineering; the first undisputed ascent did not take place until 1974.","3,128 m granite spire rising in a glacial park, one of the hardest peaks to climb","Granite peak in a national park in the far south, mountain range straddling a border","Granite spire in a park in the far south, El Chaltén area","Cerro Torre in Patagonia, Argentina"]},"E":{"sekigahara":["Battle of Sekigahara — Gifu, Japan","On 21 October 1600, Tokugawa Ieyasu's victory over the Western coalition on this misty plain ended a century of civil war and founded a shogunate that ruled for 265 years.","One misty morning in 1600, on a sodden plain, thousands of banners face each other; by nightfall, a single man rules, and an era of endless peace begins.","In 1600, two coalitions of feudal lords clash on a plain; a betrayal decides the outcome and opens 265 years of dynasty.","The battle that founded the Tokugawa shogunate, in Japan.","The Battle of Sekigahara, in Gifu, Japan."],"waterloo":["Battle of Waterloo — Walloon Brabant, Belgium","On 18 June 1815, Napoleon's defeat by Wellington and Blücher ended the Hundred Days and the Empire, redrawing Europe for a century.","On a rainy Sunday in June 1815, an empire collapses on a muddy plateau before a coalition.","In June 1815, an emperor back from exile is crushed by an Anglo-Prussian coalition on a farming plateau, south of a great capital.","Napoleon's last battle, in Belgium.","The Battle of Waterloo, at Waterloo, Belgium."],"gettysburg":["Battle of Gettysburg — Pennsylvania, USA","From 1 to 3 July 1863, the bloodiest battle of the American Civil War (51,000 casualties) shattered Lee's invasion of the North and marked the turning point of the conflict.","In the summer heat of 1863, for three days, fields and orchards are covered with the dead; on these hills, a torn nation gambles its future.","In 1863, a three-day battle halts a Southern invasion during a civil war; the president will later deliver a famous speech there.","The turning point of the American Civil War, in the United States.","The Battle of Gettysburg, in Pennsylvania, United States."],"cannae":["Battle of Cannae — Apulia, Italy","In 216 BC, Hannibal encircled and annihilated a Roman army twice the size of his own with an enveloping manoeuvre that remains a model of military art.","In 216 BC, under a scorching sun, a vast and overconfident army lets itself be trapped in a ring of iron, and vanishes in a single day.","In 216 BC, a Carthaginian general encircles and annihilates an army twice his size — a textbook case of ancient tactics.","Hannibal's victory over Rome, in Italy.","The Battle of Cannae, in Apulia, Italy."],"hastings":["Battle of Hastings — East Sussex, England","On 14 October 1066, William the Conqueror's victory over Harold II began the Norman conquest of England and transformed its language and its nobility.","In 1066, at the end of a long autumn day, a crown rolls in the dust of a hillside; an entire island swings towards another destiny.","In 1066, a duke from the Continent conquers an island; the opposing king dies from an arrow, a scene embroidered on a famous tapestry.","The Norman conquest of England.","The Battle of Hastings, in Sussex, England."],"mur_berlin":["Fall of the Berlin Wall — Berlin, Germany","On 9 November 1989, the sudden opening of the crossing points hastened the end of the city's division and, two years later, that of the Cold War.","One November evening in 1989, the unbelievable becomes real: people dance and weep on top of a hated wall, hammer in hand, under the spotlights of the whole world.","In November 1989, an urban border standing since 1961 collapses in a single evening as crowds pour through the checkpoints.","The fall of the Wall, in Germany.","The fall of the Berlin Wall, in Berlin, Germany."],"omaha_beach":["Omaha Beach — Calvados, France","On 6 June 1944, the deadliest sector of the Allied landings cost nearly 3,000 American casualties in a few hours under fire from the cliffs.","At dawn on 6 June 1944, under a hail of steel, thousands of men surging from a grey sea rush towards a stretch of sand where death mows them down in waves.","On 6 June 1944, the deadliest amphibious assault of the five beaches, under fire from bunkers perched on cliffs.","The bloodiest Allied landing, in France.","Omaha Beach, in Calvados (Normandy), France."],"sarajevo_1914":["Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand — Bosnia and Herzegovina","On 28 June 1914, Archduke Franz Ferdinand was shot dead by Gavrilo Princip near the Latin Bridge, setting off the chain of events that led to the First World War.","One summer's day in 1914, in a street of a Balkan town, two shots make an old order totter; within weeks, a continent is ablaze.","In 1914, a nationalist student shoots dead the heir to an empire and his wife — the spark of a world war.","The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, in Bosnia and Herzegovina.","The Sarajevo assassination, in Bosnia and Herzegovina."],"stalingrad":["Battle of Stalingrad — Volgograd, Russia","From 1942 to 1943, the deadliest battle in history (nearly 2 million casualties) marked the turning point on the Eastern Front.","In the winter of 1942, men fight for every cellar and every stairway of a city reduced to rubble; frost, hunger and gunfire swallow whole armies.","In 1942–1943, a ferocious urban siege along a great river ends with the encirclement of an entire army — the turning point of the war in the East.","The turning point on the Eastern Front, in Russia.","The Battle of Stalingrad (Volgograd), in Russia."],"magna_carta":["Signing of Magna Carta — Surrey, England","In 1215, at Runnymede, King John sealed Magna Carta, forced on him by his barons, a founding milestone in limiting royal power.","In 1215, under a stormy sky, a cornered king, hemmed in by his great vassals, grudgingly sets his seal at the foot of a text that will one day curb every throne.","In 1215, rebel barons wrest a founding document from an unpopular king, in a meadow beside a river.","The signing of Magna Carta, in England.","Magna Carta, at Runnymede (Surrey), England."],"bastille":["Storming of the Bastille — Paris, France","On 14 July 1789, the storming of the fortress-prison, a symbol of royal tyranny, marked the tipping point of the French Revolution.","On 14 July 1789, a feverish crowd sweeps over some old towers; barely a handful of prisoners are found inside, yet a whole world totters.","On 14 July 1789, a crowd storms a fortress-prison, a symbol of arbitrary rule, in the east of a great capital.","The storming of the Bastille, in France.","The storming of the Bastille, in Paris, France."],"marathon":["Battle of Marathon — Attica, Greece","In 490 BC, the Athenian victory over Darius's Persians inspired the legend of the messenger who ran to announce the triumph, the origin of the modern race.","In 490 BC, on an open plain facing the sea, a handful of free men, against all odds, put to flight a vast power from afar.","In 490 BC, citizen-soldiers repel an empire from the East on a coastal plain; a runner carries the news.","The victory of the Greek city-states over the Persians, in Greece.","The Battle of Marathon, in Attica, Greece."],"appomattox":["Surrender at Appomattox — Virginia, USA","On 9 April 1865, Lee's surrender to Grant in a village parlour effectively ended the American Civil War.","One April afternoon in 1865, in a hushed room of an isolated house, two weary men shake hands; four years of bloodshed between brothers finally come to an end.","In April 1865, a Southern general surrenders in the parlour of a village house, ending a civil war.","The Confederate surrender, in the United States.","The surrender at Appomattox, in Virginia, United States."],"trafalgar":["Battle of Trafalgar — Cádiz, Spain","On 21 October 1805, Nelson destroyed the Franco-Spanish fleets there at the cost of his life, securing British naval supremacy for a century.","In 1805, amid the smoke and din of a naval battle, the victory is total; but on the deck of his ship, the victor dies at the very moment of his glory.","In 1805, a fleet is crushed off an Atlantic cape; the victorious admiral, one-eyed and one-armed, dies at the moment of triumph.","Nelson's naval victory, off the coast of Spain.","The Battle of Trafalgar, off Cádiz, Spain."],"constantinople_1453":["Fall of Constantinople — Istanbul, Turkey","In 1453, the capture of the Byzantine capital by Mehmed II ended the Eastern Roman Empire and tipped Europe into a new era.","In 1453, after weeks of siege, ramparts thought to be eternal finally give way; the last heir of a very old world vanishes in the fray.","In 1453, giant cannons breach thousand-year-old walls and wipe out an ancient empire, on a strait between two continents.","The Ottoman capture of Constantinople, in Turkey.","The fall of Constantinople (Istanbul), in Turkey."],"utah_beach":["Normandy landings — Utah Beach — Manche, France","On 6 June 1944, the westernmost beach of the landings was taken with limited losses, opening the road to Cherbourg.","At daybreak on 6 June 1944, a beach a little kinder than the others receives the first assault waves; for once, fate spares the men.","On 6 June 1944, the westernmost of the five beaches of a great landing; a current pushed the landing craft towards a less defended sector.","An Allied landing in 1944, in France.","Utah Beach, in Manche (Normandy), France."],"hiroshima_1945":["Atomic bombing of Hiroshima — Japan","On 6 August 1945, the first atomic bomb used in war razed the city and hastened Japan's surrender.","One August morning in 1945, a single flash brighter than the sun wipes out a city in a second; humanity has just entered an age of terror.","On 6 August 1945, the first nuclear weapon used in war strikes a port city; a gutted dome preserves its memory.","The first atomic bomb, in Japan.","The bombing of Hiroshima, in Japan."],"thermopyles":["Battle of Thermopylae — Phthiotis, Greece","In 480 BC, 300 Spartans and their allies held up Xerxes' Persian army in a coastal pass, a sacrifice that became legend.","Around 480 BC, in the shadow of a narrow pass between the mountains and the sea, a handful of men stand up to a human tide — until treachery brings them down.","Around 480 BC, a handful of warriors defend a coastal pass against a vast army, before being betrayed by a roundabout path.","The last stand of the 300 Spartans, in Greece.","Thermopylae, in Phthiotis, Greece."],"independance_us":["Signing of the Declaration of Independence — Philadelphia, USA","On 4 July 1776, thirteen colonies proclaimed their independence in Independence Hall, the founding act of the United States.","One summer in 1776, in the stifling air of a closed room, solemn men put their names at the foot of a text that gives birth to a nation and defies an empire.","In 1776, thirteen colonies proclaim their break with their crown, on a 4th of July, in a clock-towered hall in a great port city.","The signing of the Declaration of Independence, in the United States.","The Declaration of Independence, in Philadelphia, United States."],"azincourt":["Battle of Agincourt — Pas-de-Calais, France","In 1415, Henry V's English archers crushed a French chivalry bogged down in mud, a turning point in the Hundred Years' War.","In 1415, in pouring rain, the pride of an armoured nobility sinks into the mire and collapses beneath a sky blackened with arrows.","In 1415, heavy cavalry gets bogged down and is mown down by longbowmen, during a long dynastic war.","Henry V's English victory, in France.","The Battle of Agincourt, in Pas-de-Calais, France."],"pompei_79":["Destruction of Pompeii — Campania, Italy","In AD 79, the eruption of Vesuvius buried Pompeii under ash, freezing Roman life for eternity.","In the year 79, on a summer's day, a mountain splits open and spews fire; an entire city disappears under a rain of ash, frozen forever at the moment of its death.","In the year 79, a volcanic eruption petrifies an entire city under ash, rediscovered intact 17 centuries later.","The burial of Pompeii by Vesuvius, in Italy.","The eruption at Pompeii, in Campania, Italy."],"bannockburn":["Battle of Bannockburn — Stirling, Scotland","In 1314, Robert the Bruce defeated Edward II's English army there, securing Scotland's independence.","In 1314, in the mud of a marshy field, the pride of a great army gets bogged down before a small, determined people; a long-dreamed-of freedom becomes real.","In 1314, a king triumphs over an army three times larger from the south, a milestone on the road to regained independence.","Robert the Bruce's victory, in Scotland.","The Battle of Bannockburn, near Stirling, Scotland."],"congres_vienne":["Congress of Vienna — Austria","From 1814 to 1815, the European powers redrew the continent there after Napoleon's fall, ushering in a century of balance.","In 1814, amid the salons and balls of a capital, the powerful of the day remake the world to suit themselves, a map spread out between two dances.","In 1814–1815, a great diplomatic conference carves up a continent anew to the sound of waltzes, after the fall of an emperor.","The congress of the European Restoration, in Austria.","The Congress of Vienna, in Vienna, Austria."],"little_bighorn":["Battle of the Little Bighorn — Montana, USA","In 1876, Sioux and Cheyenne warriors wiped out Custer's regiment, the last great Native American victory.","In 1876, on the grassy heights above a river, horsemen who came to impose their law are overwhelmed by numbers; their foes savour a victory with no tomorrow.","In 1876, a cavalry regiment is wiped out by a coalition of Indigenous nations, a last blaze of glory before subjugation.","Custer's defeat by the Sioux, in the United States.","Little Bighorn, in Montana, United States."],"poitiers_732":["Battle of Tours (732) — Vienne, France","In 732, Charles Martel halted the Arab-Berber advance there, an event long seen as checking the expansion of Islam into Europe.","In 732, a mounted army launched from the lands of the South shatters against the tightly packed spears of a seasoned leader; history will make it a turning point.","In 732, a Frankish leader stops a column coming from the south, an episode later raised into a symbol.","Charles Martel's victory, in France.","The Battle of Tours–Poitiers of 732, in Vienne, France."],"fondation_rome":["Founding of Rome (legendary) — Lazio, Italy","According to tradition, Romulus founded Rome on the Palatine in 753 BC after killing his brother Remus.","Around 753 BC, on a hill beside a river, a man ploughs a sacred furrow and strikes down his brother for crossing it; from a fratricide is born a city destined for eternity.","Around 753 BC, the legendary birth of a city on a hill, by a twin suckled by a she-wolf who became his brother's killer.","The legendary founding of Rome, in Italy.","The founding of Rome, in Lazio, Italy."],"yorktown":["Siege of Yorktown — Virginia, USA","In 1781, Cornwallis's surrender to Washington and Rochambeau sealed American independence.","In 1781, hemmed in by land and by sea, an exhausted army lays down its arms; a young nation has just wrested its freedom.","In 1781, a British army surrenders, caught in a vice between insurgents and allies arriving by sea — the lock that sealed an independence.","Cornwallis's surrender, in the United States.","The Siege of Yorktown, in Virginia, United States."],"worms_1521":["Diet of Worms — Rhineland-Palatinate, Germany","In 1521, Luther refused there to recant before Charles V, the founding act of the Reformation.","In 1521, alone before an assembly of the mighty, a pale but unbending churchman refuses to disown his writings; his voice will tear apart the faith of an entire continent.","In 1521, a reforming monk appears before an emperor and refuses to retract, splitting Western Christendom.","The diet where Luther defied Charles V, in Germany.","The Diet of Worms, in Worms, Germany."],"lepante":["Battle of Lepanto — Gulf of Patras, Greece","In 1571, the Christian Holy League destroyed the Ottoman fleet, the last great galley battle in history.","In 1571, on an autumn sea, hundreds of ships ram and board one another amid the crash of splintering wood — the last great duel fought by the strength of arms alone.","In 1571, the last great clash of oared galleys; a Christian league wins the day, and a future one-handed novelist fought there.","The Holy League's victory over the Ottomans, in Greece.","The Battle of Lepanto, in the Gulf of Patras, Greece."],"sacre_charlemagne":["Coronation of Charlemagne — Rome, Italy","On Christmas Day 800, the pope crowned Charlemagne emperor in Old St Peter's Basilica, reviving the imperial idea in the West.","On Christmas Day in the year 800, beneath the vaults of a candlelit basilica, a crown is placed on a king's head; the West dreams of empire once more.","On Christmas Day in the year 800, a pope crowns an emperor, reviving in the West a title extinct for three centuries.","The coronation of Charlemagne as emperor, in Italy.","The coronation of Charlemagne, in Rome, Italy."],"verdun":["Battle of Verdun — Meuse, France","In 1916, the longest battle of the First World War (300 days, 700,000 casualties) became the symbol of the hell of the trenches.","In 1916, for ten months, two armies sink into the same patch of shell-ploughed earth, where men die by the hundreds of thousands for a few acres of mud.","In 1916, a 300-day trench hell around pounded forts, summed up in one watchword: “They shall not pass”.","The emblematic battle of 1916, in France.","The Battle of Verdun, in Meuse, France."],"orleans_1429":["Joan of Arc at the Siege of Orléans — Orléans, France","In 1429, Joan of Arc lifted the siege of Orléans, turning the Hundred Years' War in favour of the King of France.","In 1429, before a city starved by a long siege, a slight figure in white armour rouses disheartened soldiers and turns the tide of an endless war.","In 1429, a young peasant girl guided by voices gets a siege lifted and turns the tide of a long war.","Joan of Arc lifts the siege, in France.","Joan of Arc rallying the troops at the Siege of Orléans (France)."],"ladysmith":["Siege of Ladysmith — KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa","During the Boer War, the Siege of Ladysmith (1899–1900) saw a British force encircled for 118 days by Boer commandos.","In 1899, under the sun of a distant veld, armed farmers hold a proud imperial garrison at bay for months; colonial pride takes a battering.","In 1899–1900, farmer-fighters besiege an imperial garrison for 118 days, in a colonial war in southern Africa.","The Siege of Ladysmith, in South Africa.","The Siege of Ladysmith, in KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa."],"zama":["Battle of Zama — Siliana, Tunisia","In 202 BC, Scipio Africanus defeated Hannibal, ending the Second Punic War.","In 202 BC, in the dust of an African plain, the most feared of conquerors finally falls; his star fades on the very soil where, as a child, he had sworn eternal hatred.","In 202 BC, a Carthaginian strategist is defeated, his own elephants turned against him by a rival general.","Hannibal's defeat by Scipio, in Tunisia.","The Battle of Zama, in the Siliana region, Tunisia."],"tordesillas":["Treaty of Tordesillas — Valladolid, Spain","In 1494, Spain and Portugal divided the New World between them along a meridian, under the auspices of the pope.","In 1494, bent over a map of a world still almost blank, two crowns draw a line across the ocean and share out lands neither has ever seen.","In 1494, two Iberian crowns divide up a barely discovered world, a line drawn across the ocean at a pope's behest.","The division of the New World, in Spain.","The Treaty of Tordesillas, province of Valladolid, Spain."],"midway":["Battle of Midway — Midway Atoll, Pacific","In June 1942, the American air-and-sea victory destroyed four Japanese aircraft carriers, the turning point of the Pacific War.","In June 1942, in a single day, four steel giants sink in the middle of an ocean; intelligence, more than guns, turns the tide of a war.","In June 1942, an air-and-sea battle won thanks to breaking the enemy's codes; four carriers sunk in a day tip the balance of an ocean.","The turning point of the Pacific War, in the Pacific.","The Battle of Midway, at Midway Atoll, in the Pacific."],"grenade_1492":["Fall of Granada — Andalusia, Spain","In 1492, the surrender of the Emirate of Granada completed the Reconquista and the unity of Christian Spain.","In 1492, at the end of a long siege, the gates of a city of crimson palaces open to two sovereigns; seven centuries of reconquest come to an end.","In 1492, the peninsula's last Muslim kingdom falls; the keys to a city of red palaces pass to two Catholic sovereigns.","The end of the Reconquista, in Spain.","The fall of Granada, in Andalusia, Spain."],"actium":["Battle of Actium — Ambracian Gulf, Greece","In 31 BC, Octavian defeated Antony and Cleopatra, paving the way for the Roman Empire.","In 31 BC, on sunlit waters, a battle decides who will be master of the world; the defeated lovers will soon choose death over chains.","In 31 BC, a naval victory hands an empire to its victor and drives a pair of defeated lovers to suicide.","The defeat of Antony and Cleopatra, in Greece.","The Battle of Actium, in the Ambracian Gulf, Greece."],"jeu_de_paume":["Tennis Court Oath — Versailles, France","On 20 June 1789, the deputies of the Third Estate swore not to disband until they had given France a constitution.","In June 1789, finding the doors shut, men crowd into a vast bare hall and swear, hands raised, not to part until they have changed everything.","In June 1789, deputies locked out take an oath in a royal sports hall, promising to rebuild a kingdom from the ground up.","The Tennis Court Oath, in France.","The Tennis Court Oath, at Versailles, France."],"iena":["Battle of Jena–Auerstedt — Thuringia, Germany","In 1806, Napoleon crushed the Prussian army, hastening the collapse of the Kingdom of Prussia.","In 1806, the proud army of an old kingdom, which believed itself invincible, is swept away in a few hours; the myth of a great military power collapses at a stroke.","In 1806, an emperor from the West crushes in a single day an army reputed to be invincible, a lightning double victory.","Napoleon's victory over Prussia, in Germany.","The Battle of Jena, in Thuringia, Germany."],"troisieme_guerre_servile":["Third Servile War — Sele Valley — Campania, Italy","Third Servile War (73–71 BC): the slave gladiator Spartacus led Rome's greatest slave revolt before being crushed by Crassus.","Around 73 BC, a man escaped from the arena rouses a multitude of the oppressed and makes the masters tremble; defeated, his band will end up nailed along a great road.","In 73–71 BC, an escaped gladiator leads an army of slaves, ultimately crucified along a great highway.","The Third Servile War (Spartacus's revolt), in Italy.","Spartacus's Third Servile War (73–71 BC), in Campania, Italy."],"chute_de_tenochtitlan":["Fall of Tenochtitlan — Mexico City, Mexico","In 1521, Cortés and his Indigenous allies took the Aztec capital after a devastating siege, bringing down the empire.","In 1521, the most dazzling of cities, set upon a mirror of water, falls after months of siege; with it fades the splendour of a people who believed themselves eternal.","In 1521, a handful of adventurers from across the sea and their local allies besiege a lake city — the end of an empire of the sun.","The capture of Tenochtitlan by Cortés, in Mexico.","The fall of Tenochtitlan, in Mexico City, Mexico."],"bataille_de_culloden":["Battle of Culloden — Highlands, Scotland","In 1746, the crushing of the Jacobites ended the Stuarts' hopes and upended Scottish clan society.","In 1746, on a grey, rain-lashed moor, the mountain clans are cut down within an hour; with them dies the dream of an old dynasty.","In 1746, an army of mountain clansmen is massacred on a rain-swept moor, the twilight of a dynastic cause.","The Jacobite defeat, in Scotland.","The Battle of Culloden, in the Highlands, Scotland."],"grand_incendie_de_rome":["Great Fire of Rome — Rome, Italy","In AD 64, a huge fire ravaged Rome for six days; Nero laid the blame on the Christians.","In the year 64, for six days and six nights, flames devour an ancient capital; whispers say the master sang before the blaze, and there must be culprits to punish.","In the year 64, a six-day blaze devours an ancient capital; the emperor, accused of singing over the ruins, points the finger at scapegoats.","The great fire under Nero, in Italy.","The fire of Rome, in Rome, Italy."],"bataille_de_nagashino":["Battle of Nagashino — Aichi, Japan","In 1575, Oda Nobunaga used massed arquebus volleys there to break the Takeda cavalry, a tactical revolution.","In 1575, crouched behind palisades, gunmen fire volley after volley; a glorious cavalry charge collapses — firepower has changed war.","In 1575, volleys from entrenched arquebusiers shatter a renowned cavalry charge, a firearms revolution in the Far East.","Oda Nobunaga's victory, in Japan.","The Battle of Nagashino, in the Aichi region, Japan."],"debarquement_de_provence":["Operation Dragoon — Var, France","In August 1944, Operation Dragoon opened a second front in France via the Mediterranean, swiftly liberating the South.","In August 1944, a second landing reaches a sun-drenched southern coast; forgotten in the shadow of the one in the North, it nonetheless opens another door.","In August 1944, a second amphibious assault on a sunny southern coast, less famous than the one in the North two months earlier.","The Provence landings, in France.","The Provence landings, in the Var, France."],"bataille_de_bouvines":["Battle of Bouvines — Nord, France","In 1214, Philip Augustus's victory over a coalition strengthened the kingdom of France and its royal authority.","On a Sunday in 1214, under a summer sun, a king stands up to a whole league of princes who have come to bring him down; from his victory, a young monarchy emerges united.","On a Sunday in 1214, a king triumphs over a coalition of an emperor and counts, cementing a young monarchy.","The victory of Philip Augustus, in France.","The Battle of Bouvines, in the Nord, France."],"grand_incendie_de_londres":["Great Fire of London — England","In 1666, a fire that started in a Pudding Lane bakery destroyed the medieval heart of London in four days.","In 1666, a spark from an oven sets a timber town ablaze; for four days the flames devour it — yet they purge, so it is said, the scourge that was eating away at it.","In 1666, a blaze that started in a bakery razes an old timber city in four days and, so it is said, puts an end to a plague epidemic.","The Great Fire, in England.","The Great Fire of London, in London, England."],"bataille_de_salamine":["Battle of Salamis — Saronic Gulf, Greece","In 480 BC, the Greek fleet trapped and destroyed the Persian armada in a strait, saving Greece.","In 480 BC, in a channel too narrow for its numbers, a vast armada gets tangled up and sinks under the rams of nimbler ships.","In 480 BC, in a tight strait, agile triremes wipe out an armada too large to manoeuvre.","The victory of the Greek city-states over the Persian fleet, in Greece.","The Battle of Salamis, in the Saronic Gulf, Greece."],"defenestration_de_prague":["Defenestration of Prague — Czech Republic","In 1618, imperial governors were thrown from the windows of Prague Castle, triggering the Thirty Years' War.","In 1618, hurled from a castle window, envoys miraculously survive their fall; this grotesque flight signals the start of thirty years of war.","In 1618, imperial representatives thrown from the windows of a castle survive — the spark of a thirty-year war.","The defenestration at the castle, in the Czech Republic.","The Defenestration of Prague, in Prague, Czech Republic."],"bataille_de_gaugameles":["Battle of Gaugamela — Nineveh, Iraq","In 331 BC, Alexander the Great crushed Darius III, opening the way to the conquest of the Persian Empire.","In 331 BC, in the dust of a boundless plain, a young man of dazzling genius puts to flight the greatest king on earth and his ocean of soldiers; the whole East lies open before him.","In 331 BC, a young Macedonian conqueror routs a “king of kings” and his vast army, on a plain in Mesopotamia.","Alexander's victory over Darius III, in Iraq.","The Battle of Gaugamela, near Nineveh, Iraq."],"bataille_de_kadesh":["Battle of Kadesh — Homs, Syria","Around 1274 BC, Ramesses II's Egyptians and the Hittites clashed in one of the first great documented battles, followed by the oldest known peace treaty.","Around 1274 BC, amid a din of wheels and horses, two great empires of the ancient world tear each other apart with no victor; from their weariness will be born the first peace ever carved in stone.","Around 1274 BC, a chariot clash between two Bronze Age empires, followed by the oldest known peace treaty, in the Near East.","Ramesses II's clash with the Hittites, in Syria.","The Battle of Kadesh, near Homs, Syria."],"chute_de_saigon":["Fall of Saigon — Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam","On 30 April 1975, the capture of Saigon by Northern forces ended the Vietnam War and reunified the country.","On 30 April 1975, in sticky heat, a city tips into chaos: desperate queues, burning papers and fleeing hopes, as a long war breathes its last.","On 30 April 1975, tanks smash through the gates of a palace and a helicopter evacuates the last foreigners: a southern capital falls, in South-East Asia.","The fall of Saigon, in Vietnam.","The fall of Saigon (Ho Chi Minh City), in Vietnam."],"bataille_de_vienne_(1683)":["Battle of Vienna (1683) — Austria","In 1683, the Polish-Imperial relief army led by John III Sobieski lifted the Ottoman siege of Vienna, marking the beginning of the Ottoman Empire's retreat from Europe.","In 1683, as a bled-white capital is on the verge of giving way, a swarm of horsemen sweeps down from the hills at dusk and drives back a tide that threatened a whole continent.","In 1683, a charge of winged cavalry from the East lifts the siege of a capital and halts an Ottoman advance, in Central Europe.","The Ottoman siege lifted by Sobieski, in Austria.","The Battle of Vienna of 1683, in Vienna, Austria."],"marche_du_sel_de_gandhi":["Gandhi's Salt March — Gujarat, India","In 1930, Gandhi completed his 385 km march at Dandi to gather salt in defiance of the colonial tax, a major act of civil disobedience.","In 1930, after long weeks of walking barefoot, a frail old man bends down on the shore and picks up a little salt; this tiny gesture shakes an empire.","In 1930, at the end of a 385 km march to the sea, a loincloth-clad leader scoops up a handful of salt in defiance of an empire, on a coast of the Indian subcontinent.","Gandhi's Salt March, in India.","The Salt March, at Dandi (Gujarat), India."],"bataille_des_champs_catalauniques":["Battle of the Catalaunian Plains — Marne, France","In 451, a Romano-Visigothic coalition halted Attila and his Huns, one of Rome's last great victories.","In 451, yesterday's enemies seal a last-minute alliance to bar the way to an invader from the steppes whose very name made people tremble.","In 451, an unlikely coalition stops a conqueror from the steppes nicknamed the “Scourge of God”, in the north-east of a Western European kingdom.","Attila halted, in France.","The Catalaunian Plains, in Champagne, France."],"prise_de_la_smala_d_abd_el_Kader":["Capture of Abd el-Kader's Smala — Algeria","In 1843, the capture of Abd el-Kader's smala marked a turning point in the French conquest of Algeria.","In 1843, at a gallop and by surprise, a handful of horsemen swoop on a vast nomadic city on the move and capture the moving heart of a long resistance.","In 1843, a column surprises the 20,000-strong mobile camp of a resistance leader, on the high plateaus of North Africa.","The capture of Abd el-Kader's smala, in Algeria.","The capture of the Smala, at Taguin (Tiaret region), in Algeria."],"bataille_de_plassey":["Battle of Plassey — West Bengal, India","In 1757, Clive's victory secured the English East India Company's dominance over Bengal, a prelude to the British Empire in India.","In 1757, under monsoon rain, a battle decided in advance by gold and treachery more than by arms delivers a vast kingdom to the appetites of distant merchants.","In 1757, a victory won more by bribing an enemy general than by force of arms opens up a vast territory to a trading company, in the north-east of the Indian subcontinent.","The East India Company's victory, in India.","The Battle of Plassey, in Bengal, India."],"bataille_de_dien_bien_phu":["Battle of Dien Bien Phu — Vietnam","In 1954, the French defeat in this basin sealed the end of French Indochina.","In 1954, in the hollow of a valley ringed by heights, an army believes itself safe; but from the tops of the slopes, a deluge of shells out of nowhere grinds it down until it surrenders.","In 1954, an expeditionary force is trapped in a basin whose ridges are crowned with artillery hauled up on men's backs, in South-East Asia.","The defeat of a French expeditionary force, in Vietnam.","The Battle of Dien Bien Phu, in Vietnam."],"bataille_de_platees":["Battle of Plataea — Boeotia, Greece","In 479 BC, the Greek victory on land completed the repulse of the second Persian invasion.","In 479 BC, on a plain, a last great land battle finishes breaking an invasion from the East; a world of city-states keeps its freedom.","In 479 BC, a clash on land seals the failure of an invasion from the East, a year after a decisive naval battle, in south-eastern Europe.","The decisive victory of the Greek city-states over the Persians, in Greece.","The Battle of Plataea, in Boeotia, Greece."],"prise_de_babylone_par_cyrus":["Capture of Babylon by Cyrus — Babil, Iraq","In 539 BC, Cyrus the Great took the city almost without a fight and brought Mesopotamia into the Achaemenid Persian Empire.","In 539 BC, a vast city with walls reputed to be impregnable opens its gates almost without a cry; its conqueror, so it is said, shows exiles the road back to their homeland.","In 539 BC, a Mesopotamian city falls almost without a fight; its new master frees captives, in the Near East.","The capture of Babylon by Cyrus, in Iraq.","The capture of Babylon, in the Babil region, Iraq."],"bataille_d_issos":["Battle of Issus — Hatay, Turkey","In 333 BC, Alexander defeated Darius III, opening the way to the conquest of the Levant.","In 333 BC, hemmed in between the mountains and the sea, the greatest ruler of the East panics and flees, abandoning even his own kin to a young king from the west.","In 333 BC, a young Macedonian king forces a “king of kings” to flee, leaving his family behind, on a coastal plain at the crossroads of two continents.","Alexander's victory over Darius, in Turkey.","The Battle of Issus, in Hatay, Turkey."],"bataille_de_pharsale":["Battle of Pharsalus — Thessaly, Greece","In 48 BC, Caesar crushed Pompey, a decisive turning point in the Roman civil war.","In 48 BC, on a plain, two giants of the same republic play for mastery of the world; one wins, paving the way for the rule of one man.","In 48 BC, two rival Roman generals clash; the victor had crossed a small river a year earlier, in south-eastern Europe.","Caesar's victory over Pompey, in Greece.","The Battle of Pharsalus, in Thessaly, Greece."],"passage_du_rubicon":["Crossing of the Rubicon — Emilia-Romagna, Italy","In 49 BC, Caesar crossed this frontier river under arms, triggering the civil war.","In 49 BC, at the head of his legions, a general pauses on the bank of a modest stream, then crosses it; with a single step, he plunges the republic into civil war.","In 49 BC, a general crosses a small river under arms, an act of defiance summed up in a famous phrase, in the north of a Mediterranean peninsula.","Caesar's crossing of the Rubicon, in Italy.","The crossing of the Rubicon, in Emilia-Romagna, Italy."],"assassinat_de_jules_cesar":["Assassination of Julius Caesar — Rome, Italy","On the Ides of March 44 BC, Caesar was stabbed by senators in the Curia of Pompey, at the foot of the statue of his former rival.","One March morning in 44 BC, at the foot of a statue, an all-powerful master collapses under a hail of daggers; among the raised hands, he recognises that of one he loved like a son.","On the Ides of March 44 BC, a dictator is stabbed by senators, one of whom was dear to him, in the heart of a great ancient city.","The assassination of Caesar, in Italy.","The assassination of Julius Caesar, in Rome, Italy."],"bataille_de_teutobourg":["Battle of the Teutoburg Forest — Lower Saxony, Germany","In AD 9, Germanic tribes wiped out three Roman legions, halting expansion beyond the Rhine.","In the year 9, in the dark marshes of a forest, three whole armies vanish never to return; far away, an old emperor will lose sleep over it.","In the year 9, three legions disappear in a forest ambush, wringing a cry of despair from an emperor, in Central Europe.","The annihilation of Varus's legions, in Germany.","The Battle of the Teutoburg Forest, in Lower Saxony, Germany."],"siege_d_alesia":["Siege of Alesia — Côte-d'Or, France","In 52 BC, Caesar encircled Vercingetorix and completed the conquest of Gaul.","In 52 BC, the besieger becomes the besieged, caught between a stronghold and a relief army; at the end of the ordeal, a defeated chief comes to throw down his arms at the victor's feet.","In 52 BC, a besieger digs in against a relief army in turn and wins the surrender of a Gaulish chief, in the centre-east of a Western European kingdom.","The surrender of Vercingetorix to Caesar, in France.","The Siege of Alesia, in Burgundy, France."],"destruction_de_carthage":["Destruction of Carthage — Tunis, Tunisia","In 146 BC, Rome razed its great rival, bringing the Punic Wars to an end.","In 146 BC, a great city of the sea, a hated rival, is wiped off the face of the earth stone by stone — just as an old senator had tirelessly demanded.","In 146 BC, a rival maritime city is razed, its ruin demanded by a stubborn senator, on a North African coast.","The end of Carthage at the hands of Rome, in Tunisia.","The destruction of Carthage, near Tunis, Tunisia."],"prise_de_jerusalem_par_titus":["Capture of Jerusalem by Titus — Israel","In AD 70, the Romans took the city and destroyed the Second Temple.","In the year 70, a holy city succumbs after months of famine and assaults; in the flames vanishes a great temple that the world has never seen again.","In the year 70, a long siege ends with the burning of a sanctuary whose spoils will be carved on a triumphal arch, in the Near East.","The capture of Jerusalem by Titus, in Israel.","The capture of Jerusalem in 70, in Israel."],"bataille_du_pont_milvius":["Battle of the Milvian Bridge — Rome, Italy","In 312, Constantine's victory brought him to power and fostered the rise of Christianity.","In 312, on the eve of a battle by a river, a leader believes he sees a sign blazing in the sky; his victory will turn a persecuted cult into the faith of an empire.","In 312, a victory won after a vision in the sky changes the destiny of a religion, on a river of a great ancient city.","Constantine's victory, in Italy.","The Battle of the Milvian Bridge, in Rome, Italy."],"eruption_minoenne_de_thera":["Minoan eruption of Thera — Cyclades, Greece","Around 1600 BC, the volcanic explosion of Thera upended Minoan civilisation.","In the Bronze Age, a peaceful island tears itself apart in a titanic explosion and collapses into the sea; perhaps the distant memory of a sunken world.","In the Bronze Age, a volcanic cataclysm blows the centre of an island out into a caldera, perhaps the origin of a myth of a sunken land, in the Aegean Sea.","The eruption of Thera (Santorini), in Greece.","The Minoan eruption of Santorini, in the Cyclades, Greece."],"bataille_de_yarmouk":["Battle of the Yarmuk — Jordan","In 636, the Arab armies crushed the Byzantines, opening the way to the conquest of the Levant.","In 636, blinded by a sandstorm at the edge of a ravine, the soldiers of an old empire break and scatter; in a single day, a whole East changes hands.","In 636, along a deep-cut tributary and in the teeth of a sandstorm, a Byzantine army collapses and yields an entire Levant, in the Near East.","The Byzantine defeat at the hands of the Arabs, in Jordan.","The Battle of the Yarmuk, in Jordan."],"bataille_de_qadisiyya":["Battle of al-Qadisiyyah — Iraq","Around 636, the Arab victory broke the Sasanian Empire.","In 636, desert horsemen stand their ground against the elephants of a thousand-year-old empire; the wall of beasts gives way, and the road to a capital lies open.","In 636, the elephant charge of a thousand-year-old Persian empire is broken, opening its capital to the conquerors, in Mesopotamia.","The Sasanian defeat at the hands of the Arabs, in Iraq.","The Battle of al-Qadisiyyah, in Iraq."],"hegire":["Hijra of Muhammad — Medina, Saudi Arabia","In 622, Muhammad's exile to Yathrib marks the starting point of the Islamic calendar.","In 622, driven out of his city, a man who preaches one God takes the road to a distant oasis; this simple departure will become the starting point of a new era.","In 622, a persecuted preacher migrates to an oasis in the north, year one of a new calendar, on a desert peninsula.","The Hijra to Medina, in Saudi Arabia.","The Hijra, in Medina, Saudi Arabia."],"bataille_de_talas":["Battle of Talas — Talas, Kyrgyzstan","In 751, the Abbasid victory over the Tang near the Talas River halted Chinese expansion westwards; prisoners are said to have passed on the secret of papermaking.","In 751, on the fringes of the steppes, two worlds poles apart collide; through their captives, so it is said, passed an Eastern secret that would transform writing.","In 751, two empires clash on the fringes of the steppes; prisoners are said to have passed on the secret of paper there, in Central Asia.","The halt of Tang expansion against the Abbasids, in Central Asia (Kyrgyz–Kazakh border).","The Battle of Talas, in the Talas Valley, in Kyrgyzstan."],"bataille_de_manzikert":["Battle of Manzikert — Muş, Turkey","In 1071, the Byzantine defeat by the Seljuks opened Anatolia to the Turks.","In 1071, the unthinkable: an emperor falls alive into enemy hands; through the breach thus opened, horsemen from the east will overrun an entire land.","In 1071, an emperor is taken prisoner, opening a breach through which Turkish horsemen pour, in eastern Anatolia.","The Byzantine defeat at the hands of the Seljuks, in Turkey.","The Battle of Manzikert, in the Muş region, Turkey."],"prise_de_jerusalem_par_les_croises":["Capture of Jerusalem by the Crusaders — Israel","In 1099, the First Crusade took the city after a bloody siege.","In 1099, warriors from very far away seize a thrice-holy city after a ferocious siege; the streets fill with blood in the name of faith.","In 1099, a siege ends in a massacre, the culmination of an expedition preached four years earlier, in the Near East.","The capture of Jerusalem by the Crusaders, in Israel.","The capture of Jerusalem in 1099, in Israel."],"bataille_de_hattin":["Battle of Hattin — Galilee, Israel","In 1187, Saladin annihilated the Crusader army, reopening the road to Jerusalem.","In 1187, under a merciless sun and without a drop of water, an exhausted army lets itself be surrounded on arid heights; its downfall opens the gates of the holy city.","In 1187, a parched army is trapped near twin hills; the defeat hands back a holy city, in Galilee.","Saladin's victory over the Crusaders, in Israel.","The Battle of Hattin, in Galilee, Israel."],"sac_de_constantinople_(1204)":["Sack of Constantinople (1204) — Istanbul, Turkey","In 1204, the Fourth Crusade was diverted from its goal and plundered the Christian Byzantine capital.","In 1204, crusaders led astray from their path loot and burn a Christian capital instead of their goal; even its bronze horses are carried off.","In 1204, a diverted crusade sacks a Christian capital instead of its objective, on a strait between two continents.","The sack of Constantinople by the Crusaders, in Turkey.","The sack of Constantinople of 1204, in Istanbul, Turkey."],"prise_de_bagdad_par_les_mongols":["Capture of Baghdad by the Mongols — Iraq","In 1258, Hulagu destroyed the Abbasid capital, the end of the Islamic Golden Age.","In 1258, a wave from the distant steppes engulfs the most brilliant of cities and burns centuries of knowledge; a golden age ends in flames.","In 1258, a capital of learning is sacked, its libraries thrown into a river said to have turned black with ink, in Mesopotamia.","The capture of Baghdad by Hulagu, in Iraq.","The capture of Baghdad in 1258, in Iraq."],"bataille_d_ain_djalout":["Battle of Ain Jalut — Israel","In 1260, the Mamluks halted the Mongol advance, a historic turning point.","In 1260, in a valley of the East, soldiers born in chains stop dead a tide that no one had been able to halt; the myth of the invincible crumbles.","In 1260, an army of former slave soldiers halts a wave of conquest reputed to be invincible, in a valley of the Levant.","The Mongols halted by the Mamluks, in Israel.","The Battle of Ain Jalut, in Galilee, Israel."],"batailles_de_la_baie_de_hakata":["Battles of Hakata Bay (Mongol invasions) — Fukuoka, Japan","In 1274 and 1281, two providential typhoons destroyed the Mongol invasion fleets.","Twice, in the 1270s and 1280s, a colossal invasion fleet is swallowed by storms off an archipelago; people will see in it the breath of the gods.","In the 1270s and 1280s, two invasion attempts are wrecked by providential typhoons, on a bay of an East Asian archipelago.","The Mongol invasions repelled, in Japan.","The Mongol invasions of Japan, at Hakata Bay (Fukuoka), Japan."],"courtrai":["Battle of the Golden Spurs (Courtrai) — Flanders, Belgium","In 1302, the Flemish militias crushed the French knights.","In 1302, trapped by ditches and marshes, a proud body of knights is cut to pieces by humble craftsmen; their spurs are picked up by the hundred.","In 1302, knights trapped in the ditches are massacred by town militias, who collect hundreds of spurs, in the north of a Western European country.","The victory of the Flemish militias, in Belgium.","The Battle of the Golden Spurs, at Courtrai, Belgium."],"bataille_de_crecy":["Battle of Crécy — Somme, France","In 1346, English archers broke the French army, at the dawn of the Hundred Years' War.","In 1346, beneath a cloud of arrows and the first rumblings of a new kind of fire, a large and self-assured body of knights falls in whole waves.","In 1346, the longbow and early cannon mow down a large force of knights at the start of a long dynastic war, in the north of a Western European kingdom.","The English victory of Edward III, in France.","The Battle of Crécy, in the Somme, France."],"bataille_de_kulikovo":["Battle of Kulikovo — Tula, Russia","In 1380, the Russian victory over the Golden Horde set Muscovy on the road to emancipation.","In 1380, in the mist of a plain, a prince at last raises his head and defeats the masters from Asia who had been crushing him for generations; their grip begins to loosen.","In 1380, a prince defeats the tribute-taking horsemen, the first crack in a long yoke from Asia, on the plains of eastern Europe.","The victory over the Golden Horde, in Russia.","The Battle of Kulikovo, in the Tula region, Russia."],"bataille_de_kosovo_polje":["Battle of Kosovo — Kosovo","In 1389, the Serbian-Ottoman clash became a national myth in the Balkans.","In 1389, on a field swept by crows, two sovereigns fall on the same day; from this defeat, a people will make its sacred wound for centuries.","In 1389, a clash in which both sovereigns perish leaves its mark for ever on a people's memory, in the Balkans.","Serbs against Ottomans, in Kosovo.","The Battle of Kosovo (“Field of Blackbirds”), in Kosovo."],"bataille_de_grunwald":["Battle of Grunwald — Warmia-Masuria, Poland","In 1410, the Polish-Lithuanian union crushed the Teutonic Order.","In 1410, on a vast field, united peoples fall upon an order of warrior monks feared by all and annihilate it; rarely did the Middle Ages see such a mêlée.","In 1410, a Slavic-Baltic coalition crushes an order of religious knights, one of the largest battles of the Middle Ages, in the north-east of Central Europe.","The defeat of the Teutonic Knights, in Poland.","The Battle of Grunwald, in Masuria, Poland."],"bataille_de_castillon":["Battle of Castillon — Gironde, France","In 1453, French artillery brought the Hundred Years' War to an end.","In 1453, the roar of the guns gets the better of a final charge; on this field ends a war that will have lasted more than a hundred years.","In 1453, cannon gets the better of an army in the last great battle of a conflict lasting more than a century, in the south-west of a Western European kingdom.","The end of the Hundred Years' War, in France.","The Battle of Castillon, in the Gironde, France."],"bataille_de_marignan":["Battle of Marignano — Lombardy, Italy","In 1515, Francis I's victory over the Swiss, in the midst of the Italian Wars, delivered him the Duchy of Milan.","In 1515, for two days, a clash of unheard-of violence shatters the aura of invincibility of fierce pikemen from the mountains, to the benefit of a very young king.","In 1515, a two-day “battle of giants” shatters the reputation of a formidable mountain infantry, in the north of a Mediterranean peninsula.","Francis I's victory over the Swiss, in Italy.","The Battle of Marignano, in Lombardy, Italy."],"bataille_de_pavie":["Battle of Pavia — Lombardy, Italy","In 1525, the capture of Francis I confirmed the power of Charles V.","In 1525, on a field of disaster, a proud king falls into enemy hands; from the depths of his cell, he will have nothing left to save but his honour.","In 1525, a king is taken prisoner and writes that all is lost “save honour”, in the north of a Mediterranean peninsula.","The capture of Francis I by Charles V, in Italy.","The Battle of Pavia, in Lombardy, Italy."],"massacre_de_la_saint-barthelemy":["St Bartholomew's Day Massacre — Paris, France","In 1572, thousands of Protestants were killed in Paris and in the provinces.","One August morning in 1572, at the first bells, a capital descends into madness; people are slaughtered by the thousand, door to door, in the name of faith.","One August morning in 1572, to the sound of the tocsin, a religious slaughter bloodies a capital, in Western Europe.","The massacre of the Protestants, in France.","The St Bartholomew's Day Massacre, in Paris, France."],"bataille_de_la_montagne_blanche":["Battle of White Mountain — Prague, Czech Republic","In 1620, the Czech defeat ushered in three centuries of Habsburg rule.","In 1620, in barely an hour, an army collapses beneath the walls of a capital; for a whole people, a long servitude then begins.","In 1620, a defeat on a hill on the outskirts of a capital ushers in a long night for a nobility, in Central Europe.","The defeat of the Protestant rebels by the Habsburgs, in the Czech Republic.","The Battle of White Mountain, near Prague, Czech Republic."],"traites_de_westphalie":["Peace of Westphalia — Münster, Germany","In 1648, these treaties ended the Thirty Years' War and founded the modern order of states.","In 1648, after thirty years of fire and steel, a few signatures at last silence the weapons of an entire continent and sketch out a world of equal states.","In 1648, a peace signed in two towns ends thirty years of war and founds an order of sovereign states, in central Europe.","The end of the Thirty Years' War, in Germany.","The Peace of Westphalia, in Münster, Germany."],"bataille_de_rocroi":["Battle of Rocroi — Ardennes, France","In 1643, the French victory shattered the reputation of the Spanish tercios.","In 1643, a general barely out of childhood makes an infantry of squares, thought unbeatable, first buckle and then fall; the glory of an old power wavers.","In 1643, a very young general brings down an invincible square infantry, on a northern frontier of a Western European kingdom.","The victory over the Spanish tercios, in France.","The Battle of Rocroi, in the Ardennes, France."],"bataille_de_poltava":["Battle of Poltava — Ukraine","In 1709, Peter the Great crushed Charles XII, making Russia a great power.","In 1709, a thunderbolt of war from the North, unbeaten until then, goes astray and shatters far from his bases; a new power takes its place in the sun.","In 1709, a warrior king from the North is defeated at the end of an exhausting march eastwards, heralding a new great power, on the plains of the East.","Peter the Great's victory over Sweden, in Ukraine.","The Battle of Poltava, in Ukraine."],"bataille_de_plaines_d_abraham":["Battle of the Plains of Abraham — Quebec City, Canada","In 1759, the British victory sealed the fate of New France.","In 1759, at dawn, on heights overlooking a great river, a brief battle claims both opposing commanders and tips an entire overseas empire into other hands.","In 1759, on a plateau overlooking a great river, both generals perish and a colony changes hands, in North America.","The British capture of Quebec, in Canada.","The Battle of the Plains of Abraham, in Quebec City, Canada."],"boston_tea_party":["Boston Tea Party — Massachusetts, USA","In 1773, colonists threw a cargo of tea into the sea, a prelude to the American Revolution.","One night in 1773, men with painted faces hurl a hated commodity into the sea, chest after chest; this defiance of taxation lights the powder keg.","In 1773, disguised colonists drown a taxed cargo in a harbour, the spark of a revolt, on the east coast of a continent.","The tea revolt, in the United States.","The Boston Tea Party, in Boston, United States."],"bataille_de_saratoga":["Battles of Saratoga — New York, USA","In 1777, the American victory persuaded France to enter the war.","In 1777, the unhoped-for victory of a handful of rebels persuades an old monarchy to join their war; suddenly, their cause is no longer lost.","In 1777, an insurgent victory convinces a European monarchy to ally itself with young rebels, in the east of a continent.","The turning point of the War of Independence, in the United States.","The Battle of Saratoga, in New York State, United States."],"bataille_de_fort_alamo":["Battle of the Alamo — Texas, USA","In 1836, the heroic resistance to the siege became a symbol of Texan independence.","In 1836, a handful of men defend the walls of an old mission to the last; their death will become a cry that sets an entire people ablaze.","In 1836, a fortified mission falls after a siege in which all its defenders perish, becoming a rallying cry, in the south of North America.","The siege of the Alamo, in the United States.","The Battle of the Alamo, in San Antonio (Texas), United States."],"grito_de_dolores":["Cry of Dolores — Dolores Hidalgo, Mexico","In 1810, the call of the parish priest Hidalgo launched the Mexican War of Independence.","At dawn in 1810, from the top of a village bell tower, a priest's voice sends out a call that, passed from mouth to mouth, rouses an entire country against its masters.","At dawn in 1810, from a bell tower, a priest sends out a call that sparks a war of independence, in North America.","Hidalgo's cry for independence, in Mexico.","The Cry of Dolores, in the village of Dolores (Guanajuato), Mexico."],"bataille_de_boyaca":["Battle of Boyacá — Boyacá, Colombia","In 1819, Bolívar's victory sealed the independence of New Granada.","In 1819, emerging from a reckless march over icy peaks, an exhausted army swoops on the enemy and, within an hour, wrests a nation's freedom.","In 1819, after a heroic crossing of high peaks, a lightning victory at a bridge frees a territory, in the northern Andes.","Bolívar's victory, in Colombia.","The Battle of Boyacá, in Boyacá, Colombia."],"bataille_d_ayacucho":["Battle of Ayacucho — Ayacucho, Peru","In 1824, this victory sealed the independence of Spanish South America.","In 1824, on a wind-lashed roof of the world, one last great battle ends three centuries of a yoke from overseas; an entire continent breathes again.","In 1824, on a high Andean plateau, a battle ends three centuries of domination over an entire continent.","The last great battle of South American independence, in Peru.","The Battle of Ayacucho, near Quinua (Ayacucho region), in Peru."],"bataille_de_cajamarca":["Battle of Cajamarca — Cajamarca, Peru","In 1532, the capture of the Inca Atahualpa delivered the Inca Empire to Pizarro.","In 1532, in a high mountain square, a few hundred strangers treacherously seize a sovereign borne on a throne; his ransom will fill a room with gold.","In 1532, in a square, a handful of adventurers capture by trickery an emperor carried in a litter, on a high Andean plateau.","Pizarro's capture of Atahualpa, in Peru.","The Battle of Cajamarca, in Cajamarca, Peru."],"debarquement_de_christophe_colomb":["Landing of Columbus — Bahamas","In 1492, Columbus reached a Caribbean island, the first lasting contact between Europe and the Americas.","In October 1492, after long weeks of watching for land, sails reach a small unknown island; without knowing it, two worlds have just met.","In October 1492, after a long ocean crossing, a navigator lands on a small island — a contact that will upend two worlds, in a Caribbean archipelago.","Columbus's first landing, in the Bahamas.","Columbus's landing, at Guanahani, in the Bahamas."],"botany_bay":["Cook's landing at Botany Bay — Sydney, Australia","In 1770, James Cook reached the east coast, a prelude to British colonisation.","In 1770, at the end of the world, a navigator lands in a bay where a thousand unknown plants bloom; without realising it, he opens the way to a distant colonisation.","In 1770, a navigator and map-maker lands in a bay teeming with new plants, on the east coast of a southern continent.","Cook's landing, in Australia.","The landing at Botany Bay, near Sydney, Australia."],"traite_de_waitangi":["Treaty of Waitangi — Northland, New Zealand","In 1840, this treaty between the Crown and Māori chiefs founded the colony.","In 1840, beneath a tent lashed by the wind off the open ocean, a crown from afar and the chiefs of an ancient people set their mark on a pact that is still debated today.","In 1840, beneath a tent, a distant crown and indigenous chiefs sign a founding treaty, on an island in the South Pacific.","The treaty between the Crown and the Māori, in New Zealand.","The Treaty of Waitangi, in Northland, New Zealand."],"bataille_d_austerlitz":["Battle of Austerlitz — Moravia, Czech Republic","In 1805, Napoleon's victory over the Austro-Russians was his tactical masterpiece.","On an icy December morning in 1805, a sun breaks through the mist above frozen plateaus; within a few hours, a brilliant commander traps superior armies and passes into legend.","In 1805, a tactical masterpiece of the “Three Emperors”, a sun piercing the mist, in a region of central Europe.","Napoleon's victory of the Three Emperors, in the Czech Republic.","The Battle of Austerlitz, in Moravia, Czech Republic."],"bataille_de_la_moskova":["Battle of Borodino — Russia","In 1812, the bloodiest battle of the Russian campaign opened the road to Moscow.","In 1812, before a mound turned charnel house, an army prevails at the cost of its own blood; ahead of it lies open a capital soon to be given over to the flames.","In 1812, a slaughter in front of a redoubt, a Pyrrhic victory opening up a capital soon in flames, on the plains of the East.","The bloody battle of the 1812 campaign, in Russia.","The Battle of Borodino (the Moskva), in Russia."],"bataille_de_leipzig":["Battle of Leipzig — Saxony, Germany","In 1813, the “Battle of the Nations” hastened the fall of the Napoleonic Empire.","In 1813, an entire continent in league sweeps down on an isolated conqueror; under sheer weight of numbers, the greatest battle of the age swallows him up.","In 1813, an immense clash “of the Nations” in which a coalition of an entire continent overwhelms an emperor, in central Europe.","The Battle of the Nations, in Germany.","The Battle of Leipzig, in Saxony, Germany."],"sacre_de_napoleon":["Coronation of Napoleon — Paris, France","In 1804, Napoleon crowned himself emperor in Notre-Dame Cathedral.","In 1804, beneath the gilding of an illuminated cathedral, a man who rose from nothing takes the crown himself and sets it upon his own brow; no one will have given it to him.","In 1804, a new master places the crown on his own head before the eyes of the Pope, in a cathedral in a great capital.","Napoleon's imperial coronation, in France.","The coronation of Napoleon, in Paris, France."],"bataille_de_valmy":["Battle of Valmy — Marne, France","On 20 September 1792, this cannonade halted the Prussians and saved the Revolution, on the eve of the proclamation of the Republic.","In 1792, amid rain and mud, a few cannon volleys and a cry from the ranks are enough to drive back an invading army; a revolution is saved.","In 1792, a mere cannonade is enough to turn back an invading army and save a revolution, in the north-east of a Western European country.","The revolutionary victory, in France.","The Battle of Valmy, in the Marne, France."],"execution_de_louis_XVI":["Execution of Louis XVI — Paris, France","In 1793, the king was guillotined on what is now the Place de la Concorde.","On an icy January morning in 1793, in a square black with people, a king climbs the steps of a killing machine; a roll of drums drowns out his last words.","In January 1793, a king is publicly beheaded in a great square, to a roll of drums, in a Western European capital.","The execution of Louis XVI, in France.","The execution of Louis XVI, in Paris, France."],"bataille_des_pyramides":["Battle of the Pyramids — Cairo, Egypt","In 1798, Bonaparte defeated the Mamluks at Embabeh, within sight of the pyramids, during the Egyptian campaign.","In 1798, under the gaze of stone giants that have stood for millennia, a general sends his men forward, reminding them that from the heights of these monuments, the centuries look down upon them.","In 1798, a victory within sight of age-old monuments, a general haranguing his troops, in north-eastern Africa.","Bonaparte's victory over the Mamluks, in Egypt.","The Battle of the Pyramids, at Embabeh near Cairo, in Egypt."],"bataille_d_aboukir_(navale)":["Battle of the Nile — Egypt","In 1798, Nelson destroyed the French fleet, cutting off the army in Egypt.","One night in 1798, a fleet dozing at anchor is wiped out in the darkness; the explosion of a giant lights up the sea and seals the fate of an army stranded on a distant shore.","In 1798, the night-time annihilation of a fleet at anchor, a flagship exploding, on a coast of north-eastern Africa.","Nelson's victory over the French fleet, in Egypt.","The Battle of the Nile, in Aboukir Bay, Egypt."],"attaque_de_pearl_harbor":["Attack on Pearl Harbor — Hawaii, USA","On 7 December 1941, the Japanese attack brought the United States into the war.","One Sunday morning in 1941, the calm of a sunlit harbour is torn apart by a sky suddenly black with aircraft; within minutes, a giant's peace collapses in flames.","One Sunday morning in 1941, a surprise air raid on a naval base — “a date which will live in infamy” — on a Pacific island.","The surprise attack on a fleet at anchor, in the United States (Hawaii).","The attack on Pearl Harbor, in Hawaii, United States."],"debarquement_d_inchon":["Battle of Inchon — South Korea","In 1950, MacArthur's daring landing turned the tide of the Korean War.","In 1950, exploiting monstrous tides that everyone saw as an obstacle, a landing strikes where no one expected it and takes the enemy from behind; a war is turned on its head.","In 1950, an amphibious landing in a port with extreme tides, a daring gamble taking the enemy in the rear, in East Asia.","MacArthur's landing, in South Korea.","The Inchon landing, near Seoul, South Korea."],"bataille_de_gallipoli":["Gallipoli campaign — Çanakkale, Turkey","In 1915–1916, the Allied failure in the Dardanelles cost hundreds of thousands of lives.","In 1915, on sea-battered cliffs, an assault from the sea bogs down in blood; far from home, soldiers from the antipodes forge a bitter legend there.","In 1915, an Allied debacle on a peninsula commanding a strait, the deadly baptism of fire of young nations, at the crossroads of two continents.","The Allied failure in the Dardanelles, in Turkey.","The Battle of Gallipoli, at Çanakkale, Turkey."],"bataille_de_la_somme":["Battle of the Somme — Somme, France","In 1916, the offensive caused more than a million casualties, a symbol of industrial slaughter.","In 1916, on the first morning of a vast offensive, an army loses more men in a few hours than ever before; soon, strange steel shells crawl through the mud.","In 1916, an offensive whose first day remains the bloodiest in an army's history, with the first tanks, in the north of a Western European country.","The deadly offensive of 1916, in France.","The Battle of the Somme, in the Somme, France."],"bataille_de_la_marne":["First Battle of the Marne — Marne, France","In 1914, the “Miracle of the Marne” halted the German advance on Paris.","In 1914, a few leagues from a threatened capital, a last-ditch effort stops the invader dead; legend has it that hackney cabs carried the reinforcements there.","In 1914, a last-ditch effort saves a capital, with reinforcements rushed to the front in requisitioned taxis, in the north-east of a Western European country.","The “miracle” that saved Paris, in France.","The Battle of the Marne, in the Marne, France."],"revolution_d_octobre":["October Revolution — Saint Petersburg, Russia","In 1917, the Bolshevik seizure of power upended the 20th century.","One October night in 1917, a ship's gun fires a blank over a river; at this signal, the seat of power is stormed, and an old world collapses.","In October 1917, a coup around a palace and a cruiser's salvo, in a northern capital on the Baltic.","The Bolshevik revolution, in Russia.","The October Revolution, in Saint Petersburg, Russia."],"armistice_du_11_novembre_1918":["Armistice of 11 November 1918 — Oise, France","On 11 November 1918, the armistice signed in a railway carriage ended the Great War.","One November morning in 1918, in the silent clearing of a forest, a few signatures exchanged aboard a train at last silence four years of guns.","In November 1918, a ceasefire signed in a railway carriage deep in a forest, at the eleventh hour of the eleventh day, in the north of a Western European country.","The signing of the 1918 armistice, in France.","The 1918 armistice, at Rethondes (Oise), France."],"evacuation_de_dunkerque":["Dunkirk evacuation — Nord, France","In 1940, Operation Dynamo saved 340,000 encircled Allied soldiers.","In spring 1940, its back to the sea and pounded without respite, an entire army is snatched from the beaches by a motley flotilla of little boats.","In 1940, hundreds of thousands of encircled soldiers are evacuated from a beach under the bombs, on a coast in the north of a Western European country.","The Allied evacuation of 1940, in France.","The Dunkirk evacuation, in the Nord, France."],"bataille_d_el_alamein":["Second Battle of El Alamein — Egypt","In 1942, the British victory turned the tide of the desert war in North Africa.","In autumn 1942, in the scorching sands of a coastal desert, a line finally holds and breaks the momentum of an army that thought itself unstoppable.","In 1942, in a coastal desert, a bottleneck halts an advance towards a strategic canal, in north-eastern Africa.","The turning point of the desert war, in Egypt.","The Battle of El Alamein, on the Egyptian coast, Egypt."],"liberation_de_paris":["Liberation of Paris — France","In August 1944, the uprising and the arrival of the Allies liberated the capital.","In August 1944, a city rises up and friendly armour rolls through its gates; every bell peals out to hail the end of long years of occupation.","In August 1944, an uprising backed by an armoured division; every bell rings out the liberation of a Western European capital.","The liberation of the capital, in France.","The liberation of Paris, France."],"bataille_des_ardennes":["Battle of the Bulge — Belgium","In late 1944, the last German offensive in the west failed in the snow.","In winter 1944, a final onslaught bursts out of a snow-covered forest; called on to surrender, a surrounded general replies with a single scornful word.","In the winter of 1944, a last surprise offensive in a snow-covered forest; a besieged general replies with a single word, in the south-east of a small Western European country.","The last great German offensive of 1944, in Belgium.","The Battle of the Bulge, in Belgium."],"prise_du_reichstag":["Capture of the Reichstag — Berlin, Germany","In 1945, the Soviet flag raised over the Reichstag symbolised the fall of the Reich.","In spring 1945, in a capital in ashes and amid the last bursts of gunfire, soldiers haul themselves to the top of a gutted building to plant their colours; a war is drawing to a close.","In spring 1945, a flag raised on the dome of a ruined parliament, the image of a regime's fall, in a central European capital.","The fall of Berlin, in Germany.","The capture of the Reichstag, in Berlin, Germany."],"bombardement_de_nagasaki":["Atomic bombing of Nagasaki — Japan","On 9 August 1945, the second atomic bomb hastened Japan's surrender.","Three days after the unthinkable, in August 1945, a second blinding sun bears down on a port city; humanity at last grasps what it is capable of.","On 9 August 1945, the second nuclear weapon in history strikes a port city, three days after the first, on an East Asian archipelago.","The second atomic bomb, in Japan.","The bombing of Nagasaki, Japan."],"conference_de_yalta":["Yalta Conference — Crimea","In February 1945, the Big Three mapped out the post-war world there.","In February 1945, in a palace facing the sea, three exhausted men bent over a map draw the world to come — and, without saying so, its future fault lines.","In February 1945, three Allied leaders carve up the post-war world in a palace on the Black Sea, on a peninsula.","The meeting of the Big Three, in Crimea.","The Yalta Conference, in Crimea."],"proces_de_nuremberg":["Nuremberg trials — Bavaria, Germany","In 1945–1946, Nazi leaders were put on trial, laying the foundations of international criminal law.","In 1945, in the courtroom of a city in ruins, the fallen masters of a regime at last answer for their crimes; a justice that transcends nations is born there.","In 1945–1946, former leaders of a fallen regime are put on trial — the birth of international justice, in central Europe.","The trial of the Nazi leaders, in Germany.","The Nuremberg trials, in Bavaria, Germany."],"pont_aerien_de_berlin":["Berlin Airlift — Germany","In 1948–1949, an airlift supplied West Berlin, blockaded by the Soviets.","In 1948, a city surrounded and deprived of everything refuses to bow to hunger; day and night, an unbroken ballet of aircraft keeps it alive from the sky.","In 1948–1949, a city besieged by a land blockade is supplied by air, with a plane every few minutes, in central Europe.","The blockade broken by air, in Germany.","The Berlin Airlift, in Germany."],"construction_du_mur_de_berlin":["Construction of the Berlin Wall — Germany","In 1961, the building of the Wall froze the city's division for 28 years.","In a single night in August 1961, barbed wire and then concrete rise across a sleeping city; on waking, streets and families find themselves cut in two.","In a single night in August 1961, a barrier thrown up across a city splits families and neighbourhoods, in a central European capital.","The building of the Wall, in Germany.","The construction of the Berlin Wall, in Germany."],"discours_Â«_I_Have_a_Dream_Â»":["“I Have a Dream” speech — Washington, USA","In 1963, Martin Luther King galvanised the civil rights movement.","One day in 1963, at the foot of a great marble memorial, before a crowd stretching as far as the eye can see, a voice puts words to a dream of equality — and the century remembers them.","In 1963, a civil rights speech delivered at the foot of a marble memorial before a vast crowd, in a North American capital.","Martin Luther King's speech, in the United States.","The “I Have a Dream” speech, in Washington, United States."],"assassinat_de_jfk":["Assassination of John F. Kennedy — Dallas, Texas, USA","In 1963, the American president was shot dead in a motorcade, an event that shook the world.","One November noon in 1963, a few seconds of film capture the unthinkable: an official motorcade cut down in the middle of a city; a country loses its young hope.","In November 1963, shots are fired at a presidential motorcade crossing a square, a tragedy filmed by an amateur, in the south of North America.","The assassination of an American president, in the United States.","The assassination of JFK, in Dallas (Texas), United States."],"massacre_de_tian_anmen":["Tiananmen Square massacre — Beijing, China","In 1989, the crushing of the democracy movement left a lasting mark on China.","In spring 1989, a wind of freedom lifts a youth on the march; in a single night, force crushes it, leaving the world the image of naked courage.","In spring 1989, the crackdown on a student movement in a vast square, the image of a lone man facing the tanks, in East Asia.","The crackdown on the democracy movement, in China.","Tiananmen, in Beijing, China."],"liberation_de_nelson_mandela":["Release of Nelson Mandela — Cape Town, South Africa","In 1990, Mandela's release opened the way to the end of apartheid.","In 1990, after twenty-seven years behind bars, a man who has become a symbol for the whole world steps back into daylight, fist raised before a sea of tearful faces.","In 1990, a prisoner of 27 years turned global figure is freed, fist raised on the balcony of a city hall, at the tip of a continent.","Mandela's release, in South Africa.","The release of Nelson Mandela, in Cape Town, South Africa."],"premier_vol_des_freres_wright":["Wright brothers' first flight — North Carolina, USA","In 1903, the first controlled powered flight opened the age of aviation.","In December 1903, in the icy wind of deserted dunes, a flimsy machine of canvas and wood tears itself from the ground for a few seconds — and humanity at last takes flight.","In December 1903, the first controlled powered flight — a dozen seconds above the dunes — on a coast in the east of a continent.","The Wright brothers' first flight, in the United States.","The Wright brothers' first flight, at Kitty Hawk (North Carolina), United States."],"naufrage_du_titanic":["Sinking of the Titanic — North Atlantic","In 1912, the liner said to be unsinkable went down after striking an iceberg.","On an April night in 1912, freezing and moonless, the proudest of ships, sworn to be invincible, grazes a mountain of ice and slowly slips beneath the black waters.","In April 1912, a liner said to be unsinkable strikes an iceberg and sinks on a freezing night, off the Grand Banks of Newfoundland.","The sinking of the Titanic, in the North Atlantic.","The sinking of the Titanic, off Newfoundland, in the Atlantic."],"la_decouverte_d_or_de_sutter_s_mill":["Sutter's Mill gold rush — California, USA","In 1848, the discovery of gold sparked a rush that transformed the American West.","In 1848, a few flakes glinting at the bottom of a stream are enough to send tens of thousands of men, drunk on the same dream, down distant trails.","In 1848, nuggets found in a sawmill's millrace spark a rush of tens of thousands of prospectors, in the west of a continent.","The discovery of gold at Sutter's Mill, in the United States.","The 1849 gold rush, in California, United States."],"achevement_du_transcontinental_americain":["First transcontinental railroad — Utah, USA","In 1869, the joining of the rails linked the two coasts of the United States.","In 1869, in the middle of an endless desert, a final golden spike unites two railway lines coming from the two oceans; a continent is now stitched together from edge to edge.","In 1869, a golden spike seals the junction of two rail networks in the middle of a vast continent.","The joining of the transcontinental railway, in the United States.","The American transcontinental railroad, at Promontory (Utah), United States."],"inauguration_du_canal_de_suez":["Opening of the Suez Canal — Egypt","In 1869, the opening of the canal linked the Mediterranean and the Red Sea.","In 1869, amid pomp and bunting, a procession of ships glides for the first time from one sea to the other across the sands; two worlds draw closer.","In 1869, a procession of flag-decked ships inaugurates a canal linking two seas, on an isthmus in north-eastern Africa.","The opening of a waterway between the Mediterranean and the Red Sea, in Egypt.","The opening of the Suez Canal, at Ismailia, in Egypt."],"bataille_de_solferino":["Battle of Solferino — Lombardy, Italy","In 1859, this bloody battle inspired the founding of the Red Cross.","In 1859, on the evening of a battle, the sight of thousands of wounded left to die overwhelms an ordinary traveller; from his compassion a great relief effort will be born.","In 1859, a carnage whose horror inspires a witness to create a great humanitarian organisation, in the north of a Mediterranean peninsula.","The battle that gave rise to the Red Cross, in Italy.","The Battle of Solferino, in Lombardy, Italy."],"bataille_de_sedan":["Battle of Sedan — Ardennes, France","In 1870, the capture of Napoleon III hastened the fall of the Second Empire.","In 1870, surrounded on all sides, an emperor surrenders his sword and his entire army; at the news, a regime collapses and the streets rumble.","In 1870, an emperor surrenders with his whole army after being encircled, bringing down a regime, in the north-east of a Western European country.","The surrender of Napoleon III, in France.","The Battle of Sedan, in the Ardennes, France."],"commune_de_paris":["Paris Commune — France","In 1871, the Parisian uprising and its bloody suppression left their mark on working-class history.","In the spring of 1871, a capital governs itself, drunk on a dream of equality; two months later, a week of blood buries it against a cemetery wall.","In the spring of 1871, a capital runs its own affairs for two months before being crushed during a “Bloody Week”, in Western Europe.","The insurrectionary Commune, in France.","The Paris Commune, in France."],"bataille_d_isandhlwana":["Battle of Isandlwana — KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa","In 1879, the Zulus inflicted a heavy defeat on the British army.","In 1879, at the foot of a sphinx-shaped hill, a tide of near-naked warriors overwhelms a force armed with rifles; an empire's pride is swallowed up there.","In 1879, spear-wielding warriors overwhelm a column equipped with modern rifles — a colonial disaster, in the east of a country in southern Africa.","The British defeat at the hands of the Zulus, in South Africa.","The Battle of Isandlwana, in KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa."],"bataille_d_adoua":["Battle of Adwa — Tigray, Ethiopia","In 1896, the Ethiopian victory halted Italian colonisation, a case unique in Africa.","In 1896, on African highlands, an ancient kingdom routs an overconfident colonial army and, alone on an entire continent, saves its freedom.","In 1896, an African empire defeats a European colonial power and preserves its independence, in the north of the Horn of Africa.","The Ethiopian victory over a colonial power, in Ethiopia.","The Battle of Adwa, in Tigray, Ethiopia."],"bataille_d_omdurman":["Battle of Omdurman — Khartoum, Sudan","In 1898, British machine guns annihilated the Mahdist army.","In 1898, along a great river, new weapons mow down by the thousand an army charging in the open; courage is no match for the machine.","In 1898, machine guns mow down a charging army of dervishes — a technological massacre, along a great river in north-east Africa.","The British victory over the Mahdists, in Sudan.","The Battle of Omdurman, near Khartoum, in Sudan."],"revolte_des_cipayes":["Indian Rebellion of 1857 — Delhi, India","In 1857, the great Indian rebellion shook British rule.","In 1857, rumours of impure cartridges set a subjugated army ablaze; for one summer, an old, forgotten emperor is raised onto a throne of shadows.","In 1857, an uprising sparked by suspect cartridges briefly restores an old emperor to a puppet throne.","The great Indian rebellion, in India.","The Sepoy Mutiny, in Delhi, India."],"bataille_de_panipat_(1526)":["First Battle of Panipat — Haryana, India","In 1526, Babur founded the Mughal Empire with his decisive victory.","In 1526, the still-unknown thunder of cannon scatters an overconfident multitude; on this field of dust rises an empire destined to last for centuries.","In 1526, artillery and chained carts break a far larger army, founding an empire that would last for centuries.","The founding of the Mughal Empire by Babur, in India.","The Battle of Panipat, in Haryana, India."],"bataille_de_l_hydaspe":["Battle of the Hydaspes — Punjab, Pakistan","In 326 BC, Alexander defeated King Porus but reached the limit of his conquest.","Around 326 BC, beneath monsoon downpours, a tireless conqueror crushes a wall of elephants; it will be his last great pitched battle, as his men soon refuse to go any further.","In 326 BC, during the monsoon, a conqueror prevails against war elephants — his last great pitched battle, shortly before his troops refuse to advance.","Alexander's victory over Porus, in Pakistan.","The Battle of the Hydaspes, in Punjab, Pakistan."],"tsushima":["Battle of Tsushima — Korea Strait","In 1905, the Japanese fleet annihilated the Russian squadron, a geopolitical earthquake.","In 1905, in a swell-swept strait, a young Eastern navy sends to the bottom an armada worn out from sailing round the globe; the world order totters.","In 1905, a rising Asian navy annihilates a fleet that has come from the other side of the world — an earthquake in the colonial order.","The crushing Japanese naval victory, off the coast of Japan.","The Battle of Tsushima, in the Korea Strait, off the coast of Japan."],"meiji":["Meiji Restoration — Kyoto, Japan","In 1868, the imperial restoration launched Japan's lightning-fast modernisation.","In 1868, an archipelago suddenly turns a page several centuries long: the warriors' sword bows before a very young sovereign, and the country rushes headlong into the machine age.","In 1868, power returns to a young emperor; the end of a warrior regime and an archipelago's leap into the industrial age.","The Meiji imperial restoration, in Japan.","The Meiji Restoration, in Kyoto, Japan."],"dan_no_ura":["Battle of Dan-no-ura — Yamaguchi, Japan","In 1185, this naval battle ended the Genpei War and founded the shogunate.","In 1185, in a strait of seething currents, a defeated clan throws itself into the waves with its child sovereign; on these waters the age of warriors begins.","In 1185, a naval battle in a strait with strong currents swallows up a child emperor and his defeated clan — the dawn of warrior rule.","The end of the Genpei War, in Japan.","The Battle of Dan-no-ura, Shimonoseki Strait, in Japan."],"rpc_1949":["Proclamation of the People's Republic of China — Beijing, China","In 1949, Mao proclaimed the PRC from the Gate of Heavenly Peace.","In October 1949, from atop an immense gate, a deep voice announces to an endless crowd the birth of a new world, after decades of fratricidal fury.","In October 1949, from the balcony of a monumental gate, a leader proclaims a people's republic at the end of a long civil war.","The proclamation of a great people's republic, in China.","The proclamation of the PRC, in Beijing, China."],"boxers":["Boxer Rebellion — Beijing, China","In 1900, the anti-foreign uprising was crushed by an international coalition.","In 1900, an ancestral fury rises up against the foreigner; behind besieged walls, people watch for weeks for relief coming from afar.","In 1900, secret martial-arts societies rise up against foreigners; besieged legations are relieved by eight powers.","The Boxer Rebellion, in China.","The Boxer Rebellion, in Beijing, China."],"longue_marche":["Start of the Long March — Jiangxi, China","In 1934, the Chinese communists' epic 12,000 km retreat forged their myth.","In 1934, a hunted army sets off on an endless flight over mountains and torrents; this titanic ordeal will forge a regime and the legend of its leader.","In 1934, the start of a retreat of over 10,000 km across mountains and rivers, the founding ordeal of a future regime and its leader.","The beginning of the Long March, in China.","The Long March, starting from Jiangxi, in China."],"tet":["Tet Offensive — Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam","In 1968, the surprise offensive turned American public opinion against the war.","In the midst of the 1968 lunar new year festivities, an offensive springing from nowhere strikes into the heart of the cities; on the other side of the earth, public opinion shifts.","In 1968, a surprise lunar new year offensive carries a commando unit right into an embassy — a shock that turns distant public opinion.","The Tet Offensive, in Vietnam.","The Tet Offensive, in Saigon (Ho Chi Minh City), Vietnam."],"revolution_iran":["Iranian Revolution — Tehran, Iran","In 1979, the fall of the Shah established an Islamic republic.","In 1979, a human tide sweeps away a throne; from a long exile returns an old man in robes, hailed as a guide by millions of voices.","In 1979, a revolution overthrows a monarchy and brings back from exile a religious dignitary cheered by immense crowds.","The Islamic revolution, in Iran.","The Iranian Revolution, in Tehran, Iran."],"bunker_hill":["Battle of Bunker Hill — Charlestown (Boston), USA","In 1775, this costly battle showed that the rebels could stand up to the British.","In 1775, on a height overlooking a harbour, so much blood is spilt to take a position that the victor weeps over his victory.","In 1775, on a hill overlooking a harbour, a victory so costly for the redcoats that it tasted of defeat.","The Battle of Bunker Hill, in the United States.","The Battle of Bunker Hill, near Boston, in the United States."],"trenton":["Battle of Trenton — New Jersey, USA","In 1776, Washington crossed the ice-choked Delaware by night for a surprise victory that revived the revolution.","In the depths of a desperate winter, on Christmas night 1776, a cornered force attempts a daring stroke in the cold and ice, and revives a faltering cause.","On Christmas night 1776, the crossing of an ice-choked river makes it possible to surprise a sleeping garrison.","Washington's surprise victory, in the United States.","The Battle of Trenton, in New Jersey, in the United States."],"puebla":["Battle of Puebla (Cinco de Mayo) — Puebla, Mexico","In 1862, the Mexican victory over the French is commemorated every 5 May.","On 5 May 1862, an outnumbered force stands its ground and brings to its knees an army from Europe said to be unbeatable.","On 5 May 1862, an outnumbered army prevails against a European expeditionary force reputed to be invincible.","The Cinco de Mayo victory, in Mexico.","The Battle of Puebla, in Puebla, Mexico."],"camerone":["Battle of Camarón — Veracruz, Mexico","In 1863, the Foreign Legion's heroic resistance became its founding legend.","In 1863, in a sun-scorched farmhouse, a handful of men swear never to surrender and hold out against a tide of attackers to the very last.","In 1863, a handful of legionnaires entrenched in a farmhouse hold out at odds of one against sixty; a captain's wooden hand became a relic.","The Foreign Legion's stand at Camerone, in Mexico.","The Battle of Camarón (Camerone), in the state of Veracruz, Mexico."],"krach_1929":["Wall Street Crash of 1929 — New York, USA","In 1929, the stock market collapse triggered the worldwide Great Depression.","In the autumn of 1929, in a narrow street where the heart of money beats, the figures come tumbling down; a decade of misery begins.","In October 1929, a stock market collapse in a narrow street of finance ushers in a decade of worldwide misery.","The stock market crash of 1929, in the United States.","The Wall Street Crash, in New York, in the United States."],"marche_rome":["March on Rome — Italy","In 1922, the Fascist march brought Mussolini to power.","In 1922, a tide of dark shirts converges on a capital; almost without firing a shot, it carries a man to the summit of the state.","In 1922, an intimidating parade of coloured shirts towards a capital opens the doors of power to their leader, without any real fighting.","The Fascist march on the capital, in Italy.","The March on Rome, in Rome, Italy."],"onze_septembre":["September 11 attacks — New York, USA","In 2001, the attacks on the Twin Towers upended world geopolitics.","Under a clear September sky in 2001, the world tips over live on screen: two glass colossi collapse, and an era of certainties with them.","One September morning in 2001, coordinated attacks strike two twin towers in a business district — the geopolitical turning point of the new century.","The attacks on the World Trade Center, in the United States.","The 9/11 attacks, in New York, in the United States."],"cheroneen":["Battle of Chaeronea — Boeotia, Greece","In 338 BC, Philip II of Macedon subdued the Greek city-states.","In 338 BC, the freedom of jealous city-states dies under the spear of a king from the north; an elite corps falls there to the last man, watched over ever since by a stone lion.","In 338 BC, a king from the North subdues jealous city-states; a battalion of lovers reputed invincible is wiped out beneath a stone lion.","Philip II's victory over the Greek city-states, in Greece.","The Battle of Chaeronea, in Boeotia, Greece."],"leuctres":["Battle of Leuctra — Boeotia, Greece","In 371 BC, Thebes broke Spartan hegemony.","In 371 BC, a daring manoeuvre overthrows on the battlefield the city of warriors thought invincible; a supremacy crumbles.","In 371 BC, an innovative oblique order brings down the supremacy of a warrior city reputed unbeatable.","The Theban victory over Sparta, in Greece.","The Battle of Leuctra, in Boeotia, Greece."],"alexandrie":["Founding of Alexandria — Egypt","In 331 BC, Alexander founded the city that would become a beacon of ancient learning.","Around 331 BC, by the waves, a conqueror traces on the ground the outline of a city that will soon bear a lighthouse touching the sky and all the knowledge of the world.","In 331 BC, a conqueror founds a city on a coast, the future home of a giant lighthouse and a universal library.","The founding of Alexandria by Alexander, in Egypt.","The founding of Alexandria, on the Nile Delta, in Egypt."],"boudicca":["Boudica's revolt — London, England","Around AD 60–61, Queen Boudica roused the Britons and burned Londinium.","In AD 60, a queen's humiliation sets a whole island ablaze; three cities go up in smoke before the revolt is drowned in blood.","In AD 60, an outraged Celtic queen leads an uprising; three Roman towns are torched before the final crushing defeat.","Boudica's revolt, in England.","Boudica's revolt, in London (Londinium), England."],"las_navas":["Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa — Jaén, Spain","In 1212, the Christian coalition won a decisive victory of the Reconquista.","In 1212, long-divided kingdoms at last unite their banners and break the power of a caliphate — a long reconquest reaches its tipping point.","In 1212, a coalition of Christian kingdoms prevails over a caliphate — a turning point in a long reconquest of the peninsula.","A decisive victory of the Reconquista, in Spain.","Las Navas de Tolosa, in the province of Jaén, Spain."],"acre_1291":["Siege of Acre (1291) — Israel","In 1291, the capture of the last crusader stronghold put an end to the Latin states of the East.","In 1291, on a Mediterranean shore, the last fortress held by fighters from Europe is taken by storm; a long martial saga fades away into the waves.","In 1291, the last coastal bastion of a Latin presence in the East falls — the end of two centuries of crusades.","The capture of Acre by the Mamluks, in Israel.","The fall of Acre, in Galilee, Israel."],"morat":["Battle of Morat — Fribourg, Switzerland","In 1476, the Confederates crushed Charles the Bold.","In 1476, the pride of a conquering prince shatters for the second time against the human wall of a small, free people; his end is already near.","In 1476, mountain pikemen crush an ambitious duke — the second in a series of defeats that will soon cost him his life.","The victory of the Swiss Confederate cantons over Charles the Bold, in Switzerland.","The Battle of Morat, canton of Fribourg, Switzerland."],"peste_1347":["Arrival of the Black Death — Messina, Sicily, Italy","In 1347, Genoese ships from Caffa brought the plague into Western Europe through this port.","In 1347, ships come alongside, their holds full of the dead and the dying; within them travels a scourge that will mow down a third of a continent.","In 1347, ships dock with dying crews, carrying from the East a scourge that would carry off a third of a continent.","The arrival of the Black Death in Western Europe, in Italy.","The arrival of the Black Death, in Messina (Sicily), Italy."],"lechfeld":["Battle of Lechfeld — Bavaria, Germany","In 955, Otto I stopped the Hungarians, securing Central Europe.","In 955, a single day puts an end to decades of raids from the east; the victorious king already pictures himself girded with an imperial crown.","In 955, a victory ends the devastating raids of horsemen from the east and crowns a king soon to be emperor.","Otto I's victory over the Hungarians, in Germany.","The Battle of Lechfeld, near Augsburg (Bavaria), Germany."],"stamford_bridge":["Battle of Stamford Bridge — Yorkshire, England","In 1066, Harold drove back the Norwegians just before Hastings.","In September 1066, a king triumphs over the raiders from the North; but his glory lasts no more than a breath, as another peril is already rising from the sea, to the south.","In September 1066, a Saxon king defeats invaders from the North — a fleeting triumph three weeks before a disaster in the south.","Harold's victory over the Norwegians, in England.","The Battle of Stamford Bridge, in Yorkshire, England."],"nicopolis":["Battle of Nicopolis — Bulgaria","In 1396, the last great crusade failed against the Ottomans.","In 1396, the flower of an overconfident chivalry comes to shatter, on the banks of a great river, against an enemy it despised.","In 1396, an overconfident Western chivalry is routed by the Ottomans on the banks of a great river — the last great crusade.","A crusade crushed on the Danube, in Bulgaria.","The Battle of Nicopolis, on the Danube, in Bulgaria."],"varna":["Battle of Varna — Bulgaria","In 1444, the death of King Władysław sealed the failure of the anti-Ottoman crusade.","In 1444, a young king's recklessness costs him his life in a mad charge; with him collapses the last hope of stemming a tide rising from the East.","In 1444, a young king falls in a reckless charge, shattering hopes of stemming an Ottoman advance into Europe.","A crusade crushed near the Black Sea, in Bulgaria.","The Battle of Varna, on the Black Sea, in Bulgaria."],"blenheim":["Battle of Blenheim (Höchstädt) — Bavaria, Germany","In 1704, Marlborough and Prince Eugene broke the Franco-Bavarian army.","In 1704, on the banks of a great river, two allied generals break the momentum of a king who dreamt of dominating Europe.","In 1704, a pair of allied generals wins on the banks of a great river, halting the ambitions of a Sun King.","Marlborough's victory, in Germany.","The Battle of Blenheim, on the Danube (Bavaria), Germany."],"fontenoy":["Battle of Fontenoy — Hainaut, Belgium","In 1745, the French victory epitomised the courtly warfare of the 18th century.","In 1745, two armies clash for hours on an undecided plain; in the end it is the arrival of a reserve and the composure of an old marshal that carry the day.","In 1745, a victory preceded, legend has it, by a courteous invitation to the enemy to fire first.","Marshal de Saxe's victory, in Belgium.","The Battle of Fontenoy, in Hainaut, Belgium."],"friedland":["Battle of Friedland — Kaliningrad, Russia","In 1807, Napoleon's victory forced Russia to negotiate the Peace of Tilsit.","In 1807, its back to the river and with no way out, an army is broken; shortly afterwards, two emperors embrace on a raft in the middle of the water.","In 1807, an army is pinned against a river; the defeat drives two emperors to seal a peace on a raft a few days later.","A Napoleonic victory before Tilsit, in present-day Russia.","The Battle of Friedland, Kaliningrad region, Russia."],"wagram":["Battle of Wagram — Lower Austria, Austria","In 1809, Napoleon defeated Austria after a massive battle.","In 1809, after a first assault drowned in blood on the banks of a river, an unprecedented deluge of fire finally decides the outcome.","In 1809, an unprecedented concentration of artillery snatches victory after a first bloody setback on a river island.","Napoleon's victory over the Austrians, in Austria.","The Battle of Wagram, north of Vienna, in Austria."],"navarin":["Battle of Navarino — Messenia, Greece","In 1827, the Ottoman fleet was destroyed, paving the way for Greek independence.","In 1827, trapped in an enclosed bay, an empire's sails burn under the crossfire of three powers come to the aid of a people in revolt.","In 1827, three European navies annihilate an Ottoman fleet in an enclosed bay, to support an insurrection.","The destruction of the Ottoman fleet, in Greece.","The Battle of Navarino, in Messenia, Greece."],"alger_1830":["Invasion of Algiers — Algeria","In 1830, the French landing opened 132 years of colonisation.","In 1830, to avenge the insult of a mere gesture, a fleet lands on the shore of a southern bay; so begins more than a century of domination.","In 1830, a landing west of a bay, on the pretext of a “fly-whisk incident”, launches more than a century of colonisation.","The capture of Algiers, in Algeria.","The capture of Algiers, in the Bay of Algiers, Algeria."],"sadowa":["Battle of Königgrätz (Sadowa) — Czech Republic","In 1866, the Prussian victory over Austria paved the way for German unification around Prussia.","In 1866, in a single day, rifles of a new kind settle an old rivalry and decide who will build an empire.","In 1866, an army with breech-loading rifles settles in a single day the rivalry over the unification of a future empire.","The Prussian victory over the Austrians, in the Czech Republic.","The Battle of Königgrätz (Sadowa), in Bohemia, Czech Republic."],"koursk":["Battle of Kursk — Russia","In 1943, the largest tank battle in history broke the German offensive in the East.","In the summer of 1943, amid furnace heat and dust, thousands of steel monsters collide — never had the earth borne such a clash.","In the summer of 1943, the largest armoured clash in history: a salient caught in a pincer where thousands of tanks collide.","The largest tank battle, in Russia.","The Battle of Kursk, in Russia."],"iwo_jima":["Battle of Iwo Jima — Ogasawara Islands, Japan","In 1945, the capture of the volcanic island gave rise to a world-famous photograph.","In 1945, on an island of black ash, a flag rises on the smoking summit; the moment, caught by a lens, will become eternal.","In 1945, an assault on a volcanic island of black ash; a flag planted on the summit is immortalised in a photo that became an icon.","The capture of an island at Mount Suribachi, in the Pacific.","The Battle of Iwo Jima, Ogasawara Islands, Japan."],"dresde":["Bombing of Dresden — Saxony, Germany","In 1945, Allied raids destroyed the city in a firestorm.","In February 1945, in a few hours of fire, a city of art and honey-coloured stone becomes nothing but an inferno; only ashes will remain.","In February 1945, raids unleash a firestorm that ravages a Baroque city nicknamed “Florence on the Elbe”.","The bombing of a Baroque city on the Elbe, in Germany.","The bombing of Dresden, in Saxony, Germany."],"reichstag_1933":["Reichstag fire — Berlin, Germany","In 1933, the parliament fire served as a pretext for the Nazi seizure of power.","In 1933, the flames of a parliament set a night ablaze; on their ashes, a nascent regime stifles every freedom at a stroke.","In 1933, the arson of a parliament serves as an immediate pretext for suspending civil liberties and seizing total power.","The burning of a parliament, in Germany.","The Reichstag fire, in Berlin, Germany."],"potsdam":["Potsdam Conference — Brandenburg, Germany","In 1945, the victors organised occupied Germany and the post-war order there.","In the summer of 1945, beneath a palace's panelled ceilings, the masters of a world in ashes divide up the spoils of a fallen empire — already eaten away by mistrust.","In the summer of 1945, in a palace, three victors — one of them replaced midway — seal the fate of a defeated country.","The 1945 conference of the victors, in Germany.","The Potsdam Conference, in Brandenburg, Germany."],"missiles_cuba":["Cuban Missile Crisis — Cuba","In 1962, the discovery of Soviet missiles brought the world to the brink of nuclear war.","In the autumn of 1962, an unprecedented fear grips the planet: at home, people prepare shelters and stay glued to the radio, while at the summit of power, two men hold back the thunderbolt.","In October 1962, for thirteen days, missile launchers spotted on a tropical island bring two blocs to the edge of the abyss.","The 1962 missile crisis, in Cuba.","The Cuban Missile Crisis, in Cuba."],"woodstock":["Woodstock Festival — Bethel (New York State), USA","In 1969, the festival drew nearly 500,000 people, a symbol of the counterculture.","In the summer of 1969, in the mud of a rain-soaked field, a tide of young people turns three days of music into the manifesto of a generation.","In the summer of 1969, half a million spectators in a muddy field make a music festival the symbol of a generation.","A legendary music festival, in the United States.","The Woodstock Festival, in New York State, in the United States."],"golfe_1991":["Gulf War (liberation of Kuwait) — Kuwait City, Kuwait","In 1991, an international coalition drove the Iraqi army out of Kuwait.","In 1991, a coalition strikes down an invader in a matter of days; behind it, blazing wells stretch a sky of soot over the desert.","In 1991, a lightning coalition offensive liberates a small, invaded oil emirate; burning wells blacken the desert sky.","The liberation of an invaded emirate, in Kuwait.","The Gulf War (liberation of Kuwait), in Kuwait."],"cuzco_1533":["Fall of Cusco — Peru","In 1533, the capture of the Inca capital completed the Spanish conquest of Peru.","In 1533, armed strangers seize a city perched in the clouds, its stones so tightly fitted that no blade can slip between them — the heart of an empire of the sun.","In 1533, conquerors take a high-altitude capital with perfectly fitted stone walls, the heart of an empire of the sun.","The fall of the Inca capital, in Peru.","The fall of Cusco, in the Andes, in Peru."]}});
